import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "Acts",
  display: "Acts",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Acts can be considered a continuation of the Gospel of Luke. It is supposed that Luke is the writer of this book for a few reasons, but the main reason is that he starts this book off the same way he starts his gospel account, mentioning this “Theophilus” person. Who Theophilus is, we will discuss in the coming verses."},{"kind":"para","text":"We see a theme of travel and gospel sharing throughout this entire book. We also witness the initiation and growth of the first church that Christ starts after His ascension. The disciples may not have known that they were building churches off the bat, but the Holy Spirit eventually makes it very clear what their purpose and direction are supposed to be."},{"kind":"para","text":"People have a desire to learn about locations, directions, and maps of all the places mentioned. I won’t be focusing too much on those details (though I will explain as much as I can in writing about where they were likely to be located and the distances between the travels). My goal is to focus on the people. That is what the church is; it’s the people. Not the location, not the demographic, not the religion… The people are what matter to the story, and it’s what matters to the moving of the Spirit. May we keep our hearts open to people as we go through these pages."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"The Writers Introduction","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Luke continues his letter to Theophilus, who was the main dictator of the gospel of Luke."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"It seemed good to me also, having had perfect understanding of all things from the very first, to write unto thee in order, most excellent Theophilus,","ref":"Luke 1:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"No one knows much about who Theophilus is, but some have given interesting assertions about who and how important Theophilus was. The name “Theophilus” means “the lover of God.” This could insinuate that this man may have been a Gentile, and probably Greek, seeing as the name was of Greek origin. He seems to have been a relatively new believer, recently instructed about Jesus and the Christian faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"My assumption is that Theophilus was either a leader in some regards and had paid Luke to gather as much of an account as possible about the life and legacy of Jesus Christ and whether these things that were being talked about regarding who He was were actually true, or he had a genuine interest in the ministry and desired to learn as much as possible straight from the mouth of the believers. Luke took it upon himself (possibly knowing who Paul is, because they journeyed quite extensively together as we will see soon enough) to gather the information to support both the gospel and what takes place from those days forward."},{"kind":"para","text":"Luke’s gospel has some extremely deep details that some of the other gospels had left out. Luke certainly took his time in speaking to as many people as possible who were involved, which gives credence and clarity to what he is writing in both the gospel of Luke and the book of Acts. Possibly seeing the church growing in believers, and Luke being a believer himself, he wanted to get an account of how the church came to be, not just end off as if the ascension of Jesus was all that was left."}]},{"start":2,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That’s why this second verse starts the way it does in the “instructions” to the disciples to go into all the world and preach the gospel, which is referenced in every single gospel account after Jesus rose again. The term “infallible” means “from which something is surely and plainly known.” Some may say that belief in Christ is childish because you are believing in something that is so simple and plain. In all honesty, it is."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And said, Verily I say unto you, Except ye be converted, and become as little children, ye shall not enter into the kingdom of heaven.","ref":"Matthew 18:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"The reason it is so plain and simple, and the proof is too easy and good to be true, is because Christ desires even the little children to come to Him in understanding. We must grow in our faith towards Jesus Christ, start completely new, and learn everything we can about Him until our death."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":8,"title":"The Holy Spirit Will Be Given","entries":[{"start":4,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The promise that is spoken about is the empowering of the Holy Spirit."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And as the people were in expectation, and all men mused in their hearts of John, whether he were the Christ, or not; John answered, saying unto them all, I indeed baptize you with water; but one mightier than I cometh, the latchet of whose shoes I am not worthy to unloose: he shall baptize you with the Holy Ghost and with fire: Whose fan is in his hand, and he will throughly purge his floor, and will gather the wheat into his garner; but the chaff he will burn with fire unquenchable.","ref":"Luke 3:15-17"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And, behold, I send the promise of my Father upon you: but tarry ye in the city of Jerusalem, until ye be endued with power from on high.","ref":"Luke 24:49"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is speaking primarily about the day of Pentecost, which will be looked at further (spoiler alert) in the next chapter. But John the Baptist even speaks about his “repentance” baptism with Jesus’ “Holy Spirit” baptism."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I indeed have baptized you with water: but he shall baptize you with the Holy Ghost.","ref":"Mark 1:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Acts speaks A LOT about baptism and gifts of the Holy Spirit. 6 When they therefore were come together, they asked of him, saying, Lord, wilt thou at this time restore again the kingdom to Israel?"},{"kind":"para","text":"The place in which they came together was “The Mount of Olives” as verse 12 references. They are asking about an Old Testament promise of Jesus restoring His kingdom to Israel, after the Savior is victorious. They seem to be expectant of a military and political kingdom, but as Matthew determines, that kingdom is “at hand.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And saying, Repent ye: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.","ref":"Matthew 3:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is to insinuate that it is already here! Jesus Christ is already on the throne!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And immediately I was in the spirit: and, behold, a throne was set in heaven, and one sat on the throne.","ref":"Revelation 4:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is also not to mention that John the Revelator reveals all the military and kingship of the end times in his book of Revelation (which was not quite written at this particular time)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"After this I beheld, and, lo, a great multitude, which no man could number, of all nations, and kindreds, and people, and tongues, stood before the throne, and before the Lamb, clothed with white robes, and palms in their hands;","ref":"Revelation 7:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, Jesus gives an answer to this in the following verse… 7 And he said unto them, It is not for you to know the times or the seasons, which the Father hath put in his own power. 8 But ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me both in Jerusalem, and in all Judaea, and in Samaria, and unto the uttermost part of the earth."},{"kind":"para","text":"In other words, “Don’t worry about that! Worry about the task I’ve already given you, which is to be witnesses for the kingdom that has already been established through my death, burial, and resurrection!” God controls the events of history. He determines when things will take place. Our job is not to look for this sign or that sign, but to simply be faithful to God’s calling, waiting, and anticipating His return. We are to wait and pray! Someone once said, “The promises of God are the moulds into which we pour our prayer.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Verse 8 gives the theme to this entire book of the Bible. God’s spirit is what begins the empowering of our witness for Jesus. If we don’t have the spirit, we don’t have the desire to tell others about that self-same spirit. If we do have the spirit, we cannot but help ourselves to the necessity of teaching others about Christ."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For though I preach the gospel, I have nothing to glory of: for necessity is laid upon me; yea, woe is unto me, if I preach not the gospel!","ref":"1 Corinthians 9:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Even more so, this specific chapter and verse incredibly (either intentionally or spiritually led) outlines the rest of the book! It speaks about Jerusalem, which will be the basis location of chapters 1-7. It speaks about Judea and Samaria, which will take place in chapters 8-12. It speaks about the uttermost parts of the earth, taking place in chapters 13-28 of this book."},{"kind":"para","text":"Not only does the Spirit of God bring power to preach (and lay necessity on sharing your faith), it also brings the ability to overcome sin."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Likewise reckon ye also yourselves to be dead indeed unto sin, but alive unto God through Jesus Christ our Lord. Let not sin therefore reign in your mortal body, that ye should obey it in the lusts thereof. Neither yield ye your members as instruments of unrighteousness unto sin: but yield yourselves unto God, as those that are alive from the dead, and your members as instruments of righteousness unto God. For sin shall not have dominion over you: for ye are not under the law, but under grace.","ref":"Romans 6:11-14"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I am crucified with Christ: nevertheless I live; yet not I, but Christ liveth in me: and the life which I now live in the flesh I live by the faith of the Son of God, who loved me, and gave himself for me.","ref":"Galatians 2:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Spirit brings the ability to overcome Satan…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For though we walk in the flesh, we do not war after the flesh: (For the weapons of our warfare are not carnal, but mighty through God to the pulling down of strong holds;)","ref":"2 Corinthians 10:3-4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ye are of God, little children, and have overcome them: because greater is he that is in you, than he that is in the world.","ref":"1 John 4:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"And the Spirit provides gifts for ministry… “And he gave some, apostles; and some, prophets; and some, evangelists; and some, pastors and teachers; for the perfecting of the saints, for the work of the ministry, for the edifying of the body of Christ: till we all come in the unity of the faith, and of the knowledge of the Son of God, unto a perfect man, unto the measure of the stature of the fulness of Christ:” Ephesians 4:11-13"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"As every man hath received the gift, even so minister the same one to another, as good stewards of the manifold grace of God.","ref":"1 Peter 4:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"All of these aspects will be highlighted in this book as well! Every time the word “power” is represented in the book of Acts, it is always followed by a proclamation of the gospel. So, when we see the Holy Spirit working through the book of Acts, look for the word “power” and look for the gospel because they will always walk hand in hand with God’s Spirit."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the Holy Ghost shall teach you in the same hour what ye ought to say.","ref":"Luke 12:12"}]}]},{"start":9,"end":11,"title":"Christ’s Ascension","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God uses the clouds a lot when revealing His presence. Luke has mentioned the presence of God once before with a cloud."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass about an eight days after these sayings, he took Peter and John and James, and went up into a mountain to pray. And as he prayed, the fashion of his countenance was altered, and his raiment was white and glistering. And, behold, there talked with him two men, which were Moses and Elias: Who appeared in glory, and spake of his decease which he should accomplish at Jerusalem. But Peter and they that were with him were heavy with sleep: and when they were awake, they saw his glory, and the two men that stood with him. And it came to pass, as they departed from him, Peter said unto Jesus, Master, it is good for us to be here: and let us make three tabernacles; one for thee, and one for Moses, and one for Elias: not knowing what he said. While he thus spake, there came a cloud, and overshadowed them: and they feared as they entered into the cloud. And there came a voice out of the cloud, saying, This is my beloved Son: hear him. And when the voice was past, Jesus was found alone. And they kept it close, and told no man in those days any of those things which they had seen.","ref":"Luke 9:28-36"},{"kind":"para","text":"This verse gives proof that Jesus wasn’t just taken or teleported. He was physically taken into the sky, which also bears record that He was in His physical body when He left this Earth. His hands and feet were never healed, to reveal the necessity of our faith in Him to be healed from our own sin, through His scars will never be healed. 10 And while they looked stedfastly toward heaven as he went up, behold, two men stood by them in white apparel;"},{"kind":"para","text":"These two men were believed to be angels in human form. The assumption of them being angels is because of their white robes in which they wore, linking to John’s account of the angels."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And seeth two angels in white sitting, the one at the head, and the other at the feet, where the body of Jesus had lain.","ref":"John 20:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"It would make the most sense, especially because they reveal that they know a lot about Jesus and His second coming in the next verse. 11 Which also said, Ye men of Galilee, why stand ye gazing up into heaven? this same Jesus, which is taken up from you into heaven, shall so come in like manner as ye have seen him go into heaven."},{"kind":"para","text":"He will come in the same body, and He will come back on a cloud in preparation to rule forever, completely destroying death, sin, and pain."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Behold, he cometh with clouds; and every eye shall see him, and they also which pierced him: and all kindreds of the earth shall wail because of him. Even so, Amen.","ref":"Revelation 1:7"}]}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"title":"Praying Together","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Fun fact, a Sabbath day’s journey was the maximum distance one could travel on the Sabbath without it being considered work, according to later Jewish tradition. Rabbis set the limit at 2,000 cubits (which is about 0.6 miles). I don’t believe the disciples went that far specifically out of Jewish custom or tradition, or some sort of works-based faith. I believe they did it (mostly because of Christ who is still Lord of the harvest but) because they were to start their ministry in Jerusalem and work outwards. God will eventually lead them all in the direction they must go for the start and growth of the churches and ministries. 13 And when they were come in, they went up into an upper room, where abode both Peter, and James, and John, and Andrew, Philip, and Thomas, Bartholomew, and Matthew, James the son of Alphaeus, and Simon Zelotes, and Judas the brother of James."},{"kind":"para","text":"This verse gives the account of the remaining disciples, all of whom were present for Jesus’ ascension and could give eyewitness testimony to the account. Luke is pointing out that his facts weren’t just made up but were given to him by several trusted sources, including Jesus’ family and other women in the next verse… 14 These all continued with one accord in prayer and supplication, with the women, and Mary the mother of Jesus, and with his brethren."},{"kind":"para","text":"The women were more than likely those who ministered to Jesus’ followers."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And certain women, which had been healed of evil spirits and infirmities, Mary called Magdalene, out of whom went seven devils, and Joanna the wife of Chuza Herod’s steward, and Susanna, and many others, which ministered unto him of their substance.","ref":"Luke 8:2-3"},{"kind":"para","text":"This could also include many of the apostles’ wives as well. We do know, and have numerous accounts, that there were other women that followed Jesus aside from the twelve disciples. Mary Magdalene being the first one to take record of the resurrection of Jesus, as Mark points out, and Luke gives accounts of those that traveled with them in his gospel account. Mark also gives account of Jesus having four brothers through His mother Mary."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Is not this the carpenter, the son of Mary, the brother of James, and Joses, and of Juda, and Simon? And are not his sister here with us? And they were offended by him. But Jesus said unto them, A prophet is not without honour, but in his own country, and among his own kin, and in his own house.","ref":"Mark 6:3-4"},{"kind":"para","text":"Anyone that may worship the virgin Mary as remaining a virgin must have missed the scripture stating otherwise (not to mention both letters from James and Jude were written by Jesus’ brothers James and Jude (or Juda as he’s referred to here)). It was not news that Jesus had brothers, and if it was news, Luke doesn’t seem to really be surprised that they were with the disciples and Mary on this particular day of meeting."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":26,"title":"Judas Replaced","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Peter becomes quite the leader and spokesman for Christ and the disciples through the book of Acts. In fact, a good majority of the first part of Acts (chapters 1-15) gives credit to Peter being the main speaker. Which reveals that (even though Peter denied Christ three times) Christ can use those that are unfaithful and change their entire lives through repentance and salvation (not to say that Peter was not saved before his denial, but to say that his faith was renewed through repentance). 16 Men and brethren, this scripture must needs have been fulfilled, which the Holy Ghost by the mouth of David spake before concerning Judas, which was guide to them that took Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter is literally giving account and validity to the scripture being inspired by God. David (in the book of Psalms) has so many references to Jesus’ life, betrayal, crucifixion, and foreknowledge of salvation. Peter didn’t miss that connection, and this gives further credence to God’s Word being solid and infallible."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Yea, mine own familiar friend, in whom I trusted, which did eat of my bread, hath lifted up his heel against me.","ref":"Psalm 41:9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He keepeth all his bones: not one of them is broken.","ref":"Psalm 34:20"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"They part my garments among them, and cast lots upon my vesture.","ref":"Psalm 22:18"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For dogs have compassed me: the assembly of the wicked have inclosed me: they pierced my hands and my feet.","ref":"Psalm 22:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"17 For he was numbered with us, and had obtained part of this ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"Fair warning. Satan can attack any number of us, and will do his best to divide. None of us are untouchable, and all of us are unprepared. We cannot afford to keep our guards down, and we must always be ready to be spiritually weighed down by a “friend” who has fought against you. God forbid it show up in your church, but there will be no doubt in my mind that there are people who claim to be friendly and loving, who are anything but."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep’s clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.","ref":"Matthew 7:15"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But I say unto you, Love your enemies, bless them that curse you, do good to them that hate you, and pray for them which despitefully use you, and persecute you; That ye may be the children of your Father which is in heaven: for he maketh his sun to rise on the evil and on the good, and sendeth rain on the just and on the unjust.","ref":"Matthew 5:44-45"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now I beseech you, brethren, mark them which cause divisions and offenses contrary to the doctrine which ye have learned; and avoid them. For they that are such serve not our Lord Jesus Christ, but their own belly; and by good words and fair speeches deceive the hearts of the simple.","ref":"Romans 16:17-18"},{"kind":"para","text":"My hopes and prayers for the ministry are not to just have Bible study (which is great and certainly a part of our growth and walk together in the Lord) but to prepare and disciple those for the work of the ministry, so that they are not surprised by the attacks of Satan, but rather anticipating it and waiting for it, with love and compassion."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Be sober, be vigilant; because your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour:","ref":"1 Peter 5:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"18 Now this man purchased a field with the reward of iniquity; and falling headlong, he burst asunder in the midst, and all his bowels gushed out. 19 And it was known unto all the dwellers at Jerusalem; insomuch as that field is called in their proper tongue, Aceldama, that is to say, The field of blood."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Judas, which had betrayed him, when he saw that he was condemned, repented himself, and brought again the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and elders, Saying, I have sinned in that I have betrayed the innocent blood. And they said, What is that to us? see thou to that. And he cast down the pieces of silver in the temple, and departed, and went and hanged himself. And the chief priests took the silver pieces, and said, It is not lawful for to put them into the treasury, because it is the price of blood. And they took counsel, and bought with them the potter's field, to bury strangers in.","ref":"Matthew 27:3-7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, while he never purchased a field, the chief priests took the money and purchased the field, and it was considered the same as if Judas had purchased it himself. We also notice that in this verse, there seems to be a different record of Judas’ death."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he cast down the pieces of silver in the temple, and departed, and went and hanged himself.","ref":"Matthew 27:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"There have been assumptions that the field in which Judas hung himself was overlooking a cliff, and after Judas hung himself, the rope (or branch) could have broken so that his body fell over the edge of the cliff onto the rocks below. Others have assumed that Judas’ body may have remained hanging for so long that it started to decay, eventually falling to the ground and bursting open. Either gives credibility and brutality, and both acclaim the fact that Judas hung himself as accurate."},{"kind":"para","text":"20 For it is written in the book of Psalms, Let his habitation be desolate, and let no man dwell therein: and his bishoprick let another take."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, even Judas’ death fulfills another prophecy of psalm."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let their habitation be desolate; and let none dwell in their tents.","ref":"Psalm 69:25"},{"kind":"para","text":"The reason this is brought up, is because (in order for the prophecy to be fulfilled, and to keep the promise in which God had made), they had to fill the spot in which Judas left open."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let his days be few; and let another take his office.","ref":"Psalm 109:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"That’s what ministers do when they leave their church, or when they leave their teaching or pastoring. They leave an empty spot for the enemy to attack. While I won’t go into Judas’ actual salvation and lifestyle, I will say that all of the other disciples were fooled by Judas and surprised when he did what he did, which gives credibility to the idea that Judas was a kind minister, who said the right things, did the right things, and looked the right part. When someone who has been an asset to a ministry or church for so long decides they no longer want to be a part of it, they leave a gap in the hedge."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I sought for a man among them, that should make up the hedge, and stand in the gap before me for the land, that I should not destroy it: but I found none.","ref":"Ezekiel 22:30"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is why some pray for a “hedge of protection” around specific churches, families, or areas that need the Lord’s providence. What’s even worse (and I’ve seen this all too often) is when the hedge attacks itself, forming large gaps that can get in."},{"kind":"para","text":"A reminder to men, and spiritual leaders of the family, we are to stand in the gap so that Satan does not pass through and attack those that are following behind us! We are to take charge, and we are to fill the gap (by preparing and equipping others) with those that are trusted and faithful. This is what I believe the disciples are doing: filling the gap."},{"kind":"para","text":"Not only so, but these leaders parallel the heads of the 12 tribes of Israel in the Old Testament."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That ye may eat and drink at my table in my kingdom, and sit on thrones judging the twelve tribes of Israel.","ref":"Luke 22:30"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And had a wall great and high, and had twelve gates, and at the gates twelve angels, and names written thereon, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel…And the wall of the city had twelve foundations, and in them the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.","ref":"Revelation 21:12,14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Eventually these disciples will become “apostles,” and we don’t see the apostles even replaced from this point forward. 21 Wherefore of these men which have companied with us all the time that the Lord Jesus went in and out among us, 22 Beginning from the baptism of John, unto that same day that he was taken up from us, must one be ordained to be a witness with us of his resurrection."},{"kind":"para","text":"They needed an eyewitness, and someone who was trustworthy. Someone who hadn’t left them even during difficult seasons. Someone who never betrayed the faith. 23 And they appointed two, Joseph called Barsabas, who was surnamed Justus, and Matthias. 24 And they prayed, and said, Thou, Lord, which knowest the hearts of all men, shew whether of these two thou hast chosen, 25 That he may take part of this ministry and apostleship, from which Judas by transgression fell, that he might go to his own place."},{"kind":"para","text":"The group landed on two notable characters, and then prayed to the Lord about the proper choice, leaving it in the Lord’s hands so that they were able to make an accurate and reasonable choice without rejecting the Lord’s input on the matter. May we all take note to never just move forward out of a desire for our lack of patience, but wait on the Lord until His timing reveals our necessities."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wait on the LORD: be of good courage, and he shall strengthen thine heart: wait, I say, on the LORD.","ref":"Psalm 27:14 26 And they gave forth their lots; and the lot fell upon Matthias; and he was numbered with the eleven apostles."},{"kind":"para","text":"The “lots” were probably marked stones that were placed in a pot and then shaken out."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they cast lots, as well the small as the great, according to the house of their fathers, for every gate. And the lot eastward fell to Shelemiah. Then for Zechariah his son, a wise counsellor, they cast lots; and his lot came out northward. To Obededom southward; and to his sons the house of Asuppim. To Shuppim and Hosah the lot came forth westward, with the gate Shallecheth, by the causeway of the going up, ward against ward.","ref":"1 Chronicles 26:13-16"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is not an indication of how we should make our decisions today. There is no command in the New Testament to “cast lots” for the leadership of the church or for who can be involved in what manner of ministry. We are called by the same Lord to do the same ministry as the disciples were set forth to do. Apostles are meant to be Jesus’ special representatives, while “disciples” is used as a broad term of anyone who believes in Jesus. We cannot become apostles. We can only become disciples, because we were not with Jesus in His ministry, nor have we seen Him since His resurrection (save Paul, but more on him later)."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":5},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":15,"end":15}],"verses":{"1":"The former treatise have I made, O Theophilus, of all that Jesus began both to do and teach,","2":"Until the day in which he was taken up, after that he through the Holy Ghost had given commandments unto the apostles whom he had chosen:","3":"To whom also he shewed himself alive after his passion by many infallible proofs, being seen of them forty days, and speaking of the things pertaining to the kingdom of God:","4":"And, being assembled together with them, commanded them that they should not depart from Jerusalem, but wait for the promise of the Father, which, saith he, ye have heard of me.","5":"For John truly baptized with water; but ye shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost not many days hence.","9":"And when he had spoken these things, while they beheld, he was taken up; and a cloud received him out of their sight.","12":"Then returned they unto Jerusalem from the mount called Olivet, which is from Jerusalem a sabbath day's journey.","15":"And in those days Peter stood up in the midst of the disciples, and said, (the number of names together were about an hundred and twenty,)"}},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"And thou shalt observe the feast of weeks, of the first fruits of wheat harvest, and the feast of ingathering at the year’s end.","ref":"Exodus 34:22"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And thou shalt keep the feast of weeks unto the Lord thy God with a tribute of a freewill offering of thine hand, which thou shalt give unto the Lord thy God, according as the Lord thy God hath blessed thee:","ref":"Deuteronomy 16:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"During this time, Jews would travel to Jerusalem for Pentecost or stay there after Passover to wait for it."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, we get the opening instructions to this book and to the disciples to preach the gospel far and wide. The ministry shouldn’t stop at Jesus’ death and resurrection, and Christ made it clear to the disciples that there was still plenty for them to do and get started with. They were instructed to preach to the world, start the church, and they took the time to replace Judas with Matthias as the twelfth apostle. This chapter marks “The Day of Pentecost,” which was a yearly occurrence that the Jews would hold 50 days after the Passover. “Pentecost” comes from the Greek “pentekoste,” which means the fiftieth day. Originally, in the Old Testament, it was remembered as the wheat harvest or to commemorate the giving of the law on Mount Sinai. It was called “The Festival of Weeks” or “Feast of Weeks.\""],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":13,"title":"The Holy Spirit Comes","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When it uses the term “All,” we are to believe that it is speaking about the 120 assembled in the upper room from the last chapter (Verse 15)."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It’s interesting (and obviously not by accident) that the Holy Spirit comes to the believers in this way. Jesus says, “The wind bloweth where it listeth, and thou hearest the sound thereof, but canst not tell whence it cometh, and whither it goeth: so every one that is born of the Spirit” (John 3:8)."},{"kind":"para","text":"The wind is invisible, but the work of it won’t be. The work of the Holy Ghost is showcased in the rest of Acts, but also in the people who follow those books."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Not by works of righteousness which we have done, but according to his mercy he saved us, by the washing of regeneration, and renewing of the Holy Ghost;","ref":"Titus 3:5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Likewise the Spirit also helpeth our infirmities: for we know not what we should pray for as we ought: but the Spirit itself maketh intercession for us with groaning which cannot be uttered. And he that searcheth the hearts knoweth what is the mind of the Spirit, because he maketh intercession for the saints according to the will of God.","ref":"Romans 8:26-27"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And grieve not the holy Spirit of God, whereby ye are sealed unto the day of redemption.","ref":"Ephesians 4:30"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Know ye not that ye are the temple of God, and that the Spirit of God dwelleth in you? If any man defile the temple of God, him shall God destroy; for the temple of God is holy, which temple ye are.","ref":"1 Corinthians 3:16-17"},{"kind":"para","text":"And we know that the Word of God is written to those of that time, but can be applied to us in this time!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, long-suffering, gentleness, goodness, faith, Meekness, temperance: against such there is no law.","ref":"Galatians 5:22-23"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Holy Spirit is prevalent in the lives of believers today, and can be proven by the amazing workings of God in the local church. I’ve been to a few churches in my day, and I’ve seen God’s Spirit move on numerous accounts, through people’s giving, in caring, in serving, and in growing! If this is not proof of God’s Spirit, it’s certainly proof of something supernatural. 3 And there appeared unto them cloven tongues like as of fire, and it sat upon each of them."},{"kind":"para","text":"These “tongues of fire” are not literal flames, but the best way that Luke could represent what was seen was by how flames looked and worked, as if they were intense and wildly flicking, like fire would do. Fire was used quite a bit in the Old Testament to symbolize God’s holiness and purity."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the angel of the LORD appeared unto him in a flame of fire out of the midst of a bush: and he looked, and, behold, the bush burned with fire, and the bush was not consumed.","ref":"Exodus 3:2"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And mount Sinai was altogether on a smoke, because the LORD descended upon it in fire: and the smoke thereof ascended as the smoke of a furnace, and the whole mount quaked greatly.","ref":"Exodus 19:18"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the LORD will create upon every dwelling place of mount Zion, and upon her assemblies, a cloud and smoke by day, and the shining of a flaming fire by night: for upon all the glory shall be a defence.","ref":"Isaiah 4:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s also an indication of trials, and being refined through them!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That the trial of your faith, being much more precious than of gold that perisheth, though it be tried with fire, might be found unto praise and honour and glory at the appearing of Jesus Christ: Whom having not seen, ye love; in whom, though now ye see him not, yet believing, ye rejoice with joy unspeakable and full of glory:","ref":"1 Peter 1:7-8"},{"kind":"para","text":"And these apostles will certainly be tried, and those to come and establish the church for future generations."},{"kind":"para","text":"4 And they were all filled with the Holy Ghost, and began to speak with other tongues, as the Spirit gave them utterance."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus completes every promise He makes, and this was no different."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For John truly baptized with water; but ye shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost not many days hence.","ref":"Acts 1:5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me both in Jerusalem, and in all Judaea, and Samaria, and unto the uttermost part of the earth.","ref":"Acts 1:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"This verse marks the beginning of the “new covenant” age, which will continue until Jesus returns to Earth. To clarify and prevent misunderstandings, as many in the church have today, the term “tongues” here originates from the Greek word “glossa,” meaning “the language or dialect used by a particular people distinct from that of other nations.” In simpler terms, it refers to another language so that different cultures could understand God’s Word being expounded. It is not some strange or new language; it is a regular language, and the ability to translate without using a translator. It was likely the language spoken before Babel and will probably be the language we speak in Heaven."},{"kind":"para","text":"The phrase “…As the Spirit gave them utterance” suggests that they would only speak in “tongues (or languages rather)” when the Spirit provided it and found it necessary, which will happen shortly."},{"kind":"para","text":"5 And there were dwelling at Jerusalem Jews, devout men, out of every nation under heaven."},{"kind":"para","text":"We understand at this point, there are more than likely (about) 3,000 people at this temple in Jerusalem. We assume they are at the temple grounds (because they were originally in the upper room when they received the Holy Ghost), and the temple grounds is the only place that would be large enough to accommodate 3,000 people. We get that 3,000 number from verse 41 of this chapter (something we will focus on in a moment)."},{"kind":"para","text":"6 Now when this was noised abroad, the multitude came together, and were confounded, because that every man heard them speak in his own language. 7 And they were all amazed and marvelled, saying one to another, Behold, are not all these which speak Galilaeans? 8 And how hear we every man in our own tongue, wherein we were born?"},{"kind":"para","text":"I love this scene. This may seem like such a small thing to most people, but God often uses seemingly small things to glorify Himself. They are speaking in one language, yet every language understands what they are saying, and they are completely mystified by it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Howbeit when he, the Spirit of truth, is come, he will guide you into all truth: for he shall not speak of himself; but whatsoever he shall hear, that shall he speak: and he will shew you things to come.","ref":"John 16:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"I love this because it shows these people seeing these men speaking in their native tongues, yet understanding that they are not all fully capable of knowing these languages. They could have thought to themselves, “Well, maybe they are just really clever and well-studied men!” But then they took one look at them and said, “There’s no way they are well-studied… They are Galileans!” The only thing they could come up with was, “They must be drunk!” (Ver. 13)"},{"kind":"para","text":"There could be no other excuse or clarity that these men were not filled with wine, but with the Spirit!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And be not drunk with wine, wherein is excess; but be filled with the Spirit;","ref":"Ephesians 5:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"No, it was evident that these men had something much more powerful than alcohol in them…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now when they saw the boldness of Peter and John, and perceived that they were unlearned and ignorant men, they marveled; and they took knowledge of them, that they had been with Jesus.","ref":"Acts 4:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Holy Spirit makes the simple wise in understanding and application. It leaves no room or excuse for those that find purpose in reason to reject God’s calling on their lives. It gives unction and demand to people that are filled with the Holy Spirit to talk to others about the gospel. It forces our hands on the matter so that we have no choice, and it makes us seem wiser than we actually are, when in reality we are unskilled and unlearned men. 9 Parthians, and Medes, and Elamites, and the dwellers in Mesopotamia, and in Judaea, and Cappadocia, in Pontus, and Asia, 10 Phrygia, and Pamphylia, in Egypt, and in the parts of Libya about Cyrene, and strangers of Rome, Jews and proselytes, 11 Cretes and Arabians, we do hear them speak in our tongues the wonderful works of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"This list of nations covers most of the first-century Roman world, specifically areas where Jewish communities existed. This is where Christians start their worldwide witness. Obviously, here at this point, the converts were mainly Jewish. The only Gentiles at Pentecost would have been proselytes, which were Gentiles that became full converts to Judaism. “Proselyte” means “one who has come over from a Gentile religion to Judaism.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"12 And they were all amazed, and were in doubt, saying one to another, What meaneth this? 13 Others mocking said, These men are full of new wine."},{"kind":"para","text":"If people are not in amazement and asking “what does this mean?” or mocking you, you may be doing something wrong in your witnessing. Not because you want to be weird and flamboyant, but because you want them to think about the truth of the gospel as much as possible. You may not see many people come to Christ right away, but you never want them to leave without thinking about it when you’re gone."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":41,"title":"The Gospel Is Shared","entries":[{"start":14,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Peter did not waste this opportunity to witness and preach the truth. He uses the Old Testament prophet to bring them into an understanding of what is going on. He does this for a few reasons, but the main purpose of using this scripture is because these Jews would have known these verses very well. Remember in verse 5 it says these Jews were “devout men.” They knew their Old Testament scripture. They knew Joel very well."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh; and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions: And also upon the servants and upon the handmaid in those days will I pour out my spirit. And I will shew wonders in the heavens and in the earth, blood, and fire, and pillars of smoke. The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and the terrible day of the LORD come. And it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall call on the name of the LORD shall be delivered: for in mount Zion and in Jerusalem shall be deliverance, as the LORD hath said, and in the remnant whom the LORD shall call.","ref":"Joel 2:28-32"},{"kind":"para","text":"But even if they don’t know anything about Joel, Peter knows they will certainly know David and Psalms, and quotes Psalms as well!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I have set the LORD always before me: because he is at my right hand, I shall not be moved. Therefore my heart is glad, and my glory rejoiceth: my flesh also shall rest in hope. For thou wilt not leave my soul in hell; neither wilt thou suffer thine Holy One to see corruption. Thou wilt shew me the path of life: in thy presence is fulness of joy; at thy right hand there are pleasures for evermore.","ref":"Psalm 16:8-11"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The LORD said unto my Lord, Sit thou at my right hand, until I make thine enemies thy footstool.","ref":"Psalm 110:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For thy servant David’s sake turn not away the face of thine anointed.","ref":"Psalm 132:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"The best way to witness to others, as the disciples did, is to bring the gospel into their intellect. If you can help others understand their need for the gospel, it will make it more appealing to them, prompting them to desire to know more. Use a common denominator to appeal to their intellect. Everything interesting in this life can be connected back to the gospel."},{"kind":"para","text":"Football and its strategies can be used to connect the gospel to those interested in sports. Dr. Tony Evans is a great resource for such parables. You could use cars and automobiles to connect the gospel if you are skilled enough. Even Jesus used bread and a door to connect the gospel! Simple things can become extremely useful in appealing to people’s intellect to introduce them to the gospel."},{"kind":"para","text":"I once saw a man outside eating chips while I was soulwinning. When I approached him, I asked how he was doing. He replied, “I’m doing well, just having a snack and sitting.” My soulwinning partner asked if he could have a chip, which I found very strange, especially coming from him because he was so shy. The man obliged and allowed him a chip. It was a new flavor, and the man said he had never had it before. I asked, “Why are you sitting out here all alone, are you sure you are okay?” He said he needed to see all the beauty outside for a change and had never really just sat and looked at everything. I used the elements he was fascinated with around him to lead into the gospel. I used the chip he offered me for free to represent the free gift of salvation from our Lord, and he said he had never heard it said like that. I asked, “You didn’t expect me to pay you back for that chip, right?” Thankfully, he didn’t. He didn’t accept Christ, but it certainly got him thinking about his eternity."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are so many ways you can witness to others, and it doesn’t have to be anything extraordinary; it just has to be relevant to the person you are speaking to. You have to be able to read people and judge them correctly. Not so you can point out their sins (there will be time for the Lord to do that anyway), but so that you can have the compassion to tell them the truth in love."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But speaking the truth in love, may grow up into him in all things, which is the head, even Christ:","ref":"Ephesians 4:15 17 And it shall come to pass in the last days, saith God, I will pour out of my Spirit upon all flesh: and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, and your young men shall see visions, and your old men shall dream dreams: 18 And on my servants and on my handmaidens I will pour out in those days of my Spirit; and they shall prophesy:"},{"kind":"para","text":"There are many preachers who speak of “the last days” and relate it to the book of Revelation and the coming judgement. They aren’t wrong, but the apostles and prophets of old considered “the last days” to be those days that take place after the reception of the Holy Spirit. So the last days truly began at the day of Pentecost."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Hath in these last days spoken unto us by his Son, whom he hath appointed heir of all things, by whom also he made the worlds;","ref":"Hebrews 1:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Rabbis believed that the Spirit stopped speaking through human prophets with the last of the Old Testament prophets. Joel’s prophecy, however, proves there was an outpouring of the Spirit on “ALL FLESH” (as this verse implies), and it is understood as referring to a new messianic age. 19 And I will shew wonders in heaven above, and signs in the earth beneath; blood, and fire, and vapour of smoke: 20 The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and notable day of the Lord come: 21 And it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall call on the name of the Lord shall be saved."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is obviously referring to the end of the last days (which part of the prophecy hadn’t been fulfilled yet, and still isn’t fulfilled until the great tribulation). Peter still includes the full prophecy here for some reason that isn’t quite revealed (maybe because he memorized Scripture better if he learned it as a whole?). This gives validity to the fact that Peter really cared about Scripture, and/or the Holy Spirit was evidently working through his voice to produce obvious knowledge so that others will come to know Christ and be saved."},{"kind":"para","text":"22 Ye men of Israel, hear these words; Jesus of Nazareth, a man approved of God among you by miracles and wonders and signs, which God did by him in the midst of you, as ye yourselves also know: 23 Him, being delivered by the determinate counsel and foreknowledge of God, ye have taken, and by wicked hands have crucified and slain:"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter now leads these Jews back to the knowledge of Jesus. He even casually blames these specific people for crucifying Him personally. Let me just remind everyone, though we were not present at the death of Jesus, you too are to blame for His crucifixion. Not because we desired to kill Him (though many today would attest to the desire for their hate of Christ), but because He desired to save us by purchasing us through His blood. His crucifixion, though blamed on us for our sin and transgression, was also a “determinate counsel and foreknowledge” God needed for the payment of our transgression and sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is how the Holy Spirit would be delivered. This is how the gospel would reach the world. This is how people would be able to walk freely in the grace and love of God. Without this payment of sin (without Christ’s perfect life and sacrifice), we would still be in bondage and controlled by our own sins and flesh."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now the Lord is that Spirit: and where the Spirit of the Lord is, there is liberty.","ref":"2 Corinthians 3:17"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If the Son therefore shall make you free, ye shall be free indeed.","ref":"John 8:36 24 Whom God hath raised up, having loosed the pains of death: because it was not possible that he should be holden of it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Take a note from Peter here. It’s not enough to just tell people their flaws and sins. It’s not enough to make them feel bad and then tell them to repent. We must preach Jesus crucified, and there is certainly a need for that. But we MUST also preach Jesus resurrected, defeating death and conquering the grave."},{"kind":"para","text":"The good news is not good unless you remind others about the payment for their sin. That’s how the gospel is the good news. Not because we did anything. Not because we try to work for our salvation. Not because we are pure and sinless. Not because we are perfect and holy. Only because Jesus lived a perfect life, died a painful death, and rose again, defeating death. That’s the good news. That’s the gospel. Only Jesus. 25 For David speaketh concerning him, I foresaw the Lord always before my face, for he is on my right hand, that I should not be moved: 26 Therefore did my heart rejoice, and my tongue was glad; moreover also my flesh shall rest in hope: 27 Because thou wilt not leave my soul in hell, neither wilt thou suffer thine Holy One to see corruption. 28 Thou hast made known to me the ways of life; thou shalt make me full of joy with thy countenance. 29 Men and brethren, let me freely speak unto you of the patriarch David, that he is both dead and buried, and his sepulchre is with us unto this day. 30 Therefore being a prophet, and knowing that God had sworn with an oath to him, that of the fruit of his loins, according to the flesh, he would raise up Christ to sit on his throne; 31 He seeing this before spake of the resurrection of Christ, that his soul was not left in hell, neither his flesh did see corruption."},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, Peter is quoting Psalms here. However, there’s one spot that most people stumble on because they have no literal or actual proof written in scripture as evidence of what happened during the three days that Jesus was buried. We know Jesus completes every promise and prophecy in the scripture ever written and foretold. But we see one here that has many people scratching their heads, and Peter doesn’t really give clarity to it, and it’s found in verse 27."},{"kind":"para","text":"Is it possible that Jesus was cast into Hell during those three days He was in the grave? There are actually a few theories on this, and I’ve spoken on this subject extensively in the past (plus I’ve researched this in a book I wrote). I don’t believe for a second that Jesus just lay in the grave for three days waiting for His time to be resurrected. I have no concrete evidence to back up any particular claim, but I know that people have pulled from different ideologies and scripture to try and attest to what happened."},{"kind":"para","text":"One particular belief is that Jesus went to Hell and preached the gospel, showing proof of salvation to all those who rejected Him already (specifically angels, or demons in this case). They pull this idea from Peter."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"By which also he went and preached unto the spirits in prison;","ref":"1 Peter 3:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"There has been much debate on this topic, and the Greek term for “spirits” can mean human spirits or angels, depending on the context. You can look to Numbers 27:16, Acts 7:59, and Hebrews 12:23 for context. Some interpreters say that the “spirits” refer to the unsaved people of Noah’s day (one of which I hold close to me because of the context Peter gives; you can study that in the commentary for 1 & 2 Peter). Christ “in the spirit” (1 Peter 3:18) preached the gospel “in the days of Noah” (ver. 20) through Noah."},{"kind":"para","text":"Some believe that the spirits are fallen angels who were cast into hell to await the final judgment. This interpretation would make a lot more sense to me, seeing as they were among the first to reject Christ before all of humankind had the chance. Additionally, not to mention, God cares a lot for the lost."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The Lord is not slack concerning his promise, as some men count slackness; but is long-suffering to us-ward, not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance.","ref":"2 Peter 3:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Some have a belief that Christ was giving those in Hell a second chance for salvation, but this assumption couldn’t be any more contradictory to the truths of Scripture and the Bible as a whole."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And beside all this, between us and you there is a great gulf fixed: so that they which would pass from hence to you cannot; neither can they pass to us, that would come from thence.","ref":"Luke 16:26"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And as it is appointed unto men once to die, but after this the judgment:","ref":"Hebrews 9:27"},{"kind":"para","text":"Even just reading through 1 Peter alone would claim that Christ was not giving those in Hell a second chance. Others have shared the idea that there was a battle taking place in Hell between Jesus and Satan, putting Satan in his place once and for all. However, Satan isn’t in Hell yet, and that’s proven through our world’s spiritual conflict today and the battle that will commence in the tribulation, as labeled in Revelation. I don’t hold to this theory at all."},{"kind":"para","text":"The last assumption is that Jesus, in those three days in the grave, was separating Heaven and Hell. I find this assumption to be fascinating and like this idea and concept better, but once again, there’s no actual scripture to back it up fully. The idea that Heaven and Hell were close together comes from Jesus’ story about the rich man and Lazarus."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And in hell he lift up his eyes, being in torments, and seeth Abraham afar off, and Lazarus in his bosom. And he cried and said, Father Abraham, have mercy on me, and send Lazarus, that he may dip the tip of his finger in water, and cool my tongue; for I am tormented in this flame. But Abraham said, Son, remember that thou in thy lifetime receivedst thy good things, and likewise Lazarus evil things: but now he is comforted, and thou art tormented.","ref":"Luke 16:23-25"},{"kind":"para","text":"A rule of thumb that I have learned from another preacher is that if a parable has a name, it isn’t a parable but a true account. This is the only acclaimed “parable” where a name is mentioned, so it is therefore assumed that this is not a parable at all, but a true story."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe that Jesus never went to Hell. He never talked about going there for any reason. In fact, He repeatedly expressed His excitement for going home! Here’s one such occasion…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ye have heard now I said unto you, I go away, and come again unto you. If ye loved me, ye would rejoice, because I said, I go unto the Father: for my Father is greater than I.","ref":"John 14:28"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is also not to mention that He tells the thief on the cross next to Him that he will be with Him in paradise on that day. We also have to ask the question, “Who are the ones that end up in Hell?” Well, those are the ones that deny Christ and His salvation. Christ never denied Himself, and He never loses His salvation on the cross (another debate we can get into, saved for a different setting), so therefore it doesn’t make sense that Christ would end up in Hell."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, no matter what theory you hold to, the best way to glorify God through His death and resurrection is “Glory to the most high!” I will say, however, that this is not speaking specifically about “Hell” in the sense of eternal damnation. What is being spoken of here is “the grave.” The word “Hell” comes from the Greek and is referring to “Hades,” which is also proclaimed to be the “god of the underworld.” This is a direct quote from Psalms, and David uses that term to describe the grave. Jonah uses this same term…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And said, I cried by reason of mine affliction unto the LORD, and he heard me; out of the belly of hell cried I, and thou hardest my voice.","ref":"Jonah 2:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, people use this term in assuming that even Jonah died in the belly of the whale and came back to life three days later. Christ used this event to describe what He will personally do, rising from the dead three days later."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For as Jonas was three days and three nights in the whale’s belly; so shall the Son of man be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.","ref":"Matthew 12:40"},{"kind":"para","text":"The focal point should be on Jesus, and God’s focus was not on the fact that Jesus was dead for three days, but on the unprecedented event of His resurrection. This should also be our focus, rather than theories or lack of evidence. Furthermore, the proof that David is speaking about the grave is found not only in the Psalms but is also clarified by Peter, who states in verse 31 that David is not referring to eternal damnation as “Hell” but to the grave."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Corruption” comes from the Greek word “diaphthora,” which means “decay.” It is physically proven and evidential that a body left in the grave after three days will start to decay. Jesus rose from the dead on the third day, preventing His body from decaying. While Jesus was certainly in the afterlife during the three days in the grave, to say He was in Hell is a stretch, based on evidence that suggests otherwise. 32 This Jesus hath God raised up, whereof we all are witnesses. 33 Therefore being by the right hand of God exalted, and having received of the Father the promise of the Holy Ghost, he hath shed forth this, which ye now see and hear. 34 For David is not ascended into the heavens: but he saith himself, The Lord said unto my Lord, Sit thou on my right hand, 35 Until I make thy foes thy footstool. 36 Therefore let all the house of Israel know assuredly, that God hath made the same Jesus, whom ye have crucified, both Lord and Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter brings it back to the focus of Jesus coming back from the dead and giving life to all that will accept Him. Those that reject Him will not have life. These people were destined for the grave and for death, but Christ can save them!"},{"kind":"para","text":"37 Now when they heard this, they were pricked in their heart, and said unto Peter and to the rest of the apostles, Men and brethren, what shall we do?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Music to a preacher’s ears. Peter has set them up so they have nowhere to go. He brings them to the point where they have no choice but to ask, “If this is all true, and we are to blame for the death of God, yet Christ lives on forever, what shall we do?” Then Peter says, start attending church, and do good works, and make sure that God will forgive you for killing His Son by praying to Him repeatedly until you feel okay about it and give money to the church, and confess your sins to the priests! Or wait… He says something far different in these next verses…"},{"kind":"para","text":"38 Then Peter said unto them, Repent, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of sins, and ye shall receive the gift of the Holy Ghost. 39 For the promise is unto you, and to your children, and to all that are afar off, even as many as the Lord our God shall call. 40 And with many other words did he testify and exhort, saying, Save yourselves from this untoward generation. 41 Then they that gladly received his word were baptized: and the same day there were added unto them about three thousand souls."},{"kind":"para","text":"No, Peter simply says, “repent and be baptized.” May I also point out that only those who “received” Peter’s Word about Jesus Christ were baptized? They were the only ones who had understanding. You are wasting your time baptizing someone who cannot understand sin and repentance. They cannot be saved without an understanding of what they are being saved from and a repentance of those things that have caused them to be lost to begin with. But that will be touched on more in the coming chapters."},{"kind":"para","text":"There’s a direct correlation that I thought was interesting in verse 41 to Exodus."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the children of Levi did according to the word of Moses: and there fell of the people that day about three thousand men.","ref":"Exodus 32:28"},{"kind":"para","text":"D.L. Moody said, “The first time the law was preached 3,000 were killed. The first time grace was preached 3,000 were saved.”"}]}]},{"start":42,"end":47,"title":"The Church Expands","entries":[{"start":42,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is what the church is about. It’s a community where believers can come together to worship God and fellowship. It’s a place where we can speak about the things pertaining to truth and Scripture and grow from it. It’s a place where we can be loved and give love according to the grace given to every believer. It’s a place where we can carry one another’s burdens and feel for others so we are able to point each other back to Christ and the Will of God."}]},{"start":44,"end":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There’s no better friendship in the world than friendships with other believers. We may not have very much in common as far as hobbies and interests, but one thing we do have in common is our love for the Lord, and that makes the friendship worth having. “Can two walk together, except they be agreed?” Amos 3:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"We may not agree on a great deal of things, but one thing we must agree on in order to be in unity together is the Lord Jesus Christ. 45 And sold their possessions and goods, and parted them to all men, as every man had need. 46 And they, continuing daily with one accord in the temple, and breaking bread from house to house, did eat their meat with gladness and singleness of heart, 47 Praising God, and having favour with all the people. And the Lord added to the church daily such as should be saved."},{"kind":"para","text":"The church grew mightily through one simple sermon. The sermon doesn’t have to be grand or intricate; it just has to be truthful and scriptural. It has to have Jesus. If our lessons or preaching do not centralize Christ, we have wasted our time and efforts and should not preach and teach."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But we preach Christ crucified, unto the Jews a stumblingblock, and unto the Greeks foolishness; But unto them which are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ the power of God, and the wisdom of God. Because the foolishness of God is wiser than men; and the weakness of God is stronger than men. For ye see your calling, brethren, how that not many wise men after the flesh, not many mighty, not many noble, are called: But God hath chosen the foolish things of the world to confound the wise; and God hath chosen the weak things of the world to confound the things which are mighty; And base things of the world, and things which are despised, hath God chosen, yea, and things which are not, to bring to nought things that are: That no flesh should glory in his presence. But of him are ye in Christ Jesus, who of God is made unto us wisdom, and righteousness, and sanctification, and redemption:","ref":"1 Corinthians 1:23-30"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":14,"end":16},{"start":42,"end":43},{"start":44,"end":44}],"verses":{"1":"And when the day of Pentecost was fully come, they were all with one accord in one place.","2":"And suddenly there came a sound from heaven as of a rushing mighty wind, and it filled all the house where they were sitting.","14":"But Peter, standing up with the eleven, lifted up his voice, and said unto them, Ye men of Judaea, and all ye that dwell at Jerusalem, be this known unto you, and hearken to my words:","15":"For these are not drunken, as ye suppose, seeing it is but the third hour of the day.","16":"But this is that which was spoken by the prophet Joel;","42":"And they continued stedfastly in the apostles' doctrine and fellowship, and in breaking of bread, and in prayers.","43":"And fear came upon every soul: and many wonders and signs were done by the apostles.","44":"And all that believed were together, and had all things common;"}},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"But ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me both in Jerusalem, and in all Judaea, and in Samaria, and unto the uttermost part of the earth.","ref":"Acts 1:8"}],"previously":["Our last chapter gives us the introduction to the Holy Spirit being placed on the believers. The power here was given so that they might start the ministry work they needed to in order to establish the church and build the faith in Christ after Christ’s ascension. Jesus left the task to the disciples and gave them room to learn from the Spirit as they progressed through the turmoil and pains of the culture of the day (which we will see more of in the coming chapters). Peter gave a great sermon about the Old Testament and how it all led up to Jesus Christ coming and dying for the sins of the world, and casually blamed the congregation for His death (there may have been some actually there who were literally involved with killing Christ, but we are all to blame because of our sinful nature). This chapter continues off of chapter 2, with the highs of about 3,000 people being saved, the ministry was looking up! The disciples were preparing the way of the gospel being preached in Jerusalem, just as Acts 1 promised they would."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":10,"title":"The Beggar Healed","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“The Hour of Prayer” being the ninth hour, means it was about 3:00 PM. Jews would observe prayer three times at the temple, once at 9 AM, once at 12 PM, and once at 3 PM."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now this is that which thou shalt offer upon the altar; two lambs of the first year day by day continually. The one lamb thou shalt offer in the morning; and the other lamb thou shalt offer at even: And with the one lamb a tenth deal of flour mingled with the fourth part of an hin of beaten oil; and the fourth part of an hin of wine for a drink offering. And the other lamb thou shalt offer at even, and shalt do thereto according to the meat offering of the morning, and according to the drink offering thereof, for a sweet savour, an offering made by fire unto the LORD. This shall be a continual burnt offering throughout your generations at the door of the tabernacle of the congregation before the LORD: where I will meet you, to speak there unto thee.","ref":"Exodus 29:38-42"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the LORD spake unto Moses, saying, Command the children of Israel, and say unto them, My offering, and my bread for my sacrifices made by fire, for a sweet savour unto me, shall ye observe to offer unto me in their due season. And thou shalt say unto them, This is the offering made by fire which ye shall offer unto the LORD; two lambs of the first year without spot day by day, for a continual burnt offering. The one lamb shalt thou offer in the morning, and the other lamb shalt thou offer at even; And a tenth part of an ephah of flour for a meat offering, mingled with the fourth part of an hin of beaten oil. It is a continual burnt offering, which was ordained in mount Sinai for a sweet savour, a sacrifice made by fire unto the LORD. And the drink offering thereof shall be the fourth part of an hin for the one lamb: in the holy place shalt thou cause the strong wine to be poured unto the LORD for a drink offering. And the other lamb shalt thou offer at even: as the meat offering of the morning, and as the drink offering thereof, thou shalt offer it, a sacrifice made by fire, of a sweet savour unto the LORD.","ref":"Numbers 28:1-8"},{"kind":"para","text":"David speaks about the importance of prayer in his life as well!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Evening, and morning, and at noon, will I pray, and cry aloud: and he shall hear my voice.","ref":"Psalm 55:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"The right time for the Christian to pray is right now."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Pray without ceasing.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"You don’t need to wait for a specific time to shut your eyes and pray. As soon as you talk to someone, even while you are shaking their hand, pray for them. 2 And a certain man lame from his mother's womb was carried, whom they laid daily at the gate of the temple which is called Beautiful, to ask alms of them that entered into the temple; 3 Who seeing Peter and John about to go into the temple asked an alms."},{"kind":"para","text":"The term “alms” means “money.” We often like to think of people being “handicapped” as an accident. Granted, there are times when someone becomes handicapped through an accident, but we also understand that God doesn’t make accidents. He has a purpose for everyone everywhere. This man did not go through an accident to become lame; this says he was born that way. I believe he was born that way for one purpose, and one purpose alone, and that was for this specific moment in history. You can even look to some of the people that Jesus healed as an example of people being handicapped for a reason."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When he had thus spoken, he spat on the ground, and made clay of the spittle, and he anointed the eyes of the blind man with the clay,","ref":"John 9:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"This man comes back seeing, not because he did anything special. Jesus literally spits on the ground and then rubs the dirt in this guy’s eyes. It was this man’s faith that heals him because Jesus tells him directly after this to “Go, wash in the pool of Siloam, (which is by interpretation, Sent.) He went his way therefore, and washed, and came seeing.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"This man could have been disgusted at Jesus’ act of spitting and rubbing dirt in his eyes and just walked away. Jesus could have just healed him on the spot, but the faith of this man is ultimately what saved his vision. I guarantee this man was walking closer to God every day since that moment. God even says it himself that He doesn’t make any accidents."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the LORD said unto him, Who hath made man’s mouth? Or who maketh the dumb, or deaf, or the seeing, or the blind? Have not I the LORD?","ref":"Exodus 4:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"This man, here in Acts, was going to serve a much bigger purpose with his handicap, and it wasn’t just to simply show that we are able to “heal” people in miraculous ways."},{"kind":"para","text":"4 And Peter, fastening his eyes upon him with John, said, Look on us. 5 And he gave heed unto them, expecting to receive something of them."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is indicated here that the beggar was just in a repeated pattern of asking for money without even thinking much about it. He more than likely had his head down, not wishing to really even be noticed or seen because it’s a humbling thing to be without money and to be handicapped. He didn’t know how to function. He couldn’t, as the world was getting more and more fast-paced. He sits at this “beautiful” gate, which seems like a pleasant place for him to just sit in general, but he doesn’t want to look up. He literally has to be carried there every day, as verse 2 tells us."},{"kind":"para","text":"I picture us in the same state of apparel before our salvation. We don’t know it, but we are simply beggars near the beautiful gate. We are looking for money to go into this gate, a place we feel we don’t belong unless we are whole. The “beautiful” gate was known as one of the gates belonging to the temple in Jerusalem until the Romans destroyed it in AD 70. We want to enter the Lord’s temple. We want to receive the Heavenly gifts, and we think money will certainly get us there. However, what we need is not to buy our way in, but to simply walk in through the door of Jesus Christ. And that’s exactly why this man needed to look up at Peter and John, but he was expecting more material gain, when instead what he needed was spiritual change. 6 Then Peter said, Silver and gold have I none; but such as I have give I thee: In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth rise up and walk. 7 And he took him by the right hand, and lifted him up: and immediately his feet and ankle bones received strength."},{"kind":"para","text":"More important than giving money, or even food (which isn’t bad in itself), is giving life. Peter didn’t just raise this man from off the ground to get him to walk again. He used Jesus Christ’s power to get this man to walk again. May I just say, in case it wasn’t obvious enough, only the apostles had this kind of power to heal people and raise them up."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Are all apostles? are all prophets? are all teachers? are all workers of miracles? Have all the gifts of healing? do all speak with tongues? do all interpret? But covet earnestly the best gifts: and yet shew I unto you a more excellent way.","ref":"1 Corinthians 12:29-31"},{"kind":"para","text":"No doubt, there are those doctors in the faith with the gift of healing, in particular through medication, prayer, and the necessity of surgery and the likes. However, those of us who think we can hit someone with a jacket or push them over to heal them are in great error. We do not hold the spiritual gifts of speaking in tongues or healing. We are not in the office of presenting miracles and giving prophecies."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are not apostles. The Apostles were those who knew and saw Christ personally. They were the ones commissioned to do incredible things such as healing a lame man and making him walk for the first time in his life. We are, however, disciples of Jesus, and it is far more important that we are trained in the faith and grow in our training than it is for us to become “leaders” of the faith, thinking we are better than we actually are. In fact, I’d argue that you will never become a leader of the faith if you believe you are smarter, wiser, or better at ministering than others. We are no better than each other, and we should never think ourselves to be better than we actually are. When we do this, we are then guilty of being prideful and arrogant, and God does not take kindly to those traits."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Every one that is proud in heart is an abomination to the LORD: though hand join in hand, he shall not be unpunished.","ref":"Proverbs 16:5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But he giveth more grace. Wherefore he saith, God resisteth the proud, but giveth more grace unto the humble.","ref":"James 4:6 8 And he leaping up stood, and walked, and entered with them into the temple, walking, and leaping, and praising God. 9 And all the people saw him walking and praising God: 10 And they knew that it was he which sat for alms at the Beautiful gate of the temple: and they were filled with wonder and amazement at that which had happened unto him."},{"kind":"para","text":"This lame man walking was not simply for the benefit of the lame man. It was for the benefit of the onlookers. There were still doubts about Jesus’ power and resurrection looming between the religionists of the temple that day. However, they had just witnessed a miracle, which brought questions into their minds. They began to second-guess all the traditions and customs they were clinging to."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":26,"title":"Peter’s Answer","entries":[{"start":11,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Thankfully, Peter didn’t just say, “Well, you see the kind of power I have now! You see what Jesus has done for me to give me such great strength and authority over life, and you too can have the same authority and power by surrendering your life.” No, Peter passed the credit. He didn’t want credit for the miracle that just took place. Instead, he witnessed to them again about Jesus Christ and said in verse 16, “Through faith in the name of Jesus, this man was given the ability to walk.” Not by the power of Peter, not by the willingness of Peter, not by the faith of Peter, but by the faith of this lame man, he was given the ability to walk again."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter was just the vessel that God used to pour power out through the Holy Ghost for the healing of this lame man, but Peter didn’t dare take the credit for it. It’s easy for any of us to see God do something miraculous through our lives, and it should amaze and astound us. However, the moment we take credit for it as if it’s something we achieved, it’s the moment we lose our reward from the Lord for such a blessed miracle. May we take note of the Apostles through this book, because every time something miraculous happens, they do as Jesus did and point it back to God, not to themselves."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life? And he said unto him, Why callest thou me good? There is none good but one, that is, God…","ref":"Matthew 19:16-17"}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They didn’t know, or fully understand, that Jesus was in fact the Messiah and the true Son of God, which is why salvation could still be provided to them. But they are still guilty of their actions and had to repent in order to be saved. Whereas, now that they know, they cannot reject repentance and salvation. 18 But those things, which God before had shewed by the mouth of all his prophets, that Christ should suffer, he hath so fulfilled."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, as we noticed last week, Peter brings up the Old Testament prophets. These people would understand the references here because they knew their Old Testament. Devout Jews today know the Old Testament very well even still. If you asked a Jew about the Old Testament, they could probably name nearly all 613 of the laws that were established during that time."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I was in ministry at a church in Texas, we often visited a retirement home where we would hold Bible study. The home was filled with prominent Jewish people, and many rejected our Bible study because of it. There was a handful that would still come and hang around, but when we would ask others, they didn’t want a part of it if we were going to talk from the New Testament. My brother and I had the privilege of speaking to many Jewish men and women who knew their stuff. We would use the Old Testament to bring up the New Testament Jesus (usually citing Isaiah and the law of Moses because they knew those). Isaiah is a great leeway into Jesus. When it was asked of one Jewish lady about who she believed Jesus to be, she said, “I believe He was a prophet.” And my brother asked, “Well, can a prophet lie?” She said, “No.” And my brother said, “Well, Jesus said that He and His Father are one, do you think Jesus lied about this?” This ultimately got her thinking. She still didn’t join our Bible study, but she definitely got the gospel message that day."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter uses what they know about the Old Testament to prove the fulfilled promises of God through the New Testament. 19 Repent ye therefore, and be converted, that your sins may be blotted out, when the times of refreshing shall come from the presence of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Repentance will be a key word used throughout the book of Acts. I’ve met several people who have serious grief with this term, repentance. They think of it as a term for people who are not saved, and it’s mostly used in that context. But repentance is not for the lost. The lost cannot repent. The only way that repentance can take place is through the acknowledgement of sin. How else can you turn away from sin, unless you know and call it what it is? Repentance is for the Christian, and it doesn’t stop just at salvation. A true Christian is in constant repentance."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.","ref":"1 John 1:9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the times of this ignorance God winked at; but now commandeth all men every where to repent:","ref":"Acts 17:30"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Remember therefore from whence thou art fallen, and repent, and do the first works; or else I will come quickly, and will remove thy candlestick out of his place, except thou repent.","ref":"Revelation 2:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"Repentance should be held in high reverence to a Christian’s walk with God. When you think you are okay without it, that is when you are most needful of it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"From that time Jesus began to preach, and to say, Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.","ref":"Matthew 4:17 20 And he shall send Jesus Christ, which before was preached unto you:"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a pretty clear reference to the second coming of Christ. As much as we desire that all would be saved, we also desire Christ’s return. 21 Whom the heaven must receive until the times of restitution of all things, which God hath spoken by the mouth of all his holy prophets since the world began."},{"kind":"para","text":"The time of restoration will only come in Christ’s return. His kingdom will be established, and the earth will be renewed beyond what it was before Adam and Eve’s fall."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the creature was made subject to vanity, not willingly, but by reason of him who hath subjected the same in hope, Because the creature itself also shall be delivered from the bondage of corruption into the glorious liberty of the children of God.","ref":"Romans 8:20-21 22 For Moses truly said unto the fathers, A prophet shall the Lord your God raise up unto you of your brethren, like unto me; him shall ye hear in all things whatsoever he shall say unto you. 23 And it shall come to pass, that every soul, which will not hear that prophet, shall be destroyed from among the people."},{"kind":"para","text":"Then he directly quotes from the book of Deuteronomy."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The LORD thy God will raise up thee a Prophet from the midst of thee, of thy brethren, like unto me; unto him ye shall hearken;","ref":"Deuteronomy 18:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter is reiterating how dangerous it is to reject the promises of God through Jesus Christ, especially because these promises are proven throughout Old Testament prophecy and scripture. Not only are they rejecting the Son of God and the promised Messiah, but they are also rejecting the Old Testament commands and the working of their own religions and traditions. They will be literally rejecting God if they reject Jesus. 24 Yea, and all the prophets from Samuel and those that follow after, as many as have spoken, have likewise foretold of these days."},{"kind":"para","text":"He once again gives them a character they would know well to further illustrate that Jesus Christ wasn’t just some sort of surprise to humanity as if God had kept it in secret. God was open about the Savior that would come for humanity; we just didn’t know when that would happen, and even worse, when it did happen, we killed Him. But these prophets of old spoke about what was to come and gave credibility to Jesus, to His death, and to the Holy Ghost being given afterwards. 25 Ye are the children of the prophets, and of the covenant which God made with our fathers, saying unto Abraham, And in thy seed shall all the kindreds of the earth be blessed. 26 Unto you first God, having raised up his Son Jesus, sent him to bless you, in turning away every one of you from his iniquities."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter’s gospel message was already being presented to the world as it was already promised, but later, Peter would more fully understand what that mission really entailed in chapter 10. He is only preaching to a specific people group in Jerusalem, but will eventually realize that the gospel must be presented to the entire world!"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":11,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17}],"verses":{"1":"Now Peter and John went up together into the temple at the hour of prayer, being the ninth hour.","11":"And as the lame man which was healed held Peter and John, all the people ran together unto them in the porch that is called Solomon's, greatly wondering.","12":"And when Peter saw it, he answered unto the people, Ye men of Israel, why marvel ye at this? or why look ye so earnestly on us, as though by our own power or holiness we had made this man to walk?","13":"The God of Abraham, and of Isaac, and of Jacob, the God of our fathers, hath glorified his Son Jesus; whom ye delivered up, and denied him in the presence of Pilate, when he was determined to let him go.","14":"But ye denied the Holy One and the Just, and desired a murderer to be granted unto you;","15":"And killed the Prince of life, whom God hath raised from the dead; whereof we are witnesses.","16":"And his name through faith in his name hath made this man strong, whom ye see and know: yea, the faith which is by him hath given him this perfect soundness in the presence of you all.","17":"And now, brethren, I wot that through ignorance ye did it, as did also your rulers."}},
    "4": {"chapter":4,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 04"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we saw a miracle happen, which led to another opportunity for Peter to witness to the world around him, clarifying and proving the power of the Holy Ghost as well as the legitimacy of the acclamations of Christ. This got people thinking much deeper about what was true based on their own personal religious experiences as well as what the Bible says from the Old Testament prophets. They would either have to reject the Old Testament prophecies and call God a liar, or they would have to believe and trust in who Christ said He was and the evidence given to them through the Apostles (namely Peter at the moment)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In today’s chapter, we will see exactly what happens to those that are willing to live for Christ and preach the gospel with fervency and compassion."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, we saw a miracle happen, which led to another opportunity for Peter to witness to the world around him, clarifying and proving the power of the Holy Ghost as well as the legitimacy of the acclamations of Christ. This got people thinking much deeper about what was true based on their own personal religious experiences as well as what the Bible says from the Old Testament prophets. They would either have to reject the Old Testament prophecies and call God a liar, or they would have to believe and trust in who Christ said He was and the evidence given to them through the Apostles (namely Peter at the moment). In today’s chapter, we will see exactly what happens to those that are willing to live for Christ and preach the gospel with fervency and compassion."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"The First Persecution","entries":[{"start":1,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The term “captain of the temple” indicates a “second rank” to the high priest. The Sadducees only accepted the Pentateuch (or the first five books of the Hebrew Bible) as scripture. They also wouldn’t accept the resurrection."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the Sadducees say that there is no resurrection, neither angel, nor spirit: but the Pharisees confess both.","ref":"Acts 23:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"They worked closely with the Romans to protect their own political and economic interests. I find it funny that they were “grieved” because of what was being taught about Jesus. They didn’t like the idea of free salvation. They didn’t want to accept a faith that believes in a resurrecting power. They didn’t want to accept Jesus as God in the flesh. The Sadducees were pretty sad, you see… Sorry I couldn’t help myself… 3 And they laid hands on them, and put them in hold unto the next day: for it was now eventide."},{"kind":"para","text":"They only met in the morning, so they couldn’t meet until the following day."},{"kind":"para","text":"4 Howbeit many of them which heard the word believed; and the number of the men was about five thousand. 5 And it came to pass on the morrow, that their rulers, and elders, and scribes,"},{"kind":"para","text":"There was a large group of Christians at this point, and it would be a lot for these anti-Christians to douse the crowd with their hate for them and Christ. But because the majority of the high court was run by Sadducees, it would be difficult to overthrow the power of the high court."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":12,"title":"Peter Addresses the Sanhedrin","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Annas was named high priest, and he was so named a high priest according to their customs, which would be for the rest of his life. He couldn’t lose that title according to their customs."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now Annas had sent him bound unto Caiaphas the high priest.","ref":"John 18:24"},{"kind":"para","text":"Anna’s son-in-law Caiaphas was the official high priest (according to their customs) at this time. But a true Christian knows the real high priest."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Seeing then that we have a great high priest, that is passed into the heavens, Jesus the Son of God, let us hold fast our profession.","ref":"Hebrews 4:14"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Whither the forerunner is for us entered, even Jesus, made an high priest for ever after the order of Melchisedec.","ref":"Hebrews 6:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Going through the book of Hebrews will be a great help and encouragement about this specific case study and the importance of Jesus being our high priest. 7 And when they had set them in the midst, they asked, By what power, or by what name, have ye done this?"},{"kind":"para","text":"What a great question. It was a loaded one, and they were hoping to make these Christians stumble, but of course God gives power to Peter to speak the truth in boldness and be able to stand up in this trial for what is right and good."},{"kind":"para","text":"8 Then Peter, filled with the Holy Ghost, said unto them, Ye rulers of the people, and elders of Israel, 9 If we this day be examined of the good deed done to the impotent man, by what means he is made whole;"},{"kind":"para","text":"Remember the lame man sitting by the gate called “beautiful?” Well, apparently, that was not okay to do. Peter points out how ridiculous it is to reject helping those that need help. It wasn’t for doing something bad or evil that they were in trouble for. It was because they did something good and right that they are getting in trouble for. Jesus warns the disciples that they were going to face persecution, and here they are right in the middle of it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Remember the word that I said unto you, The servant is not greater than his lord. If they have persecuted me, they will also persecute you; if they have kept my saying, they will keep yours also.","ref":"John 15:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"But if Peter hadn’t received the Holy Ghost, he wouldn’t have had the proper answer to give."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when they bring you unto the synagogues, and unto magistrates, and powers, take ye no thought how or what thing ye shall answer, or what ye shall say: For the Holy Ghost shall teach you in the same hour what ye ought to say.","ref":"Luke 12:11-12"},{"kind":"para","text":"10 Be it known unto you all, and to all the people of Israel, that by the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom ye crucified, whom God raised from the dead, even by him doth this man stand here before you whole."},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet again, Peter places the blame on the listeners directly. This is a very bold claim for Peter to make to such highly respected leaders of this place and day. But that’s what God’s Spirit does… It gives you boldness to speak during persecution."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"According to my earnest expectation and my hope, that in nothing I shall be ashamed, but that with all boldness, as always, so now also Christ shall be magnified in my body, whether it be by life, or by death.","ref":"Philippians 1:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"While it was bold to speak to other Jews about pointedly being responsible for killing Jesus, to accuse the Sanhedrin judges of this was on another level of boldness. 11 This is the stone which was set at nought of you builders, which is become the head of the corner."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter is, yet again, using vocabulary they would understand to prove Jesus."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner.","ref":"Psalm 118:22"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD, Behold, I lay in Zion for a foundation a stone, a tried stone, a precious corner stone, a sure foundation: he that believeth shall not make haste.","ref":"Isaiah 28:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"He’s essentially proving Old Testament prophecy through their rejection (and they can’t deny that they are rejecting Christ), to show the necessity placed on believing scripture, and then in Christ alone for their salvation as verse 12 indicates…"},{"kind":"para","text":"12 Neither is there salvation in any other: for there is none other name under heaven given among men, whereby we must be saved."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter is giving them no other choice but to trust in Jesus alone. He’s not doing this so that the Sanhedrin feel bad, or so that he can prove their religion wrong. He’s doing this because he knows what is right and true."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For we can do nothing against the truth, but for the truth.","ref":"2 Corinthians 13:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"He’s pushing the Sanhedrin and the higher-ups to look at the necessity that is placed upon them for salvation, seeing as they are also in their sins and in need of a Savior (whom Peter accused them of killing). Peter knew these truths to be self-evident, and it was something that he lived through and experienced himself."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"All things are delivered unto me of my Father: and no man knoweth the Son, but the Father; neither knoweth any man the Father, save the Son, and he to whomesoever the Son will reveal him.","ref":"Matthew 11:27"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He that believeth on him is not condemned: but he that believeth not is condemned already, because he hath not believed in the name of the only begotten Son of God.","ref":"John 3:18"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus saith unto him, I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me.","ref":"John 14:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He that hath the Son hath life; and he that hath not the Son of God hath not life.","ref":"1 John 5:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"This really could ruin Peter’s life at this point. This could be where he signs his death certificate for these accusations to people that had killed for less."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":22,"title":"Don’t Talk About Jesus","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Low and behold, Peter’s boldness pays off. These people see their boldness, and not only so, they claim them to be uneducated or “unlearned,” and common or “ignorant” men. There was nothing specifically special about Peter or John. They seemed like lower-class people, that were just trying to spread a message that they had learned about Jesus. Amazed at what was being said because of their boldness of it, they truly believed that they had been with Jesus. They wouldn’t deny that."},{"kind":"para","text":"But boldness didn’t come simply because they knew Jesus. If that were true, this same man (Peter) would have had boldness when Jesus was being put to death. Boldness simply came through God’s Spirit alone. The Spirit inspired boldness to the believers, and it’s something you will see the Spirit do multiple times throughout Acts."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And now, Lord, behold their threatenings: and grant unto thy servants, that with all boldness they may speak thy word,","ref":"Acts 4:29"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when they had prayed, the place was shaken where they were assembled together; and they were all filled with the Holy Ghost, and they spake the word of God with boldness.","ref":"Acts 4:31"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Preaching the kingdom of God, and teaching those things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ, with all confidence, no man forbidding him.","ref":"Acts 28:31"},{"kind":"para","text":"Just to name a few…"}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What could you say against proof literally staring you in the face? Yet many today in the world who try and reject Christ literally turn down physical evidence of salvation. You are physical evidence of God’s salvation. 15 But when they had commanded them to go aside out of the council, they conferred among themselves, 16 Saying, What shall we do to these men? for that indeed a notable miracle hath been done by them is manifest to all them that dwell in Jerusalem; and we cannot deny it. 17 But that it spread no further among the people, let us straitly threaten them, that they speak henceforth to no man in this name."},{"kind":"para","text":"This simply can’t work. Even if our government today were to give us orders to never speak about Jesus again (which is what they try to do often), we couldn’t help but speak the things we know."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For though I preach the gospel, I have nothing to glory of: for necessity is laid upon me; yea, woe is unto me, if I preach not the gospel!","ref":"1 Corinthians 9:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the only reason you have to be alive. If we are not sharing the gospel with others, we will feel empty. If you don’t feel an emptiness from missed opportunities of witnessing, there is certainly something wrong with your walk with God that needs to be looked into."},{"kind":"para","text":"I remember as a new Christian having a desire to speak to anyone and everyone I could possibly come across (even if they didn’t want to listen). One particular day, I went to Ross and overheard a conversation happening in the aisle next to the one I was in. They spoke about a car crash that recently killed their friend whom they knew for years. As I listened to the conversation, the spirit in me was stirred to walk around the corner and talk to them. When I got to them, however, I saw that they had Ross vests on and were stocking shelves. I thought to myself, “They don’t have time for me; they are on the clock. I will let this one go.” And I walked away. I have regretted that decision every day since. The Spirit reminds me of the missed opportunity all the time, and I now take every stipulation much more seriously."},{"kind":"para","text":"The disciples understood the necessity of talking about Jesus to everyone they possibly could (saved or unsaved, it didn’t matter to them). Let’s see how that new rule works out for the disciples in which it is to be implemented to… 18 And they called them, and commanded them not to speak at all nor teach in the name of Jesus. 19 But Peter and John answered and said unto them, Whether it be right in the sight of God to hearken unto you more than unto God, judge ye. 20 For we cannot but speak the things which we have seen and heard."},{"kind":"para","text":"To not talk about Christ is to literally deny speaking the truth. How can we live with ourselves when we have the truth of eternity and life and yet not speak about it? Peter states that listening to God is far more important than listening to the authorities on Earth. This is proven time and time again throughout the Bible, including (but not limited to) the Pentateuch, which is all they paid attention to anyway."},{"kind":"para","text":"Moses still gave God’s Word, even though Pharaoh’s heart was hardened to it. David still served the Lord, even though Saul was chasing him and trying to kill him. Daniel still prayed, even though they wrote into law making it illegal. Even take Jeremiah as an example. He tried to literally talk himself out of ever speaking about God’s Word again because of the amount of persecution he was receiving for it. He was thrown in jail and spoke to people about the truth of God’s Word, but grew tired of it because he felt he wasn’t seeing the proper fruit of his labor."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then I said, I will not make mention of him, nor speak any more in his name. But his word was in mine heart as a burning fire shut up in my bones, and I was weary with forbearing, and I could not stay.","ref":"Jeremiah 20:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Or look to the prophet Isaiah, who did some really strange and embarrassing things for the Word of God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"at that time the LORD spoke by Isaiah the son of Amoz, saying, “Go, and loose the sackcloth from your waist and take off your sandals from your feet,” and he did so, walking naked and barefoot. Then the LORD said, “As my servant Isaiah has walked naked and barefoot for three years as a sign and a portent against Egypt and Cush, so shall the king of Assyria lead away the Egyptian captives and the Cushite exiles, both the young and the old, naked and barefoot, with buttocks uncovered, the nakedness of Egypt.","ref":"Isaiah 20:2-4"},{"kind":"para","text":"If Isaiah can preach God’s Word naked, what’s our excuse for not preaching it? We shouldn’t be able to help ourselves (if we are true, born-again Christians) but to listen to God on the matter of telling others about who Christ is. 21 So when they had further threatened them, they let them go, finding nothing how they might punish them, because of the people: for all men glorified God for that which was done. 22 For the man was above forty years old, on whom this miracle of healing was shewed."},{"kind":"para","text":"No amount of threatening should keep our mouths shut."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":31,"title":"The Spirit Fills","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Christian brothers and sisters should be able to share with one another the ways in which God used us. That’s what church ministry is for. Bible study is great and certainly has its place and part, but we should also be actively using what we learn and then telling our own “company” about what we just experienced! 24 And when they heard that, they lifted up their voice to God with one accord, and said, Lord, thou art God, which hast made heaven, and earth, and the sea, and all that in them is: 25 Who by the mouth of thy servant David hast said, Why did the heathen rage, and the people imagine vain things? 26 The kings of the earth stood up, and the rulers were gathered together against the Lord, and against his Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a direct quote from Psalm chapter 2. They are praying in this way to display that they were able to apply their Bibles to the current situation. They may not have fully understood the purpose of this time of trial, but they understood that God has everything prepared for those that love Him and are called by Him."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And we know that all things work together for good to them that love God, to them who are the called according to his purpose.","ref":"Romans 8:28 27 For of a truth against thy holy child Jesus, whom thou hast anointed, both Herod, and Pontius Pilate, with the Gentiles, and the people of Israel, were gathered together, 28 For to do whatsoever thy hand and thy counsel determined before to be done."},{"kind":"para","text":"Herod and Pontius Pilate were both indicated as the “kings” and “rulers” mentioned in verse 26 and in Psalm 2. They were made to be in charge at this time for this specific purpose according to God’s sovereign plan and will. 29 And now, Lord, behold their threatenings: and grant unto thy servants, that with all boldness they may speak thy word, 30 By stretching forth thine hand to heal; and that signs and wonders may be done by the name of thy holy child Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"They didn’t want to stop speaking about Jesus, and so they asked for boldness to continue even in the face of threats from authority. They knew that they couldn’t continue to do the things that God tells them to do unless God gives them access to do it. They understood what they were up against, and they needed the Lord’s moving in order to continue to do what the Lord required."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find, knock, and it shall be opened unto you:","ref":"Matthew 7:7 31 And when they had prayed, the place was shaken where they were assembled together; and they were all filled with the Holy Ghost, and they spake the word of God with boldness."},{"kind":"para","text":"They received an immediate answer to their prayer. This doesn’t happen often, but it’s amazing to see it happen when it does. This verse also indicates that people can be “filled” with the Holy Spirit more than once. Peter and the disciples had already been filled with the Holy Spirit, so how could they be filled again? We must renew our filling of God’s Spirit continually, so that we aren’t running dry the next time we are to do God’s work."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":37,"title":"Continuing to Share Christ","entries":[{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were able to unify with one another and really connect through God’s Word and Spirit. This is how you know if the church is doing the right things (when we share the Spirit with one another, having one heart, and one soul). We may not have much in common with others, but if we are Christians, we have Christ in common, and that is all we need to have in common in order for us to be of one heart and soul."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Only let your conversation be as it becometh the gospel of Christ: that whether I come and see you, or else be absent, I may hear of your affairs, that ye stand fast in one spirit, with one mind striving together for the faith of the gospel;","ref":"Philippians 1:27 33 And with great power gave the apostles witness of the resurrection of the Lord Jesus: and great grace was upon them all."},{"kind":"para","text":"Instead of silencing the Christians (which was what the Sanhedrin intended to do with their threats), the talks of Christ and Christianity became more fluent and grew much mightier. The trial gave them MORE unction to speak about God’s Word rather than give them fear and turmoil because of it. 34 Neither was there any among them that lacked: for as many as were possessors of lands or houses sold them, and brought the prices of the things that were sold, 35 And laid them down at the apostles' feet: and distribution was made unto every man according as he had need."},{"kind":"para","text":"It should go without saying that Christians should be there for one another. Specifically for the saved. If we are saved, we are family, and good family will do whatever it takes to take care of each other. These men were selling their own possessions and properties to help each other grow in the right way. That’s a difficult thing to do for people you know, but these men and women of the faith loved each other with such Godly love that they didn’t have a single issue about it. 36 And Joses, who by the apostles was surnamed Barnabas, (which is, being interpreted, The son of consolation,) a Levite, and of the country of Cyprus, 37 Having land, sold it, and brought the money, and laid it at the apostles' feet."},{"kind":"para","text":"The introduction of Barnabas should not be looked at as a small thing. Barnabas will be an important and major character in Acts later on, especially when Paul comes on the scene. I won’t spoil it, but Barnabas is a man that lives up to his name as “the son of consolation (encouragement).”"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":32,"end":32}],"verses":{"1":"And as they spake unto the people, the priests, and the captain of the temple, and the Sadducees, came upon them,","2":"Being grieved that they taught the people, and preached through Jesus the resurrection from the dead.","6":"And Annas the high priest, and Caiaphas, and John, and Alexander, and as many as were of the kindred of the high priest, were gathered together at Jerusalem.","13":"Now when they saw the boldness of Peter and John, and perceived that they were unlearned and ignorant men, they marvelled; and they took knowledge of them, that they had been with Jesus.","14":"And beholding the man which was healed standing with them, they could say nothing against it.","23":"And being let go, they went to their own company, and reported all that the chief priests and elders had said unto them.","32":"And the multitude of them that believed were of one heart and of one soul: neither said any of them that ought of the things which he possessed was his own; but they had all things common."}},
    "5": {"chapter":5,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 05"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter we saw Peter’s boldness continue to grow as he confronts the religious leaders of that day try and rebuke his preaching of the gospel. He used the Old Testament proofs to show Jesus’ legitimacy, as well as their own false teachings to prove the need for truth. It was astounding to the religious of that day, and instead of inflicting pain on Peter, they just warned them to never speak about Jesus again and sent them on their way. They prayed about what to do, and decided it was better to just continue trusting in God."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see the first church becoming more established and people being consistently added to the church, and we see the consequences of trying to ruin the church with sin entering in, and how God chose to protect this very fragile start."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, we saw Peter’s boldness continue to grow as he confronted the religious leaders of that day who tried to rebuke his preaching of the gospel. He used the Old Testament proofs to show Jesus’ legitimacy, as well as their own false teachings to prove the need for truth. It was astounding to the religious of that day, and instead of inflicting pain on Peter, they just warned them to never speak about Jesus again and sent them on their way. They prayed about what to do and decided it was better to just continue trusting in God. In this chapter, we see the first church becoming more established, and people being consistently added to the church. We see the consequences of trying to ruin the church with sin entering in, and how God chose to protect this very fragile start."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":11,"title":"Ananias and Sapphire Lie","entries":[{"start":1,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The term “kept back” doesn’t mean that they just withheld knowledge of some important facts. This means they secretly desired to lie about what they were doing (as if to have one over on the apostles). In case you don’t remember from our last chapter…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Neither was there any among them that lacked: for as many as were possessors of lands or houses sold them, and brought the prices of the things that were sold, And laid them down at the apostles’ feet: and distribution was made unto every man according as he had need.","ref":"Acts 4:34-35"},{"kind":"para","text":"The term means “to purloin, embezzle, withdraw covertly, and appropriate to one’s own use.” There was no reason at all for Ananias and Sapphira to bring anything to the apostles, let alone to lie, other than if they were trying to take some of the possessions of others for themselves in some way so they would have more. But the fact that they desired to lie proves they were out to make division with this group of Christians, and God wasn’t having that with the first church."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is where the church was to start, and if it started on sin, we would have a serious problem today (more than we do now). If our example church started off as the garden started, what a poor example Christ would be in our church today. 3 But Peter said, Ananias, why hath Satan filled thine heart to lie to the Holy Ghost, and to keep back part of the price of the land? 4 Whiles it remained, was it not thine own? and after it was sold, was it not in thine own power? why hast thou conceived this thing in thine heart? thou hast not lied unto men, but unto God."},{"kind":"para","text":"That’s the ultimate issue here. It’s one thing to lie to your brothers and sisters in Christ, it’s another thing entirely to lie to the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Better is it that thou shouldest not vow, than that thou shouldest vow and not pay.","ref":"Ecclesiastes 5:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"No one was forced to sell their property, or even coerced into it. The only reason anyone was to give anything was simply because they desired to help serve others through the Will and Work of God by what God had already blessed them with. It would have been sufficient and understood if Ananias and Sapphira just simply didn’t give anything at all, rather than gamble their lives with deceit and lies in this first church. 5 And Ananias hearing these words fell down, and gave up the ghost: and great fear came on all them that heard these things. 6 And the young men arose, wound him up, and carried him out, and buried him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Was this necessary? I can’t really say. There’s obviously a reason that God does this, and my guess is He desired the church would have a good start. They didn’t need attacks from the inside right off the get-go. While you may not die today, God does not bless a church that is openly sinning against Him. Too many churches have fallen apart because they have lost their first love, or because they desire to spread disunity among the brethren."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"These six things doth the LORD hate: yea, seven are an abomination unto him: A proud look, a lying tongue, and hands that shed innocent blood, An heart that deviseth wicked imaginations, feet that be swift in running to mischief, A false witness that speaketh lies, and he that soweth discord among brethren.","ref":"Proverbs 6:16-19"},{"kind":"para","text":"Think about this list for a second. Every single one of these is literally the thought and definition of Ananias and Sapphira. They were proud of what they already owned and probably thought they were much better off than others in the church (and they very well might have been). They lied about what they did even though they literally didn’t have to do anything to begin with. They had a heart that sought to do wicked and hurtful things to others. They were quick to cause mischief among the church members by trying to take what they didn’t need and then some. They were sowing seeds of discord among the brethren by allowing them to think they were doing exactly what they were supposed to be doing. They were playing the part just as Judas did, and the disciples already knew how that played out. The only thing that they can’t be blamed for is “shedding innocent blood.” That’s because God put a stop to it before it got that far. The early church needed to understand how serious this was. 7 And it was about the space of three hours after, when his wife, not knowing what was done, came in. 8 And Peter answered unto her, Tell me whether ye sold the land for so much? And she said, Yea, for so much. 9 Then Peter said unto her, How is it that ye have agreed together to tempt the Spirit of the Lord? behold, the feet of them which have buried thy husband are at the door, and shall carry thee out. 10 Then fell she down straightway at his feet, and yielded up the ghost: and the young men came in, and found her dead, and, carrying her forth, buried her by her husband."},{"kind":"para","text":"A second chance was given here. Granted, she didn’t know her husband had died yet, but she had a chance to save herself and chose to lie about it with her husband, once again, for no reason. There was no need to lie about this at all. There was no reason to make such a case for something that really wasn’t even that vital to their personal growth. But they chose to continue to deceive others and try to put themselves on top. It’s a dangerous game to play when you are attempting to deceive those of the Lord’s household."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is not to say they were “false believers.” I am not the one to judge who is genuine and who is not. This is to say they were not faithful to God’s House, and the Christian faith is no game. Though we can have joy in this faith, it’s one where we must take serious precautions to only show forth Christ in everything we do. Not to get people to look at us, but to get people to look at Him. If we cannot do that, we should not be involved. 11 And great fear came upon all the church, and upon as many as heard these things."},{"kind":"para","text":"This served as an example of how important the church was to God. God gave His Son to die for it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Husbands, love your wives, even as Christ also loved the church, and gave himself for it;","ref":"Ephesians 5:25"},{"kind":"para","text":"God certainly wasn’t going to allow sin to run rampant in this very first example of it. Still today, He takes care of His church. You look at mega churches or prosperity churches today who run this same kind of game, but their motives will ultimately lead them to the grave. Their rewards in those churches are served here. They have no reward in eternity because they have robbed themselves of it. They desire to live their best life now when Christ has called us to store up our treasures in Heaven."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Lay not up for yourselves treasures upon earth, where moth and rust doth corrupt, and where thieves break through and steal: But lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust doth corrupt, and where thieves do not break through nor steal: For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.","ref":"Matthew 6:19-21"},{"kind":"para","text":"We should be looking to Christ, and Him alone, to establish our faith. Earthly rewards rob you of Heavenly ones. Just before I moved to Texas for full-time ministry, we were praying at the front of the church, and a couple saw us praying. They came up to us after the service and asked if we would like to join them for dinner at In-N-Out. We accepted their invitation and drove to In-N-Out. Once we arrived, I told my wife, “I’m going to pay for their meal.” We got in there, they ordered first, and we ordered right after them and paid without hesitation. The man asked, “Why did you do that? We invited you!” I said, “I just wanted to pay for you guys, I hope that is okay.” I’ll never forget his response, “You robbed me of a blessing!” he said."},{"kind":"para","text":"That was funny to me at first, but the more I thought about it, the more I realized that his blessing was eternal. It wasn’t about the food; it was about the treasures he was storing in eternity, and I literally robbed him of that. I say this all to say, if you ever want to buy me In-N-Out, I will gladly let you today… No, but seriously, when we expect everyone else to take care of us, we allow ourselves to lose out on eternal blessings that could impact and benefit us for our own eternities, and blessings even present on Earth."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":16,"title":"Miracles at Jerusalem","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The believers had prayed for the power to give signs and wonders in the last chapter."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"By stretching forth thine hand to heal; and that signs and wonders may be done by the name of thy holy child Jesus.","ref":"Acts 4:30"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, God granted them that power, and they were witnessing and preaching often to those around them, allowing God’s gift of the Spirit to really show forth who they belonged to. 13 And of the rest durst no man join himself to them: but the people magnified them."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are under the consideration and assumption that the term “them” is the Apostles. The Apostles were gifted preachers because they were with Christ, and Christ had anointed them above all the others for this specific purpose. They were joined with the Apostles, but they were not doing the same kinds of things as the Apostles were, because the Apostles were the only ones with that kind of power to do “signs and wonders” like this. 14 And believers were the more added to the Lord, multitudes both of men and women.)"},{"kind":"para","text":"Two deaths within seemingly hours, and the church was still growing. Obviously, there was a power in this church, and it was all through the belief and structure of Jesus Christ being the focal point. A church today may not grow numerically like this church was, usually because it is required that they grow spiritually first. The Apostles had their spiritual growth more times than not (which won’t last forever, because they still mess up, including Peter, which Paul will address later on), but for the start of this church, they were growing numerically because they had the right spiritual standing in the preaching and teaching of God’s Word."},{"kind":"para","text":"Numerical growth, however, means nothing if there’s no spiritual growth within the believers. In order for this numerical growth to be worth the investment, they were going to have to continually pour into each other through God’s Word daily."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they, continuing daily with one accord in the temple, and breaking bread from house to house, did eat their meat with gladness and singleness of heart.","ref":"Acts 2:46 15 Insomuch that they brought forth the sick into the streets, and laid them on beds and couches, that at the least the shadow of Peter passing by might overshadow some of them. 16 There came also a multitude out of the cities round about unto Jerusalem, bringing sick folks, and them which were vexed with unclean spirits: and they were healed every one."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is understood that the Apostles were so indwelled with the Spirit, and God’s Spirit was so powerful in them, that others would experience healings. Once again, not because they were anything special—Peter had already acknowledged how his power does nothing for the healing and salvation of others—but because God was using the Apostles mightily for this first church."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, just because we see God working doesn’t mean we see Satan dormant and fleeing. No, when God works, Satan is always working as well, looking to use whatever he can to stop God’s working in every situation."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":28,"title":"Second Persecution","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The term “indignation” means “an envious and contentious rivalry, jealousy.” These Sadducees were jealous because their own teachings were being triumphed over by the truth of the gospel, and so they were about to take matters into their own hands. 18 And laid their hands on the apostles, and put them in the common prison."},{"kind":"para","text":"Every single time the apostles are thrown into prison, it always gives them a platform to speak EVEN MORE about the truths of the gospel and Scripture. This is a good reminder to us that when there is a trial or persecution, the platform for preaching is much larger. 19 But the angel of the Lord by night opened the prison doors, and brought them forth, and said, 20 Go, stand and speak in the temple to the people all the words of this life. 21 And when they heard that, they entered into the temple early in the morning, and taught. But the high priest came, and they that were with him, and called the council together, and all the senate of the children of Israel, and sent to the prison to have them brought."},{"kind":"para","text":"You cannot shut up God’s Word from doing what He desires it to do. They may bind us physically, but spiritually we are only bound to God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherein I suffer trouble, as an evil doer, even unto bonds; but the word of God is not bound.","ref":"2 Timothy 2:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"These religionists can do whatever they want, but in the end, their will will always be overpowered by God’s will. 22 But when the officers came, and found them not in the prison, they returned and told, 23 Saying, The prison truly found we shut with all safety, and the keepers standing without before the doors: but when we had opened, we found no man within. 24 Now when the high priest and the captain of the temple and the chief priests heard these things, they doubted of them whereunto this would grow. 25 Then came one and told them, saying, Behold, the men whom ye put in prison are standing in the temple, and teaching the people. 26 Then went the captain with the officers, and brought them without violence: for they feared the people, lest they should have been stoned."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even with all of the obvious signs of the apostles, and God’s leading in their lives and ways, these Sadducees were still not convinced of what they were doing. They hated the idea of them being wrong. They could not take Christ personally because of their own arrogance and pride."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But he giveth more grace. Wherefore he saith, God resisteth the proud, but giveth grace unto the humble.","ref":"James 4:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"God cannot meet with a prideful man, because a prideful man thinks he is better without God. 27 And when they had brought them, they set them before the council: and the high priest asked them, 28 Saying, Did not we straitly command you that ye should not teach in this name? and, behold, ye have filled Jerusalem with your doctrine, and intend to bring this man's blood upon us."},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems the message was leaving them convicted?"}]}]},{"start":29,"end":32,"title":"The Answer of The Apostles","entries":[{"start":29,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, Peter boldly lays accusations on these governmental and religious leaders. However, this time it does not bode well for him. It goes to show that, because the message was always the same, the truth didn’t change. The truth doesn’t change. There are no holes or gaps in the truth."},{"kind":"para","text":"They have turned what they considered to be “bold” speaking before into “stupidity.” They were devising ways to take care of this without actually killing them because they were afraid that the others would start a revolt and cause mayhem on the Apostles’ behalf."}]}]},{"start":33,"end":39,"title":"Take Heed","entries":[{"start":33,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This “Gamaliel” was bringing back historical evidence of two other revolts that had caused issues for them before. He mentioned Theudas, a revolutionary who was killed because of his ideas regarding the government and rulership of that time. His death apparently caused a stir among his followers. Gamaliel then reminded them of Judas the Galilean, who is said to have come “after him.” Judas led a tax revolt in AD 6. Although there is no other historical record of “Theudas,” he was probably a leader of a revolt following the death of Herod the Great in 4 BC."},{"kind":"para","text":"Gamaliel reminded the leaders that they would not want another uprising like before. No doubt, many people died, but now it seemed there were much more than those revolts who would rise up against the government of the day if they were to do something foolish like kill the Apostles. They would face an all-out war against thousands of men and women right in their own backyard."}]},{"start":38,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, this is the wisest thing anyone has said so far in this counsel. If it’s a man, then it won’t matter anyway because it will ultimately fail. If it’s of God, though, you will have really messed up, and you will be in great opposition against a power you will never be able to overthrow."}]}]},{"start":40,"end":42,"title":"The Apostles Beaten","entries":[{"start":40,"end":40,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t suppose the “beating” was just a slap on the wrist and a warning. My assumption is the beating was not kind to them. They were probably hurt beyond measure, to the point where they may have even wondered in their minds if this was all worth it. It was probably a beating through stripes, which was required by the Jewish law."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Of the Jews five times received I forty stripes save one.","ref":"2 Corinthians 11:24"},{"kind":"para","text":"But afterward this is how they are seen… 41 And they departed from the presence of the council, rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer shame for his name."},{"kind":"para","text":"Most Christians today would not consider a beating something to rejoice over. When we are tried and we are persecuted, the last thing we desire to do is rejoice and claim how amazingly worthy we feel for being whipped for Christ. Yet that is exactly how the Apostles felt. They knew they were making a huge change for the future, and the fact that they were the ones chosen to be in this ministry and they were able to preach to thousands and see thousands converted was a huge win to them. May we seek to have this kind of countenance when there is a trial in our lives."},{"kind":"para","text":"42 And daily in the temple, and in every house, they ceased not to teach and preach Jesus Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"The beatings did nothing to deter them from continuing to do what they knew they needed to do. In fact, the beatings just further clarified their necessity to tell others about Jesus. If anything, it cemented in their minds that this world needs Christ more than ever because of the cruelty they have towards Him. This is quite an encouraging conclusion to a chapter that seems mostly dismal and harsh. May we take the stripes of others who set the faith as a motivating factor to continue to preach and teach the Bible to everyone as often as we can."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":29,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":37},{"start":38,"end":39},{"start":40,"end":40}],"verses":{"1":"But a certain man named Ananias, with Sapphira his wife, sold a possession,","2":"And kept back part of the price, his wife also being privy to it, and brought a certain part, and laid it at the apostles' feet.","12":"And by the hands of the apostles were many signs and wonders wrought among the people; (and they were all with one accord in Solomon's porch.","17":"Then the high priest rose up, and all they that were with him, (which is the sect of the Sadducees,) and were filled with indignation,","29":"Then Peter and the other apostles answered and said, We ought to obey God rather than men.","30":"The God of our fathers raised up Jesus, whom ye slew and hanged on a tree.","31":"Him hath God exalted with his right hand to be a Prince and a Saviour, for to give repentance to Israel, and forgiveness of sins.","32":"And we are his witnesses of these things; and so is also the Holy Ghost, whom God hath given to them that obey him.","33":"When they heard that, they were cut to the heart, and took counsel to slay them.","34":"Then stood there up one in the council, a Pharisee, named Gamaliel, a doctor of the law, had in reputation among all the people, and commanded to put the apostles forth a little space;","35":"And said unto them, Ye men of Israel, take heed to yourselves what ye intend to do as touching these men.","36":"For before these days rose up Theudas, boasting himself to be somebody; to whom a number of men, about four hundred, joined themselves: who was slain; and all, as many as obeyed him, were scattered, and brought to nought.","37":"After this man rose up Judas of Galilee in the days of the taxing, and drew away much people after him: he also perished; and all, even as many as obeyed him, were dispersed.","38":"And now I say unto you, Refrain from these men, and let them alone: for if this counsel or this work be of men, it will come to nought:","39":"But if it be of God, ye cannot overthrow it; lest haply ye be found even to fight against God.","40":"And to him they agreed: and when they had called the apostles, and beaten them, they commanded that they should not speak in the name of Jesus, and let them go."}},
    "6": {"chapter":6,"intro":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"Likewise must the deacons be grave, not doubletongued, not given to much wine, not greedy of filthy lucre; Holding the mystery of the faith in a pure conscience. And let these also first be proved; then let them use the office of a deacon, being found blameless. Even so must their wives be grave, not slanderers, sober, faithful in all things. Let the deacons be the husbands of one wife, ruling their children and their own houses well. For they that have used the office of a deacon well purchase to themselves a good degree, and great boldness in the faith which is in Christ Jesus.","ref":"1 Timothy 3:8-13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Deacons are to serve alongside the pastor as helpful ministers directly with the pastor. This is not to take away the pastor’s ministering. If a deacon has been set up to completely rid the pastor’s assembly with other believers, or what some pastors have referred to as “lesser” believers, then the deacon is more a pastor than the pastor himself. The role of the deacon is to keep the pastor’s mind focused in areas that are important, while simultaneously reminding the pastor of needs that must be met, allowing opportunity to personally meet those needs if possible."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, we focused on the boldness of the apostles’ doctrine and the trouble that led them into it. The false religious leaders of that day couldn’t do much about it at this point because there were so many people already believing in Christ (little did they know that they were meek and lowly people because of Christ’s testimony), so they decided to beat the apostles into submission and commanded that they not speak about Christ again. However, it didn’t stop the apostles, and our last verse says they continued to preach and meet to talk about Christ. Many consider this next chapter to be about the order of the first deacons in the church. Deacons are recognized to be extremely valuable assets to the church and pastors as those who take care of the needs that the pastors don’t necessarily have time for (though they wish they could). Pastors are being pulled in hundreds of different directions all the time and (should anyway) desire to reach out to each individual need of the church and ministering, but don’t always get the chance to. When we are talking about the church of this size (thousands of people are in this church, and it’s nearly impossible for the apostles to get to every need), they source out deacons (faithful men) who can take some of the overbearing load off the pastors’ shoulders. Paul gives an adept summary of the purpose, position, and evaluation of proper deacons…"],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Seven Chosen","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Grecians” were Jewish Christians whose primary language was Greek rather than Aramaic like the Hebrews. Obviously, the widows of the Grecians were being unintentionally overlooked, and they were making it known to the leaders of the church that there has to be something done in regards to helping those that are in spiritual need with relation to this matter."},{"kind":"para","text":"There’s an importance in the church of taking care of the widows and children, and as children of God, we should take special care to look out for those that have this spiritual need!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Pure religion and undefiled before God and the Father is this, To visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction, and to keep himself unspotted from the world.","ref":"James 1:27 2 Then the twelve called the multitude of the disciples unto them, and said, It is not reason that we should leave the word of God, and serve tables. 3 Wherefore, brethren, look ye out among you seven men of honest report, full of the Holy Ghost and wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business. 4 But we will give ourselves continually to prayer, and to the ministry of the word."},{"kind":"para","text":"This can come off insanely distastefully if we read this in the wrong tone. They are not saying that these issues are lesser issues, or too little for them to really care about and should be left to those that are not pastors and preachers. They are giving notice to the fact that they have been called to do something that will ultimately save lives and lead people, and they have a necessity to focus on that. If these Apostles didn’t truly care about the widows, they wouldn’t be taking the time to find men of honest faith that can do what needs to be done."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you look at verse 3, they have very specific demands. They say to pick seven men specifically, who have an honest report (being transparent and open about their past, having repentance and acceptance of salvation), they have to be full of the Holy Ghost, and have to be extremely wise. The Apostles were driving the importance of these characteristics, because these people needed to be able to take on a large load of ministry that they were not even sure they were prepared for."},{"kind":"para","text":"Again, this is not to say the Apostles were not to take these same responsibilities, but they had more important matters at the moment, and they knew that there were some compassionate, loving, and faithful men among them who could help lighten the load, unless the Apostles absolutely had to leave their position to attend to the many thousands of needs that these people were facing outside of the church. 5 And the saying pleased the whole multitude: and they chose Stephen, a man full of faith and of the Holy Ghost, and Philip, and Prochorus, and Nicanor, and Timon, and Parmenas, and Nicolas a proselyte of Antioch:"},{"kind":"para","text":"This verse further proves that the Apostles weren’t being staunch in their decision to stay and just preach God’s Word over ministering outside of the church. If this is who they really were, what they said would not “please the whole multitude.” I find it interesting that Stephen is the only one of these chosen men who has a label of “full of faith and the Holy Ghost…”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Granted, each man would have the right spirit, and each one was carefully chosen based on the same qualities and attributes. However, Stephen would be a very different man, as we will soon find out in the chapters to come. Philip, as well, both of these first “deacons,” have incredible testimonies and will make the faith of God prosper abundantly moving outside of the first church! We are not sure what happens with the other five, however… 6 Whom they set before the apostles: and when they had prayed, they laid their hands on them."},{"kind":"para","text":"This “laying of hands” was a confirmation that the church was fully behind these men and trusted that the Lord would use them for these specific and intricate needs. We will see “laying on of hands” several times throughout Acts. It is used as healing (9:7), as the gift of the Spirit (8:18; 9:17; 19:6), and as the commissioning to a ministry (here and 13:3). This is where most churches get the idea that these men were deacons, as they were sent out for this specific ministry to help serve."},{"kind":"para","text":"The word “Deacon” means “to serve, help, or give assistance.” In verse 2, it uses the verb “serve,” which is the same verb used to describe this specific office in 1 Timothy 3:10 and 13. However, this is also a common verb for “render service.” So whether or not you believe they were the first deacons doesn’t really matter for the context referred to here. We can only hope that we would be just as full of the Spirit and faith as these men to be leaned on in the same way and given apt opportunities to speak, teach, and preach, just like they do, and (most importantly) be trusted to minister to the needs of others with love and care through the Spirit. 7 And the word of God increased; and the number of the disciples multiplied in Jerusalem greatly; and a great company of the priests were obedient to the faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"Allowing these men to take these needs gave the Apostles more opportunity and time to preach and exhort to these people in Jerusalem so that even more people would continue to be saved. The first church was growing immensely, and obviously the spreading out of the ministry gave a huge opportunity to the Apostles to grow the church bigger than they were before."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then they that gladly received his word were baptized: and the same day there were added unto them about three thousand souls.","ref":"Acts 2:41"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Praising God, and having favour with all the people. And the Lord added to the church daily such as should be saved.","ref":"Acts 2:47"},{"kind":"para","text":"But now we get to this verse and it no longer says that there were people “added,” but instead it says they were “multiplied!” I’m not a mathematician, but I know that I can get to a greater number if I multiply rather than add. We see the numbers increasing, not because they were just trying to add people to the church. They were increasing because their Spirit was increasing. We must receive an increased Spirit before we can receive an increased church."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":15,"title":"Stephen Is Seized","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Stephen was joyously taking his new role with strides! He enjoyed the work he was tasked with, and people were starting to take notice of the faith of these “deacons,” if you will. Stephen’s faith in God was producing amazing power from God, so that people were amazed and in awe of who these men of faith were."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Stephen was tasked with speaking to the Grecians, and now was speaking to much more than just them. “The Libertines” were Jews who had been enslaved and then granted freedom. Cyrenians and Alexandrians represented North Africa. Those from Cilicia and Asia represented the area which is now called Turkey. Stephen had a large assortment of people that were hearing the gospel for the first time and would, no doubt, bring what they were hearing back to their hometown to those that would more than likely be saved!"}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is incredible! It wasn’t so much about his miracles and wonders. It wasn’t even about the feeding and the charity he was giving to those that were needful (these people were mostly poor and helpless). They were drawn in by his wisdom and spirit. Oh, how I wish for such a testimony."},{"kind":"para","text":"I desire a Spirit and Wisdom that would draw people to the Lord and salvation. It should be the Christians’ utmost focus that we would be so filled with the Spirit that it radiates in everything we do, so that others would be drawn to it and desire to know more. Alas, many of us are callous, and because of that, people are kind of taken back by our spirit and instead of not being able to resist our wisdom and spirit, they are able to run the opposite direction without an issue. 11 Then they suborned men, which said, We have heard him speak blasphemous words against Moses, and against God."},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, there are always those who make an issue for those of the faith. Satan is always looking to cause issues when God is doing something. Even still, this issue is going to lead to something far greater, better than it will seem as it’s unfolding, but Stephen is about to set up one of the greatest movements in history."},{"kind":"para","text":"12 And they stirred up the people, and the elders, and the scribes, and came upon him, and caught him, and brought him to the council,"},{"kind":"para","text":"We are told to “stir up” our spirit…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore I put thee in remembrance that thou stir up the gift of God, which is in thee by the putting on of my hands.","ref":"2 Timothy 1:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now while Paul waited for them at Athens, his spirit was stirred in him, when he saw the city wholly given to idolatry.","ref":"Acts 17:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"But Satan “stirs up” too, and whenever he causes a stir up, he truly believes he has one over on God, but always makes a better outcome for the BENEFIT of God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the Jews stirred up the devout and honourable women, and the chief men of the city, and raised persecution against Paul and Barnabas, and expelled them out of their coasts.","ref":"Acts 13:50"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the unbelieving Jews stirred up the Gentiles, and made their minds evil affected against the brethren.","ref":"Acts 14:2 13 And set up false witnesses, which said, This man ceaseth not to speak blasphemous words against this holy place, and the law: 14 For we have heard him say, that this Jesus of Nazareth shall destroy this place, and shall change the customs which Moses delivered us."},{"kind":"para","text":"My assumption is that Stephen was quoting Jesus from Mark about the rebuilding of the temple."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"We heard him say, I will destroy this temple that is made with hands, and within three days I will build another made without hands.","ref":"Mark 14:58"},{"kind":"para","text":"But John clarifies what Jesus meant as referring to His own body…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus answered and said unto them, Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up. Then said the Jews, Forty and six years was this temple in building, and wilt thou rear it up in three days? But he spake of the temple of his body.","ref":"John 2:19-21"},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, the odds were stacked against Stephen because these people were literally set up to lie against anything Stephen actually did and said. They, however, may have taken half-truths to stretch their lie and make it more believable so that there would be a case against Stephen. 15 And all that sat in the council, looking stedfastly on him, saw his face as it had been the face of an angel."},{"kind":"para","text":"This man was a spectacle. He’s so innocent and compassionate. So filled with God’s Spirit, not a mean bone in His body. God’s glory was shining on this man and on Stephens’ face because of his closeness to God and His Spirit. The same happened with Moses…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, when Moses came down from mount Sinai with the two tables of testimony in Moses' hand, when he came down from the mount, that Moses wist not that the skin of his face shone while he talked with him. And when Aaron and all the children of Israel saw Moses, behold, the skin of his face shone; and they were afraid to come nigh him.","ref":"Exodus 34:29-30"},{"kind":"para","text":"And with Jesus…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And was transfigured before them: and his face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light.","ref":"Matthew 17:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"You can see the brightness and closeness in others as well who are full of the Spirit of God. Stephen was not there to try and cause issues or prove everyone else’s religion wrong. Stephen was there to love people, show Christ to people, and help those that needed support. Now they will put him to the ultimate test in the next chapter…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10}],"verses":{"1":"And in those days, when the number of the disciples was multiplied, there arose a murmuring of the Grecians against the Hebrews, because their widows were neglected in the daily ministration.","8":"And Stephen, full of faith and power, did great wonders and miracles among the people.","9":"Then there arose certain of the synagogue, which is called the synagogue of the Libertines, and Cyrenians, and Alexandrians, and of them of Cilicia and of Asia, disputing with Stephen.","10":"And they were not able to resist the wisdom and the spirit by which he spake."}},
    "7": {"chapter":7,"intro":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore, brethren, look ye out among you seven men of honest report, full of the Holy Ghost and wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business.","ref":"Acts 6:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"Our main focus in this chapter will be on Stephen, who was doing a great job in the work he was doing, but some were not too thrilled about the things he was saying. Supposing they would put him in his place (and were willing to allow those in need to suffer without the help that Stephen was supplying), they spread rumors and lies to bring Stephen before the “Council” of elders, scribes, and people from multiple walks of life."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then there arose certain of the synagogue, which is called the synagogue of the Libertines, and Cyrenians, and Alexandrians, and of them of Cilicia and of Asia, disputing with Stephen.","ref":"Acts 6:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Stephen wasn’t doing anything heinous or wrong. Everything he was doing was backed up and proven by the scripture he was teaching."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And all that sat in the council, looking stedfastly on him, saw his face as it had been the face of an angel.","ref":"Acts 6:15"}],"previously":["The Apostles needed some help taking care of some of the ministry that had been neglected. They didn’t desire to leave it uncared for or unnoticed, so they chose seven individuals who were ready to serve and full of the Holy Ghost, proving to be fit for this specific type of work."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"Stephen Speaks","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Stephen’s defense is the longest speech in Acts. He basically uses the time he has to summarize Old Testament history that makes his charges completely different than before. He pointed out that as religious as they have made themselves to be, they are the ones disobeying God because they rejected His appointed leaders."},{"kind":"para","text":"1 Then said the high priest, Are these things so?"},{"kind":"para","text":"“Is what they are saying about you true?” “Are you really speaking about a man named Jesus that will try and destroy our temple, and will try and change our religious viewpoints?” The answer that Stephen could have given was no. He could have said, “I told these men that Jesus said He will destroy the temple of His body, and in three days He rose again, which ultimately proved and finished any customs you might think are correct and holy.” Stephen didn’t choose quite this route, however, and instead preached!"}]}]},{"start":2,"end":8,"title":"Linking to Abraham","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Stephen gives a perfect culmination of the start of the Bible throughout these coming verses. 2 And he said, Men, brethren, and fathers, hearken; The God of glory appeared unto our father Abraham, when he was in Mesopotamia, before he dwelt in Charran, 3 And said unto him, Get thee out of thy country, and from thy kindred, and come into the land which I shall shew thee. 4 Then came he out of the land of the Chaldaeans, and dwelt in Charran: and from thence, when his father was dead, he removed him into this land, wherein ye now dwell. 5 And he gave him none inheritance in it, no, not so much as to set his foot on: yet he promised that he would give it to him for a possession, and to his seed after him, when as yet he had no child."},{"kind":"para","text":"He starts off by mentioning Abraham. He digs deep and talks about THEIR lineage, and the place in which THEY are residing. He does this masterfully in verse 4, connecting their lineage to their current position. 6 And God spake on this wise, That his seed should sojourn in a strange land; and that they should bring them into bondage, and entreat them evil four hundred years."},{"kind":"para","text":"Those 400 years were spent in Egypt, and that’s proven through the Torah, which these religious leaders would have known extremely well. 7 And the nation to whom they shall be in bondage will I judge, said God: and after that shall they come forth, and serve me in this place."},{"kind":"para","text":"Stephen is combining a couple verses here, but still rightly dividing the word of Truth."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said unto Abram, Know of a surety that thy seed shall be a stranger in a land that is not theirs, and shall serve them; and they shall afflict them four hundred years; And also that nation, whom they shall serve, will I judge: and afterward shall they come out with great substance.","ref":"Genesis 15:13-14"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said, Certainly I will be with thee; and this shall be a token unto thee, that I have sent thee: When thou hast brought forth the people out of Egypt, ye shall serve God upon this mountain.","ref":"Exodus 3:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"God clarified to Abraham that after the 400 years, his descendants would come to Canaan and worship “God in this place.” Stephen, once again, linked their heritage back to the present tense. 8 And he gave him the covenant of circumcision: and so Abraham begat Isaac, and circumcised him the eighth day; and Isaac begat Jacob; and Jacob begat the twelve patriarchs."},{"kind":"para","text":"The lineage continued, and Stephen points out that God was keeping His promise to Abraham because of the covenant He had made with him through circumcision (which is something I don’t think I have to explain). Then Stephen switches gears a little bit, allowing the chance to bring the message closer and closer to home."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":16,"title":"Linking to Joseph","entries":[{"start":9,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This theme of rejecting God’s chosen leaders of Israel will be hammered on often throughout Stephen’s speech. In this section of his speech, he points to the rejection of Joseph, which is proof that these people continuously reject every single person that God has appointed over them, which ultimately will lead to their rejection of Jesus Christ Himself, which is a rejection of God specifically!"}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Interesting fact! The Greek translation of the Old Testament account of Jacob’s kindred included 75, whereas the Hebrew account suggests only 70. Many Hebrews didn’t want to include Jacob and his wife with their descendants born to Ephraim and Manasseh in Egypt."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And all the souls that came out of the loins of Jacob were seventy souls: for Joseph was in Egypt already.","ref":"Exodus 1:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"This isn’t necessarily a translational error, as it is more of a topical debate and discussion. Either way, the people listening to Stephen’s speech here won’t really bat an eye at this minute issue, seeing as they were being convicted of rejecting God’s anointed. 15 So Jacob went down into Egypt, and died, he, and our fathers, 16 And were carried over into Sychem, and laid in the sepulchre that Abraham bought for a sum of money of the sons of Emmor the father of Sychem."},{"kind":"para","text":"Stephen continues to bring them back and forth to this time, and uses the exact story they already know. He does this to prove that he isn’t just some ignorant or dumb man that had an idea about who Jesus was, with no proof to back himself up. This could certainly be Stephen’s own testimony, and he’s relating it to these spiritual leaders. Stephen could have been one that rejected all the spiritual leaders that God had put throughout the oracles of time and then proved through His Word."},{"kind":"para","text":"Whatever the case was, these religious leaders had no grounded case to do anything to Stephen based solely off of religious logic, or their own knowledge of the scripture. They could not deny anything Stephen had said. In verse 16, it speaks about Abraham buying a tomb from the sons of Hamor in Shechem. But that’s not what happens."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he bought a parcel of a field, where he had spread his tent, at the hand of the children of Hamor, Shechem's father, for an hundred pieces of money.","ref":"Genesis 33:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is, of course, where Joseph was buried."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the bones of Joseph, which the children of Israel brought up out of Egypt, buried they in Shechem, in a parcel of ground which Jacob bought of the sons of Hamor the father of Shechem for an hundred pieces of silver: and it became the inheritance of the children of Joseph.","ref":"Joshua 24:32"},{"kind":"para","text":"My assumption is that Stephen (with as much as he had to try and cram into this short time, he had to plead his case) may have just labelled Abraham to just combine Abraham’s family into one simple category, and he included Jacob. Or it’s possible that Abraham had earlier bought the same piece of land when he built an altar there…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Abram passed through the land unto the place of Sichem, unto the plain of Moreh. And the Canaanite was then in the land. And the LORD appeared unto Abram, and said, Unto thy seed will I give this land: and there builded he an altar unto the LORD, who appeared unto him.","ref":"Genesis 12:6-7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Then Jacob later purchased it. Once again, though, semantics. Either way, Stephen was doing a good job at keeping their attention. So far, it’s not mentioned that he was interrupted or condemned for anything that he has said."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":43,"title":"Linking to Moses","entries":[{"start":17,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Pharaoh was a terrible leader because Joseph had been forgotten. The reason rulership and leadership fall apart today is because of the people forgetting who God is and putting Him under our authority. History will continue to repeat itself because we continue to repeat ourselves. This is why Christians put up a fight today over biblical issues that plague our nation. We don’t want to continue to repeat the same stupid mistakes that we have made continuously since the beginning of time."}]},{"start":20,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"From this verse on, we see Stephen break the story of Moses up into three different parts. Stephen spends a great deal of time speaking about Moses because it will hit them the hardest, and, prayerfully (at least for Stephen anyway), it would open their eyes to see the necessity placed on looking to Jesus! Each of the three sections is broken up into 40 years of Moses. From birth to Pharaoh’s court will be 40 years, that’s covered from verses 17-22. Then the flight from Egypt and staying in Midian is 40 years, covered in verses 23-29. Then the divine call at Sinai and leading Israel into the wilderness is 40 years and covered in verses 30-43. 23 And when he was full forty years old, it came into his heart to visit his brethren the children of Israel. 24 And seeing one of them suffer wrong, he defended him, and avenged him that was oppressed, and smote the Egyptian: 25 For he supposed his brethren would have understood how that God by his hand would deliver them: but they understood not. 26 And the next day he shewed himself unto them as they strove, and would have set them at one again, saying, Sirs, ye are brethren; why do ye wrong one to another? 27 But he that did his neighbour wrong thrust him away, saying, Who made thee a ruler and a judge over us? 28 Wilt thou kill me, as thou diddest the Egyptian yesterday? 29 Then fled Moses at this saying, and was a stranger in the land of Madian, where he begat two sons."},{"kind":"para","text":"This particular scene, which is masterfully dealt with by Stephen here, illustrates further that Israel continuously rejects God-sent leaders! Moses was trying to reconcile the fighting Israelites…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass in those days, when Moses was grown, that he went out unto his brethren, and looked on their burdens: and he spied an Egyptian smiting an Hebrew, one of his brethren. And he looked this way and that way, and when he saw that there was no man, he slew the Egyptian, and hid him in the sand. And when he went out the second day, behold, two men of the Hebrews strove together: and he said to him that did the wrong, Wherefore smitest thou thy fellow? And he said, Who made thee a prince and a judge over us? intendest thou to kill me, as thou killedst the Egyptian? And Moses feared, and said, Surely this thing is known. Now when Pharaoh heard this thing, he sought to slay Moses. But Moses fled from the face of Pharaoh, and dwelt in the land of Midian: and he sat down by a well.","ref":"Exodus 2:11-15"},{"kind":"para","text":"But of course he was rejected…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"This Moses whom they refused, saying, Who made thee a ruler and a judge? the same did God send to be a ruler and a deliverer by the hand of the angel which appeared to him in the bush.","ref":"Acts 7:35 30 And when forty years were expired, there appeared to him in the wilderness of mount Sina an angel of the Lord in a flame of fire in a bush. 31 When Moses saw it, he wondered at the sight: and as he drew near to behold it, the voice of the Lord came unto him, 32 Saying, I am the God of thy fathers, the God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob. Then Moses trembled, and durst not behold. 33 Then said the Lord to him, Put off thy shoes from thy feet: for the place where thou standest is holy ground. 34 I have seen, I have seen the affliction of my people which is in Egypt, and I have heard their groaning, and am come down to deliver them. And now come, I will send thee into Egypt."},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, the only person that would know about this experience would be Moses. People would have to take his word for it. The only reason that people DID take his word for it is because God proved him, and God proved Himself. By the very fact that these religious leaders were listening to the account while Stephen was giving it, and the fact that Stephen believed in this account as he was giving it, further proves that Moses was an obvious given leader from God."},{"kind":"para","text":"35 This Moses whom they refused, saying, Who made thee a ruler and a judge? the same did God send to be a ruler and a deliverer by the hand of the angel which appeared to him in the bush. 36 He brought them out, after that he had shewed wonders and signs in the land of Egypt, and in the Red sea, and in the wilderness forty years."},{"kind":"para","text":"The proof of Moses, and all those that God puts into leadership continuously throughout history, displays the proof of Jesus Christ Himself. All these leaders from before were accounts given to proclaim the promise of Jesus, and when Jesus came, He proved every single promise that God made. Moses and Christ were redeemers!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But we trusted that it had been he which should have redeemed Israel: and beside all this, to day is the third day since these things were done.","ref":"Luke 24:21"},{"kind":"para","text":"Both performed wonders and signs!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ye men of Israel, hear these words; Jesus of Nazareth, a man approved of God among you by miracles and wonders and signs, which God did by him in the midst of you, as ye yourselves also know:","ref":"Acts 2:22"},{"kind":"para","text":"Moses was a very important figure to these Jews, and he was better studied than anything else in their religion. It’s impossible not to link God’s Word to Jesus Christ. Christ is the prophet whom Moses even predicted as verse 37 states!"},{"kind":"para","text":"37 This is that Moses, which said unto the children of Israel, A prophet shall the Lord your God raise up unto you of your brethren, like unto me; him shall ye hear."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The LORD thy God will raise up unto thee a Prophet from the midst of thee, of thy brethren, like unto me; unto him ye shall hearken;","ref":"Deuteronomy 18:15"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For Moses truly said unto the fathers, A prophet shall the Lord your God raise up unto you of your brethren, like unto me; him shall ye hear in all things whatsoever he shall say unto you.","ref":"Acts 3:22 38 This is he, that was in the church in the wilderness with the angel which spake to him in the mount Sina, and with our fathers: who received the lively oracles to give unto us:"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Jews believed that the law was mediated by angels, which is why Stephen speaks of this here. It’s repeated multiple times over throughout the New Testament."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore then serveth the law? It was added because of transgressions, till the seed should come to whom the promise was made; and it was ordained by angels in the hand of a mediator.","ref":"Galatians 3:19"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For if the word spoken by angels was stedfast, and every transgression and disobedience received a just recompence of reward;","ref":"Hebrews 2:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"39 To whom our fathers would not obey, but thrust him from them, and in their hearts turned back again into Egypt, 40 Saying unto Aaron, Make us gods to go before us: for as for this Moses, which brought us out of the land of Egypt, we wot not what is become of him. 41 And they made a calf in those days, and offered sacrifice unto the idol, and rejoiced in the works of their own hands."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, Stephen brings it back home and has continuously positioned himself in the same boat as them. He says, “our fathers” refused, putting the blame on himself because he knows it’s easier for the listeners to swallow if they don’t feel like they are just being attacked. Once again, the golden calf that is represented here is another illustration of Israel rejecting Moses’ leadership."},{"kind":"para","text":"The main issue with religionists is not that they have a false sense of worship. The main issue with religionists is that they worship falsely. They are given to idols! They break the first two commandments by not putting God first and not worshipping God alone. They think they are worshipping the God of the Bible, but they are worshipping their own idea of who God is. They make a god that will cozy up to their sins and idolatry, one that will tell them everything is okay and they can keep their traditions and vain attempts of faith because their god likes that. That’s not a holy and just God; that’s a cheap god. 42 Then God turned, and gave them up to worship the host of heaven; as it is written in the book of the prophets, O ye house of Israel, have ye offered to me slain beasts and sacrifices by the space of forty years in the wilderness? 43 Yea, ye took up the tabernacle of Moloch, and the star of your god Remphan, figures which ye made to worship them: and I will carry you away beyond Babylon."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is taken from Amos and illustrates their issue of idolatry."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Have ye offered unto me sacrifices and offerings in the wilderness forty years, O house of Israel? But ye have borne the tabernacle of your Moloch and Chiun your images, the star of your god, which ye made to yourselves. Therefore will I cause you to go into captivity beyond Damascus, saith the LORD, whose name is The God of hosts.","ref":"Amos 5:25-27"},{"kind":"para","text":"Moloch was the Canaanite sun god. I once conducted an extensive study of this false god, unaware of what I was getting myself into. Moloch was not a very forgiving god. In fact, it was used as a furnace for babies. People would offer their own children to this god in hopes of receiving wealth, healing, or honor. It is said that even Solomon gave his own child to this false god, and even today, people worship this god in hopes of obtaining something from him. It’s nothing more than a satanic symbol. People today who offer up their babies through abortion are worshipping this god for the same reasons (whether they know they are worshipping it or not is a story for a different day). They sacrifice their children through abortion so they can retain peace in this life because they believe that children come with too much baggage. I suppose children do, but they are also a gift from the Lord and worth the investment. Not to mention, murder is despicable and disgusting."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t know anything about Remphan. It’s assumed to be Repa, which is the Egyptian name for Saturn. Either way, the point that Stephen is making here is demonstrating their continued lineage of hardcore idolatry. It hasn’t left their lineage for thousands of years, and it’s not surprising at this point that they are still living in it."}]}]},{"start":44,"end":53,"title":"Linking Back to Them","entries":[{"start":44,"end":48,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is when Stephen ultimately pleads his case and focuses on the charges against him. He connects the tabernacle (or tent) with the temple. The temple wasn’t necessary for God’s purposes, because even in the wilderness God was worshipped in a tent. In distinction from the temple, it was movable, and yet it contained the “witness.” What Stephen was trying to portray to those that made a huge case against him was to not make a big deal about the temple more than the God over it."}]},{"start":49,"end":50,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Stephen quotes from the prophet Isaiah. Another prophet who these people would have known very well."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Thus saith the LORD, The heaven is my throne, and the earth is my footstool: where is the house that ye build unto me? and where is the place of my rest? For all those things hath mine hand made, and all those things have been, saith the LORD: but to this man will I look, even to him that is poor and of a contrite spirit, and trembleth at my word.","ref":"Isaiah 66:1-2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, the tabernacle and the temple were meant to point to something greater, which was to come eventually, and that is what Stephen wanted to talk about. He wanted to talk about the one greater than their religious practices and Torah knowledge."}]},{"start":51,"end":53,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Could this have been a little more graceful? Probably. I don’t know, but Stephen isn’t wrong. He proves throughout history that nothing has changed. They were a “stiff-necked” people during the time of Moses, and they are still a stiff-necked people during this speech! They treat God and His people the same way their fathers did throughout history. It’s no longer “our fathers” here, it’s now “YOUR fathers.” Stephen was not about to group himself with a bunch of people who continuously reject God. Stephen couldn’t, because he already accepted Christ. He approaches this end of his speech with power and conviction, but as we all know, convicted people don’t like to feel their conviction."}]}]},{"start":54,"end":60,"title":"Stephen Stoned to Death","entries":[{"start":54,"end":54,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The reason they were enraged was because they couldn’t help but accept that everything Stephen said was exactly and precisely right. They couldn’t argue against it. 55 But he, being full of the Holy Ghost, looked up stedfastly into heaven, and saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing on the right hand of God, 56 And said, Behold, I see the heavens opened, and the Son of man standing on the right hand of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"This slight indication of Stephen’s end was also a small push and reminder to those listening that Jesus is the focal point of this whole conversation. The “Son of Man” being at God’s right hand would have been an accusation to Jesus being involved with the Lord’s throne as a necessity. The Jews wouldn’t believe that, and this is what will ultimately lead these religionists into a spiral of constant hatred for Stephen and his message. They say the truth hurts. Obviously, it does on both sides of the field. 57 Then they cried out with a loud voice, and stopped their ears, and ran upon him with one accord,"},{"kind":"para","text":"Come on… This is childish. The way they were acting is not very common for how adults are supposed to act. Yet it’s 100% believable. I don’t know if you’ve watched the news lately with the protestors acting this exact same way. They were doing this when Roe V. Wade was overturned quite recently. Honestly, these people who are for killing babies prove there is something mentally wrong with them already, but they further prove it by acting like psychopaths who are unworthy to even complete a coherent conversation, let alone have the right mind on the matters of life and death. Our society has not changed in the least bit, even if we are a country founded upon biblical principles. People are born here, in luxury, and without worry, and then have the audacity to say that their place of birth is tyrannical and evil by throwing a fit and crying on the doorsteps of those who approve the sanctity of life. Honestly, the fact that they act as such further proves that they don’t have any truth in them. They wouldn’t know the truth if it slapped them in the face, which is spiritually what is happening here with Stephen."},{"kind":"para","text":"58 And cast him out of the city, and stoned him: and the witnesses laid down their clothes at a young man's feet, whose name was Saul."},{"kind":"para","text":"This verse is key. This is a pivotal point for Saul of Tarsus. The laying of garments at his feet indicates the approval of the Romans. The Sanhedrin were not allowed to stone people without permission from the Romans. But the things in which Saul heard will change his life drastically, as we will see in the coming chapters. 59 And they stoned Stephen, calling upon God, and saying, Lord Jesus, receive my spirit. 60 And he kneeled down, and cried with a loud voice, Lord, lay not this sin to their charge. And when he had said this, he fell asleep."},{"kind":"para","text":"He fell asleep, because death does not belong to the born-again. His physical death was but a hiccup to an eternity of life. His body saw death, but his spirit doesn’t know it. His final words were to plead with those that were killing him. Not to plead for them to stop, but to plead for God to forgive them. Just as Jesus pleaded with the Father during His own death…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then said Jesus, Father, forgive them; for they know not what they do. And they parted his raiment, and cast lots.","ref":"Luke 23:34"},{"kind":"para","text":"Many others would have this same cry as they were martyred for their faith."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":9,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":17,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":22},{"start":44,"end":48},{"start":49,"end":50},{"start":51,"end":53},{"start":54,"end":54}],"verses":{"9":"And the patriarchs, moved with envy, sold Joseph into Egypt: but God was with him,","10":"And delivered him out of all his afflictions, and gave him favour and wisdom in the sight of Pharaoh king of Egypt; and he made him governor over Egypt and all his house.","11":"Now there came a dearth over all the land of Egypt and Chanaan, and great affliction: and our fathers found no sustenance.","12":"But when Jacob heard that there was corn in Egypt, he sent out our fathers first.","13":"And at the second time Joseph was made known to his brethren; and Joseph's kindred was made known unto Pharaoh.","14":"Then sent Joseph, and called his father Jacob to him, and all his kindred, threescore and fifteen souls.","17":"But when the time of the promise drew nigh, which God had sworn to Abraham, the people grew and multiplied in Egypt,","18":"Till another king arose, which knew not Joseph.","19":"The same dealt subtilly with our kindred, and evil entreated our fathers, so that they cast out their young children, to the end they might not live.","20":"In which time Moses was born, and was exceeding fair, and nourished up in his father's house three months:","21":"And when he was cast out, Pharaoh's daughter took him up, and nourished him for her own son.","22":"And Moses was learned in all the wisdom of the Egyptians, and was mighty in words and in deeds.","44":"Our fathers had the tabernacle of witness in the wilderness, as he had appointed, speaking unto Moses, that he should make it according to the fashion that he had seen.","45":"Which also our fathers that came after brought in with Jesus into the possession of the Gentiles, whom God drave out before the face of our fathers, unto the days of David;","46":"Who found favour before God, and desired to find a tabernacle for the God of Jacob.","47":"But Solomon built him an house.","48":"Howbeit the most High dwelleth not in temples made with hands; as saith the prophet,","49":"Heaven is my throne, and earth is my footstool: what house will ye build me? saith the Lord: or what is the place of my rest?","50":"Hath not my hand made all these things?","51":"Ye stiffnecked and uncircumcised in heart and ears, ye do always resist the Holy Ghost: as your fathers did, so do ye.","52":"Which of the prophets have not your fathers persecuted? and they have slain them which shewed before of the coming of the Just One; of whom ye have been now the betrayers and murderers:","53":"Who have received the law by the disposition of angels, and have not kept it.","54":"When they heard these things, they were cut to the heart, and they gnashed on him with their teeth."}},
    "8": {"chapter":8,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 08"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: After being sent out to take care of some of the neglected needs of the church, Stephen (a man full of the Spirit and full of wisdom) is accused of desiring a revolt which leads him to stand before the Jewish counsel. This gives Stephen a platform to point out the hypocrisy and rejection of the leaders that the Jewish people kept falling under. They rejected the leadership of Abraham, Joseph, Moses, and all the way through to Jesus. They haven’t changed. He refers to them as “stiff-necked” and “uncircumcised” which ultimately leads them to allow the mob of people there to have their way in killing Stephen. They laid their clothes at Saul’s feet (indicating they were requesting permission to kill this man) and Saul consented which is where we pick up at in this chapter."}],"previously":["After being sent out to take care of some of the neglected needs of the church, Stephen (a man full of the Spirit and full of wisdom) is accused of desiring a revolt, which leads him to stand before the Jewish council. This gives Stephen a platform to point out the hypocrisy and rejection of the leaders that the Jewish people kept falling under. They rejected the leadership of Abraham, Joseph, Moses, and all the way through to Jesus. They haven’t changed. He refers to them as “stiff-necked” and “uncircumcised,” which ultimately leads them to allow the mob of people there to have their way in killing Stephen. They laid their clothes at Saul’s feet (indicating they were requesting permission to kill this man), and Saul consented, which is where we pick up in this chapter."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Saul Attacks the Church","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Saul may have “consented” to or “approved” the execution, but we know that nothing in the Christian’s life happens without God’s ultimate authority on the matter. There’s always a purpose to these situations. We wonder why people die, or even why they are murdered even still today. Especially if they are people of faith. They are confused about how a holy, loving, and just God could allow such a thing to happen to His children."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of his saints.","ref":"Psalm 116:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"God doesn’t want this to happen. This is what ultimately stores up His wrath for the end times. It will only be worse during that time!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they cried with a loud voice, saying, How long, O Lord, holy and true, dost thou not judge and avenge our blood on them that dwell on the earth?","ref":"Revelation 6:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"We will soon see how an event like that (which was done to Stephen) will lead to God’s ultimate moving on the matter. 2 And devout men carried Stephen to his burial, and made great lamentation over him."},{"kind":"para","text":"There was certainly reason to cry over this death. What a cruel and unjust way to die. Even more so, it was without a cause, and that is what makes it all the more difficult to accept. 3 As for Saul, he made havock of the church, entering into every house, and haling men and women committed them to prison."},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul, approving these deaths and going out of his way to make sure these people of faith faced punishment, was just as guilty of killing as those that actually did the crime themselves. His hate for the church proves his envy of murder against the brothers of Christ."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Whosoever hateth his brother is a murderer: and ye know that no murderer hath eternal life abiding in him.","ref":"1 John 3:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul only focused on one thing, and I don’t believe it was because he just simply hated the church. He had zeal, which will be spoken on later. He was zealous for his own traditions and faith. The issue is, God is doing a work on Saul’s heart, and anytime God does a work on someone’s heart (especially when they are prideful and stuck in their traditions and religions), they always fight against it, and seeing as they can’t fight God physically, they fight the people of God."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":8,"title":"Philip Proclaims Christ","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Persecution should not stop a true child of God from continuing to do what God tells them to. Persecution is not a dead-end street. It’s but a ditch in the road that can be overcome if we simply trust the bridge for it. 5 Then Philip went down to the city of Samaria, and preached Christ unto them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Preaching Christ should never end. Philip understood the necessity to preach Christ more than ever because of this world’s sinful and sick nature. People didn’t need to continue to feed into their own lusts, traditions, and desires; they needed to have their desires changed. The only way that will happen is through a changed faith in Jesus Christ. We should take this same viewpoint as Philip today."},{"kind":"para","text":"We see our world and nation falling apart all around us, and we all know things will not get better; they will only get worse. Instead of crying and complaining about how difficult life is, we should be trying to convince those who make life difficult to come to Christ. This way, they can help change the world for the right things rather than personal opinion. Obviously, personal truth doesn’t help the problem (because the problem should be fixed already if that’s the case), but we’ve seen Christ change the course of thinking and human history over and over again. It only makes sense to look to Him and focus on His Will and way to have an appropriate change for cause. 6 And the people with one accord gave heed unto those things which Philip spake, hearing and seeing the miracles which he did. 7 For unclean spirits, crying with loud voice, came out of many that were possessed with them: and many taken with palsies, and that were lame, were healed. 8 And there was great joy in that city."},{"kind":"para","text":"May it be understood… We do not have this kind of power to heal the physically impaired. Those that had this kind of power were positioned by the apostles to hold this kind of power. The apostles had it simply because Jesus gave them this power."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when he had said this, he breathed on them, and saith unto them, Receive ye the Holy Ghost:","ref":"John 20:22"},{"kind":"para","text":"We are not apostles. We were not chosen by the apostles, but by Jesus Christ. We do all have gifts, however…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he gave some, apostles; and some, prophets; and some, evangelists; and some, pastors and teachers; For the perfecting of the saints, for the work of the ministry, for the edifying of the body of Christ:","ref":"Ephesians 4:11-12"},{"kind":"para","text":"These spiritual gifts were given to confirm the truth of his message as well."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":25,"title":"Simon the Magician","entries":[{"start":9,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It’s so funny, a lot of magicians today try to give off this same persona. They look to Harry Houdini as a perfect source of inspiration as one of the first and greatest magicians. If they opened their Bibles, they would have seen one that started well before Houdini and made people think he had some really amazing powers. But even his magic was no match for the truths of scripture… 12 But when they believed Philip preaching the things concerning the kingdom of God, and the name of Jesus Christ, they were baptized, both men and women. 13 Then Simon himself believed also: and when he was baptized, he continued with Philip, and wondered, beholding the miracles and signs which were done."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you have ever practiced magic before (slight of hand), you know very well that the most important task of learning “illusions” and “tricks” is to impress someone else who does magic. They even have a TV show about it today called “Penn and Teller Fool Us.” The premise of the show is that magicians from all around the world try and do a magic trick that world-renowned atheist Penn, or his silent partner Teller, cannot figure out. Little do they know, this idea was not something new. They just capitalized on something that had already been written about."},{"kind":"para","text":"The difference here, however, is that the acts and signs that were being performed by Philip were not magical or illusional. These were real things that were happening, unexplainably, and this magician was astounded by this, making it a point to follow Philip around to learn about this power. 14 Now when the apostles which were at Jerusalem heard that Samaria had received the word of God, they sent unto them Peter and John: 15 Who, when they were come down, prayed for them, that they might receive the Holy Ghost: 16 (For as yet he was fallen upon none of them: only they were baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus.)"},{"kind":"para","text":"John and Peter’s prayers meant a great deal because of the power in which they had been given from the Lord Himself. The Apostles at Jerusalem were still placed in great authority over the entire church, and so when they were sent to Philip’s mission, they confirmed the accuracy of Philip’s message. 17 Then laid they their hands on them, and they received the Holy Ghost."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord waited to demonstrate the full power of the Holy Spirit until some of the apostles themselves could be present. Now there was no doubt that the Samaritans would receive the Spirit the same way that the Jewish Christians did."},{"kind":"para","text":"18 And when Simon saw that through laying on of the apostles' hands the Holy Ghost was given, he offered them money, 19 Saying, Give me also this power, that on whomsoever I lay hands, he may receive the Holy Ghost."},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, Simon was missing the point here. He was acting as a Judas, except with no intention to kill or betray. He had assumed that the signs and wonders being performed were simply sleight of hand or some sort of illusion. Magicians today try to explain away all the ideas and concepts of what Jesus did, except that the things Jesus did back then were not just magic tricks (how could they be when they didn’t have the kinds of material necessary for some of these so-called “tricks?”). They cannot explain away the death, burial, and resurrection of Jesus either, which is why they drew the line there. They say things like, “Well, Jesus would have done this, this way, and would have used one of these things to help Him do this trick or that trick, except I don’t believe that Jesus was real because nobody can rise from the dead… That’s an impossible trick.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"It doesn’t seem to occur to them that every trick they perform today isn’t because they had some sort of new idea to try and create an illusion. The tricks they perform today are based on the real-life signs and wonders that Jesus and His Apostles performed. They have been trying to mimic Christ for years and then play it off as if they had come up with something new. They take credit away from the Lord, claim that it was just a trick, and then say that He never existed while simultaneously saying they believe He pulled it off in their own delusional concepts. There’s nothing more arrogant and confused than an atheist magician. 20 But Peter said unto him, Thy money perish with thee, because thou hast thought that the gift of God may be purchased with money. 21 Thou hast neither part nor lot in this matter: for thy heart is not right in the sight of God. 22 Repent therefore of this thy wickedness, and pray God, if perhaps the thought of thine heart may be forgiven thee. 23 For I perceive that thou art in the gall of bitterness, and in the bond of iniquity."},{"kind":"para","text":"Simon revealed his heart on the matter. I would pray that anyone that tries to just talk away the notion of Christ’s full power of God as a mere illusion would have their hearts revealed as well. The heart of the problem is a problem of the heart. 24 Then answered Simon, and said, Pray ye to the Lord for me, that none of these things which ye have spoken come upon me."},{"kind":"para","text":"Whether or not Simon repented and turned to Christ is not exactly clear. One could hope, though. 25 And they, when they had testified and preached the word of the Lord, returned to Jerusalem, and preached the gospel in many villages of the Samaritans."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is where they unlock a new territory. This displays perfectly what was promised in the first chapter of this book."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me both in Jerusalem, and in all Judaea, and in Samaria, and unto the uttermost part of the earth.","ref":"Acts 1:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"The church will not expand above what they thought they were capable of expanding to…"}]}]},{"start":26,"end":40,"title":"The Ethiopian Eunuch","entries":[{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A Eunuch is a royal official. Some were emasculated, but not all of them."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Neither let the son of the stranger, that hath joined himself to the LORD, speak, saying, The LORD hath utterly separated me from his people: neither let the eunuch say, Behold, I am a dry tree. For thus saith the LORD unto the eunuchs that keep my sabbaths, and choose the things that please me, and take hold of my covenant; Even unto them will I give in mine house and within my walls a place and a name better than of sons and of daughters: I will give them an everlasting name, that shall not be cut off.","ref":"Isaiah 56:3-5 26 And the angel of the Lord spake unto Philip, saying, Arise, and go toward the south unto the way that goeth down from Jerusalem unto Gaza, which is desert. 27 And he arose and went: and, behold, a man of Ethiopia, an eunuch of great authority under Candace queen of the Ethiopians, who had the charge of all her treasure, and had come to Jerusalem for to worship,"},{"kind":"para","text":"Just to give you an idea of where Gaza was, it was the last place before the desert on the road from Jerusalem into Egypt. There wasn’t much there. This Eunuch was traveling (probably back to Ethiopia) after coming to Jerusalem to worship. He more than likely heard the gospel being preached from the apostles or disciples, and decided to look into these things himself. 28 Was returning, and sitting in his chariot read Esaias the prophet."},{"kind":"para","text":"What a great book to lead people to Christ through."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed.","ref":"Isaiah 53:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"This Eunuch was searching the scriptures just like he had been commanded to."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Search the scriptures; for in them ye think ye have eternal life: and they are they which testify of me.","ref":"John 5:39 29 Then the Spirit said unto Philip, Go near, and join thyself to this chariot."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I don’t know about you, but if I were coming to a chariot out in the middle of nowhere and the Spirit of the Lord told me to go speak to that person, I would be extremely hesitant. This was not a usual occurrence, I’m sure. This Eunuch probably didn’t meet many people out in the middle of nowhere often. He was in charge of the queen’s treasures, and there’s no doubt he had been trained to protect his chariot, the queen’s treasures, and himself from anything and anyone at all costs. This could have been a dangerous venture for Philip, but he took the chance because he knew the Lord had led him there for a purpose. 30 And Philip ran thither to him, and heard him read the prophet Esaias, and said, Understandest thou what thou readest?"},{"kind":"para","text":"That’s the issue with many churches and religions these days. They don’t understand what they are reading. They think they have an idea, and they put it to practical use, but in all actuality, they have misinterpreted scripture and are using it for the opposite of what it was meant for. This is why it’s important not to just pick and choose whatever scripture your personal needs call for, but to read the whole chapter and context for that chapter so that there is accuracy in the way it should be used. The context of scripture always explains how to use the scripture. 31 And he said, How can I, except some man should guide me? And he desired Philip that he would come up and sit with him. 32 The place of the scripture which he read was this, He was led as a sheep to the slaughter; and like a lamb dumb before his shearer, so opened he not his mouth: 33 In his humiliation his judgment was taken away: and who shall declare his generation? for his life is taken from the earth."},{"kind":"para","text":"God ordained this scripture for this perfect purpose. Though I have no doubt that Philip would have been able to preach Christ to this Eunuch no matter where he was reading, this particular passage of scripture was literally the most useful for what Christ came to this Earth for. It was the perfect complementary verse to provide the message of salvation to this eunuch. 34 And the eunuch answered Philip, and said, I pray thee, of whom speaketh the prophet this? of himself, or of some other man? 35 Then Philip opened his mouth, and began at the same scripture, and preached unto him Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s not often that Christians get chances like this. They are so rare and uncommon that just the fact that Philip was able to share the gospel with someone who really desired to know the gospel is a miracle in itself to us, let alone the fact that it’s literally in the middle of nowhere… 36 And as they went on their way, they came unto a certain water: and the eunuch said, See, here is water; what doth hinder me to be baptized?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Baptized? They weren’t even talking about baptism, but this Eunuch was interested in it. He desired to put forth that first step of obedience to Christ, and the next verse gives credibility to his salvation! 37 And Philip said, If thou believest with all thine heart, thou mayest. And he answered and said, I believe that Jesus Christ is the Son of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"What a verse! This is a great verse to keep in your minds and hearts. Taken out of most translations, it is an insanely important and prominent verse to leave in. There’s so much rich information and ammunition for the Christian to use here. This verse gives credence to a lot of truths in scripture regarding Baptism, Salvation, and Preaching."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was required that this Eunuch admit his salvation first before he was baptized. This Eunuch announces his salvation with confidence and anticipation, which is exactly how you want to see someone with a need for baptism. Many in the church today get baptized without even thinking twice about it. They think they are doing a service to God for getting baptized, but ultimately all they are doing is laying in water. When we know someone is genuinely saved and has confessed their trust in the Lord, their baptism becomes so much more meaningful and prominent. We should never make little of baptism in faith. 38 And he commanded the chariot to stand still: and they went down both into the water, both Philip and the eunuch; and he baptized him. 39 And when they were come up out of the water, the Spirit of the Lord caught away Philip, that the eunuch saw him no more: and he went on his way rejoicing."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a funny scene to me. I imagine Philip laying this guy in the water and then just walking away before bringing him back up. The man now sitting there, confused about where Philip disappeared to, yet rejoicing in his new life given to him. The symbol of new life through his baptism being the last memory of Philip. Philip being taken away like this is an interesting comparison to 2 Kings."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, as they still went on, and talked, that, behold, there appeared a chariot of fire, and horses of fire, and parted them both asunder; and Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven.","ref":"2 Kings 2:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Whether or not Philip was taken like this or just walked away quickly, I could not tell you. It is assured, though, that it was the Spirit that led Philip to the next place that he was to go to."},{"kind":"para","text":"40 But Philip was found at Azotus: and passing through he preached in all the cities, till he came to Caesarea."},{"kind":"para","text":"Philip is now at the coast, and seems to stay there permanently."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the next day we that were of Paul's company departed, and came unto Caesarea: and we entered into the house of Philip the evangelist, which was one of the seven; and abode with him.","ref":"Acts 21:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Caesarea had a large Greek-speaking population. This was a difficult yet needful area to minister to. The Jews and Gentiles here had a very severe and difficult relationship with each other, which we will speak more of in later chapters."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":9,"end":11},{"start":26,"end":26}],"verses":{"1":"And Saul was consenting unto his death. And at that time there was a great persecution against the church which was at Jerusalem; and they were all scattered abroad throughout the regions of Judaea and Samaria, except the apostles.","4":"Therefore they that were scattered abroad went every where preaching the word.","9":"But there was a certain man, called Simon, which beforetime in the same city used sorcery, and bewitched the people of Samaria, giving out that himself was some great one:","10":"To whom they all gave heed, from the least to the greatest, saying, This man is the great power of God.","11":"And to him they had regard, because that of long time he had bewitched them with sorceries."}},
    "9": {"chapter":9,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 09"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: There’s an interweaving pattern that’s been happening in the last couple chapters. We talked about Stephen and Philip, and then Stephen is executed and Saul is spoken of, and then Saul desires to destroy the church and the people of it, and then it goes back to Peter & John, and then back to Philip, and now we are coming back to Saul, and this chapter is an extremely fascinating one."},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul’s conversion has always been interesting to me, and it’s been a great picture of God’s grace and desire for even the worst of people to be saved and come to know Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"So far, in Acts chapters 6-9 and extending until about chapter 12, the focus has been on the witness of the Jerusalem church to the Greek-speaking Jews (or “Hellenists” is what they were referred to). Saul was a Hellenist, and he will be the spearhead in the outran to the Gentiles."}],"previously":["There has been an interweaving pattern in the last couple of chapters. We discussed Stephen and Philip, followed by Stephen’s execution and the mention of Saul. Saul then desires to destroy the church and its people, and the narrative shifts back to Peter and John, then to Philip, and now we return to Saul. This chapter is particularly fascinating. Saul’s conversion has always intrigued me, serving as a powerful illustration of God’s grace and His desire to save even the most wicked individuals and bring them to know Him. So far, in Acts chapters 6-9 and extending until about chapter 12, the focus has been on the witness of the Jerusalem church to the Greek-speaking Jews, or “Hellenists” as they were referred to. Saul was a Hellenist, and he will become the spearhead in the outreach to the Gentiles."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":19,"title":"The Conversion of Saul","entries":[{"start":1,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"After the events in Stephen’s life, Saul received permission slips from religious leaders to go to churches that preached Christ and arrest them, bringing them before these same religious leaders for persecution and beatings. Perhaps even killings would occur if the opportunity arose. Saul was a zealous man for his traditions and country, and he desired that the talking of these “Jesus-freaks” be put to a stop because he felt spiritually attacked by it."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is no different today. People would rather have others killed because they are proclaiming their faith instead of just listening or disagreeing. Christ brings out the worst in unbelievers and the best in those who believe. 3 And as he journeyed, he came near Damascus: and suddenly there shined round about him a light from heaven:"},{"kind":"para","text":"I like the juxtaposition here from verse 2. Saul is given permission to persecute the people of “The Way” (a fancy name for Christians before they were labeled Christians), but Saul goes on his way, supposing it’s the correct way and that he believes and thinks what he knows to be right is, in fact, right. That’s how the Lord proves Himself to us when we are going on our own way and find that it leads down a path that will keep us blind to the truth. The light that Saul was experiencing was no ordinary light. It wasn’t just a solar flare or a gleam of light reflecting off of something nearby. This light surrounded Saul. He was on his way to Damascus from Jerusalem, which was about one hundred and thirty-five miles from the northeast side of Jerusalem. It would have taken Saul about six days to travel by foot, but his determinedness kept him motivated. He wanted to put an end to this Christianity nonsense."},{"kind":"para","text":"I do believe, if Jesus didn’t stop him, he more than likely would have put a rather large dent into Christianity today. I don’t think the church would be where it is today. In fact, our Bibles would never be the same without Romans, 1&2 Corinthians, Galatians, Ephesians, Philippians, 1&2 Thessalonians, 1&2 Timothy, Titus, Philemon, and (probably) Hebrews (depending on who wrote it, even if it wasn’t the Apostle Paul, I have no doubt Paul would have been an influence in whoever did write it, judging by the terms used in the letter). Such amazing truths we would miss out on. 4 And he fell to the earth, and heard a voice saying unto him, Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul falls to the ground, not because he was weak, but because he was confused. He couldn’t see anything anymore. He had never experienced anything like this. Maybe he thought he was dehydrated from walking so long. Maybe he believed he was dying because there was this massive light suddenly taking his vision. Then he hears a VOICE from nowhere calling out his name. When Jesus says, “Why are you persecuting ME?” it must make Saul realize that it has to be Jesus… 5 And he said, Who art thou, Lord? And the Lord said, I am Jesus whom thou persecutest: it is hard for thee to kick against the pricks."},{"kind":"para","text":"He knows exactly who it is. Who else is he persecuting at this time? Only those that follow Jesus were going to be arrested. That’s why he says, “Lord” here. Not to mention, I believe Saul actually, and physically sees Christ in the flesh here."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Am I not an apostle? am I not free? have I not seen Jesus Christ our Lord? are not ye my work in the Lord?","ref":"1 Corinthians 9:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul cannot mistake who he is looking at… 6 And he trembling and astonished said, Lord, what wilt thou have me to do? And the Lord said unto him, Arise, and go into the city, and it shall be told thee what thou must do."},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul is now commissioned by Jesus Himself. This is such an awesome picture to me. Jesus has the ability to stop a hateful man, dead in his tracks, and position him in any direction He desires. If only He did this with Jonah. But with Jonah, God didn’t have to force anything. Jonah realized that God was going to make it happen whether Jonah was used or not, but God desired to change Jonah and use him specifically for that occasion."},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul, on the other hand, would not have stopped, even if swallowed by a whale. He had to have direct revelation from the Lord, and this gave him the belief he needed to turn everything around on its head. 7 And the men which journeyed with him stood speechless, hearing a voice, but seeing no man."},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul proclaims in chapter 22 that they saw the light and heard the voice, but they didn’t understand the voice."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they that were with me saw indeed the light, and were afraid; but they heard not the voice of him that spake to me.","ref":"Acts 22:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"They couldn’t verify that Saul actually spoke to Christ, but they COULD verify that they saw something miraculous happen, and obviously the changed life of Saul gives proof enough to these fellow men. 8 And Saul arose from the earth; and when his eyes were opened, he saw no man: but they led him by the hand, and brought him into Damascus. 9 And he was three days without sight, and neither did eat nor drink."},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul couldn’t see even before he was blinded. He was spiritually blind to the necessities of Christ. God will use his physical blindness to show his spiritual blindness."},{"kind":"para","text":"John Newton penned the words, “…was blind, but now I see…” to one of the most amazing hymns in history. According to researchers, he wrote the song when he was later in years, and he became blind. His sight was not physical, but spiritual. He was physically blinded, but spiritually visioned. 10 And there was a certain disciple at Damascus, named Ananias; and to him said the Lord in a vision, Ananias. And he said, Behold, I am here, Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"I love this tie-in to the Old Testament children."},{"kind":"para","text":"Abraham “And it came to pass after these things, that God did tempt Abraham, and said unto him, Abraham: and he said, Behold, here I am…And the angel of the LORD called unto him out of heaven, and said, Abraham, Abraham: and he said, Here am I.” Genesis 22:1;11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Jacob “And God spake unto Israel in the visions of the night, and said, Jacob, Jacob. And he said, Here am I.” Genesis 46:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Moses “And when the LORD saw that he turned aside to see, God called unto him out of the midst of the bush, and said, Moses, Moses. And he said, Here am I.” Exodus 3:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"Samuel “That the LORD called Samuel: and he answered, Here am I. And he ran unto Eli, and said, Here am I; for thou calledst me. And he said, I called not; lie down again. And he went and lay down. And the LORD called yet again, Samuel. And Samuel arose and went to Eli, and said, Here am I; for thou didst call me. And he answered, I called not, my son; lie down again. Now Samuel did not yet know the LORD, neither was the word of the LORD yet revealed unto him. And the LORD called Samuel again the third time. And he arose and went to Eli, and said, Here am I; for thou didst call me. And Eli perceived that the LORD had called the child.” 1 Samuel 3:4-8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Also I heard the voice of the Lord, saying, Whom shall I send, and who will go for us? Then said I, Here am I; send me.","ref":"Isaiah 6:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"When the Lord gets your attention, it’s best to respond in readiness. Though Ananias was ready to respond, he was about to also be tested. 11 And the Lord said unto him, Arise, and go into the street which is called Straight, and enquire in the house of Judas for one called Saul, of Tarsus: for, behold, he prayeth,"},{"kind":"para","text":"The street called “Straight” is actually still around today. It’s one of the oldest continually occupied streets in the world. I love that it’s called “Straight,” and that is where Saul is seen praying after he had just witnessed the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And make straight paths for your feet, lest that which is lame be turned out of the way; but let it rather be healed.","ref":"Hebrews 12:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul was being straightened out. Traditionally, Saul was probably a praying man. When it came to festivities of his religious practices, prayer was probably a necessity so he could rightly partake. Now, Saul is a man of FERVENT prayer. He has seen the Lord, and now he is leaning on his prayer. For three days, he was sitting in darkness. For three days, all he had time to do was pray. No doubt, this was the same with Jonah the three days he spent in the belly of the fish. Three days in darkness and peril would help even an atheist pray. 12 And hath seen in a vision a man named Ananias coming in, and putting his hand on him, that he might receive his sight."},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul’s vision, given by the Lord, was that he would receive visions. Obviously, God could have just given Saul’s visions back to him without requiring anyone else to help out. But Ananias needed to be a witness of the things that were to take place here shortly after. Not to mention, no one would believe the testimony of Saul if there was no one to prove his testimony. Especially because of how sinister he was being to the church. 13 Then Ananias answered, Lord, I have heard by many of this man, how much evil he hath done to thy saints at Jerusalem: 14 And here he hath authority from the chief priests to bind all that call on thy name."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, I’m in the same boat as Ananias here. If I knew the type of person Saul was, I would be equally as confused as to why God was moving this way. I would think, “Are you sure about this man? What if he is just putting on a front like Judas so he can get into our congregation and just destroy the church from the inside?” But of course, I also understand that God does not make a mistake. I’d desire that even the worst person in the world would come to know Christ. 15 But the Lord said unto him, Go thy way: for he is a chosen vessel unto me, to bear my name before the Gentiles, and kings, and the children of Israel:"},{"kind":"para","text":"This proves God’s foreknowledge. Saul ends up, in this same book, preaching to Gentiles, kings (Agrippa II in chapter 26), and to the children of Israel. 16 For I will shew him how great things he must suffer for my name's sake."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even through Paul’s suffering, he will have joy! He loved his ministry, which he was able to continue over the next few years of his life. He enjoyed the churches he started and cared for them deeply, writing letters to them and visiting them as often as possible. He suffered a lot, but not because he was a terrible person. He certainly deserved the suffering for such, but God didn’t desire that life for him."},{"kind":"para","text":"He suffered for the sake of the Lord, and anyone who would proclaim Christ as their Savior should be prepared for the same circumstances of suffering. No doubt, suffering will come to each and every one of us, one way or another. Whether it be small or great is not up to us. The Lord allows suffering for His ultimate purpose. Our suffering is not for our own sake but for His. Unless we are suffering for sin… Then that is our own fault… 17 And Ananias went his way, and entered into the house; and putting his hands on him said, Brother Saul, the Lord, even Jesus, that appeared unto thee in the way as thou camest, hath sent me, that thou mightest receive thy sight, and be filled with the Holy Ghost."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Brother Saul” suggests that Ananias accepts God’s will on the matter. Saul was now family with Ananias, whether Ananias agreed with it or not. 18 And immediately there fell from his eyes as it had been scales: and he received sight forthwith, and arose, and was baptized."},{"kind":"para","text":"There’s that pattern again. After his sight (both physically and spiritually), Saul was baptized. He was not baptized to become saved. He was baptized BECAUSE he was saved. I’m under the impression that you cannot start ministry unless you take that “first step” of obedience in this ordinance of God. Baptism is not something that should just be brushed aside, but should be taken seriously if you want to join in on the Lord’s work. Look to John, the disciples, Jesus, the first 3,000 that were saved in the new church, the Eunuch, and now Saul (rather than the Apostle Paul). 19 And when he had received meat, he was strengthened. Then was Saul certain days with the disciples which were at Damascus."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe in these days that Saul was with the disciples. He learned how to share his faith and was solidified in his decision for Christ (as if he needed any more solidarity). The disciples that were there gave him assurance and a foundation for him to follow. This is why he was able to preach nearly instantly to those around him…"}]}]},{"start":20,"end":22,"title":"Saul Proclaims Jesus","entries":[{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A lot of accusations can be thrown at me for my history. In fact, some accusations may arise and be proclaimed, and you might hear this or that in regards to who I was, what I used to be like, and how I used to act before I was a Christian. Even before becoming a Christian, people will accuse me of all sorts of arrogant and hateful things, no doubt, and they have! If people tell you that I am a sinner and rotten, don’t believe them… I am worse than they know… 22 But Saul increased the more in strength, and confounded the Jews which dwelt at Damascus, proving that this is very Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"Accusations didn’t stop Saul from being bold and being strong in his faith. Actually, he used the accusations to further prove the grace and salvation of Jesus Christ. Use your past failures to show the change that Christ has put into your life."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":25,"title":"Saul Escapes","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The tables have turned. Once again, this proves that Jesus always brings out the worst in unbelievers. The Jews wanted the “persecuting” Saul, not the “preachy and Spirit-filled” Saul. The people that wanted him dead the most were the leaders, specifically the governor under King Aretas, as Saul proclaims in Corinthians."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In Damascus the governor under Aretas the king kept the city of the Damascenes with a garrison, desirous to apprehend me: And through a window in a basket was I let down by the wall, and escaped his hands.","ref":"2 Corinthians 11:32-33 24 But their laying await was known of Saul. And they watched the gates day and night to kill him. 25 Then the disciples took him by night, and let him down by the wall in a basket."},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, the disciples here knew the importance that God placed on Saul’s life and desired to do whatever it took to rescue Saul so that he could grow and continue to preach. They probably told him to head back to Jerusalem and find the church there to get the best training he could."}]}]},{"start":26,"end":31,"title":"Saul In Jerusalem","entries":[{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Yet again, understandable… but still really sad… It should not matter the background of an individual that joins our ranks; we should desire that they would grow in faith just like the rest of us. 27 But Barnabas took him, and brought him to the apostles, and declared unto them how he had seen the Lord in the way, and that he had spoken to him, and how he had preached boldly at Damascus in the name of Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"According to Saul in his letter to Galatia he says,"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then after three years I went up to Jerusalem to see Peter, and abode with him fifteen days. But other of the apostles saw I none, save James the Lord's brother.","ref":"Galatians 1:18-19"},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul waited three years after his conversion before he met with Peter. He was with Peter for fifteen days, and didn’t even speak to the other apostles, except for meeting James (Jesus’ brother) briefly. Barnabas took special interest in Saul, and desired to see him be built up."},{"kind":"para","text":"Remember, “Barnabas” means “The son of consolation.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Joses, who by the apostles was surnamed Barnabas, (which is, being interpreted, The son of consolation,) a Levite, and of the country of Cyprus,","ref":"Acts 4:36"},{"kind":"para","text":"“Consolation” means “Comfort” or “encouragement.” Barnabas was an encourager. When no one else would even look twice at Saul, Barnabas looked for a way to encourage him. How we should desire to be a Barnabas… 28 And he was with them coming in and going out at Jerusalem. 29 And he spake boldly in the name of the Lord Jesus, and disputed against the Grecians: but they went about to slay him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Saul was preparing for his chance to preach and teach. He couldn’t wait for God to give him the opening. Anytime God calls His children to something, He will prepare His children, and then He will give an opening to do it. But there will always be those that hate your message, waiting for the opportunity to come to shut you down… 30 Which when the brethren knew, they brought him down to Caesarea, and sent him forth to Tarsus."},{"kind":"para","text":"This was a form of protection for Saul. This was where Saul grew up. It’s where he came from. Paul was still in Tarsus for the next eight years. It’s a place that was located southeast of Asia Minor on the way from Syria into Asia Minor. It’s assumed that his vision of heaven happened during this time while he was in Tarsus."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I knew a man in Christ above fourteen years ago, (whether in the body, I cannot tell; or whether out of the body, I cannot tell: God knoweth;) such an one caught up to the third heaven. And I knew such a man, (whether in the body, or out of the body, I cannot tell: God knoweth;) How that he was caught up into paradise, and heard unspeakable words, which it is not lawful for a man to utter.","ref":"2 Corinthians 12:2-4 31 Then had the churches rest throughout all Judaea and Galilee and Samaria, and were edified; and walking in the fear of the Lord, and in the comfort of the Holy Ghost, were multiplied."},{"kind":"para","text":"The church, up until now, had been added to each and every day. Once Saul comes into the picture, the church is no longer added to, but being MULTIPLIED. This indicates that it was growing RAPIDLY. People were being reached with the gospel like never before. There were many that were hearing this for the first time who were accepting it and turning to Christ for salvation."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":35,"title":"The Healing of Aeneas","entries":[{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"An important distinction of the term “saints” is referred to here. A lot of religions have warped the term “saints” to mean more than it’s meant to. Of course, the term “saint” used in the Bible does not mean a sinless person. Rather, it means, “A saved sinner.” “Lydda” was twenty-three miles northwest of Jerusalem on the road to Joppa. Lydda served as a regional administrative town for Judea and was on an important trade route. 33 And there he found a certain man named Aeneas, which had kept his bed eight years, and was sick of the palsy. 34 And Peter said unto him, Aeneas, Jesus Christ maketh thee whole: arise, and make thy bed. And he arose immediately."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, it is not Peter’s healing power, it’s Christ’s. Only Christ can do miracles. 35 And all that dwelt at Lydda and Saron saw him, and turned to the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"This was the ultimate purpose of this miraculous healing. That is the desire for all Christians everywhere, that people would turn to the Lord because of the miracles that only He can do!"}]}]},{"start":36,"end":43,"title":"Dorcas Restored to Life","entries":[{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A city eleven miles northwest of Lydda lived a lady who was full of good works and charity. This isn’t necessarily wrong, but it can be used in the wrong way and for the wrong reasons. If we are doing anything loving for a “feel-good” high, or a “look at me” mentality, we aren’t doing anything good. Honestly, I’d argue it’s a sin to take glory away from the Lord in that way. With that said, this lady obviously made a godly impact, but (perhaps) did not present the Lord to all the lives she touched… until now… 37 And it came to pass in those days, that she was sick, and died: whom when they had washed, they laid her in an upper chamber. 38 And forasmuch as Lydda was nigh to Joppa, and the disciples had heard that Peter was there, they sent unto him two men, desiring him that he would not delay to come to them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, if anything, Peter could mourn with the family and possibly could minister and console those that loved Tabitha. I don’t think they anticipated the events that were about to unfold… 39 Then Peter arose and went with them. When he was come, they brought him into the upper chamber: and all the widows stood by him weeping, and shewing the coats and garments which Dorcas made, while she was with them. 40 But Peter put them all forth, and kneeled down, and prayed; and turning him to the body said, Tabitha, arise. And she opened her eyes: and when she saw Peter, she sat up. 41 And he gave her his hand, and lifted her up, and when he had called the saints and widows, presented her alive."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter (of course) had no supernatural power of his own, but the Lord had shown him what he was going to do in response to Peter’s prayer. This was significant because Tabitha’s life would now be used to lead others to the saving knowledge of Christ, which would leave a far greater impact than her good works and charity. 42 And it was known throughout all Joppa; and many believed in the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, proving the necessity for the believers to focus on it. It wasn’t just about the miracle. It was about turning to Christ in faith and believing. That’s what the ultimate desire is laid upon. 43 And it came to pass, that he tarried many days in Joppa with one Simon a tanner."},{"kind":"para","text":"A tanner worked on animal hides (in case that was not well known). Obviously, there will be more to this story in the next chapter, and it gets even more interesting from here."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":20,"end":21},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":26,"end":26},{"start":32,"end":32},{"start":36,"end":36}],"verses":{"1":"And Saul, yet breathing out threatenings and slaughter against the disciples of the Lord, went unto the high priest,","2":"And desired of him letters to Damascus to the synagogues, that if he found any of this way, whether they were men or women, he might bring them bound unto Jerusalem.","20":"And straightway he preached Christ in the synagogues, that he is the Son of God.","21":"But all that heard him were amazed, and said; Is not this he that destroyed them which called on this name in Jerusalem, and came hither for that intent, that he might bring them bound unto the chief priests?","23":"And after that many days were fulfilled, the Jews took counsel to kill him:","26":"And when Saul was come to Jerusalem, he assayed to join himself to the disciples: but they were all afraid of him, and believed not that he was a disciple.","32":"And it came to pass, as Peter passed throughout all quarters, he came down also to the saints which dwelt at Lydda.","36":"Now there was at Joppa a certain disciple named Tabitha, which by interpretation is called Dorcas: this woman was full of good works and almsdeeds which she did."}},
    "10": {"chapter":10,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We saw Saul (later called the Apostle Paul) convert to the very thing he was trying to destroy. He starts preaching Christ right away, and people are confused about how and why he would be joining himself to the disciples rather than getting rid of them, and they try to kill HIM. He is able to escape and run to Jerusalem to join himself to the first church of the Apostles. He comes in contact with Peter after a few years of getting to know others (most of the people don’t believe he is a true disciple) and he is sent back to his home town to preach the gospel."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once this happens, Peter is seen going to neighboring cities to commence healings and even bring someone back got life for the sake of the gospel there. He then stays in a tanners house nearby, which is where we pick up in this chapter…"}],"previously":["We witnessed Saul, later known as the Apostle Paul, convert to the very cause he was trying to destroy. He immediately began preaching Christ, leaving people confused about how and why he would join the disciples rather than eliminate them. Consequently, they attempted to kill him. However, Saul managed to escape and fled to Jerusalem to join the first church of the Apostles. After a few years of getting to know others, most of whom did not believe he was a true disciple, he came into contact with Peter. Peter then sent Saul back to his hometown to preach the gospel. Once this occurred, Peter was seen traveling to neighboring cities to perform healings and even raise someone from the dead for the sake of the gospel. He then stayed in a tanner’s house nearby, which is where we pick up in this chapter."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"Peter & Cornelius","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A centurion was a commander of one hundred men, and a band usually consisted of about six hundred men under the command of six centurions. Centurions were paid a lot more money than ordinary soldiers and were given special permission for ruling over the soldiers. Cornelius would have been a socially prominent and wealthy figure in Caesarea. 2 A devout man, and one that feared God with all his house, which gave much alms to the people, and prayed to God alway."},{"kind":"para","text":"A “God-fearer” in those days were Gentiles who worshipped Israel’s God but didn’t submit to Jewish conversion rites. Cornelius followed two of the primary expressions of Jewish religion, which were prayer and almsgiving. Almsgiving was giving to the needy or to the religious men of that day. He more than likely gave because he saw it as a practice, rather than a heart pursuit, as many people do even still today. He may have thought that he would have a good standing with God if he simply gave his alms and prayed frequently."},{"kind":"para","text":"3 He saw in a vision evidently about the ninth hour of the day an angel of God coming in to him, and saying unto him, Cornelius. 4 And when he looked on him, he was afraid, and said, What is it, Lord? And he said unto him, Thy prayers and thine alms are come up for a memorial before God."},{"kind":"para","text":"The 9th hour was about 3 p.m. The Jews usually prayed at this hour every day. Jewish people still have specific times of prayer today. The prayers and alms that Cornelius was giving were out of a desire to be seen by God, rather than by man. His prayers and alms (though traditionally given) were still seen by God, and now Cornelius’s faith was about to be taken to the next level with full assurance of salvation. 5 And now send men to Joppa, and call for one Simon, whose surname is Peter: 6 He lodgeth with one Simon a tanner, whose house is by the sea side: he shall tell thee what thou oughtest to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"God knew exactly what was going to be done, but it would still take Peter some convincing to approach this. Gentiles hearing the gospel was unheard of at this point. For Peter to preach the gospel to a person that the Jews looked at as unclean and unworthy was going to require a breaking of Peter’s pride. But God knew exactly where Peter was, and exactly how it would have to be played out, and so he prepared Cornelius first to send people, so by the time these people reached Peter, Peter would be ready to go and witness to the Gentiles. 7 And when the angel which spake unto Cornelius was departed, he called two of his household servants, and a devout soldier of them that waited on him continually; 8 And when he had declared all these things unto them, he sent them to Joppa."},{"kind":"para","text":"He believed in the words of this angel and did exactly what he was supposed to do without hesitation. This is a key factor in the moving of the gospel into the rest of the world, because if Cornelius didn’t believe and do what he was told at this moment, the gospel would have more than likely ended with the Jews, or the church and witness of others would be drastically different. But because God can see all things, and He knows all men’s hearts, He knew exactly what would happen and how it would unfold, and so He prepared everything for this moment."},{"kind":"para","text":"From Caesarea to Joppa was a thirty-nine-mile trek. Cornelius seemed to be sure that he was not wasting his resources or men on this long journey. If he did, it would mean his job (and possibly even his life) because of his recklessness."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":33,"title":"Peter’s Vision","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Most houses in Judea had flat roofs, so it was a perfect place to pray. They usually used it as a living space as well. 10 And he became very hungry, and would have eaten: but while they made ready, he fell into a trance, 11 And saw heaven opened, and a certain vessel descending upon him, as it had been a great sheet knit at the four corners, and let down to the earth:"},{"kind":"para","text":"He fell into a trance, not unlike how John (being led by God) was multiple times moved by the Spirit, seeing visions of the tribulations. This vision was different, however, because it almost seemed like a dream. It was repeated multiple times as well. 12 Wherein were all manner of fourfooted beasts of the earth, and wild beasts, and creeping things, and fowls of the air."},{"kind":"para","text":"The reason this demonstrates the multiple types of animals is because Jewish laws and customs forbid Jews to eat unclean animals. This is heavily spoken on in Leviticus 11:2-47. 13 And there came a voice to him, Rise, Peter; kill, and eat. 14 But Peter said, Not so, Lord; for I have never eaten any thing that is common or unclean. 15 And the voice spake unto him again the second time, What God hath cleansed, that call not thou common. 16 This was done thrice: and the vessel was received up again into heaven."},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, as we know, Jesus fulfilled all of the law on the cross. Because of this, Peter could then understand and realize that God has the authority and power to provide the right things for His children, including overturning laws. This is literally what God is doing here in two ways. One, He is showing Peter that God can cleanse all things, and that Peter should NEVER deny the Lord’s direction in any matter, even if it’s in a matter of killing (see Abraham and his son for that analogy, and sign of faith)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And why call ye me, Lord, Lord, and do not the things which I say?","ref":"Luke 6:46"},{"kind":"para","text":"Someone once said, “We cannot say ‘no’ and ‘Lord’ in the same sentence.” This statement highlights the tension between human limitations and divine authority. Additionally, God is revealing the overturning of the old dietary laws in general, along with all other “ceremonial” laws in the Mosaic covenant. This includes laws about sacrifice, festivals and special days, and circumcision."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter was treating the Gentiles as unclean, even though Jesus Himself had told him to go into the entire world and preach the gospel to every creature. Furthermore, God gives the command to Israel to minister to other nations and teach them to follow God. However, we rarely see this happening in the Old Testament because they are so stuck on their traditions and perfectionism that they fail to obey God in this matter."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now therefore, if ye will obey my voice indeed, and keep my covenant, then ye shall be a peculiar treasure unto me above all people: for all the earth is mine: And ye shall be unto me a kingdom of priests, and an holy nation. These are the words which thou shalt speak unto the children of Israel.","ref":"Exodus 19:5-6"},{"kind":"para","text":"They had failed to understand the necessity of the law."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore the law was our schoolmaster to bring us unto Christ, that we might be justified by faith. But after that faith is come, we are no longer under a schoolmaster.","ref":"Galatians 3:24-25"},{"kind":"para","text":"We (like Peter) need to be careful as well when we think we are better, or “more clean” than other people, especially when we know the Lord and what He has provided for. 17 Now while Peter doubted in himself what this vision which he had seen should mean, behold, the men which were sent from Cornelius had made enquiry for Simon's house, and stood before the gate, 18 And called, and asked whether Simon, which was surnamed Peter, were lodged there."},{"kind":"para","text":"The vision wasn’t very clear to Peter yet, but it will soon click in his brain as soon as he’s brought before this Centurion. 19 While Peter thought on the vision, the Spirit said unto him, Behold, three men seek thee. 20 Arise therefore, and get thee down, and go with them, doubting nothing: for I have sent them."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is funny. The Spirit says, “Here’s a vision!” Then “Okay, now go talk to these men.” There was no clarity, no peace of mind, no revealing connection between the vision and the people Peter was about to talk to. The Spirit knew that Peter would just figure it out all on his own. The Spirit would help guide Peter into the connection."},{"kind":"para","text":"21 Then Peter went down to the men which were sent unto him from Cornelius; and said, Behold, I am he whom ye seek: what is the cause wherefore ye are come? 22 And they said, Cornelius the centurion, a just man, and one that feareth God, and of good report among all the nation of the Jews, was warned from God by an holy angel to send for thee into his house, and to hear words of thee. 23 Then called he them in, and lodged them. And on the morrow Peter went away with them, and certain brethren from Joppa accompanied him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Apparently, somewhere in their visit, the connection was made to Peter. Maybe after he slept on it, he realized what God was meaning. It doesn’t really tell us how he came to finalize the vision in which God gives him, but (nevertheless) the vision was important for multiple distinctions, and Peter has a change of heart about the necessities that are placed on him through the vision that God gives him. It changes Peter’s whole line of thinking. 24 And the morrow after they entered into Caesarea. And Cornelius waited for them, and he had called together his kinsmen and near friends. 25 And as Peter was coming in, Cornelius met him, and fell down at his feet, and worshipped him."},{"kind":"para","text":"This was obviously really important to Cornelius, and he was amazed and thrilled that God was sending this Apostle to meet with him of all people. Most Centurions would probably feel entitled and think they deserved to be met anyway because of their standing and authority. This Jewish man would have been no different than anyone else to the Centurions. Most Gentiles probably wouldn’t have cared at all about a Jewish man ever talking to them because of how poorly the Jews treated the Gentiles."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, Cornelius was excited and had every right to be. He didn’t, however, have any right to fall at Peter’s feet and worship Peter. This is how a great deal of religions come to Peter even still today. But Peter puts a stop to that real quick… 26 But Peter took him up, saying, Stand up; I myself also am a man."},{"kind":"para","text":"Read this again… Peter didn’t just tell him to get up and stop acting like a fool to Peter; it says Peter literally and physically “took him up.” In other words, as soon as Cornelius comes to worship Peter, Peter grabs ahold of him and pulls him off the ground, saying, “Stop it, don’t worship me, I’m a human being like you’re a human being!”"},{"kind":"para","text":"I love this because Peter has already learned to stop separating himself from the rest of the world simply because he has been called out by God. He has learned (for the most part) to be in the world, but not OF the world. This gives us a direct command to not worship the disciples, straight from the disciples’ mouths. Don’t pray to them, to ask them anything, don’t even call them anything more than a man."},{"kind":"para","text":"The angels do the same thing in the book of Revelation when John tries to worship the angels… TWICE…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I fell at his feet to worship him. And he said unto me, See thou do it not: I am thy fellowservant, and of thy brethren that have the testimony of Jesus: worship God: for the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy.","ref":"Revelation 19:10"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then saith he unto me, See thou do it not: for I am thy fellowservant, and of thy brethren the prophets, and of them which keep the sayings of this book: worship God.","ref":"Revelation 22:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"The angels themselves tell us not to worship them and make themselves as equals to the saints of God. Only God is worthy to be worshipped, and Cornelius was learning this, but also giving those of future generations an example of what not to do with the apostles and saints of the Lord. Peter could not have foreseen that for thousands of years, people of false religious practices would be worshipping him in their churches and making prayers that only he could answer. Luke probably didn’t foresee this either. This was obviously inspired by the Holy Spirit, giving false religious practices no excuse for their false practices! 27 And as he talked with him, he went in, and found many that were come together. 28 And he said unto them, Ye know how that it is an unlawful thing for a man that is a Jew to keep company, or come unto one of another nation; but God hath shewed me that I should not call any man common or unclean."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter doesn’t say, “but God has shown me not to call any Gentile common or unclean.” He says, “ANY MAN.” Every person, whether sick, poor, ethnically different, culturally different, religiously different, mentally different, homosexual, transgender, murderers, rapists, even completely wrong in every area of thinking, is to be treated equally as God would treat everyone."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For there is no respect of persons with God.","ref":"Romans 2:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"If we cannot love the worst kinds of people, we cannot experience the best kind of love from God. Because when we were at our worst, God still loved us."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"This is a faithful saying, and worthy of all acceptation, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief.","ref":"1 Timothy 1:15"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.","ref":"Romans 5:8 29 Therefore came I unto you without gainsaying, as soon as I was sent for: I ask therefore for what intent ye have sent for me? 30 And Cornelius said, Four days ago I was fasting until this hour; and at the ninth hour I prayed in my house, and, behold, a man stood before me in bright clothing, 31 And said, Cornelius, thy prayer is heard, and thine alms are had in remembrance in the sight of God. 32 Send therefore to Joppa, and call hither Simon, whose surname is Peter; he is lodged in the house of one Simon a tanner by the sea side: who, when he cometh, shall speak unto thee. 33 Immediately therefore I sent to thee; and thou hast well done that thou art come. Now therefore are we all here present before God, to hear all things that are commanded thee of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"A quick recap of all the things we literally just read about happening. There’s no mistake as to why Peter had arrived at this particular place, and in front of these particular people. Peter wasn’t told to preach the gospel to the Gentiles in his vision; he was just told not to call the things in which God cleansed unclean or common. But it was evident that Peter had only one commission to this world, and that was to bring the gospel to every creature. That’s what the Gentiles were looking for, but they didn’t know they needed. That’s what was about to be shared with them through Peter."}]}]},{"start":34,"end":43,"title":"Gentiles Hear the Good News","entries":[{"start":34,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A lot of people say, “I can’t witness to others because I don’t know what to say.” Look at this straightforward gospel presentation here. It was simple. He explained Jesus’ sacrifice, what it means, and that He rose the third day defeating death and giving forgiveness to the sins of anyone that would trust in Him alone for salvation. It’s as simple as that. We don’t need to overcomplicate the gospel message, or try and sway people to one side or the other. Just give the facts and let the Spirit work in the listeners’ hearts."}]}]},{"start":44,"end":48,"title":"The Spirit Fall on Gentiles","entries":[{"start":44,"end":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Peter didn’t even get to finish sharing the gospel before God’s Spirit started working on them. The field was already prepared to be harvested, and all Peter had to do was bring them in."}]},{"start":45,"end":48,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Baptism was given to them after they received the Holy Spirit. It was a symbol of their faith, and it proved to the Jews as well that the same faith was given to each of them. You can learn a lot about faith and practices through a church’s call for baptism. It’s either because they have received God’s Word with readiness and wanted to live a life for Christ, or it’s because they believe they will be saved through it. Not one time did anyone get saved AFTER baptism in the Bible. They always, and only, were baptized BECAUSE they were saved."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":34,"end":43},{"start":44,"end":44},{"start":45,"end":48}],"verses":{"1":"There was a certain man in Caesarea called Cornelius, a centurion of the band called the Italian band,","9":"On the morrow, as they went on their journey, and drew nigh unto the city, Peter went up upon the housetop to pray about the sixth hour:","34":"Then Peter opened his mouth, and said, Of a truth I perceive that God is no respecter of persons:","35":"But in every nation he that feareth him, and worketh righteousness, is accepted with him.","36":"The word which God sent unto the children of Israel, preaching peace by Jesus Christ: (he is Lord of all:)","37":"That word, I say, ye know, which was published throughout all Judaea, and began from Galilee, after the baptism which John preached;","38":"How God anointed Jesus of Nazareth with the Holy Ghost and with power: who went about doing good, and healing all that were oppressed of the devil; for God was with him.","39":"And we are witnesses of all things which he did both in the land of the Jews, and in Jerusalem; whom they slew and hanged on a tree:","40":"Him God raised up the third day, and shewed him openly;","41":"Not to all the people, but unto witnesses chosen before God, even to us, who did eat and drink with him after he rose from the dead.","42":"And he commanded us to preach unto the people, and to testify that it is he which was ordained of God to be the Judge of quick and dead.","43":"To him give all the prophets witness, that through his name whosoever believeth in him shall receive remission of sins.","44":"While Peter yet spake these words, the Holy Ghost fell on all them which heard the word.","45":"And they of the circumcision which believed were astonished, as many as came with Peter, because that on the Gentiles also was poured out the gift of the Holy Ghost.","46":"For they heard them speak with tongues, and magnify God. Then answered Peter,","47":"Can any man forbid water, that these should not be baptized, which have received the Holy Ghost as well as we?","48":"And he commanded them to be baptized in the name of the Lord. Then prayed they him to tarry certain days."}},
    "11": {"chapter":11,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Peter see’s a vision which allows him to take the gospel to Gentiles. They receive it with gladness, and the Holy Spirit is shown evident in their lives and worship. Now Peter will take this message back to the Mother Church in Jerusalem…"}],"previously":["Peter sees a vision which allows him to take the gospel to Gentiles. They receive it with gladness, and the Holy Spirit is shown evident in their lives and worship. Now, Peter will take this message back to the Mother Church in Jerusalem."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":18,"title":"Peter Reports to the Church","entries":[{"start":1,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There will always be people who have issues with the way in which God does things. People love exclusivity and don’t desire to open their inner circles. I’ve heard of churches (even today) that would be willing to cut relationships with anyone who doesn’t agree with their viewpoints or because they are deemed less holy than the rest of the church. What do you do when you are involved with an issue like that? I believe the wisest thing to do is to preach grace and love to people who have a high line of thinking towards their own beliefs and continuously compare themselves to others."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, usually, people who feel they are mightier than others are those who don’t have any particular view of grace and preach a negative spirit towards those who don’t know any better. How sad and twisted one must be to cause this kind of harm and division simply because of the background some people have. 3 Saying, Thou wentest in to men uncircumcised, and didst eat with them."},{"kind":"para","text":"I guess the apostles forgot who Jesus sat with."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, as Jesus sat at meat in the house, behold, many publicans and sinners came and sat down with him and his disciples.","ref":"Matthew 9:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"If we are more busy dividing ourselves from others than we are sharing the truth of Gods Word with everyone, especially if it’s because they are from a different ethnicity or background, we have missed the entire point of the gospel message, which has been profoundly illustrated multiple times by Christ."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then saith the woman of Samaria unto him, How is it that thou, being a Jew, askest drink of me, which am a woman of Samaria? for the Jews have no dealings with the Samaritans.","ref":"John 4:9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And there came a leper to him, beseeching him, and kneeling down to him, and saying unto him, If thou wilt, thou canst make me clean. And Jesus, moved with compassion, put forth his hand, and touched him, and saith unto him, I will; be thou clean. And as soon as he had spoken, immediately the leprosy departed from him, and he was cleansed.","ref":"Mark 1:40-42"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting life.","ref":"John 3:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve seen many staunch and pious Christians who have mostly self-centered and negative viewpoints completely ruin their testimony and teachings simply because they have a warped viewpoint of who should be saved and who shouldn’t. It is not up for US to us to decide the salvation of others; that is put for God to decide. They hang up on the doctrine of election and find themselves backed into a corner of noncompliance to the Gospel because they think that God will save whomever He pleases anyway, and some people were just meant to be thrown into Hell. That couldn’t be farther from the truth. D.L. Moody said, “Do not stumble on the doctrine of election. Preach the gospel to all, and if you convert any one who was not ‘chosen,’ God will forgive you.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"4 But Peter rehearsed the matter from the beginning, and expounded it by order unto them, saying, 5 I was in the city of Joppa praying: and in a trance I saw a vision, A certain vessel descend, as it had been a great sheet, let down from heaven by four corners; and it came even to me: 6 Upon the which when I had fastened mine eyes, I considered, and saw fourfooted beasts of the earth, and wild beasts, and creeping things, and fowls of the air. 7 And I heard a voice saying unto me, Arise, Peter; slay and eat. 8 But I said, Not so, Lord: for nothing common or unclean hath at any time entered into my mouth. 9 But the voice answered me again from heaven, What God hath cleansed, that call not thou common. 10 And this was done three times: and all were drawn up again into heaven. 11 And, behold, immediately there were three men already come unto the house where I was, sent from Caesarea unto me. 12 And the Spirit bade me go with them, nothing doubting. Moreover these six brethren accompanied me, and we entered into the man's house: 13 And he shewed us how he had seen an angel in his house, which stood and said unto him, Send men to Joppa, and call for Simon, whose surname is Peter; 14 Who shall tell thee words, whereby thou and all thy house shall be saved. 15 And as I began to speak, the Holy Ghost fell on them, as on us at the beginning."},{"kind":"para","text":"This recap is important for us, the readers, because we tend to forget things we just read, especially when we are going through one chapter every day or per week. There are many things that call our attention, and it usually isn’t what we have already studied (sadly…). We are reminded, as Peter was so entrenched to believe at this point (because God made it clear), that salvation didn’t just belong to a small group of people. God could not save the world that way. He needed ALL to come to repentance. He needed ALL to know Him and turn from their wickedness."},{"kind":"para","text":"What I find interesting is, even if everyone in the whole world came to Christ, we would still see a fall of Christianity and salvation because Satan is always working. Just as we forget what we learn within a week, the generations to follow us will forget our faith in the decades that preceded us. That’s why group Bible study is so significant. That’s why preaching is so important. That’s why recording and writing are valuable. There’s no generation that can come after us that would say, “They obviously didn’t believe in a God, so why should I?” No, we have too much proof to state otherwise, and they are all left without excuse."},{"kind":"para","text":"The things I build up for now, as they were building up for in this first church unknowingly, are all for the unseen things that our kids and grandkids will be able to pick up and continue on with if the Lord would will them too. That’s another thing; God must do His work in His way, through His timing. We must be patient enough to wait on Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"16 Then remembered I the word of the Lord, how that he said, John indeed baptized with water; but ye shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost."},{"kind":"para","text":"This act of bringing the gospel to non-Jews sparked a memory in Peter from Jesus Himself."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For John truly baptized with water; but ye shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost not many days hence.","ref":"Acts 1:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter’s had a lot of thought cases like this. He’s starting to become a master at remembering what Jesus said and being obedient to His words. It was taking time and trust for sure, and it wasn’t without its difficulties (as we will see in future chapters…) 17 Forasmuch then as God gave them the like gift as he did unto us, who believed on the Lord Jesus Christ; what was I, that I could withstand God?"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a fantastic verse for all Christians to highlight in their Bibles. It is a question we should always be asking ourselves when it comes to the way in which others might preach, teach, minister, worship, or give. Many Christians believe in the example of the first churches’ segregation; some believe there is a structure to giving and worship. Others have an idea of how worship, praying, or teaching should be done, and if it’s not in that exact way and doesn’t please them because they enjoy it that way, then it must be wrong. There are others who are pleased by this."},{"kind":"para","text":"Furthermore, God grows people through these unusual methods, and they may seem crazy to us. However, we must say, “What was I that I could withstand God?” Or you could say it this way, “Who am I that I could stand in God’s way of doing things?” We must learn this lesson quickly if we want to see God grow us and those around us. We must recognize God’s movement in other people’s lives in ways that are different from ours."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, though God is not a respecter of persons as we learned in the last chapter, He still treats all of His children differently according to their personalities. The growth is the same, the goal is the same, but the circumstances are very different. God will probably not grow any of us in the same way. But each of us, if we are obedient to the Lord’s Will, will certainly grow for the same purpose to glorify God."},{"kind":"para","text":"18 When they heard these things, they held their peace, and glorified God, saying, Then hath God also to the Gentiles granted repentance unto life."},{"kind":"para","text":"May we take this same note for all ministry and thinking? They were put to silence for a moment because of this question. No doubt, they were all asking themselves the same one that Peter did: “What was I that I could withstand God?” Then, breaking the silence, they glorified God and rejoiced that the gospel was far spreading."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":30,"title":"The Church in Antioch","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were doing what they were taught. They were all under the same impression as Peter, that the gospel could only be understood by the Jews and that anyone that didn’t agree with the Jews, or who was not part of the Jewish bloodline, couldn’t ever understand and receive the gospel."}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Grecians here are Greek-speaking Gentiles. There were many of these that were hearing the gospel message, whether intentional or not, but there was still some uncertainty as to who the message should really be focused on. It didn’t matter though, because God’s Word does what God wants it to do."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So shall my word be that goeth forth out of my mouth: it shall not return unto me void, but it shall accomplish that which I please, and it shall prosper in the thing whereto I sent it.","ref":"Isaiah 55:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Grecians were receiving the gospel just as well as the Jews, and there was plenty to rejoice over in that."}]},{"start":22,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The church was clarified at this point, sending the most encouraging man to this city to clarify and exhort these believers in their purpose. The gospel was already spreading to the Gentiles before Barnabas was there to explain what happened to Peter and the Gentiles in Jerusalem. God’s Word works fast. 25 Then departed Barnabas to Tarsus, for to seek Saul: 26 And when he had found him, he brought him unto Antioch. And it came to pass, that a whole year they assembled themselves with the church, and taught much people. And the disciples were called Christians first in Antioch."},{"kind":"para","text":"Because they were now reaching other groups of people and not just standing in one ethnicity, they changed their definition of these Jesus followers from believers of “The Way” to “Christians.” “Christian” is not a term that describes a religion or a set of beliefs. “Christian” is a name that describes someone who simply follows Christ. Believe it or not, “Christians” are not dedicated to simply one religion or another."},{"kind":"para","text":"Many who follow their religion will call themselves whatever their religious name is. Baptists usually refer to themselves as “Christians,” but they are “Baptist Christians.” “Catholics” sometimes refer to themselves as “Christians,” but they are “Catholic Christians.” “Mormons” often refer to themselves as “Christians,” but they are “Mormon Christians.” We should not be in the game of proclaiming our religion over our following."},{"kind":"para","text":"We do not follow our church; we follow Christ. We do not follow our church’s ordinances; we follow Christ’s ordinances. We do not follow our pastor; we follow Jesus. These have all been put into place to grow us and give us direction, but ultimately, if the church is doing what it’s supposed to be doing, everything in that church will point to Jesus and implore you to follow Him alone for your life’s purpose, not the church or religion. 27 And in these days came prophets from Jerusalem unto Antioch."},{"kind":"para","text":"These prophets are mentioned again in a couple chapters…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now there were in the church that was at Antioch certain prophets and teachers; as Barnabas, and Simeon that was called Niger, and Lucius of Cyrene, and Manaen, which had been brought up with Herod the tetrarch, and Saul.","ref":"Acts 13:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Judas and Silas, being prophets also themselves, exhorted the brethren with many words, and confirmed them.","ref":"Acts 15:32"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the same man had four daughters, virgins, which did prophesy.","ref":"Acts 21:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"They gave instruction and encouragement as they spoke words revealed to them by the Holy Spirit. Some have the gift of prophecy during this time."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"To another the working of miracles; to another prophecy; to another discerning of spirits; to another divers kinds of tongues; to another the interpretation of tongues:","ref":"1 Corinthians 12:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Prophets today are not like they were in the Old Testament. Because we have the Holy Spirit, some people are gifted with the ability to tell certain circumstances based off of obvious history and connected to knowledge of circumstances to foreknow situations. It’s a revelation by God and should be evaluated by other believers."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Quench not the Spirit. Despise not prophesyings. Prove all things; hold fast that which is good.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:19-21"},{"kind":"para","text":"There are also views of this being the ability to preach God’s Word with authority. Some people have grouped this gift to preaching and teaching and prophesying as one common gift. Though there are some who are great preachers, with no vision for the future. Some who are great preachers and terrible teachers. Some who are great teachers and terrible preachers. Some who are blessed with the gift of all of them, and so they are able to give a clear picture and vision of what would please the Lord the most, and guide those who desire to please God as well into that vision. 28 And there stood up one of them named Agabus, and signified by the Spirit that there should be great dearth throughout all the world: which came to pass in the days of Claudius Caesar. 29 Then the disciples, every man according to his ability, determined to send relief unto the brethren which dwelt in Judaea:"},{"kind":"para","text":"They knew that this brother could be trusted in his foretelling of the things to come, and they acted quickly to send relief to the churches that would need it. I really love this example of the church. We should be preparing to help other churches that need the help before the trouble comes their way. We are not always gifted with people that can warn of these incidents, but when we are, we should be willing to test these things, and then to act according to how the Lord would provide for us. 30 Which also they did, and sent it to the elders by the hands of Barnabas and Saul."},{"kind":"para","text":"Barnabas and Saul were entrusted with these provisions, and that was not a job to take lightly during a time when there was great persecution against the church. We will see more persecution happen in the next chapter, but for now, there was a determination to see the Jews and Gentiles saved, growing, and providing for others in times of need. This is the model picture of the church."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":24}],"verses":{"1":"And the apostles and brethren that were in Judaea heard that the Gentiles had also received the word of God.","2":"And when Peter was come up to Jerusalem, they that were of the circumcision contended with him,","19":"Now they which were scattered abroad upon the persecution that arose about Stephen travelled as far as Phenice, and Cyprus, and Antioch, preaching the word to none but unto the Jews only.","20":"And some of them were men of Cyprus and Cyrene, which, when they were come to Antioch, spake unto the Grecians, preaching the Lord Jesus.","21":"And the hand of the Lord was with them: and a great number believed, and turned unto the Lord.","22":"Then tidings of these things came unto the ears of the church which was in Jerusalem: and they sent forth Barnabas, that he should go as far as Antioch.","23":"Who, when he came, and had seen the grace of God, was glad, and exhorted them all, that with purpose of heart they would cleave unto the Lord.","24":"For he was a good man, and full of the Holy Ghost and of faith: and much people was added unto the Lord."}},
    "12": {"chapter":12,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Peter has come back to the Mother Church to tell them the news about the gospel being given to the Gentiles and the church isn’t too thrilled about it. After Peter shares the testimony, they are excited about the news, and desire to deliver this message to the other disciples in Antioch."},{"kind":"para","text":"The news about Christianity is starting to grow immensely, to the point, now, that the king hears about it, and desires to put an end to it before it gets too big."}],"previously":["Peter has come back to the Mother Church to tell them the news about the gospel being given to the Gentiles, and the church isn’t too thrilled about it. After Peter shares the testimony, they are excited about the news and desire to deliver this message to the other disciples in Antioch. The news about Christianity is starting to grow immensely, to the point (now) that the king hears about it and desires to put an end to it before it gets too big."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"James Killed and Peter Imprisoned","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This Herod was Herod Agrippa I, a grandson of Herod the Great. Herod the Great ruled Israel and Judah from 37-4 B.C. He was appointed by Rome to be king of the Jews. He was ruthless, including murdering his own wife, several of his own sons, and other relatives. He is also responsible for killing all the children in Bethlehem two years and under because he didn’t want Jesus to have rulership over him. Obviously, the fruit doesn’t fall too far on this family tree, because his grandson is just as foolish and reckless. Apparently, this family really cares about the example of evil."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The number one attack on Herod’s mind was on the disciples of Jesus. He got rid of James quite quickly, and it seems Peter was his next target to put a stop to this Christian nonsense. 3 And because he saw it pleased the Jews, he proceeded further to take Peter also. (Then were the days of unleavened bread.)"},{"kind":"para","text":"Killing people who talk about Jesus is still a pleasing picture for the world today. The Jews loved this act from the king, and they especially loved it because it was an apostle of Christ. It was someone that knew Christ personally and walked with Christ. This is why he seeks after Peter, because Peter was well known to the people that he was a Jesus follower. The “days of unleavened bread” were the seven days following the Passover meal, and they were considered to be holy, and an execution was a violation during those days."},{"kind":"para","text":"4 And when he had apprehended him, he put him in prison, and delivered him to four quaternions of soldiers to keep him; intending after Easter to bring him forth to the people."},{"kind":"para","text":"As long as they kept their religious practices, it didn’t matter what kinds of sins they performed in their own eyes. Sadly, this is still a religious process today. As long as you believe in your own understanding of who God is, you can do whatever you want as often as you want, and there will be no repercussions for it in eternity as long as you keep to your traditions."},{"kind":"para","text":"Today, you can sin in all kinds of ways, and as long as you confess your issues to some man behind a curtain and a robe, you will be completely fine in the standing of God. There doesn’t have to be guilt, nor learning. There doesn’t have to be repentance, nor turning. It can all just be confessed and forgotten. And it’s true! Men may forget it! Except the Lord doesn’t forget… 5 Peter therefore was kept in prison: but prayer was made without ceasing of the church unto God for him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, the prayers of the people are what God desires the most. God knows exactly what needs to be done, and He knows exactly how to accomplish it. But what He desires more than anything is our reliance on Him. He wants us to be completely reliant on His authority and power. It wasn’t up to the people to decide what God was going to do with the situation that Peter was in, and it wasn’t up to the people to force God, through prayer, to move in a way they desired. It was up to God to take heed and to accomplish what needed to be done, and it was through the prayers of His people that He moves with compassion. Honestly, this is the only way God moves."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But when he saw the multitudes, he was moved with compassion on them, because they fainted, and were scattered abroad, as sheep having no shepherd.","ref":"Matthew 9:36"},{"kind":"para","text":"He knows our needs, and He sees our necessities. Some might say, “Well, if God moves with compassion, why did He allow James to die and only rescue Peter?” There are probably a few responses to this."},{"kind":"para","text":"God has all things planned for a specific purpose, He has our birthdates, and our death dates. Without the killing of James, Herod would not move to try and kill Peter, and the effects would not take place that needed to take place for the spreading of the gospel."},{"kind":"para","text":"God wants our reliance on Him. He positions us and the church to get to a point where we will earnestly seek Him. Suffering gives the lost a course of pain and hurt with no outlet and no salvation. Whereas, suffering for the Christian provides direction. Read 1 Peter to see how that works (Peter of all people understand suffering)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Herod needed to die. It had to come out this way so that God’s plan could be assured, and that it would move a host of people in the right direction."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":19,"title":"Peter Is Rescued","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Peter is in prison, chained to two guards, death is looming on the horizon, and he still finds a way to sleep. Maybe Peter was just comfortable with the fact that God has all things planned? He understood that if his life was taken, it was not taken by man. God knows the death of His people, and it’s not something that God takes lightly, especially if it’s a death by murder. No doubt, Peter was under the impression that God would do what needed to be done, regardless of Peter’s health or state. Peter had already set up so much for the gospel and the church, to the point where it was irreversible, and that appeased Peter to take the death that he believed would ultimately glorify the Lord. 7 And, behold, the angel of the Lord came upon him, and a light shined in the prison: and he smote Peter on the side, and raised him up, saying, Arise up quickly. And his chains fell off from his hands."},{"kind":"para","text":"He struck him on the side to wake him up. Peter must have been in a deep sleep. I feel like a gentle shake may have sufficed. But Peter needed to be woken up in a hurry."},{"kind":"para","text":"8 And the angel said unto him, Gird thyself, and bind on thy sandals. And so he did. And he saith unto him, Cast thy garment about thee, and follow me."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter was very obedient to this angel. He got dressed quickly, rose to his feet after just being woken up by a hit on his side, and now is following this angel wherever he is told. 9 And he went out, and followed him; and wist not that it was true which was done by the angel; but thought he saw a vision. 10 When they were past the first and the second ward, they came unto the iron gate that leadeth unto the city; which opened to them of his own accord: and they went out, and passed on through one street; and forthwith the angel departed from him. 11 And when Peter was come to himself, he said, Now I know of a surety, that the Lord hath sent his angel, and hath delivered me out of the hand of Herod, and from all the expectation of the people of the Jews."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is (perhaps) one of the funniest scenes in the whole Bible. Peter thought he was still dreaming. Everything that he was witnessing seemed impossible or unreal. He hadn’t done anything. Maybe he thought, “If only I could be free in real life and not even have to lift a finger.” He was following this angel, and gates were unlocking on their own. Guards weren’t even paying attention to Peter as he passed them. Thankfully, he didn’t test a guard or something to see if he was still sleeping."},{"kind":"para","text":"He gets to the street, and it says the angel just straight up leaves him there. I imagine Peter at this point, just standing in the middle of the street, looking around and trying to figure out if he was still dreaming or not. The quiet of the night radiated in his ears. His vision was still adjusting to the darkness after waking up. His skin began to feel the coolness of the air. Then suddenly, he realized he was actually here… in real life… not just dreaming it. Then, Peter just accepted the fact that God sent an angel to deliver him out of the expectations of Herod and the Jews. This is such an amazing circumstance! 12 And when he had considered the thing, he came to the house of Mary the mother of John, whose surname was Mark; where many were gathered together praying."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the first time we are introduced to Mark, who is cited as the writer of the gospel of Mark. Mark and Peter have a great ministry journey together and will be a pretty important figure in the next three chapters of this book."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The church that is at Babylon, elected together with you, saluteth you; and so doth Marcus my son.","ref":"1 Peter 5:13 13 And as Peter knocked at the door of the gate, a damsel came to hearken, named Rhoda. 14 And when she knew Peter's voice, she opened not the gate for gladness, but ran in, and told how Peter stood before the gate."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, this story gets even funnier here. Peter had just come to his senses, ran over for shelter from the coming danger that was (no doubt) veering to look for him, and instead of being taken in immediately by these disciples, he is left at the gate in the excitement of the moment. 15 And they said unto her, Thou art mad. But she constantly affirmed that it was even so. Then said they, It is his angel."},{"kind":"para","text":"Knock, knock. Who’s there? Peter. You are out of your mind. It must be his angel. Once again, they continue to leave Peter out in the danger and cold. Honestly, the disciples could have just gotten up to see what Rhoda was talking about, but instead, they continue to argue about who was really at the door. 16 But Peter continued knocking: and when they had opened the door, and saw him, they were astonished."},{"kind":"para","text":"Rhoda was telling the truth! But the funniest part doesn’t even end there… 17 But he, beckoning unto them with the hand to hold their peace, declared unto them how the Lord had brought him out of the prison. And he said, Go shew these things unto James, and to the brethren. And he departed, and went into another place."},{"kind":"para","text":"He literally spent all this time getting their attention. Now he has it, tells them how he escaped the prison, and in one conversation leaves them again… This seems to be kind of a waste of time, but Peter saw it important to let James (Jesus’ brother James, not John’s brother James (he was already killed at the beginning of this chapter)) know about these events. Peter probably did this to protect them, and it gives James (Jesus’ brother) a prominent role of leadership among the apostles in Jerusalem (as we will see in the coming chapters)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Some scholars say that Peter left his position as leader because he was too scared and would be sought after by the king still. Others say that he went to a different place to take up leadership so that he wouldn’t have to spend his life continuously running from the government. Whatever the case, it’s sufficient to say that Peter’s story isn’t done yet, and it gives place to continuing to grow the church in absurdly profound ways. 18 Now as soon as it was day, there was no small stir among the soldiers, what was become of Peter. 19 And when Herod had sought for him, and found him not, he examined the keepers, and commanded that they should be put to death. And he went down from Judaea to Caesarea, and there abode."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s a serious offense to lose a prisoner that you are LITERALLY CHAINED TO. Even these guards were utterly confused, and they were with Peter. Herod sticks to the typical Roman custom and kills the guards who lost the prisoner to the same death as the prisoner was to be committed too."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":25,"title":"The Death of Herod","entries":[{"start":20,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Supplication should never be dependent on men. Men will never be able to meet our needs. Government will never be enough for the people. Supplication should only come from God, and these people were about to learn this the hard way. 23 And immediately the angel of the Lord smote him, because he gave not God the glory: and he was eaten of worms, and gave up the ghost."},{"kind":"para","text":"What a gruesome and well-deserved scene. The Lord, I believe, had a reason to kill Herod for the sinful acts he took against the Lord’s children. God had reason to kill Herod for the murder of James, a disciple of Jesus. He had a right to kill Herod because he was continuously abusing people. But God doesn’t kill him for those reasons."},{"kind":"para","text":"He kills Herod because he was taking glory away from God. We are made to glorify God, and that alone is our purpose. When we take glory away from God, it displeases Him, and rightfully so. God will not pour out wrath over the death and murder of His saints, not until the second coming of Christ. 24 But the word of God grew and multiplied. 25 And Barnabas and Saul returned from Jerusalem, when they had fulfilled their ministry, and took with them John, whose surname was Mark."},{"kind":"para","text":"Through these scenarios and acts, God’s Word was growing in the community, and people were starting to rely and depend on the Lord in droves! Now it’s time to deliver this message further out, which is where Saul, Barnabas, and Mark get their start!"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":20,"end":22}],"verses":{"1":"Now about that time Herod the king stretched forth his hands to vex certain of the church.","2":"And he killed James the brother of John with the sword.","6":"And when Herod would have brought him forth, the same night Peter was sleeping between two soldiers, bound with two chains: and the keepers before the door kept the prison.","20":"And Herod was highly displeased with them of Tyre and Sidon: but they came with one accord to him, and, having made Blastus the king's chamberlain their friend, desired peace; because their country was nourished by the king's country.","21":"And upon a set day Herod, arrayed in royal apparel, sat upon his throne, and made an oration unto them.","22":"And the people gave a shout, saying, It is the voice of a god, and not of a man."}},
    "13": {"chapter":13,"intro":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"But he, beckoning unto them with the hand to hold their peace, declared unto them how the Lord had brought him out of the prison. And he said, Go shew these things unto James, and to the brethren. And he departed, and went into another place.","ref":"Acts 12:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"James (Jesus’ brother) takes up the mantle of church leadership where Peter left off, and it’s assumed that Peter went to Rome or Antioch. In this chapter, we pick back up with Barnabas, Saul, and Mark as they start their missionary journey to preach the gospel."}],"previously":["We had a really interesting look at God’s intervening. It was not time for Peter to die, and so God allowed an escape, and Peter thought the whole thing was a dream until he was standing on the street by himself with no further directions of where to go. Taking that as a sign to move on, he tells the church about the events and then leaves there to go to “another place.”"],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Barnabas and Saul Sent","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The “prophets and teachers” is a common title for the offices of the church in the New Testament."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"To another the working of miracles; to another prophecy; to another discerning of spirits; to another divers kinds of tongues; to another the interpretation of tongues:","ref":"1 Corinthians 12:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, “prophecy” doesn’t mean the same as it did in the Old Testament, though it is applied in the same stance. For instance, the Old Testament prophets would be told by God the events that were to take place, and these prophets would then tell and warn everyone else about the things that God had said without really evaluating the full picture."},{"kind":"para","text":"In the New Testament, God reveals specific situations to Christians that would come true if things are not changed or if we continue to do the things we are doing. A leader of a church today should have the capability to somewhat “prophecy” the direction of his church so that he is able to make the necessary changes to meet the needs of the church to focus on the Lord and His work. It only makes sense that a church without a vision is a blind church. You won’t have to wait too long for the church to trip if there is no vision to move the church forward."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s fine to be content with what you have in the meantime, but church leaders should always be looking forward to where they desire the church would go and grow. There needs to be a balance of contentment and desire."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Where there is no vision, the people perish: but he that keepeth the law, happy is he.","ref":"Proverbs 29:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"The name “Niger” indicates that this man more than likely came from Africa, and we understand that this is also where Lucius came from (it literally depicts that, saying he is of Cyrene). Herod the Tetrarch is the son of Herod the Great, whom we spoke about in the last chapter. He was the one who was reigning during the time of Jesus’ ministry. 2 As they ministered to the Lord, and fasted, the Holy Ghost said, Separate me Barnabas and Saul for the work whereunto I have called them."},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems, at this point, they were anticipating God’s working and moving because of the current events that were unfolding. The gospel needed to be sent out further than just this land, and they were praying and fasting to figure out what to do next. Because of this commitment to see God work, God revealed to them the necessity to send out Saul and Barnabas to preach the gospel further from where they were and start establishing more churches. This is the first “missionary” call of the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I was a baby Christian, I could not understand the desire for people to go to a third-world country, with no idea about the culture or language, and somehow start a church not knowing anyone but the Lord Himself. Now that I’ve grown in my faith, I understand the calling and necessities placed by the Lord. We should all be open to being missionaries, and in all honesty, even right where we are, here in America, we are missionaries. We are serving a land which is not our home, and we are preaching the gospel to a very lost and confusing world until we are able to return to the Lord who has sent us out. Spurgeon said, “Every Christian is either a missionary, or an imposter.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"These men were being prepared this entire time to go into this mission work, and they probably didn’t even know it until now. They could have rejected it or asked to pray about it first, but they didn’t, and this tells me that God had been working in their hearts to desire to go out into the world knowing nothing about their next place of interest. 3 And when they had fasted and prayed, and laid their hands on them, they sent them away."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, we see this “laying of hands” take place to indicate their commission and trust in God’s provision for these believers. This is also a physical symbol of giving the Holy Spirit’s power so they can do the work that God has called them to do. It’s always a sad thing to see members of the congregation go, but we should never desire that they leave without our offering and building up. A discouraging congregation to those that have a God-given call on their lives is not a Godly congregation."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":12,"title":"Barnabas and Saul on Cyprus","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Seleucia was about sixteen miles from Antioch, and then they shipped about one hundred and thirty miles to Salamis. 5 And when they were at Salamis, they preached the word of God in the synagogues of the Jews: and they had also John to their minister."},{"kind":"para","text":"John mark was an integral member of the group, not because he was leading the cause (though he was certainly in a position of leadership) but because he was helping the works of Saul and Barnabas. The work of the ministry was taking instant effect as soon as they arrived in this city. 6 And when they had gone through the isle unto Paphos, they found a certain sorcerer, a false prophet, a Jew, whose name was Barjesus:"},{"kind":"para","text":"They did some traveling in this part of the country. Paphos was ninety miles southwest of Salamis. Paul comes to describe Barjesus as the “son of the devil.” Which is funny, because the name Barjesus literally means “the son of the Savior.” Obviously, his “magic” is assisted by demonic powers and not by God. Pagans and Jews during this time commonly practiced magic. They set out to try and “heal diseases,” bringing physical blessings, cursing or otherwise harming people, and guarding against both curses and demons. They were quite “Harry Potter”-esque. They also claimed to know the future. 7 Which was with the deputy of the country, Sergius Paulus, a prudent man; who called for Barnabas and Saul, and desired to hear the word of God. 8 But Elymas the sorcerer (for so is his name by interpretation) withstood them, seeking to turn away the deputy from the faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"The “deputy of the country” was the highest-ranking official in a Roman senatorial province. They were some really powerful men that decided they wanted to know a little more about the gospel Saul and Barnabas were preaching."},{"kind":"para","text":"Elymas and Barjesus are the same person, and it was actually pretty common for Jews to have more than one name (like John Mark, for instance). Elymas, according to Luke, means “magician.” Barjesus (or Elymas if that makes it easier) didn’t want these missionaries to come because he viewed them as a threat to his profit in the proconsul. This would be the first interesting conflict in the missionaries’ journey, and it was not an easy first battle. 9 Then Saul, (who also is called Paul,) filled with the Holy Ghost, set his eyes on him."},{"kind":"para","text":"This verse is so fascinating to me. Suddenly, Saul’s name is changed, and we are not told why. His name never goes back to Saul again but remains Paul, and he only refers to himself as Paul through every letter he writes in the New Testament. The assumption for the name change is because he is dealing in Gentile territory. His Hebrew name was Saul, but his Roman name was Paul. The language was the beneficiary of the name change. He references his old name later on in Acts, but only because he is recounting past events. 10 And said, O full of all subtilty and all mischief, thou child of the devil, thou enemy of all righteousness, wilt thou not cease to pervert the right ways of the Lord?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul just blatantly calls out this false teacher. Honestly, Paul had every right and could tell who was fake and who was real. This was a bold proclamation, but it seems to play out in his favor as we will see. 11 And now, behold, the hand of the Lord is upon thee, and thou shalt be blind, not seeing the sun for a season. And immediately there fell on him a mist and a darkness; and he went about seeking some to lead him by the hand. 12 Then the deputy, when he saw what was done, believed, being astonished at the doctrine of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a crazy event that takes place. It can easily be pushed to the side to continue reading onwards, but this is a noteworthy occasion. In the early church, miracles seemed to always take place to help bring others to genuine faith. This still happens today, but it doesn’t happen in the same way as it did before. The chances of bringing fire down from Heaven at the believers’ call are extremely slim. The chances of blinding a man because of their purposeful idolatrous lifestyle would be just as difficult."},{"kind":"para","text":"We see signs, however, all around us of God’s working. The fact that we are not consumed is a sign from God. But God always answers prayer today. He answers every prayer we have ever had. Even if His answer is simply “no.”"}]}]},{"start":13,"end":52,"title":"Paul and Barnabas in Pisidia","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Perga was about eight miles from Paphos. Paul won’t stay here too long, but simply preaches the gospel on his journey returning to Antioch. This is a conflicting occasion for Paul because Mark decides to leave and go to Jerusalem. Later on, we will see this particular situation escalate division, not just between Paul and Mark, but between Paul and Barnabas."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t know the intentions of Mark leaving for Jerusalem; it’s not clarified. I do know that Mark didn’t just leave the ministry. Maybe he felt a calling to go back to Jerusalem, and there was certainly a necessity there that Mark would help with. Maybe he just felt better suited in Jerusalem or the ministry there. Maybe he knew Paul was returning to Antioch and so Mark decided to help with another ministry until it was time to set out again. Whatever the purpose, I honestly believe Paul was in the wrong with the tension that will build up between them later on. This is a key point that we have to remember when that tension comes up in a later chapter. 14 But when they departed from Perga, they came to Antioch in Pisidia, and went into the synagogue on the sabbath day, and sat down."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul goes back to the synagogues of the Jews where he started this journey in verse five. They are, of course, not done with their missionary journey yet. 15 And after the reading of the law and the prophets the rulers of the synagogue sent unto them, saying, Ye men and brethren, if ye have any word of exhortation for the people, say on."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sometimes the law can be burdensome to believers who have heard the gospel preached over and over again. I’ve seen it where preaching the law to a saved crowd simply becomes background noise. It’s not effective to a crowd that already knows the law and knows the Lord, who completed the law. Sometimes the congregation needs encouragement, application, and a lesson."},{"kind":"para","text":"Preaching the law is important, but it also has its place. If every message to every person is always beating them down with hellfire, brimstone, and perfectionism, you will not maintain a congregation’s notice for too long. The spirit you preach with behind the pulpit is the spirit that the rest of the congregation will feel. The congregation of this church wanted some encouragement or “exhortation.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"16 Then Paul stood up, and beckoning with his hand said, Men of Israel, and ye that fear God, give audience. 17 The God of this people of Israel chose our fathers, and exalted the people when they dwelt as strangers in the land of Egypt, and with an high arm brought he them out of it. 18 And about the time of forty years suffered he their manners in the wilderness. 19 And when he had destroyed seven nations in the land of Chanaan, he divided their land to them by lot."},{"kind":"para","text":"He says something interesting here, which I have no doubt that others have heard before, but just in case, I think there should be a reminder. During the time of the wandering of the Israelites in the wilderness, God was destroying and preparing the place in which His children would take over. Paul says that God destroyed seven nations to get the land prepared for His people. If they went in right away, they would have been destroyed right away. They needed to learn some lessons in the wilderness, that’s for sure, but they also needed to wait upon God’s provision for His children. The Israelites learned more in the wilderness than they did as slaves to the Egyptians. As it is with us."},{"kind":"para","text":"Many times we are so stuck in the wilderness of our issues, and we feel so far disconnected from God like His own people did in Exodus that we are completely wandering in circles and questioning God’s every move. God eventually pulls them out of the wilderness, but it takes years because of their constant disobedience and un-reliance on Him. We trap ourselves, honestly, in the wilderness because we simply can’t trust in the Lord and wait for Him to work everything out in the proper season. 20 And after that he gave unto them judges about the space of four hundred and fifty years, until Samuel the prophet. 21 And afterward they desired a king: and God gave unto them Saul the son of Cis, a man of the tribe of Benjamin, by the space of forty years. 22 And when he had removed him, he raised up unto them David to be their king; to whom also he gave their testimony, and said, I have found David the son of Jesse, a man after mine own heart, which shall fulfil all my will. 23 Of this man's seed hath God according to his promise raised unto Israel a Saviour, Jesus: 24 When John had first preached before his coming the baptism of repentance to all the people of Israel. 25 And as John fulfilled his course, he said, Whom think ye that I am? I am not he. But, behold, there cometh one after me, whose shoes of his feet I am not worthy to loose. 26 Men and brethren, children of the stock of Abraham, and whosoever among you feareth God, to you is the word of this salvation sent."},{"kind":"para","text":"Do you want some encouragement? Is the law too heavy for you? Are the works of God too much for you to bear? Then look to Jesus! Jesus is the best encouragement a Christian can give to others, and He should be examined as often as humanly possible. Every hour should be spent thinking of Jesus. Every moment and decision should involve Christ in our lives. There should be nothing done without the working of Jesus, first and foremost. If it’s without Christ, it is destined to fail and will never give you encouragement that will last. Christ is our exhortation because He continues to live and prove Himself in the life of a Christian. Anything else is but a bandaid that won’t last for very long. 27 For they that dwell at Jerusalem, and their rulers, because they knew him not, nor yet the voices of the prophets which are read every sabbath day, they have fulfilled them in condemning him. 28 And though they found no cause of death in him, yet desired they Pilate that he should be slain. 29 And when they had fulfilled all that was written of him, they took him down from the tree, and laid him in a sepulchre. 30 But God raised him from the dead: 31 And he was seen many days of them which came up with him from Galilee to Jerusalem, who are his witnesses unto the people. 32 And we declare unto you glad tidings, how that the promise which was made unto the fathers, 33 God hath fulfilled the same unto us their children, in that he hath raised up Jesus again; as it is also written in the second psalm, Thou art my Son, this day have I begotten thee. 34 And as concerning that he raised him up from the dead, now no more to return to corruption, he said on this wise, I will give you the sure mercies of David. 35 Wherefore he saith also in another psalm, Thou shalt not suffer thine Holy One to see corruption. 36 For David, after he had served his own generation by the will of God, fell on sleep, and was laid unto his fathers, and saw corruption: 37 But he, whom God raised again, saw no corruption. 38 Be it known unto you therefore, men and brethren, that through this man is preached unto you the forgiveness of sins: 39 And by him all that believe are justified from all things, from which ye could not be justified by the law of Moses. 40 Beware therefore, lest that come upon you, which is spoken of in the prophets; 41 Behold, ye despisers, and wonder, and perish: for I work a work in your days, a work which ye shall in no wise believe, though a man declare it unto you."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve been teaching and preaching for some years now (maybe not as many as most), but there is one thing I’ve learned from it all: there are not enough preachers going back to the gospel message. They preach about the law, the forgiveness of sins, the good outweighing the bad, damnation, heaven, and hell. They preach about their feelings and against certain companies in the church house (even about specific people). They preach all kinds of doctrines and ideologies that are interesting and certainly things to be thought about, but what a hungering church really wants is the message of Jesus Christ crucified and rising again for the salvation of the world."},{"kind":"para","text":"Every time I’ve taught for a season on specific topics or lessons, the ones that stand out the most and get the most communication and attention are the ones about Jesus’ sacrifice for us and His saving grace through His burial and resurrection. There’s nothing that can bring more encouragement to a people better than our Savior’s testimony. Go back to the gospel as often as possible, because that’s the most important doctrine for people to hear, and that is what we are all ultimately craving, and we don’t even know it until we taste it. 42 And when the Jews were gone out of the synagogue, the Gentiles besought that these words might be preached to them the next sabbath. 43 Now when the congregation was broken up, many of the Jews and religious proselytes followed Paul and Barnabas: who, speaking to them, persuaded them to continue in the grace of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’m thankful for leaders in the church who don’t simply ignore the congregation’s request for encouragement, but rather try to meet that need with the most important message possible. You’d be amazed at how many churches today don’t tend to the needs of the congregation, and they become a bunch of zombies simply doing the works of God because they feel like they have to."}]}]},{"start":44,"end":45,"title":"Opposition from the Jews","entries":[{"start":44,"end":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They came back for the same message. I heard a story by D.L. Moody about a message he had heard once. He thought he was doing well in his church leadership, and was praying to God for the congregation that was given to him. It seemed everything was perfectly fine, and everyone was in good spirits. He had a revival meeting where a guest speaker came in. The name of the speaker is blanking me right now, and I can’t seem to find where I had read this testimony at the moment. The speaker preached on John 3:16, and his main emphasis was on the love of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"Moody thought very little about the message topic, especially because it seemed so childish, but once the sermon was finished being preached, Moody became undone. He noticed his own lack of love in his congregation. He asked the preacher to come again and preach on the same topic the next night, and the preacher expanded his message even further, completely cutting Moody’s heart. Moody asked for him to come a third night and preach, and the message was even deeper. Moody was astounded about how much depth John 3:16 had, and his heart was forever changed in the picture of God’s love for the betterment of his congregation."},{"kind":"para","text":"The message of Christ must be the central focus of all that we do. Not for crowds, not for numbers, not for seats filled, and not for personal gain or monetary gain. The most important message for this dying world is the Savior who can change it. But, of course, you will have some people that come in and hate the message, trying to ruin what Christ has done by stirring people up falsely…"}]}]},{"start":46,"end":49,"title":"Paul and Barnabas Turn to the Gentiles","entries":[{"start":46,"end":47,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The gospel was preached, whether they wanted to accept it or not. It only takes a little bit of sin and hate to destroy the whole lump. May we not be the leaven for the ruining of God’s bread making. 48 And when the Gentiles heard this, they were glad, and glorified the word of the Lord: and as many as were ordained to eternal life believed. 49 And the word of the Lord was published throughout all the region."},{"kind":"para","text":"Preach the gospel to all. You will not always have a crowd that appreciates the message, but you will always have a few that get it. Pay attention to the few, not the majority. The few can make a difference more than the majority. The majority may be handy for God’s work, but the minority is always known to be handy for God’s Word. 50 But the Jews stirred up the devout and honourable women, and the chief men of the city, and raised persecution against Paul and Barnabas, and expelled them out of their coasts. 51 But they shook off the dust of their feet against them, and came unto Iconium. 52 And the disciples were filled with joy, and with the Holy Ghost."},{"kind":"para","text":"Iconium was ninety miles on foot from Pisidian Antioch. There was still reason for the disciples to rejoice, simply because they got to preach the gospel. That’s a perfect reason for any of us to rejoice. We should always look for opportunities to talk to people about what Scripture says, and preach Christ to them."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":44,"end":45},{"start":46,"end":47}],"verses":{"1":"Now there were in the church that was at Antioch certain prophets and teachers; as Barnabas, and Simeon that was called Niger, and Lucius of Cyrene, and Manaen, which had been brought up with Herod the tetrarch, and Saul.","4":"So they, being sent forth by the Holy Ghost, departed unto Seleucia; and from thence they sailed to Cyprus.","13":"Now when Paul and his company loosed from Paphos, they came to Perga in Pamphylia: and John departing from them returned to Jerusalem.","44":"And the next sabbath day came almost the whole city together to hear the word of God.","45":"But when the Jews saw the multitudes, they were filled with envy, and spake against those things which were spoken by Paul, contradicting and blaspheming.","46":"Then Paul and Barnabas waxed bold, and said, It was necessary that the word of God should first have been spoken to you: but seeing ye put it from you, and judge yourselves unworthy of everlasting life, lo, we turn to the Gentiles.","47":"For so hath the Lord commanded us, saying, I have set thee to be a light of the Gentiles, that thou shouldest be for salvation unto the ends of the earth."}},
    "14": {"chapter":14,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Saul and Barnabas are sent out to the work of the Holy Spirit, they come to Cyprus where they meet the “magician” Bar-Jesus, who tries to turn them away from coming before the presence of the deputy (the one that was the highest-ranking official in a Roman senatorial province) in case they are believed. Once Saul (whose name is now changed to his Roman name “Paul”) see’s him, the Lord performs a miracle of his own with the mist and darkness, which then causes the deputy to believe because he was astonished at the Lord’s teaching."},{"kind":"para","text":"After this, Paul and Barnabas continue their journey, and head to Perga where John Mark ends up taking leave for a different ministry cause. Then they came to Pisidia and taught about the law and prophets, where Paul was asked to encourage the people. Paul preached the gospel, opening up from the Israelites and leading it all the way to the redemption of Christ, and people believed, and wanted Paul to teach it again the following Sabbath. However, when they were being taught there was some Jews that saw the crowd and tried to contradict everything Paul was saying, which caused many to stop believing Paul. Now Paul turns to the Gentiles, and we see them continue their journey in this chapter."}],"previously":["Saul and Barnabas are sent out to the work of the Holy Spirit. They come to Cyprus where they meet the “magician” Bar-Jesus, who tries to turn them away from coming before the presence of the deputy (the one that was the highest-ranking official in a Roman senatorial province) in case they are believed. Once Saul (whose name is now changed to his Roman name “Paul”) sees him, the Lord performs a miracle of his own with the mist and darkness, which then causes the deputy to believe because he was astonished at the Lord’s teaching. After this, Paul and Barnabas continue their journey, and head to Perga where John Mark ends up taking leave for a different ministry cause. Then they came to Pisidia and taught about the law and prophets, where Paul was asked to encourage the people. Paul preached the gospel, opening up from the Israelites and leading it all the way to the redemption of Christ, and people believed, and wanted Paul to teach it again the following Sabbath. However, when they were being taught there were some Jews that saw the crowd and tried to contradict everything Paul was saying, which caused many to stop believing Paul. Now Paul turns to the Gentiles, and we see them continue their journey in this chapter."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Paul and Barnabas at Iconium","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"From Pisidian, where Paul had just preached, to Iconium was ninety miles by road. These were not small treks that Paul and Barnabas were making. If we did the math from the last chapter, from Antioch to Seleucia was about sixteen miles. They took a ship from there to Salamis, which was about one hundred thirty miles. From that point, they went on foot from Paphos to Salamis, which was about ninety miles. Rounding this trip off to this point, Paul and Barnabas have travelled a total of three hundred twenty-six miles so far, and are still continuing."},{"kind":"para","text":"These men were obviously dedicated, and it showed, not just in the effort they spent on traveling, but in the spirit they had preaching to the places they traveled. The people, here, in Iconium, were amazed at what they were saying, and it says a “great multitude” believed because of the way they were preaching the gospel, “and so spake, that a great multitude…believed.” But of course, we already know that when God is at work, so is Satan…"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, the best way to describe unbelievers who are constantly combating the people of God and the Word of God by making people’s minds “evil-affected against” is “poisonous.” That is literally what poison does. It causes people to be ill and feel terrible. It’s not really a surprise, but people are still like this today. 3 Long time therefore abode they speaking boldly in the Lord, which gave testimony unto the word of his grace, and granted signs and wonders to be done by their hands."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord seemed to allow Paul and Barnabas to stay to continue to prove and build up the people here in Iconium, even allowing signs and wonders to happen by their own hands. 4 But the multitude of the city was divided: and part held with the Jews, and part with the apostles."},{"kind":"para","text":"There’s obviously a pattern happening here. This was the same issue that took place in Pisidia in the last chapter. This is exactly why they moved on to Iconium to begin with. Satan always works in the same way, and never falters when it comes to trying to stop what the Lord is doing. He does it with subtlety, but it’s always asking unimportant questions, and trying to turn people against each other with those self-same questions. Satan is predictable. 5 And when there was an assault made both of the Gentiles, and also of the Jews with their rulers, to use them despitefully, and to stone them, 6 They were ware of it, and fled unto Lystra and Derbe, cities of Lycaonia, and unto the region that lieth round about: 7 And there they preached the gospel."},{"kind":"para","text":"Stoning is another interesting pattern that Satan uses in the early church. Of course, now we don’t stone people like they did back then for capital punishment, but the anger will be the same style as it was during this time if we are going to be bold for the Lord. People hate boldness in faith. They don’t want any part of someone who believes so strongly in something because it means they have to give up their traditions and their sins."},{"kind":"para","text":"Someone who has everything in the world will hate to hear that Jesus can give them eternity because they already have everything they could possibly want now. Jesus can’t give them any more than what they have already worked so hard for. They aren’t wrong. Jesus can’t give them any more. The sad part is that their rewards are for the here and now. They have earned their rewards for the present, and that is all they will ever get for all of eternity. If they only knew the eternal riches that they could obtain if they forsook their riches here."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":18,"title":"Paul and Barnabas at Lystra","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The ministry that takes place here in Lystra will be extremely important later on because of someone who might have been watching as these circumstances unfolded. We will learn more about him later, but I do believe that Timothy, or maybe his parents, were in the crowd this day to see the events unfold in the life of Paul. 8 And there sat a certain man at Lystra, impotent in his feet, being a cripple from his mother's womb, who never had walked: 9 The same heard Paul speak: who stedfastly beholding him, and perceiving that he had faith to be healed, 10 Said with a loud voice, Stand upright on thy feet. And he leaped and walked."},{"kind":"para","text":"May it be known, as we’ve researched before, that only the Apostles had the ability to perform this kind of miracle. The Spirit of God gave Paul the ability to see what was happening and led him to do something that was seemingly impossible. Today, we can preach to a crowd and discern their needs by their countenance. We can read people while we speak to them and see if there is something we can do. Our goal is not to do the convicting (the Lord will do that on His own), but rather to show the victory. We must be preaching the truth of God’s Word and then leading them to the solution of our issues. Paul saw this man’s need, understood what he needed next, and God pushed Paul to take this time to do exactly what needed to be done. 11 And when the people saw what Paul had done, they lifted up their voices, saying in the speech of Lycaonia, The gods are come down to us in the likeness of men. 12 And they called Barnabas, Jupiter; and Paul, Mercurius, because he was the chief speaker. 13 Then the priest of Jupiter, which was before their city, brought oxen and garlands unto the gates, and would have done sacrifice with the people."},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, this area was given over to a lot of idolatry. They had been worshipping false gods for so long that they completely ignored the message that Paul and Barnabas were preaching (literally just a few minutes ago) and assumed they were sent from their false gods."},{"kind":"para","text":"Idolatry is a very dangerous sin to mess around with, and yet it’s the most common in people’s lives even still today. They make up gods to suit their sins, ones they can cuddle up to and feel comfortable with so that they don’t have to change. Then, if anyone tells them how wrong that is by showing them what it says in the Bible, they get upset and fight against it, which is exactly what happens here… 14 Which when the apostles, Barnabas and Paul, heard of, they rent their clothes, and ran in among the people, crying out, 15 And saying, Sirs, why do ye these things? We also are men of like passions with you, and preach unto you that ye should turn from these vanities unto the living God, which made heaven, and earth, and the sea, and all things that are therein: 16 Who in times past suffered all nations to walk in their own ways. 17 Nevertheless he left not himself without witness, in that he did good, and gave us rain from heaven, and fruitful seasons, filling our hearts with food and gladness. 18 And with these sayings scarce restrained they the people, that they had not done sacrifice unto them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul was really trying here to keep them from making such a big mistake. You know it was a huge mistake because it says that Paul and Barnabas “rent their clothes” at the news. They really tried to bring glory back to the God they were preaching about, but the people just wouldn’t listen. The only thing that Paul and Barnabas could do at this point is escape for their lives… but they really didn’t…"}]}]},{"start":19,"end":23,"title":"Paul Stoned at Lystra","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Interestingly, at least to me, it doesn’t say they stoned Barnabas. I honestly don’t know what happened to him here. It just says they stoned Paul. Maybe these unbelieving Jews only enticed the people to go against Paul, but they thought of Barnabas as a god himself? They do call Barnabas “Zeus,” which was a Greek idol of a god. Or maybe Barnabas was able to get away before the stoning took place. They messed up Paul pretty badly though, so much so that he seemed like he might have been dead."},{"kind":"para","text":"My assumption that Timothy may have been in the crowd when this took place was because, in chapter 16, Paul makes a trip back to this location to try and preach there again (even though he was literally just stoned nearly to death, he still had a desire to see people saved there). Timothy comes highly recommended to Paul as someone who is able to get him access to the people there, and an interesting dynamic and friendship is born from it (which is something we can talk about when that chapter comes)."},{"kind":"para","text":"If this is the case, that Timothy was a witness to this situation, and he may have become saved because of it, it’s an incredible testimony. Instead of running from the ministry or turning away from God because of the amount of persecution that was taking place in the lives of the children of God, Timothy sees this and thinks, “I want that kind of ministry! I want God to use me like He’s using this Paul guy!” He finds a church in his area and learns about the faith, being built up and prepared to literally follow Paul on this same kind of ministry. 20 Howbeit, as the disciples stood round about him, he rose up, and came into the city: and the next day he departed with Barnabas to Derbe."},{"kind":"para","text":"The very next day! He’s literally seen going to the next city, ONE DAY after he was stoned nearly to death. After a stoning like that, most people would be hospitalized and would be out of commission for at least a few weeks. Not Paul. I have to assume that the Lord provided him with strength and energy to continue on with the ministry after such a brutal beating. The real crazy part comes next though…"},{"kind":"para","text":"21 And when they had preached the gospel to that city, and had taught many, they returned again to Lystra, and to Iconium, and Antioch, 22 Confirming the souls of the disciples, and exhorting them to continue in the faith, and that we must through much tribulation enter into the kingdom of God. 23 And when they had ordained them elders in every church, and had prayed with fasting, they commended them to the Lord, on whom they believed."},{"kind":"para","text":"THEY GO BACK?! They could have continued on, but Paul knew there were some in the city who were being established in the faith, and they needed to be encouraged to continue after such a beating took place. Honestly, if this didn’t happen, there probably wouldn’t have been a place for Timothy to go to be strengthened in his faith for the ministries he was about to encounter."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":28,"title":"Paul and Barnabas Return to Antioch in Syria","entries":[{"start":24,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At this point, they are making their journey back home to where they were commissioned from, and it would be a time of rejoicing at the stories that would be brought back to their sending church. The things that Paul and Barnabas experienced were kind of crazy, and they were stories that were worth sharing for the faith and moving of the gospel."},{"kind":"para","text":"No doubt, many of us have been through some serious trials and testing for our faith, and those are the stories that people need to hear. They need to hear how God continued to use you through those difficulties. They need to know how God gave you triumph through the seasons of pain. They need to be strengthened in their faith by the weaknesses you encountered in yours. Trials and difficulties give the Christian direction. 27 And when they were come, and had gathered the church together, they rehearsed all that God had done with them, and how he had opened the door of faith unto the Gentiles. 28 And there they abode long time with the disciples."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Opened a door” is a great expression to use for the opportunities in which God gives us."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I know thy works: behold, I have set before thee an open door, and no man can shut it: for thou hast a little strength, and hast kept my word, and hast not denied my name.","ref":"Revelation 3:8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For a great door and effectual is opened unto me, and there are many adversaries.","ref":"1 Corinthians 16:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"God allows doors to be opened for a purpose, and sometimes they are not easy doors to go through, but they are always doors worth going through."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":24,"end":26}],"verses":{"1":"And it came to pass in Iconium, that they went both together into the synagogue of the Jews, and so spake, that a great multitude both of the Jews and also of the Greeks believed.","2":"But the unbelieving Jews stirred up the Gentiles, and made their minds evil affected against the brethren.","19":"And there came thither certain Jews from Antioch and Iconium, who persuaded the people, and having stoned Paul, drew him out of the city, supposing he had been dead.","24":"And after they had passed throughout Pisidia, they came to Pamphylia.","25":"And when they had preached the word in Perga, they went down into Attalia:","26":"And thence sailed to Antioch, from whence they had been recommended to the grace of God for the work which they fulfilled."}},
    "15": {"chapter":15,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We continued with Paul and Barnabas’s missionary journey through Lystra where they were treated like gods, and eventually Paul was stoned nearly to death. Surprisingly enough, Paul finds an opportunity to go back to Lystra and establish the faith’s of those that understood his preaching so that the churches there could be strengthened to continue even in the face of adversity like Paul had just faced."},{"kind":"para","text":"Shortly after that, they return to their starting church in Antioch to tell them the news of the events that just unfolded, which leads into the conference that is held in this chapter we are reading here."}],"previously":["We continued with Paul and Barnabas’s missionary journey through Lystra, where they were treated like gods, and eventually Paul was stoned nearly to death. Surprisingly enough, Paul finds an opportunity to go back to Lystra and establish the faith of those that understood his preaching so that the churches there could be strengthened to continue even in the face of adversity like Paul had just faced. Shortly after that, they return to their starting church in Antioch to tell them the news of the events that just unfolded, which leads into the conference that is held in this chapter we are reading here."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":21,"title":"The Jerusalem Council","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The issue here was not that they had a lack of belief in the God of the New Testament; certainly, custom and tradition have a stronghold on faith in a lot of areas, and I won’t question or deny the necessity to stand affirmed in one point of view or the other. The issue here was about the form of salvation that they were understanding. To say you cannot be saved unless you “do this, or believe that,” denies the Lord’s choice on the matter. See, it’s the Lord that does the saving, not our works. If it were our works, we would all be doomed, including the most religious among us."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul and Barnabas understood well that the salvation of the lost does not hinge upon traditions or certain religious rights. Salvation is only given to those that believe in the Savior’s death and resurrection with trust and assurance, coupled with repentance and love for the saints. If we trust in our works for salvation, we will never be saved. It is wise not to let this matter go. If there is someone that is around you that tells you about salvation through works, bring them to this conclusion…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"What doth it profit, my brethren, though a man say he hath faith, and have not works? can faith save him? If a brother or sister be naked, and destitute of daily food, And one of you say unto them, Depart in peace, be ye warmed and filled; notwithstanding ye give them not those things which are needful to the body; what doth it profit? Even so faith, if it hath not works, is dead, being alone. Yea, a man may say, Thou hast faith, and I have works: shew me thy faith without thy works, and I will shew thee my faith by my works. Thou believest that there is one God; thou doest well: the devils also believe, and tremble.","ref":"James 2:14-19"},{"kind":"para","text":"What James essentially says here is that we work because we have faith, not work to gain faith. Faith must come before works, otherwise our works are in vain! Our works are a byproduct of our salvation. Paul obviously had a lot of thought towards this idea of the saving knowledge of grace through faith because he mentions it over and over again in other epistles."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast.","ref":"Ephesians 2:8-9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul tells us that no works can save us. And even if they could, that would mean that Christ is meaningless, and His death for us amounts to nothing!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I do not frustrate the grace of God: for if righteousness come by the law, then Christ is dead in vain.","ref":"Galatians 2:21"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, there are no good works that can save us. This has been a no-brainer since the beginning. Even look to Isaiah…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But we are all as an unclean thing, and all our righteousnesses are as filthy rags; and we all do fade as a leaf; and our iniquities, like the wind, have taken us away.","ref":"Isaiah 64:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Or let’s look at all the faiths of past heroes which we read about in the book of Hebrews…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"By faith Abel…By faith Enoch…By faith Noah…By faith Abraham…","ref":"Hebrews 11:4;5;7;8"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think we get the picture. Let’s bring this down to an even more important level. Our works can’t even please God unless we have faith."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But without faith it is impossible to please him: for he that cometh to God must believe that he is, and that he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him.","ref":"Hebrews 11:6 3 And being brought on their way by the church, they passed through Phenice and Samaria, declaring the conversion of the Gentiles: and they caused great joy unto all the brethren. 4 And when they were come to Jerusalem, they were received of the church, and of the apostles and elders, and they declared all things that God had done with them. 5 But there rose up certain of the sect of the Pharisees which believed, saying, That it was needful to circumcise them, and to command them to keep the law of Moses."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Pharisees that rose up and made this question relevant again were more than likely the same ones who brought it up in verse 2. They are kind of killing the mood and sharing of testimony by Paul and Barnabas, though this was something that they knew needed to be addressed by the most respected representatives of the church at the time, which were the Apostles (though Paul himself was an Apostle, they still didn’t quite trust his word yet. Much growing was obviously still required). 6 And the apostles and elders came together for to consider of this matter. 7 And when there had been much disputing, Peter rose up, and said unto them, Men and brethren, ye know how that a good while ago God made choice among us, that the Gentiles by my mouth should hear the word of the gospel, and believe. 8 And God, which knoweth the hearts, bare them witness, giving them the Holy Ghost, even as he did unto us; 9 And put no difference between us and them, purifying their hearts by faith. 10 Now therefore why tempt ye God, to put a yoke upon the neck of the disciples, which neither our fathers nor we were able to bear? 11 But we believe that through the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ we shall be saved, even as they."},{"kind":"para","text":"In other words, the traditions of our past are not our saving grace. If it were so, then the Gentiles themselves wouldn’t be capable of receiving salvation, as well as many others in the world. The Jewish tradition was not the end-all, be-all of salvation, but Jesus Christ’s grace on the cross is the answer and solution to whom salvation belongs to. Circumcised, uncircumcised… it didn’t matter, as long as they knew Jesus Christ personally through faith. 12 Then all the multitude kept silence, and gave audience to Barnabas and Paul, declaring what miracles and wonders God had wrought among the Gentiles by them."},{"kind":"para","text":"The fact that Paul and Barnabas were able to preach the gospel to everyone around them, no matter their background, was proof enough of how salvation was obtained, and who it belonged to. God was preparing Paul and Barnabas for this question far before the question was even asked. This is how God works. He prepares His children before we end up in a situation we never saw coming, and we can then look back and say, “Oh… THAT’S why I had to deal with that.” Or “THAT’S why I had to go through that trial, or deal with this negative spirit or person…” God’s constantly preparing us, and we need to be sensitive to that so we are ready for when the real trial hits. 13 And after they had held their peace, James answered, saying, Men and brethren, hearken unto me: 14 Simeon hath declared how God at the first did visit the Gentiles, to take out of them a people for his name. 15 And to this agree the words of the prophets; as it is written, 16 After this I will return, and will build again the tabernacle of David, which is fallen down; and I will build again the ruins thereof, and I will set it up: 17 That the residue of men might seek after the Lord, and all the Gentiles, upon whom my name is called, saith the Lord, who doeth all these things. 18 Known unto God are all his works from the beginning of the world."},{"kind":"para","text":"James (Jesus’ brother) appeals to the Old Testament text rather than just making up some sort of new idea of what God could really mean. He connected the dots between salvation to the Gentiles now and what God had said before in the Old Testament. God promised to give salvation to the Jews; much of the Old Testament proves this, but He also specifically speaks about the Gentiles and their salvation as well."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In that day will I raise up the tabernacle of David that is fallen, and close up the breaches thereof; and I will raise up his ruins, and I will build it as in the days of old: That they may possess the remnant of Edom, and of all the heathen, which are called by my name, saith the LORD that doeth this.","ref":"Amos 9:11-12"},{"kind":"para","text":"This not only shows that God made a promise to “all the nations,” referring to even the Gentiles, but also shows that it was through prophecy. James is referring to “the prophets agreeing” on the salvation message to be introduced to everyone, no matter their state of circumcision. 19 Wherefore my sentence is, that we trouble not them, which from among the Gentiles are turned to God: 20 But that we write unto them, that they abstain from pollutions of idols, and from fornication, and from things strangled, and from blood. 21 For Moses of old time hath in every city them that preach him, being read in the synagogues every sabbath day."},{"kind":"para","text":"Ritual laws are not the focus of the gospel or of Christ’s purpose to us. James and Peter agree with one another that the main thing they should be focusing on is growing in faith and being more like Christ in all modes of conduct, such as not being sexually immoral, worshipping false idols, or eating or drinking blood from animals, or blood mixed with their meat to eat. Common practices that are not to be encountered by literally ANYBODY on the face of the Earth, especially those that are called to be followers of Christ. This is just common sense practice and has nothing to do with earning salvation."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":35,"title":"The Council’s Letter","entries":[{"start":22,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A simple letter, clarifying the issue, and resolving it quickly. I can’t imagine a single Gentile Christian reading this letter and thinking, “AWE MAN! They said we can’t drink blood anymore, or worship false gods, or live in sexual immorality, otherwise they won’t accept our salvation…” Honestly, this should be common knowledge, but if these things are appealing to people who claim to know Christ, they certainly do not know the Savior."},{"kind":"para","text":"This letter is also written in a way that gives the impression that they are not under the same conviction as these religionists with their circumcision debate, nor do they want any part of this ridiculous idea of works salvation. May this also be a direct connection to the verse used earlier by James in the epistle that he writes about showing your work BECAUSE of your faith, not earning faith through works. 30 So when they were dismissed, they came to Antioch: and when they had gathered the multitude together, they delivered the epistle: 31 Which when they had read, they rejoiced for the consolation. 32 And Judas and Silas, being prophets also themselves, exhorted the brethren with many words, and confirmed them. 33 And after they had tarried there a space, they were let go in peace from the brethren unto the apostles. 34 Notwithstanding it pleased Silas to abide there still. 35 Paul also and Barnabas continued in Antioch, teaching and preaching the word of the Lord, with many others also."},{"kind":"para","text":"They handed them the letter, and they just approved of everything it said. The Gentiles rejoice; the dissension is gone, and they can move on with their lives. In fact, what else can go wrong from here, right?…"}]}]},{"start":36,"end":41,"title":"Paul and Barnabas Separate","entries":[{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is encouraging. It’s a good idea to go to the places you once helped establish and continue to encourage them. This should be the mindset of each Christian, honestly. Some places may not want to welcome you with open arms, but other places desire the encouragement and the direction from established and growing Christians. This was surely a great idea, and this should have gone off without a hitch, and joy just to be able to visit those old churches in love and kindness… But nothing is ever that simple, because we are humans and we are flawed… 37 And Barnabas determined to take with them John, whose surname was Mark. 38 But Paul thought not good to take him with them, who departed from them from Pamphylia, and went not with them to the work. 39 And the contention was so sharp between them, that they departed asunder one from the other: and so Barnabas took Mark, and sailed unto Cyprus; 40 And Paul chose Silas, and departed, being recommended by the brethren unto the grace of God. 41 And he went through Syria and Cilicia, confirming the churches."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t want this part to get lost here. I 100% disagree with Paul’s thought process in this matter. I think Barnabas was right. Paul is mistreating Mark simply because God called Mark to venture into something else. If Paul had an issue with that, he should have spoken to Mark about it before he left. This is also not to mention that Mark didn’t even ask to go; he was just suggested by Barnabas because Barnabas wanted Mark to see all the amazing things God had done since Mark left."},{"kind":"para","text":"This division is not one to be taken lightly, and it’s not one that can just be glanced at and forgotten about. The Bible doesn’t really amend this relationship, and that’s really sad to me. I do believe Paul was a prideful person, especially at this point, and he needed to be brought down a few notches (which he will, and you see that through his other letters; his grace grows, and his arrogance shrinks, but it doesn’t really happen until he sets up Timothy, and at that point, he was close to death). I want us to notice something about this interesting dynamic. The relationship between Paul and Barnabas is not really restored again, but the relationship between Paul and Mark is…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Only Luke is with me. Take Mark, and bring him with thee: for he is profitable to me for the ministry.","ref":"2 Timothy 4:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"There was some growth and learning that takes place somewhere along the lines, and this is important to US to note because there may be a time when we don’t agree with others on specific issues (sometimes big, sometimes small), but there should always be room to make amends at some point so that we can be profitable to one another for the ministry. May I also remind you, when Paul says this to Timothy, he’s in a prison cell, speaking to Luke, the writer of Acts, who is more than likely taking down the accounts of this current chapter we are on, remembering the stupid, menial, and small issues that could easily be taken care of if we are walking the right Spirit."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should not care who gets the credit for the work, we should just care that the work is being done. With that said, Christians should be giving praise to those that deserve it, but we should never expect it, nor should we complain if others don’t help. Honestly, it’s through others not getting involved that God often shows Himself mightier. We should take that opportunity when God shows Himself through these areas of victory, to rejoice with the brothers in Christ even if they weren’t involved with helping you out."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":22,"end":29},{"start":36,"end":36}],"verses":{"1":"And certain men which came down from Judaea taught the brethren, and said, Except ye be circumcised after the manner of Moses, ye cannot be saved.","2":"When therefore Paul and Barnabas had no small dissension and disputation with them, they determined that Paul and Barnabas, and certain other of them, should go up to Jerusalem unto the apostles and elders about this question.","22":"Then pleased it the apostles and elders with the whole church, to send chosen men of their own company to Antioch with Paul and Barnabas; namely, Judas surnamed Barsabas and Silas, chief men among the brethren:","23":"And they wrote letters by them after this manner; The apostles and elders and brethren send greeting unto the brethren which are of the Gentiles in Antioch and Syria and Cilicia.","24":"Forasmuch as we have heard, that certain which went out from us have troubled you with words, subverting your souls, saying, Ye must be circumcised, and keep the law: to whom we gave no such commandment:","25":"It seemed good unto us, being assembled with one accord, to send chosen men unto you with our beloved Barnabas and Paul,","26":"Men that have hazarded their lives for the name of our Lord Jesus Christ.","27":"We have sent therefore Judas and Silas, who shall also tell you the same things by mouth.","28":"For it seemed good to the Holy Ghost, and to us, to lay upon you no greater burden than these necessary things;","29":"That ye abstain from meats offered to idols, and from blood, and from things strangled, and from fornication: from which if ye keep yourselves, ye shall do well. Fare ye well.","36":"And some days after Paul said unto Barnabas, Let us go again and visit our brethren in every city where we have preached the word of the Lord, and see how they do."}},
    "16": {"chapter":16,"intro":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"Aristarchus my fellowprisoner saluteth you, and Marcus, sister's son to Barnabas, (touching whom ye received commandments: if he come unto you, receive him;)","ref":"Colossians 4:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Just like Timothy will become to Paul (spoiler alert) Peter refers to Mark as his “Son.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The church that is at Babylon, elected together with you, saluteth you; and so doth Marcus my son.","ref":"1 Peter 5:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"They end up becoming quite upset with one another, and Barnabas decides to just leave with Mark, and Paul joins up with Silas and heads back out to their journey. This disagreement shadows the same style of disagreement with the circumcision in the fact that these men should simply be rejoicing over the idea that so many accepted the message, and they were able to reach even a few."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Paul take no chances (does not tread lightly) when he comes across a young boy who is desirous of the ministry, whose name is Timothy."}],"previously":["Last we saw a debate take place about the necessity of circumcision, which was the Jews’ custom, or just leaving it be simply for the faith of the gospel. Paul and Barnabas agreed that circumcision was not necessary, but they wanted the advice of the first church in Jerusalem to be absolutely sure. Even the first church, after much debate, agreed with Paul and Barnabas. After the issue was settled, they sent a letter to the Gentile believers, letting them know that circumcision was not nearly as important as faith and continuing to abstain from Gentile-like things that went against God and His commands. Later, Paul had the idea to go back to the churches that were started from their journey and see how they were doing. Barnabas wanted to take Mark, and Paul didn’t like that idea. (I think it should be noted (it wasn’t last time) that John Mark could possibly be Barnabas’s cousin.)"],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"Timothy Joins Paul and Silas","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Timothy, his mother, and his grandmother were probably converted during the time of Paul’s stoning, based on the way in which he speaks to Timothy in multiple settings."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When I call to remembrance the unfeigned faith that is in thee, which dwelt first in thy grandmother Lois, and thy mother Eunice; and I am persuaded that in thee also.","ref":"2 Timothy 1:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"Why anybody in their right mind would desire to join the faith that nearly got a man stoned to death is honestly beyond me. But it’s an encouragement to know that there are men and women out there who understand the truth and continuously desire to seek it out. The issue here is, Timothy’s father was a Greek. Having two altering belief systems in the household is a dangerous thing, especially for a young man who is growing up in the faith of the Lord and of Christ."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Be ye not unequally yoked together with unbelievers: for what fellowship hath righteousness with unrighteousness? and what communion hath light with darkness?","ref":"2 Corinthians 6:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"This mother and grandmother had a faith that was more than likely passed down to him, 1 Timothy 1 says. But ultimately, with a father who could not agree in this area, things more than likely were not easy for Timothy, and I have no doubt they wouldn’t get much easier for him in the coming verses… 2 Which was well reported of by the brethren that were at Lystra and Iconium."},{"kind":"para","text":"Not only was Timothy a faithful steward of God’s grace and compassion, he was also “well spoken of” by others who came to know him. This is important to note, because many people put on a good show when it comes to the faith, but don’t actually lead a life that is representative of that."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.","ref":"Matthew 7:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Timothy was a proven Christian, and did whatever it took to follow Christ in faith and belief, as we will see in a moment…"},{"kind":"para","text":"3 Him would Paul have to go forth with him; and took and circumcised him because of the Jews which were in those quarters: for they knew all that his father was a Greek."},{"kind":"para","text":"This could not have been an easy decision to make. No doubt, Paul was still flustered by the fact that he had to debate so many because of this exact issue before. Remember, in the last chapter, Paul said that circumcision was not a necessity in the faith. So why circumcise Timothy when it wasn’t necessary? Wouldn’t that just prove his case more if he were just left uncircumcised?"},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe Paul was just making it easier on Timothy’s testimony when he goes to preach to others. Timothy could get him better access to the synagogues (which is seemingly always Paul’s starting points) because Timothy was a Lystra native. Having an uncircumcised Jew with him would have made any witness to Jews much more difficult. And Timothy was, strangely enough, accepting for the cause. How much of a fight was put up by Timothy is not known. It seems that Timothy was just willing to do whatever necessary as long as the gospel could be reached to more and more people. Especially when it included his family, and friends in whom he grew up with in this area who needed to know Christ!"},{"kind":"para","text":"I like this about Timothy. He wasn’t just faithful to Christ, he was faithful to Christ’s cause, no matter what that called for. Some of us have a hard time telling others about Christ, and we aren’t even commanded to do anything quite as drastic as being circumcised at an older age first. I wonder if it would have even mattered that this happened to Timothy? Was anybody going to actually check? 4 And as they went through the cities, they delivered them the decrees for to keep, that were ordained of the apostles and elders which were at Jerusalem. 5 And so were the churches established in the faith, and increased in number daily."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was important that Paul speak about the decisions being made by the opening church, because it gave a pattern to follow for all the new churches that were starting to be established everywhere else that Paul and Barnabas were preaching to. It helped them know who to reach, how to reach them, and what the important structures were to keep for that community. And as a byproduct of that, people were being added to each church, and were growing because they weren’t so much focused on how to keep their religion, but how to include people into the simple understanding of the faith of God."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":10,"title":"The Macedonian Call","entries":[{"start":6,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul starts his travels through Asia, but is prevented from preaching in certain places. Why he was forbidden to preach in these areas, I’m not exactly sure. It doesn’t really tell us, and there doesn’t seem to be much commentary as to why. My assumption is, for some reason, God wanted the gospel to be spread in Macedonia first. Asia would be a place that would eventually receive the gospel and grow churches, but now didn’t seem like the time."},{"kind":"para","text":"How the Holy Spirit prevented Paul was also a mystery. It’s possible that Paul didn’t have a great feeling about going in that direction, or it is possible that the roads and way to these areas were blocked or difficult to travel. With that said, God makes it plainly obvious in the coming verses where Paul is supposed to go… 8 And they passing by Mysia came down to Troas. 9 And a vision appeared to Paul in the night; There stood a man of Macedonia, and prayed him, saying, Come over into Macedonia, and help us. 10 And after he had seen the vision, immediately we endeavoured to go into Macedonia, assuredly gathering that the Lord had called us for to preach the gospel unto them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Luke says, “We” here, as if to insinuate that he has now joined them on their trip at some point. It seems he joined with Paul, and Silas is not mentioned. Either way, it seems that God desired that Paul go to Macedonia to “help them” with something. What they needed help with was not exactly sure yet."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":15,"title":"The Conversion of Lydia","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Samothracia was an island on the direct route between Troas and Neapolis. It was the port for Philippi, which lay eight and a half miles inland. Paul’s ship would likely have stopped (probably simply to anchor offshore for the night) on the north side of Samothracia. Neapolis was on a natural harbor still in use today. 12 And from thence to Philippi, which is the chief city of that part of Macedonia, and a colony: and we were in that city abiding certain days."},{"kind":"para","text":"Philippi was a Roman colony, the most privileged status for provincial cities. In other words, it was a place that was well taken care of, and a lot of their customs (though absurd) made them powerful and terrible. 13 And on the sabbath we went out of the city by a river side, where prayer was wont to be made; and we sat down, and spake unto the women which resorted thither. 14 And a certain woman named Lydia, a seller of purple, of the city of Thyatira, which worshipped God, heard us: whose heart the Lord opened, that she attended unto the things which were spoken of Paul. 15 And when she was baptized, and her household, she besought us, saying, If ye have judged me to be faithful to the Lord, come into my house, and abide there. And she constrained us."},{"kind":"para","text":"There were not many Jews who lived in Philippi, so it’s assumed that these women who were attending this “prayer meeting” were more than likely “God-fearers” and not Jews. Much of religion is as such today. People do not fear God; they are just afraid of Him. They believe in a higher being, but they do not think He is kind, gracious, or forgiving. They view Him as a judge (as He so is) and as a law-giver (as He so says), and they don’t believe we can ever have a relationship with this God unless we are able to sacrifice some sin and do something good."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, Paul knew better, and his most important calling and direction were to share the love of God with them through what Jesus did for them on the cross. Lydia’s household will become the prominent gathering place for Christians, as verse 40 will suggest."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":24,"title":"Paul and Silas in Prison","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This lady was a “fortune teller.” She didn’t seem like she wanted to do it, but it was providing money for her family, and how was she supposed to get rid of this awful demon who told her all these things? This demon was gathering secrets about other people’s lives and then sharing them for money. The Old Testament forbids fortune telling of any kind."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"There shall not be found among you any one that maketh his son or his daughter to pass through the fire, or that useth divination, or an observer of times, or an enchanter, or a witch, Or a charmer, or a consulter with familiar spirits, or a wizard, or a necromancer.","ref":"Deuteronomy 18:10-11"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they caused their sons and their daughters to pass through the fire, and used divination and enchantments, and sold themselves to do evil in the sight of the LORD, to provoke him to anger.","ref":"2 Kings 17:17 17 The same followed Paul and us, and cried, saying, These men are the servants of the most high God, which shew unto us the way of salvation."},{"kind":"para","text":"Demon’s can know us. They can know us well. They know how to push our buttons. They know how to get under our skins. They know what kind of person we are, and what kind of person we might be pretending to be. They know how strong we are, and how weak we are. Demon’s will always point out the fake Christians. They will also shake at the real ones. 18 And this did she many days. But Paul, being grieved, turned and said to the spirit, I command thee in the name of Jesus Christ to come out of her. And he came out the same hour."},{"kind":"para","text":"Well, that was easy. He probably should have just done this to begin with. Of course, everything has a specific timing, and maybe the timing was just to thin out Paul’s patience, but either way, this demon was easily taken care of. However, now Paul will have to deal with problems of a different sort. 19 And when her masters saw that the hope of their gains was gone, they caught Paul and Silas, and drew them into the marketplace unto the rulers, 20 And brought them to the magistrates, saying, These men, being Jews, do exceedingly trouble our city, 21 And teach customs, which are not lawful for us to receive, neither to observe, being Romans."},{"kind":"para","text":"Greed will always be more valuable to the world than their eternal destination."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For what shall it profit a man, if he shall gain the whole world, and lose his own soul?","ref":"Mark 8:36"},{"kind":"para","text":"The world holds far more value in paper than they do for their own soul. 22 And the multitude rose up together against them: and the magistrates rent off their clothes, and commanded to beat them. 23 And when they had laid many stripes upon them, they cast them into prison, charging the jailor to keep them safely: 24 Who, having received such a charge, thrust them into the inner prison, and made their feet fast in the stocks."},{"kind":"para","text":"This shows how barbaric this culture was. There was no fair hearing, or any kind of organized custom to their ruling. It was simply taking someone’s assumed word and then just beating the person for no reason."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But even after that we had suffered before, and were shamefully entreated, as ye know, at Philippi, we were bold in our God to speak unto you the gospel of God with much contention.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 2:2"}]}]},{"start":25,"end":40,"title":"The Philippians Jailer Converted","entries":[{"start":25,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hey, if our worship doesn’t release some people from their chains, we are not doing worship correctly. Being in love with the Lord enough to sing to him WHILE in prison is a sure testimony of a given people to God. 27 And the keeper of the prison awaking out of his sleep, and seeing the prison doors open, he drew out his sword, and would have killed himself, supposing that the prisoners had been fled. 28 But Paul cried with a loud voice, saying, Do thyself no harm: for we are all here. 29 Then he called for a light, and sprang in, and came trembling, and fell down before Paul and Silas,"},{"kind":"para","text":"Not a single prisoner left. They must have been so appreciative of Paul and Silas’s singing to the Lord that they wanted an encore! Or maybe they were converted simply by their singing. Whatever the case was, the jailer was not going to let the Roman magistrate kill him for a prison break, so he was going to take his own life before they could take it."},{"kind":"para","text":"This insinuates that they would have done some pretty heinous things to him if they had caught him, and that was to say that the prisoners didn’t harm him first. But the prisoners didn’t budge. They simply waited. If anyone had a right to escape at this moment, it would have been Paul and Silas, but THEY didn’t even leave. Paul may not have known why they didn’t run at the moment, but he knew there was a chance to continue prison ministry, and so he brings this jailer to the Lord. 30 And brought them out, and said, Sirs, what must I do to be saved? 31 And they said, Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and thou shalt be saved, and thy house."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is much debate about the salvation of the jailer’s family. Some have said that Paul granted even his family salvation, and they weren’t even there. Others claim that Paul already knew that his family would be saved if this jailer was saved."},{"kind":"para","text":"I am under the impression that if one from a family comes to know Christ, the chances are greatly heightened that the rest will too, especially if it’s the man of the house. They tend to set the course for the rest of the family’s spiritual countenance. The men are referred to as “Spiritual leaders” for a reason. Either way, the debate is stupid, and the debate gets more ridiculous from here…"},{"kind":"para","text":"32 And they spake unto him the word of the Lord, and to all that were in his house. 33 And he took them the same hour of the night, and washed their stripes; and was baptized, he and all his, straightway."},{"kind":"para","text":"Some are under the impression that there were infants in this man’s family who were baptized as well. Even though verse 32 specifically speaks about preaching God’s Word to them, and the common notion every single time there is baptism mentioned is that the people understood God’s Word before being baptized, people still have a hard time believing that Paul and Silas simply baptized those who trusted in God’s Word alone for salvation."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, the jailer was so excited about this message that he gathered his entire family for salvation. That’s an amazing testimony to be mentioned for all the generations after, even being written down in God’s Word. 34 And when he had brought them into his house, he set meat before them, and rejoiced, believing in God with all his house."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s a cause to rejoice when people come to know the Lord. It’s not a somber occasion, but one MADE for rejoicing and being in awe!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In that hour Jesus rejoiced in spirit, and said, I thank thee, O Father, Lord of heaven and earth, that thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them unto babes: even so, Father; for so it seemed good in thy sight.","ref":"Luke 10:21 35 And when it was day, the magistrates sent the serjeants, saying, Let those men go. 36 And the keeper of the prison told this saying to Paul, The magistrates have sent to let you go: now therefore depart, and go in peace. 37 But Paul said unto them, They have beaten us openly uncondemned, being Romans, and have cast us into prison; and now do they thrust us out privily? nay verily; but let them come themselves and fetch us out. 38 And the serjeants told these words unto the magistrates: and they feared, when they heard that they were Romans."},{"kind":"para","text":"Whoops. This is why a trial and hearing are necessary. They jailed their own! They would have known that had they just asked and listened. Now they have the humble responsibility to admit their fault and hopefully learn from this unjust occasion. 39 And they came and besought them, and brought them out, and desired them to depart out of the city."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is so funny to me. “We’re sorry for what we did. In good conscience, we ask you now to leave the city so that no one else finds out about this mistake we have just made, and so that we aren’t condemned for beating and jailing our own…” 40 And they went out of the prison, and entered into the house of Lydia: and when they had seen the brethren, they comforted them, and departed."},{"kind":"para","text":"They left the city, but only after they strengthened the faith of the people there. They finished what they entered the city to start with. They preached the gospel to those that needed it. That was the help that was needed in Paul’s vision. They started the growth of the gospel to the Romans, and it all grew out of a simple prayer warrior and a Roman jailer."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":6,"end":7},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":25,"end":26}],"verses":{"1":"Then came he to Derbe and Lystra: and, behold, a certain disciple was there, named Timotheus, the son of a certain woman, which was a Jewess, and believed; but his father was a Greek:","6":"Now when they had gone throughout Phrygia and the region of Galatia, and were forbidden of the Holy Ghost to preach the word in Asia,","7":"After they were come to Mysia, they assayed to go into Bithynia: but the Spirit suffered them not.","11":"Therefore loosing from Troas, we came with a straight course to Samothracia, and the next day to Neapolis;","16":"And it came to pass, as we went to prayer, a certain damsel possessed with a spirit of divination met us, which brought her masters much gain by soothsaying:","25":"And at midnight Paul and Silas prayed, and sang praises unto God: and the prisoners heard them.","26":"And suddenly there was a great earthquake, so that the foundations of the prison were shaken: and immediately all the doors were opened, and every one's bands were loosed."}},
    "17": {"chapter":17,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We saw the introduction of Timothy into Paul’s life, and the call to preach to the Macedonians. After they had found a place to meet for prayer, a woman named Lydia is converted to the Christian faith, and Paul and Silas venture to teach others also until they are met with a demon possessed woman who could tell people their fortunes (really it was just the demon who knew about the nature of people). After, apparently, annoying Paul, Paul rebukes the demon and the lady is freed from this control, which upsets those that were in charge of her because she was making them money. Paul and Silas are beaten, and thrown into prison without a trial, and they use the opportunity to pray and sing hymns out loud."},{"kind":"para","text":"An Earthquake takes place, and all the prisoners chains are loosed, and the doors are open but no body makes a move. This ultimately leads to the salvation of the jailer and his family, and eventually Paul and Silas are let out of prison which causes the jailers to realize they were Roman citizens. Paul visits Lydia (to find more followers visiting with her) to encourage them and then leave to the next place."},{"kind":"para","text":"This chapter picks up with them continuing their missionary journey and preaching the gospel to more areas that will become prominent churches."}],"previously":["We witnessed the introduction of Timothy into Paul’s life and the call to preach to the Macedonians. After finding a place to meet for prayer, a woman named Lydia was converted to the Christian faith. Paul and Silas then ventured to teach others until they encountered a demon-possessed woman who could tell people their fortunes. In reality, it was the demon that knew about the nature of people. After apparently annoying Paul, he rebuked the demon, and the lady was freed from its control. This upset those in charge of her because she was making them money. As a result, Paul and Silas were beaten and thrown into prison without a trial. They used this opportunity to pray and sing hymns out loud. An earthquake took place, and all the prisoners’ chains were loosed, and the doors were opened, but nobody made a move. This ultimately led to the salvation of the jailer and his family. Eventually, Paul and Silas were let out of prison, which caused the jailers to realize they were Roman citizens. Paul visited Lydia to find more followers visiting with her, encouraged them, and then left for the next place. This chapter picks up with them continuing their missionary journey and preaching the gospel to more areas that would become prominent churches."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":9,"title":"Paul and Silas in Thessalonica","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What I particularly like about the places in which Paul is visiting and starting churches in Acts is not so much the fact that churches are being started, but that they were provenly being established. Paul didn’t just start a church and hope it thrived without ever visiting it again. He visited them as often as he could and then wrote letters signifying the current spiritual growth and culture of those churches, proving their establishment as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"The church in this first part that will be established is in Thessalonica. To get a better picture of how the church was focused and growing, we can look at the letters that Paul writes to the churches here in Thessalonica by reading 1 and 2 Thessalonians. Philip, Amphipolis, Apollonia, and Thessalonica were all cities on the main east-to-west Roman Highway called the “Egnatian Way.” These cities were separated from each other by about a day’s journey on foot. It’s suffice to say it took Paul and Silas a few days to get to Thessalonica. Once again, the draw for Paul to preach and teach was the presence of a synagogue. He knew that the most religious of the bunch would be gathering to do their religious things here. 2 And Paul, as his manner was, went in unto them, and three sabbath days reasoned with them out of the scriptures,"},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, I believe Paul and Silas stayed longer than just three weeks. He preached once a week for three weeks, but his labor was daily, and possibly longer because of his letters to this city."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For ye remember, brethren, our labour and travail: for labouring night and day, because we would not be chargeable unto any of you, we preached unto you the gospel of God.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 2:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"We cannot expect people to grow by only attending one meeting a week or one service. Preaching God’s Word is not a once-in-a-while task; it’s a daily obligation. If God has called you to preach, you would be well off not to neglect it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I read in a book about “The Preacher’s Creed,” and I’ve stuck by it since being called into ministry. When I would be asked to preach at the last minute, I commissioned myself to NEVER say no because the creed was “Preach, or Die.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"3 Opening and alleging, that Christ must needs have suffered, and risen again from the dead; and that this Jesus, whom I preach unto you, is Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s funny that the Jews were against this idea that the Messiah (the Christ, the Savior of the world) would have to suffer and die, because the Old Testament (which they believed in so strongly) literally attests to this having to be the case!"},{"kind":"para","text":"“My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me? why art thou so far from helping me, and from the words of my roaring? O my God, I cry in the daytime, but thou hearest not; and in the night season, and am not silent. But thou art holy, O thou that inhabitest the praises of Israel. Our fathers trusted in thee: they trusted, and thou didst deliver them. They cried unto thee, and were delivered: they trusted in thee, and were not confounded. But I am a worm, and no man; a reproach of men, and despised of the people. All they that see me laugh me to scorn: they shoot out the lip, they shake the head, saying, He trusted on the LORD that he would deliver him: let him deliver him, seeing he delighted in him. But thou art he that took me out of the womb: thou didst make me hope when I was upon my mother's breasts. I was cast upon thee from the womb: thou art my God from my mother's belly. Be not far from me; for trouble is near; for there is none to help. Many bulls have compassed me: strong bulls of Bashan have beset me round. They gaped upon me with their mouths, as a ravening and a roaring lion. I am poured out like water, and all my bones are out of joint: my heart is like wax; it is melted in the midst of my bowels. My strength is dried up like a potsherd; and my tongue cleaveth to my jaws; and thou hast brought me into the dust of death. For dogs have compassed me: the assembly of the wicked have inclosed me: they pierced my hands and my feet. I may tell all my bones: they look and stare upon me. They part my garments among them, and cast lots upon my vesture. But be not thou far from me, O LORD: O my strength, haste thee to help me. Deliver my soul from the sword; my darling from the power of the dog. Save me from the lion's mouth: for thou hast heard me from the horns of the unicorns. I will declare thy name unto my brethren: in the midst of the congregation will I praise thee. Ye that fear the LORD, praise him; all ye the seed of Jacob, glorify him; and fear him, all ye the seed of Israel. For he hath not despised nor abhorred the affliction of the afflicted; neither hath he hid his face from him; but when he cried unto him, he heard. My praise shall be of thee in the great congregation: I will pay my vows before them that fear him. The meek shall eat and be satisfied: they shall praise the LORD that seek him: your heart shall live for ever. All the ends of the world shall remember and turn unto the LORD: and all the kindreds of the nations shall worship before thee. For the kingdom is the LORD’s: and he is the governor among the nations. All they that be fat upon earth shall eat and worship: all they that go down to the dust shall bow before him: and none can keep alive his own soul. A seed shall serve him; it shall be accounted to the Lord for a generation. They shall come, and shall declare his righteousness unto a people that shall be born, that he hath done this.” Psalm 22"},{"kind":"para","text":"“Who hath believed our report? and to whom is the arm of the LORD revealed? For he shall grow up before him as a tender plant, and as a root out of a dry ground: he hath no form nor comeliness; and when we shall see him, there is no beauty that we should desire him. He is despised and rejected of men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief: and we hid as it were our faces from him; he was despised, and we esteemed him not. Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows: yet we did esteem him stricken, smitten of God, and afflicted. But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed. All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and the LORD hath laid on him the iniquity of us all. He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth: he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his mouth. He was taken from prison and from judgment: and who shall declare his generation? for he was cut off out of the land of the living: for the transgression of my people was he stricken. And he made his grave with the wicked, and with the rich in his death; because he had done no violence, neither was any deceit in his mouth. Yet it pleased the LORD to bruise him; he hath put him to grief: when thou shalt make his soul an offering for sin, he shall see his seed, he shall prolong his days, and the pleasure of the LORD shall prosper in his hand. He shall see of the travail of his soul, and shall be satisfied: by his knowledge shall my righteous servant justify many; for he shall bear their iniquities. Therefore will I divide him a portion with the great, and he shall divide the spoil with the strong; because he hath poured out his soul unto death: and he was numbered with the transgressors; and he bare the sin of many, and made intercession for the transgressors.” Isaiah 53"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I will pour upon the house of David, and upon the inhabitants of Jerusalem, the spirit of grace and of supplications: and they shall look upon me whom they have pierced, and they shall mourn for him, as one mourneth for his only son, and shall be in bitterness for him, as one that is in bitterness for his firstborn.","ref":"Zechariah 12:10"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Awake, O sword, against my shepherd, and against the man that is my fellow, saith the LORD of hosts: smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered: and I will turn mine hand upon the little ones.","ref":"Zechariah 13:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Just to name a few… 4 And some of them believed, and consorted with Paul and Silas; and of the devout Greeks a great multitude, and of the chief women not a few."},{"kind":"para","text":"I will not entertain the idea and debate of the Bible being sexist, nor will I ascribe to the pridefulness of those who believe that the world constantly puts a cloak over women, forcing them to be something they are not. The Greco-Roman world proved that women held prominent positions quite often, and Paul even points out the authority of some women in later letters."},{"kind":"para","text":"There’s a balance that must be met when it comes to church order, but it should never be refuted as to whether or not women play a prominent role in authority or in the church. It should be biblically traced and encouraged to be in biblical roles that are provenly tested and realized so that we do not go against the order in which God has given to us."},{"kind":"para","text":"5 But the Jews which believed not, moved with envy, took unto them certain lewd fellows of the baser sort, and gathered a company, and set all the city on an uproar, and assaulted the house of Jason, and sought to bring them out to the people."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were jealous because their traditions and religions were being attacked and proven wrong. It’s the same reason people get upset and defensive today. People don’t like to have light shined on their falsities, especially when it means they have to change."},{"kind":"para","text":"Most people say that you can’t change people, and those that say this are people that haven’t changed. In all honesty, the world can only be changed by those who aren’t changed by the world, but rather by God. Real change stems from a faith and walk in Christ who “transforms” us into something new."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And be not conformed to this world: but be ye transformed by the renewing of your mind, that ye may prove what is that good, and acceptable, and perfect, will of God.","ref":"Romans 12:2 6 And when they found them not, they drew Jason and certain brethren unto the rulers of the city, crying, These that have turned the world upside down are come hither also; 7 Whom Jason hath received: and these all do contrary to the decrees of Caesar, saying that there is another king, one Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"They didn’t just change the world, they turned it upside-down and completely threw them for a loop. They weren’t expecting to be proven wrong. They weren’t expecting to be changed. They wanted to do whatever they possibly could to keep real, genuine, and necessary change from taking place in their lives so they could hold on to their traditions and their idols of fake gods that make them feel good about their lifestyle and sin. That’s really what it boils down to."},{"kind":"para","text":"A great many people are so stuck in their ways of thinking and doing that they suffer more from idolatry than they do from any other sin. Idolatry gives people comfort. They say, “Why would a righteous, loving, and justifiable God send anyone to Hell? I can’t believe in a God that would allow something like that to happen…” When they say this, they are ultimately saying, “I want a god that makes me feel good about myself, not one that can change me for His standards and glory.” Which is the ultimate form of idolatry."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we take away the righteousness of God to meet our criteria of value, we don’t give ourselves a worthy God, we give ourselves a cheap God who can be bought out by our warped idea of what is best for the rest of the world. The biblical God has standards, and it would be sad and miserable for us to lower God’s standards to ours so that we feel accepted, rather than just allowing God’s standards to be unreachable and rely on His authority and provision to help us reach Him. 8 And they troubled the people and the rulers of the city, when they heard these things. 9 And when they had taken security of Jason, and of the other, they let them go."},{"kind":"para","text":"They had to bribe the authorities in this matter so that nothing more permanent or life-taking would happen to them. It was insurance, and this was more than likely what would cause Paul and Silas to have to leave Thessalonica. This may also be what Paul talks about in his letter to them."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore we would have come unto you, even I Paul, once and again; but Satan hindered us.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 2:18"}]}]},{"start":10,"end":15,"title":"Paul and Silas in Berea","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul and Silas travelled fifty miles to the next city on foot from Thessalonica. Instead of taking a small break and trying to gather their thoughts and actions, they went straight (as per tradition at this point) to the nearest Jewish synagogue. Can anyone guess why? 11 These were more noble than those in Thessalonica, in that they received the word with all readiness of mind, and searched the scriptures daily, whether those things were so. 12 Therefore many of them believed; also of honourable women which were Greeks, and of men, not a few."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is nothing more satisfying to a church or leader who can give the word of God and not be met with hostility and rejection, but rather an openness to being proven wrong, and then looking into it to make sure it is correct. All too often, we come with a shield ready to block the advice, ideas, or the counsel of other Christians, instead of having an open heart, and a Spirit that is ready to learn and search the scriptures out personally."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we come with the attitude of pride, we will leave everyone with a worse spirit. If we come with the attitude of humility, every person will learn something new that they can use in their own lives, and be built up with one another around them. That’s the type of spirit we should be striving for in every meeting and Bible study we attend."},{"kind":"para","text":"It should never be a challenge of wits and Bible knowledge, but rather a spirit of necessity that we learn, grow, and edify one another. How can you edify someone who doesn’t agree with anything you say? How can you encourage someone who is too prideful to listen to the scripture? It’s impossible to build up someone who is always set out to challenge you. 13 But when the Jews of Thessalonica had knowledge that the word of God was preached of Paul at Berea, they came thither also, and stirred up the people."},{"kind":"para","text":"This irritates me so much. It irritates me because I know that Paul is just trying to follow the Lord, and these Jews have nothing better to do with their time but try and cause divisions and hurt people. It irritates me because it’s something that happens today. People are enjoying the Bible studies at their church, and either a neighbor in the building next to the church, an authority or official of the state or county that works around your neighborhood, people from a different church, or even sometimes your very own pastors or preachers come in and ruin it for everyone. I’ve seen pastors of churches put a stop to Bible studies because they were growing more than the church…"},{"kind":"para","text":"I saw a video of a street preacher giving the gospel to a young man who said, “If God is real, then He should give me a sign right now!” The wind blew a poster that was nearby and it swung and hit this young man in the face."},{"kind":"para","text":"These people weren’t even seeking a sign though. They were seeking to just cause havoc and division. They didn’t want to actually learn. They just wanted to press their own ideas on everyone else. There was no civil discourse. There was no openness to truth. There wasn’t even any conversation. It was just about them making sure that the gospel could not be preached, and that those that preached it would be hurt."},{"kind":"para","text":"14 And then immediately the brethren sent away Paul to go as it were to the sea: but Silas and Timotheus abode there still. 15 And they that conducted Paul brought him unto Athens: and receiving a commandment unto Silas and Timotheus for to come to him with all speed, they departed."},{"kind":"para","text":"This shows that the real person they couldn’t stand the most was Paul, specifically. Paul may have been more outspoken and bold compared to his helpers, Silas and Timothy. Silas and Timothy may have had a more gentle approach to sharing the gospel, whereas Paul was just brutally honest. There are times and necessities for both types, and this crowd apparently needed the ministry style of Timothy and Silas, while Paul needed to use his ministry style to a people that would be able to handle it a little easier. Honestly, this is where things get interesting, and Luke gives an interesting correlation between the groups."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":21,"title":"Paul in Athens","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In verse 13, we see the people being stirred up to get after Paul and attack him for the things he was saying. In this verse, we see Paul being stirred up to preach the gospel. Both are stirring, only one is stirred correctly."},{"kind":"para","text":"I made dinner with a friend one evening when I was in high school. We made some sort of rice dish, and we added a bouillon cube to bring in more flavor. We cooked the dish, stirring the cube for as long as needed, and then served it. While we were eating, I put a spoonful of this rice into my mouth and got a quick, sickly taste and feeling. I spit it out. Confused, we both looked at the dish, and the bouillon cube did not evaporate! It didn’t get evenly stirred into the dish, and the whole meal was ruined. I almost threw up from the amount of flavor that packed (we later found out the cubes were four years expired, and that might have been the reason why it didn’t dissolve)."},{"kind":"para","text":"The sin of the world should stir us up enough to where we can’t sit still for very long without telling someone about the gospel. We should not be quite as stirred up to tear down Christian preachers and be far more stirred up to preach the gospel to every creature. We MUST take note of this in Paul’s life and mimic it in ours. May the lost nature of man stir us up correctly so we can stir others up for the sake of the gospel. 17 Therefore disputed he in the synagogue with the Jews, and with the devout persons, and in the market daily with them that met with him. 18 Then certain philosophers of the Epicureans, and of the Stoicks, encountered him. And some said, What will this babbler say? other some, He seemeth to be a setter forth of strange gods: because he preached unto them Jesus, and the resurrection."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul didn’t do any sort of miracle in front of them. He didn’t even have people really helping him out for this preaching work. He did this on his own, and was successful, verse 34 tells us. This gives homage to the fact that we can easily do the same if the Spirit is stirred in you. Don’t wait for your church to do it. Don’t wait for someone else to help you. If God has a calling for you, and you know you must do it, start doing it and allow God to increase it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve seen it happen too many times, where someone is stirred to open-air preach, or someone is stirred to minister, or someone is stirred to start a specific ministry, and they wait on others, or they try and get permission from their church and they are rejected or neglected, and the time passes and it becomes nearly impossible to do it anymore and all it does is ruin the spirit of the person that knows they have a calling. Listen to God’s calling, and do what He commands as quickly as possible. We CANNOT afford to say no to the Lord because no one else has the same calling as you. You’re stirred up NOW, don’t wait for the stirring to disappear… 19 And they took him, and brought him unto Areopagus, saying, May we know what this new doctrine, whereof thou speakest, is? 20 For thou bringest certain strange things to our ears: we would know therefore what these things mean."},{"kind":"para","text":"The “Areopagus” is the “hill of Ares” (which is the Greek God of war). It’s also referred to as “Mars Hill.” The court here was the highest authority and influence as to what could be followed civilly and religiously in their land. At least they didn’t just up and stone Paul this time. They were actually concerned, and they wanted to help with the things that were being said. Granted, they brought these sayings before a group who hadn’t heard of this yet, so they were also weak to provide evidence, but it gave Paul an opening to preach to higher authority people, and if Paul could convince these men in charge, they would make a strong work for the Lord for everyone else. 21 (For all the Athenians and strangers which were there spent their time in nothing else, but either to tell, or to hear some new thing.)"},{"kind":"para","text":"Many are busy in the most useless things. To these people, wisdom and knowledge were just a case study. They were seeking after it, not to hold onto and increase in, but rather to say they know it and move onto something new. They had knowledge without understanding."},{"kind":"para","text":"Many atheists have a knowledge of the Bible, but they do not understand it. They know the stories, they know the people, they may even be able to quote verses and texts better than most Christians, but they have no idea what it means and how it’s important to their lives. These people here are the same way."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":34,"title":"Paul Addresses the Areopagus","entries":[{"start":22,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is such a good study of preaching. Paul tells people the gospel, and not one time uses a Bible verse in this presentation of it. Instead, he uses those things which the people understood and knew. This is important to note. The best way to win others over is not to just present them with the truth (though this is important) but to understand their beliefs as well. Ask them questions about what they believe."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, people like to talk about themselves, and this is great for being able to open up the gospel. Ask them what they believe, and then be prepared to share the truth with them highlighting things they believe, whether they line up with biblical reasoning, or they go against it, be sure to show them the truth either way, and be graceful about it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even in verse 28, Paul uses a Greek poet to illustrate their closeness to coming to the knowledge of the Lord. They had traditions, and they had ideas, but they weren’t absolutely sure or confident. They needed proof, and Paul didn’t present them with proof based on his own traditions and ideas, but rather used what they knew, and brought them into the gospel message. Be observant about the person you are talking to, don’t just talk to them, but actually care about them, and it will make the difference on how well they listen to what you have to say. 32 And when they heard of the resurrection of the dead, some mocked: and others said, We will hear thee again of this matter."},{"kind":"para","text":"There will always be mockers. You can’t convince everyone (even though you have given them no room to be able to deny the truth). People will just have closed minds and not care. That’s okay. God still works after you’ve given the truth. It’s not up to us to increase people’s faith. Someone once said, “When we give the gospel, we don’t have to be successful, we just have to be faithful.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"33 So Paul departed from among them. 34 Howbeit certain men clave unto him, and believed: among the which was Dionysius the Areopagite, and a woman named Damaris, and others with them."},{"kind":"para","text":"This brings us to the parable of the seed sower that Jesus spoke about."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The same day went Jesus out of the house, and sat by the sea side. And great multitudes were gathered together unto him, so that he went into a ship, and sat; and the whole multitude stood on the shore. And he spake many things unto them in parables, saying, Behold, a sower went forth to sow; And when he sowed, some seeds fell by the way side, and the fowls came and devoured them up: Some fell upon stony places, where they had not much earth: and forthwith they sprung up, because they had no deepness of earth: And when the sun was up, they were scorched; and because they had no root, they withered away. And some fell among thorns; and the thorns sprung up, and choked them: But other fell into good ground, and brought forth fruit, some an hundredfold, some sixtyfold, some thirtyfold. Who hath ears to hear, let him hear.","ref":"Matthew 13:1-9"},{"kind":"para","text":"There were some who heard and mocked (stony ground), some who heard and were uncertain (thorny ground), and some who heard and believed (soft ground). Keep this parable in your mind and use it as often as possible when it comes to witnessing to others."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":22,"end":31}],"verses":{"1":"Now when they had passed through Amphipolis and Apollonia, they came to Thessalonica, where was a synagogue of the Jews:","10":"And the brethren immediately sent away Paul and Silas by night unto Berea: who coming thither went into the synagogue of the Jews.","16":"Now while Paul waited for them at Athens, his spirit was stirred in him, when he saw the city wholly given to idolatry.","22":"Then Paul stood in the midst of Mars' hill, and said, Ye men of Athens, I perceive that in all things ye are too superstitious.","23":"For as I passed by, and beheld your devotions, I found an altar with this inscription, To The Unknown God. Whom therefore ye ignorantly worship, him declare I unto you.","24":"God that made the world and all things therein, seeing that he is Lord of heaven and earth, dwelleth not in temples made with hands;","25":"Neither is worshipped with men's hands, as though he needed any thing, seeing he giveth to all life, and breath, and all things;","26":"And hath made of one blood all nations of men for to dwell on all the face of the earth, and hath determined the times before appointed, and the bounds of their habitation;","27":"That they should seek the Lord, if haply they might feel after him, and find him, though he be not far from every one of us:","28":"For in him we live, and move, and have our being; as certain also of your own poets have said, For we are also his offspring.","29":"Forasmuch then as we are the offspring of God, we ought not to think that the Godhead is like unto gold, or silver, or stone, graven by art and man's device.","30":"And the times of this ignorance God winked at; but now commandeth all men every where to repent:","31":"Because he hath appointed a day, in the which he will judge the world in righteousness by that man whom he hath ordained; whereof he hath given assurance unto all men, in that he hath raised him from the dead."}},
    "18": {"chapter":18,"intro":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"Howbeit certain men clave unto him, and believed: among the which was Dionysius the Areopagite, and a woman named Damaris, and others with them.","ref":"Acts 17:34"},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Paul come to the next major city of Corinth, which is where we get our letters from Paul titled 1 and 2 Corinthians. I will try my best to link the books together to open up the scene a little more in this chapter."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, we see Paul and Silas head to Thessalonica (which had a direct correlation to the letters that Paul wrote in what we have titled 1&2 Thessalonians). After finding the Jews stirring up others to attack Paul and Silas, they decided to move on to Berea, where the stirrers ended up following them and stirring people up against them again. Silas and Timothy thought it was a good idea to send Paul out of the city while they stayed in Berea and strengthened the believers there. While Paul waited, he felt a call to preach the gospel to the city of Athens and used their own beliefs to further demonstrate the truth of God’s Word. Of course, not everyone believed, but there were some who believed and followed Paul to where he was going next."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"Paul in Corinth","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"For those that like to have an estimate of the length of the journey, Corinth was forty-six miles west of Athens. This was another Roman colony and the most influential city of the province of Achaia. Even still, today, Athens is a widely spoken-of city of Rome, and many history books write about the wonders and power of this city. 2 And found a certain Jew named Aquila, born in Pontus, lately come from Italy, with his wife Priscilla; (because that Claudius had commanded all Jews to depart from Rome:) and came unto them. 3 And because he was of the same craft, he abode with them, and wrought: for by their occupation they were tentmakers. 4 And he reasoned in the synagogue every sabbath, and persuaded the Jews and the Greeks."},{"kind":"para","text":"Most scholars assume that they were commanded to leave Rome because they had the gospel message already, and because of the trouble that was being stirred up about the Christian message, they were told to flee as to keep themselves out of harm’s way. Aquilla and Priscilla were assumed to be converts of the faith already because of this. Paul shared a common trait with them. He knew how to make tents. He was proficient at it, and it made for a good way to share the gospel in this area."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":11,"title":"Founding the Church at Corinth","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Paul may have sent Silas and Timothy back to the Macedonian churches to try and confirm the churches there. It’s assumed that this part of time has direct linkage to 2 Corinthians."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when I was present with you, and wanted, I was chargeable to no man: for that which was lacking to me the brethren which came from Macedonia supplied: and in all things I have kept myself from being burdensome unto you, and so will I keep myself.","ref":"2 Corinthians 11:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Silas and Timothy had brought back a contribution for Paul’s ministry to continue on! The Lord provided for His work without even being asked. God knows the needs that need to be met, and He will meet them if we are continuously faithful, even before we personally realize we need them. 6 And when they opposed themselves, and blasphemed, he shook his raiment, and said unto them, Your blood be upon your own heads; I am clean; from henceforth I will go unto the Gentiles."},{"kind":"para","text":"The religious Jews were some of the hardest people to reach. Paul understood that, but he also understood how ridiculous it is to reject the truth. Because of this, he shows a gesture of rejection by “shaking out his garments” and turning to the Gentiles, who were much easier to talk to because they didn’t have a religion that entrenched them like the Jews. This is the same aspect today. It’s much easier to talk to people with no religious affiliation than it is to talk to someone who is super religious or playing religion. People that are bathing in the filth of their traditions usually don’t like to be cleaned by the Word of God. It seems they would rather be washed by their own concepts and their fathers’ passed-down ideas over soaking in Living Water. It’s hard to walk away from people that reject the gospel, but we must have the same mindset as Paul and say, “I’ve done what I could, my hands are clean, it is not for me to save people, it is for the Lord.” We cannot give up though."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve heard many people question others who continuously go back to those that have rejected the gospel and say, “They need to just give up, they obviously aren’t going to be saved, and the more you go back the less you allow God to work.” I don’t know if that’s true, but we should have a desire to do whatever we can to make sure the gospel sinks into the ears of the hearers. Yes, our hands are clean, but compassion should push us to try and try again. This is obviously how Paul felt, because he says he is just going to go to the Gentiles now, and the Jews are on their own, and then in the very next verse he visits a Jew who literally lives right next door to where he was preaching. 7 And he departed thence, and entered into a certain man's house, named Justus, one that worshipped God, whose house joined hard to the synagogue. 8 And Crispus, the chief ruler of the synagogue, believed on the Lord with all his house; and many of the Corinthians hearing believed, and were baptized."},{"kind":"para","text":"Crispus is mentioned in 1 Corinthians."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I thank God that I baptized none of you, but Crispus and Gaius; Lest any should say that I had baptized in mine own name.","ref":"1 Corinthians 1:14-15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Not everyone is a lost cause. Sometimes we are so focused on who we are telling the gospel to that we miss out on those around us who are listening. We think that God has sent us for one person, but really it was another person that needed to hear the gospel message."},{"kind":"para","text":"I heard about a preacher during COVID who was filming his Sunday service message. Everyone was isolated and distanced, and the only way to meet was virtually through much of the pandemic. He decided on this particular setting to preach the gospel in the desert around some hills. The place was quite beautiful, and the message was very clear. After he started to finish up, he asked those who were watching to reach out to him if they wanted to know how to be saved. Apparently, there was a homeless man in the bushes right behind where he was filming who came out and said, “I would like to be saved!” That man’s life was changed on that very day, and the pastor had no idea he was even preaching to that man."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul could have said he gave up all he wanted, but the Lord won’t allow the preachers’ preaching to go unused."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So shall my word be that goeth forth out of my mouth: it shall not return unto me void, but it shall accomplish that which I please, and it shall prosper in the thing whereto I sent it.","ref":"Isaiah 55:11 9 Then spake the Lord to Paul in the night by a vision, Be not afraid, but speak, and hold not thy peace: 10 For I am with thee, and no man shall set on thee to hurt thee: for I have much people in this city. 11 And he continued there a year and six months, teaching the word of God among them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul stayed longer in this city than he did in any other city except Ephesus. During this time of his stay, it is assumed he wrote the Epistles of 1 and 2 Thessalonians (AD 49-51). It’s a blessing to know that there were many who were ready for the gospel message right there in Corinth."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":17,"title":"The Careless Gallio","entries":[{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The name given here helps us provide a date for when this took place. During this time, historically proven, Gallio served as the proconsul in 51-52 AD. Having this information helps us figure out (with the places he travelled to and how he travelled) what time and how long Paul’s journeys were. 14 And when Paul was now about to open his mouth, Gallio said unto the Jews, If it were a matter of wrong or wicked lewdness, O ye Jews, reason would that I should bear with you: 15 But if it be a question of words and names, and of your law, look ye to it; for I will be no judge of such matters. 16 And he drave them from the judgment seat."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul was about to defend himself. He knew what it would mean if he didn’t have a chance to speak and prove his cause, not to mention he has to preach the gospel."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For though I preach the gospel, I have nothing to glory of: for necessity is laid upon me; yea, woe is unto me, if I preach not the gospel!","ref":"1 Corinthians 9:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"But Gallio wasn’t about this religious “he-said/she-said” complaints. He wanted a real crime to occupy his time. He wasn’t a very good proconsul… I mean, he didn’t seem to take his job very seriously, and so he kind of allowed the people to judge these issues for themselves instead of intervening. It was a good thing for Paul, but it’s old be a bad thing in a moment… 17 Then all the Greeks took Sosthenes, the chief ruler of the synagogue, and beat him before the judgment seat. And Gallio cared for none of those things."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sosthenes, being the RULER of the synagogue, should have been treated far better than this, but because of the poor leadership in the proconsul, the people had the opportunity and power to mistreat people in leadership. It doesn’t really clarify here, but it is assumed that Sosthenes had become a Christian at some point, and even helped write 1 Corinthians with Paul."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Paul, called to be an apostle of Jesus Christ through the will of God, and Sosthenes our brother,","ref":"1 Corinthians 1:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice, though, that in verse 8 of this chapter it says that Crispus was the ruler of the synagogue, but now it says that Sosthenes is the ruler. My assumption is that there was more than one ruler of the synagogue, but many scholars believe that once Crispus became a Christian, he gave authority to Sosthenes to become ruler. I don’t know how that idea holds water seeing as they chose to beat Sosthenes (and this is assumed because he was a Christian himself. Why else would they choose him to beat?)"}]}]},{"start":18,"end":23,"title":"Paul Returns to Antioch","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul tries to stay as long as necessary in each place he preaches the gospel because he wants to set things in order before he leaves. He doesn’t know how long it will be before he visits these places again. If he can give them lasting wisdom for a time, and help them build up a little bit before heading to the next place the Lord would have him go, he would do whatever it took. Paul’s goal now was to head back to Antioch (where he was sent from) and start another missionary journey. He stopped in Cenchreae for a quick haircut to complete “a vow.” A common tradition for the Jews."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the LORD spake unto Moses, saying, Speak unto the children of Israel, and say unto them, When either man or woman shall separate themselves to vow a vow of a Nazarite, to separate themselves unto the LORD: He shall separate himself from wine and strong drink, and shall drink no vinegar of wine, or vinegar of strong drink, neither shall he drink any liquor of grapes, nor eat moist grapes, or dried. All the days of his separation shall he eat nothing that is made of the vine tree, from the kernels even to the husk. All the days of the vow of his separation there shall no razor come upon his head: until the days be fulfilled, in the which he separateth himself unto the LORD, he shall be holy, and shall let the locks of the hair of his head grow. All the days that he separateth himself unto the LORD he shall come at no dead body. He shall not make himself unclean for his father, or for his mother, for his brother, or for his sister, when they die: because the consecration of his God is upon his head. All the days of his separation he is holy unto the LORD. And if any man die very suddenly by him, and he hath defiled the head of his consecration; then he shall shave his head in the day of his cleansing, on the seventh day shall he shave it. And on the eighth day he shall bring two turtles, or two young pigeons, to the priest, to the door of the tabernacle of the congregation: And the priest shall offer the one for a sin offering, and the other for a burnt offering, and make an atonement for him, for that he sinned by the dead, and shall hallow his head that same day. And he shall consecrate unto the LORD the days of his separation, and shall bring a lamb of the first year for a trespass offering: but the days that were before shall be lost, because his separation was defiled. And this is the law of the Nazarite, when the days of his separation are fulfilled: he shall be brought unto the door of the tabernacle of the congregation: And he shall offer his offering unto the LORD, one he lamb of the first year without blemish for a burnt offering, and one ewe lamb of the first year without blemish for a sin offering, and one ram without blemish for peace offerings, And a basket of unleavened bread, cakes of fine flour mingled with oil, and wafers of unleavened bread anointed with oil, and their meat offering, and their drink offerings. And the priest shall bring them before the LORD, and shall offer his sin offering, and his burnt offering: And he shall offer the ram for a sacrifice of peace offerings unto the LORD, with the basket of unleavened bread: the priest shall offer also his meat offering, and his drink offering. And the Nazarite shall shave the head of his separation at the door of the tabernacle of the congregation, and shall take the hair of the head of his separation, and put it in the fire which is under the sacrifice of the peace offerings. And the priest shall take the sodden shoulder of the ram, and one unleavened cake out of the basket, and one unleavened wafer, and shall put them upon the hands of the Nazarite, after the hair of his separation is shaven: And the priest shall wave them for a wave offering before the LORD: this is holy for the priest, with the wave breast and heave shoulder: and after that the Nazarite may drink wine. This is the law of the Nazarite who hath vowed, and of his offering unto the LORD for his separation, beside that that his hand shall get: according to the vow which he vowed, so he must do after the law of his separation.","ref":"Numbers 6:1-21"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when they heard it, they glorified the Lord, and said unto him, Thou seest, brother, how many thousands of Jews there are which believe; and they are all zealous of the law: And they are informed of thee, that thou teachest all the Jews which are among the Gentiles to forsake Moses, saying that they ought not to circumcise their children, neither to walk after the customs. What is it therefore? the multitude must needs come together: for they will hear that thou art come. Do therefore this that we say to thee: We have four men which have a vow on them; Them take, and purify thyself with them, and be at charges with them, that they may shave their heads: and all may know that those things, whereof they were informed concerning thee, are nothing; but that thou thyself also walkest orderly, and keepest the law.","ref":"Acts 21:20-24"},{"kind":"para","text":"Someone would take a vow to seek divine blessing or to express thanksgiving. 19 And he came to Ephesus, and left them there: but he himself entered into the synagogue, and reasoned with the Jews. 20 When they desired him to tarry longer time with them, he consented not;"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now Paul is in Ephesus for a short time, and he uses that time to preach the gospel to as many as he can. Remember, Ephesus will be a place that Paul spends a lot of time in, and it’s where we get our connective letter for the book of Ephesians. 21 But bade them farewell, saying, I must by all means keep this feast that cometh in Jerusalem: but I will return again unto you, if God will. And he sailed from Ephesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul had every reason to return because he didn’t get the opportunity to really establish the churches there like he did in the other places he has visited. Paul had a meeting to get to, however, and it was important that he get there on time before returning to his missionary journey. 22 And when he had landed at Caesarea, and gone up, and saluted the church, he went down to Antioch. 23 And after he had spent some time there, he departed, and went over all the country of Galatia and Phrygia in order, strengthening all the disciples."},{"kind":"para","text":"Coming back where he started, he was going through the places that had been established prior and checking on their well-being, making sure to encourage them in their leadership, and helping them with whatever they needed while he was there. In his first missionary journey, he went to these areas of Galatia and Phrygia by foot and established these churches. In his second missionary journey, you may remember that he was heading westward toward Asia but was prevented from continuing that way."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now when they had gone throughout Phrygia and the region of Galatia, and were forbidden of the Holy Ghost to preach the word in Asia,","ref":"Acts 16:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now that he was able to stop in Ephesus for a short time, he headed directly west where he was prevented before, knowing that it was where the Lord wanted him to go now after establishing the churches in these other areas where the Lord needed him first. The ultimate purpose for the wait? I don’t know. I DO know that the Lord had the children of Israel wander in the wilderness for forty years, not just because they were disobedient (though that certainly took a role) but also to prepare the land and place where God was going to lead them in the future. This is something I believe was happening here with Paul and his missionary journey. His first stop was Ephesus."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":28,"title":"Apollos Speaks Boldly in Ephesus","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Alexandria is famous for having one of the biggest libraries of that time. Apollos, no doubt, spent a lot of time studying many things aside from the scriptures. 25 This man was instructed in the way of the Lord; and being fervent in the spirit, he spake and taught diligently the things of the Lord, knowing only the baptism of John."},{"kind":"para","text":"We must remember that, during this time, they didn’t have the four gospels to help give an account of the ministry of Christ and the proof of salvation. All they had was the Old Testament and word of mouth. His teachings were based primarily on the proof of Old Testament scripture and the ministry of John the Baptist. He knew a small amount about Jesus, and he linked what he understood about the Old Testament with the testimony of Christ. He more than likely didn’t know about Jesus’ death and resurrection or about the Holy Spirit’s pouring at Pentecost. 26 And he began to speak boldly in the synagogue: whom when Aquila and Priscilla had heard, they took him unto them, and expounded unto him the way of God more perfectly. 27 And when he was disposed to pass into Achaia, the brethren wrote, exhorting the disciples to receive him: who, when he was come, helped them much which had believed through grace:"},{"kind":"para","text":"Thankfully, these two disciples were able to clarify better for him, and instead of him denying everything he heard, he was able to take the truth he knew and connect it to the truth they were giving him. He may have tried to question (as a wise person would), but his questions were brought to light and answered with the basis of scripture."},{"kind":"para","text":"28 For he mightily convinced the Jews, and that publicly, shewing by the scriptures that Jesus was Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"All scripture points to Him. It’s impossible to separate scripture (whether the Old Testament or New) from Jesus. If you claim to believe in the Old Testament, but you don’t believe in Jesus, then you don’t really believe the Old Testament. If you claim to believe in the New Testament, but you don’t believe in Jesus, then you don’t really believe in the New Testament. All things point to Him and prove Him, and we cannot separate Jesus from the scripture without intentionally sinning against God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he is the head of the body, the church: who is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead; that in all things he might have the preeminence.","ref":"Colossians 1:18"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":24,"end":24}],"verses":{"1":"After these things Paul departed from Athens, and came to Corinth;","5":"And when Silas and Timotheus were come from Macedonia, Paul was pressed in the spirit, and testified to the Jews that Jesus was Christ.","12":"And when Gallio was the deputy of Achaia, the Jews made insurrection with one accord against Paul, and brought him to the judgment seat,","13":"Saying, This fellow persuadeth men to worship God contrary to the law.","18":"And Paul after this tarried there yet a good while, and then took his leave of the brethren, and sailed thence into Syria, and with him Priscilla and Aquila; having shorn his head in Cenchrea: for he had a vow.","24":"And a certain Jew named Apollos, born at Alexandria, an eloquent man, and mighty in the scriptures, came to Ephesus."}},
    "19": {"chapter":19,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 19"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Last chapter we saw Paul end his second missionary journey and head back to the mother church. On the way back, he stopped by Ephesus and had the opportunity to preach the gospel for a short time, but couldn’t stay long. He promised that he would make it back to them if the Lord was willing, and in this chapter we see Paul keep his promise. Ephesus is the place where Paul spends the most time in his missionary journey’s. It’s a wild story from here to chapter 21, and I can’t wait to dive deeper into this location."}],"previously":["Last chapter we saw Paul end his second missionary journey and head back to the mother church. On the way back, he stopped by Ephesus and had the opportunity to preach the gospel for a short time, but couldn’t stay long. He promised that he would make it back to them if the Lord was willing, and in this chapter we see Paul keep his promise. Ephesus is the place where Paul spends the most time in his missionary journey. It’s a wild story from here to chapter 21, and I can’t wait to dive deeper into this location."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Paul at Ephesus","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Like Apollos, the “disciples” mentioned here were probably those that followed the teachings of John the Baptist and didn’t know Jesus Christ yet. It seems there was a decent group that John the Baptist had touched by his faithfulness. Sadly, John was beheaded before he could experience the resurrection of Christ, but Christ says this about John:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Verily I say unto you, Among them that are born of women there hath not risen a greater than John the Baptist: notwithstanding he that is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.","ref":"Matthew 11:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"John’s work paved the way for Jesus Christ to come and reap the harvest."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He said, I am the voice of one crying in the wilderness, Make straight the way of the Lord, as said the prophet Esaias.","ref":"John 1:23"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a beautiful picture of sowing and reaping. John may not have seen the reaping, but he certainly sowed a great harvest, and the fruit of that is still being witnessed even after the death and resurrection of Christ. John (the Baptist), at this particular point, has already died. Yet his words are still having an impact. We should all hope and pray that our impact has such a lasting effect for the Lord that even after we are gone, people will come to know Christ through what we have left behind."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is why I write books. It started out as a means of helping those that I was not close to (physically, because they lived in a different state) and ended with me preparing for the future. Everything that I have written is soaked in scripture because I desire that after I’m gone, my kids (or grandkids, or their friends, or whoever comes along and finds them) can search the truth about specific topics. Maybe you aren’t a writer, but there is plenty for you to do to pass down a legacy for when you are gone, and if it’s done through God’s Will and Word, it will last for all of eternity. 2 He said unto them, Have ye received the Holy Ghost since ye believed? And they said unto him, We have not so much as heard whether there be any Holy Ghost."},{"kind":"para","text":"They didn’t know the Spirit had already come, but they did know that there was supposed to be one that they would receive."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"John answered, saying unto them all, I indeed baptize you with water; but one mightier than I cometh, the latchet of whose shoes I am not worthy to unloose: he shall baptize you with the Holy Ghost and with fire:","ref":"Luke 3:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"They more than likely moved to Ephesus from Palestine just before Jesus’ ministry actually began. They were being faithful to the message that John preached and were still waiting for the promises that were made, believing the message, and holding onto the hope. Now, Paul was here to assure them of the promise that was already completed. Such is God’s timing."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, Paul could have come to Ephesus sooner, but God needed the appropriate timing for Paul and these believers. Everything, so far (in the book of Acts), has been planned to the letter. Nothing is off timing, even with the imprisonments and stoning; God’s timing is impeccable. 3 And he said unto them, Unto what then were ye baptized? And they said, Unto John's baptism. 4 Then said Paul, John verily baptized with the baptism of repentance, saying unto the people, that they should believe on him which should come after him, that is, on Christ Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"Not that John’s baptism was wrong or anything, they just needed a faith in Jesus Christ for their baptism to be effectual. They had a faith in John’s teaching. Now they needed a faith in Jesus’ death, burial, and resurrection. John’s baptism was for a promise that would come. Paul’s baptism was for a promise kept. 5 When they heard this, they were baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, as we should know by now, being baptized in the name of Christ is the same as being baptized in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Ghost, for they all share the same name. They all have different functions, but they are all one person. When you look at John’s baptism, he doesn’t baptize in the name of the Father, Son, and Holy Ghost because he didn’t have that authority yet, for Jesus hadn’t redeemed the world through his death and resurrection. He had to baptize on a future promise that was yet to be fulfilled."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s suffice to say, even today, if you were baptized in a religion or teaching that was NOT based on Jesus’ crucifixion and resurrection, it’s a good idea to be “re” baptized for the correct faith and doctrine. I honestly and truly believe that ministry can only start if you are baptized correctly. Without the baptism, you are rejecting God’s ordinance to take a step of faith. Most churches call it the “first step” of faith because it symbolizes that you are trusting Christ for your life. If it is the “first step” (which seems to be the case in every single one of these instances in the book of Acts—everyone hears the gospel, receives the Spirit, repents, and then is baptized in water to start their ministry), then it only stands to reason that we must make sure it’s a step in the right direction and for the right reasons. 6 And when Paul had laid his hands upon them, the Holy Ghost came on them; and they spake with tongues, and prophesied. 7 And all the men were about twelve."},{"kind":"para","text":"Going back to speaking in tongues, these first apostles and church leaders were given the authority to prove the Spirit by placing hands."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the manifestation of the Spirit is given to every man to profit withal. For to one is given by the Spirit the word of wisdom; to another the word of knowledge by the same Spirit; To another faith by the same Spirit; to another the gifts of healing by the same Spirit; To another the working of miracles; to another prophecy; to another discerning of spirits; to another divers kinds of tongues; to another the interpretation of tongues: But all these worketh that one and the selfsame Spirit, dividing to every man severally as he will.","ref":"1 Corinthians 12:7-11"},{"kind":"para","text":"This list further proves the least necessity in speaking in tongues. The church at Corinth placed a real emphasis on the ability to speak in other languages, and Paul was trying to emphasize that the other gifts that God gives should not go unnoticed simply because they desire to speak in tongues."},{"kind":"para","text":"If there are people who are capable of doing this, we don’t need to single them out as more special or more spiritual than all of the other things that the Spirit gifts, like, for example, the first thing Paul mentions, which is wisdom given by the Spirit. Actually, Paul even says that if we are capable of speaking in tongues, it should be a means of prayer and praise to God through love to one another."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Though I speak with the tongues of men and of angels, and have not charity, I am become as sounding brass, or a tinkling cymbal. And though I have the gift of prophecy, and understand all mysteries, and all knowledge; and though I have all faith, so that I could remove mountains, and have not charity, I am nothing.","ref":"1 Corinthians 13:1-2"},{"kind":"para","text":"The only way that these people could receive the gift of tongues (which isn’t a foreign language, but rather the capability to speak to people of different dialects) was only given to people that first believed in the Lord. The Apostles didn’t just lay hands on a bunch of random people as they felt it was worth it. Paul laid hands on people that the Lord allowed to prove the faith further."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":10,"title":"Paul In the Synagogue","entries":[{"start":8,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Apparently, the derogatory term “Christian” (which started in Antioch) had not reached the vocabulary of the Ephesians yet. They were still referring to Jesus’ followers as people of that “Way” as if to say there were multiple ways. But Jesus Himself says,"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"…I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me.","ref":"John 14:6 10 And this continued by the space of two years; so that all they which dwelt in Asia heard the word of the Lord Jesus, both Jews and Greeks."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul was stopped from going into Asia in his second missionary journey, and now it says that his stay in Ephesus (which was longer than two years) helped to spark a large audience in all of Asia Minor to hear the gospel message. Think about this for a second. It doesn’t say a few heard it, or there was some who heard, or there was a great multitude like it did all the other times when Paul preached. It says “ALL!” I LOVE it when the Bible uses that term, “all.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God;","ref":"Romans 3:23"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For whosoever shall keep the whole law, and yet offend in one point, he is guilty of all.","ref":"James 2:10"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me.","ref":"Philippians 4:13"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the love of money is the root of all evil…","ref":"1 TImothy 6:10a"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A friend loveth at all times…","ref":"Proverbs 17:17a"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"All scripture is given by inspiration of God, and is profitable for doctrine, for reproof, for correction, for instruction in righteousness: That the man of God may be perfect, thoroughly furnished unto all good works.","ref":"1 Timothy 3:16-17"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Casting all your care upon him; for he careth for you.","ref":"1 Peter 5:7"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Prove all things; hold fast that which is good.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:21"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And thou shalt love the LORD thy God with all thine heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy might.","ref":"Deuteronomy 6:5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Praying always with all prayer and supplication in the Spirit, and watching thereunto with all perseverance and supplication for all saints;","ref":"Ephesians 6:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"When God says “all,” what He really means is “all.” All of Asia Minor heard the gospel. These are also the churches in which John writes to in Revelation, which were also all located in Asia Minor."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"John to the seven churches which are in Asia: Grace be unto you, and peace, from him which is, and which was, and which is to come; and from the seven Spirits which are before his throne;","ref":"Revelation 1:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"It could be that the churches which John writes to were some of the biggest churches in Asia, which Paul may have had a hand in helping start! Either way, Paul’s outreach was effective, and he was wise in how he did it. Paul started in the major cities of Asia, and then sent co-laborers into the cities that surrounded it to establish churches so the gospel could be spread quicker and have a broader impact."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":22,"title":"Miracles By Paul","entries":[{"start":11,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think it should be well understood at this point that it was not Paul’s power that was healing people, nor was there any magic specifically in the handkerchiefs or aprons. Rather, the Holy Spirit’s presence sometimes remained evident in connection with objects that Paul had touched. This is something we spoke on in earlier chapters. In case you don’t remember…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Insomuch that they brought forth the sick into the streets, and laid them on beds and couches, that at the least the shadow of Peter passing by might overshadow some of them.","ref":"Acts 5:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"All this did was showcase the power of the Holy Spirit in the Apostles. Once again, we do not have this kind of power today. We are not apostles. However, every individual person does have the capability of being filled by the Spirit of God if they turn to Christ, and so each person can have God’s power if they are willing to submit to the Lord and accept the gift of salvation, which is the provider of the Spirit of God. 13 Then certain of the vagabond Jews, exorcists, took upon them to call over them which had evil spirits the name of the Lord Jesus, saying, We adjure you by Jesus whom Paul preacheth. 14 And there were seven sons of one Sceva, a Jew, and chief of the priests, which did so. 15 And the evil spirit answered and said, Jesus I know, and Paul I know; but who are ye?"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is funny. Not the fact that evil spirits were rejecting these men (though that’s funny too because they were doing it in their own power without an actual faith in Christ), but rather because the spirits ultimately are mocking these religionists. I believe the evil spirits were doing this on purpose. They probably knew the religionists well. In fact, the evil spirits probably knew that they were religionists simply trying to do something new because they saw Paul doing it in the name of Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"Instead of the spirits just ignoring them or attacking them right off the bat, they mocked them first. They just said, “I know Paul, and I know Jesus whom you are proclaiming to try and get rid of me, but seeing as you don’t have Christ’s power, that makes you very weak and little, and probably even weaker seeing as you are trying to subdue us without that power thinking you can just say Jesus’ name and that will work everything out.” Then the demons attack… 16 And the man in whom the evil spirit was leaped on them, and overcame them, and prevailed against them, so that they fled out of that house naked and wounded."},{"kind":"para","text":"How sad. Religionists today still have the same mentality as the Jews do here. They present Christ to the demons without knowing Christ themselves and think that if they say a few fancy words and names, the person will be saved and better. A relationship with God is the only way to receive the power of God to lead others to salvation. 17 And this was known to all the Jews and Greeks also dwelling at Ephesus; and fear fell on them all, and the name of the Lord Jesus was magnified. 18 And many that believed came, and confessed, and shewed their deeds."},{"kind":"para","text":"This really proves what God’s Word says in 1 Corinthians."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But God hath chosen the foolish things of the world to confound the wise; and God hath chosen the weak things of the world to confound the things which are mighty;","ref":"1 Corinthians 1:27"},{"kind":"para","text":"What’s even more interesting to me is the demons are actually CAUSING people to believe in Christ more because of the lack of power that religion actually has. But it doesn’t just end there… 19 Many of them also which used curious arts brought their books together, and burned them before all men: and they counted the price of them, and found it fifty thousand pieces of silver. 20 So mightily grew the word of God and prevailed."},{"kind":"para","text":"There was a lot of belief and emphasis on magic in this particular area and age. When they found out that it was a sham, they didn’t just throw their books away or simply leave their books on the shelf. They went out of their way to have a book burning. They didn’t want them to even be in their homes at all. They knew it wasn’t pleasing to God, who says:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"There shall not be found among you any one that maketh his son or his daughter to pass through the fire, or that useth divination, or an observer of times, or an enchanter, or a witch,","ref":"Deuteronomy 18:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Why wouldn’t God allow this? Was it too powerful? Too good? Too worldly? Too dangerous? It was certainly dangerous, but not because it was real. It was dangerous because it allowed Satanic influence and also caused people to do things that were really stupid, like (for instance, and not limited to) burning/sacrificing their own children, murdering others, and drinking their blood."},{"kind":"para","text":"Many say that our society has evolved from such barbaric acts. Maybe we have, but we haven’t changed the purpose of doing them, just the way in which we do it. Many abortions take place, not because of rape or because some are simply too young, but rather for religious and Satanic purposes. In fact, there have been many who commit abortions who do it knowing they are killing a child and treat it as a sacrificial offering to their god, which is ultimately Satan. We can’t legally burn children anymore, so the next best thing is to rip them to pieces before they are even born…"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is why it’s important to stay away from these types of rituals. God doesn’t want us to allow any kind of bad spirit to influence us away from what is good and right, and if we allow a little bit of sin, like cancer, it increases rapidly. These people were ready to give up everything they believed because they wanted to see God use HIS power in their city. Well, some of them did anyway… 21 After these things were ended, Paul purposed in the spirit, when he had passed through Macedonia and Achaia, to go to Jerusalem, saying, After I have been there, I must also see Rome. 22 So he sent into Macedonia two of them that ministered unto him, Timotheus and Erastus; but he himself stayed in Asia for a season."},{"kind":"para","text":"Though God had all things planned and knew all things beforehand, Paul might regret sending his helpers away for the events that were about to transpire…"}]}]},{"start":23,"end":41,"title":"The Silversmiths Riot","entries":[{"start":23,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There’s so much that could be said here, but what an amazing picture of revival taking place. Of course, as we’ve mentioned before, the love of money is the root of all evil, and these silversmiths loved their income from their idolatrous works. However, now that people were being converted to Christ, they were losing out on all that money because these people no longer wanted to believe in false idols. How I desire and pray for this same circumstance to take place here in California."},{"kind":"para","text":"What a mighty state we would see if so many people were converted that they stopped backing LGBT corporations, anti-country companies, or satanic and unhealthy music. If we saw a massive crowd of people burning their idols on the street because they want to see God’s power continue to move where they are. The best line here is when Demetrius says, “Paul says that gods made with hands are not gods!” Like, “How dare Paul say that the gods we literally just make up and make with the materials we find on earth are not real gods!” Just because Paul doesn’t want to believe in the god I just made up doesn’t mean that my god is any less real than his God, which he witnessed with his own eyes and has been proven throughout generations of His children who have turned to Him and followed Him. 27 So that not only this our craft is in danger to be set at nought; but also that the temple of the great goddess Diana should be despised, and her magnificence should be destroyed, whom all Asia and the world worshippeth."},{"kind":"para","text":"If Paul’s word and presence are able to overthrow a god that they have been worshipping for a very long time, then that god is probably not as strong as you’d like to think. Someone once said, “A faith that can’t be tested can’t be trusted.” And a god that can’t be tested can’t be trusted either."},{"kind":"para","text":"28 And when they heard these sayings, they were full of wrath, and cried out, saying, Great is Diana of the Ephesians. 29 And the whole city was filled with confusion: and having caught Gaius and Aristarchus, men of Macedonia, Paul's companions in travel, they rushed with one accord into the theatre."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is all religious pride leads to. Every time you see a riot take place, or a disaster happen from people being wild and crazy, it’s because of their pride for a specific cause."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Pride goeth before destruction, and an haughty spirit before a fall.","ref":"Proverbs 16:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"The crowd was able to get ahold of Gaius and Aristarchus. This had to be a pretty intense moment for the disciples. Gaius and Aristarchus will eventually be released, and Aristarchus reappears later as Paul’s companion again."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Aristarchus my fellowprisoner saluteth you, and Marcus, sister's son to Barnabas, (touching whom ye received commandments: if he come unto you, receive him;)","ref":"Colossians 4:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"“Marcus, Aristarchus, Demas, Lucas, my fellowlabourers.” Philemon 24"},{"kind":"para","text":"30 And when Paul would have entered in unto the people, the disciples suffered him not. 31 And certain of the chief of Asia, which were his friends, sent unto him, desiring him that he would not adventure himself into the theatre."},{"kind":"para","text":"Instead of Paul running away, Paul wanted to come and stand arm in arm with the brothers so that they wouldn’t be the only ones receiving the brunt of the riot. The disciples didn’t let him go, however, and kept him away from the crowd. Even the Asiarchs (who were the keepers of the imperial Roman cult in Asia) were concerned about the safety of Paul in this matter. 32 Some therefore cried one thing, and some another: for the assembly was confused: and the more part knew not wherefore they were come together."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is kind of humorous as well. You have some screaming and complaining because of one thing, and some screaming and complaining because of something completely different, and there was a group of people in there who didn’t even know why they were there rioting with them but just saw the opportunity to cause some chaos. 33 And they drew Alexander out of the multitude, the Jews putting him forward. And Alexander beckoned with the hand, and would have made his defence unto the people. 34 But when they knew that he was a Jew, all with one voice about the space of two hours cried out, Great is Diana of the Ephesians."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were screaming for two hours about how great their fake god was because she was about to be overthrown by the real God simply by miracles. People hate it when God is proven and He starts to take place of their traditions or their beliefs. They hold so tightly to their falsities, and when God comes and says, “I have to take these away now,” they throw a fit, not unlike an infant when you are weaning them from a pacifier."},{"kind":"para","text":"Actually, surprisingly enough, my daughter was weaned from the pacifier pretty easily. I was told it would be a nightmare. I wasn’t looking forward to it. She was about one year old and had the ability to walk, understand, and say little words here and there. I was told that she would probably cry a lot more and that she would have a really hard time with it because it was like an addiction to babies. I was told to cut the tip off of her favorite pacifier a little bit each day until there was nothing left, and it would help her to not desire it anymore. I figured I would try that, and I accidentally cut too far down. The worst part was, it was her last pacifier. (What happened to the other ones? I don’t remember.) When I gave the pacifier to her, she put it in her mouth instantly, took one suck from it, and then took it out, looking right at the tip. She handed it to me and said, “No…” I said, “What’s wrong? You don’t want it?” And she said, “No…” I said, “You’re going to have to throw it away then.” She walked over to the trash can, lifted the lid, looked at her pacifier, then at me, then back to the pacifier, then at me again, and made an “eh” noise, motioning at her pacifier hanging over the open trash can. I said, “If you don’t want it, you have to throw it away.” She thought a little bit longer and then let it go. She started crying like crazy and came over to me for a hug, which I happily gave her and told her I was proud of her for throwing it away. She had no fusses or anything after that and had no reason to go back to the pacifier."},{"kind":"para","text":"These people, depicted here, however, are far less mature than my one-year-old daughter quitting her pacifier. Honestly, this is the same state of man today. They don’t want to give up what they prize the most, and they are willing to kick, scream, and cry until they get their way. 35 And when the townclerk had appeased the people, he said, Ye men of Ephesus, what man is there that knoweth not how that the city of the Ephesians is a worshipper of the great goddess Diana, and of the image which fell down from Jupiter?"},{"kind":"para","text":"So according to historical finds, meteorites were associated with Diana worship. This means that the “image that fell down from Jupiter” was probably a meteorite that hit the Earth, and they seemingly decided to just worship it because they had never seen anything like that before. 36 Seeing then that these things cannot be spoken against, ye ought to be quiet, and to do nothing rashly. 37 For ye have brought hither these men, which are neither robbers of churches, nor yet blasphemers of your goddess. 38 Wherefore if Demetrius, and the craftsmen which are with him, have a matter against any man, the law is open, and there are deputies: let them implead one another. 39 But if ye enquire any thing concerning other matters, it shall be determined in a lawful assembly. 40 For we are in danger to be called in question for this day's uproar, there being no cause whereby we may give an account of this concourse."},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems there was at least one voice of reason in the crowd, and he pointed out that, at this point, because of the way people were acting, they don’t have a case against the Christians here. They might have had a case if they didn’t act so rashly, but they blew it all out of the water by throwing a tantrum. In all honesty, though, these silversmiths still didn’t have a case against the message of the gospel because the Christian message didn’t go against any form of Roman law. 41 And when he had thus spoken, he dismissed the assembly."},{"kind":"para","text":"This picture demonstrates how weak idolatry really is. Out of all the sins to commit, idolatry is probably the most pathetic because it basically says, “I see the proof of God, I understand the truth of God, but I don’t accept proof or truth.” Anyone who rejects the message of the Bible and the God of the Word is rejecting proof and truth. It’s really sad, but it’s also extremely common."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s why many don’t want to accept the God of the Bible because they are able to make a god that can cozy up to their lifestyle and sin and accept them for who they are and what they do rather than correct them and tell them the truth."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":8,"end":9},{"start":11,"end":12},{"start":23,"end":26}],"verses":{"1":"And it came to pass, that, while Apollos was at Corinth, Paul having passed through the upper coasts came to Ephesus: and finding certain disciples,","8":"And he went into the synagogue, and spake boldly for the space of three months, disputing and persuading the things concerning the kingdom of God.","9":"But when divers were hardened, and believed not, but spake evil of that way before the multitude, he departed from them, and separated the disciples, disputing daily in the school of one Tyrannus.","11":"And God wrought special miracles by the hands of Paul:","12":"So that from his body were brought unto the sick handkerchiefs or aprons, and the diseases departed from them, and the evil spirits went out of them.","23":"And the same time there arose no small stir about that way.","24":"For a certain man named Demetrius, a silversmith, which made silver shrines for Diana, brought no small gain unto the craftsmen;","25":"Whom he called together with the workmen of like occupation, and said, Sirs, ye know that by this craft we have our wealth.","26":"Moreover ye see and hear, that not alone at Ephesus, but almost throughout all Asia, this Paul hath persuaded and turned away much people, saying that they be no gods, which are made with hands:"}},
    "20": {"chapter":20,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter we see Paul finally go to Ephesus and start the church. There was a lot of people from all around the area coming to hear the gospel for the first time. Paul performs multiple miracles which causes a great multitude of silversmiths very upset because it’s taking business away from them. Apparently there was a lot of people making fake gods, and Paul was converting too many to the real God, and that caused many to stop making fake gods, burn their books from their false magic, and start following after the Lord and Paul’s teaching."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we pick up with Paul continuing from that city to the next with a rejoicing that they were able to escape the riot that took place in the last chapter with their lives still intact."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, we see Paul finally go to Ephesus and start the church. There were a lot of people from all around the area coming to hear the gospel for the first time. Paul performs multiple miracles, which causes a great multitude of silversmiths to be very upset because it’s taking business away from them. Apparently, there were a lot of people making fake gods, and Paul was converting too many to the real God, and that caused many to stop making fake gods, burn their books from their false magic, and start following after the Lord and Paul’s teaching. In this chapter, we pick up with Paul continuing from that city to the next with a rejoicing that they were able to escape the riot that took place in the last chapter with their lives still intact."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"Paul in Macedonia and Greece","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In case we don’t remember from the last chapter, Paul sent Timothy and Erastus to Macedonia, which is the church in Corinth."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So he sent into Macedonia two of them that ministered unto him, Timotheus and Erastus; but he himself stayed in Asia for a season.","ref":"Acts 19:22"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is when he would have written the first letter to the Corinthians. Paul seemed to have a lot of conflict with the Corinthian church, saying that his visit was “painful.” He also wrote a “tearful” letter to them when he returned to Ephesus."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But I determined this with myself, that I would not come again to you in heaviness. For if I make you sorry, who is he then that maketh me glad, but the same which is made sorry by me? And I wrote this same unto you, lest, when I came, I should have sorrow from them of whom I ought to rejoice; having confidence in you all, that my joy is the joy of you all. For out of much affliction and anguish of heart I wrote unto you with many tears; not that ye should be grieved, but that ye might know the love which I have more abundantly unto you.","ref":"2 Corinthians 2:1-4"},{"kind":"para","text":"Because this Corinthian church was not acting correctly and was completely against and opposed Paul’s leadership, it made it difficult for Paul to rejoice in the idea of their so-called “faith.” Paul sent Titus ahead of them while he was in Ephesus to check on the churches and confirmed the churches on the way up there as Paul went to meet with Titus. Paul was hoping to see Titus return with good news before Paul got there."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Furthermore, when I came to Troas to preach Christ's gospel, and a door was opened unto me of the Lord, I had no rest in my spirit, because I found not Titus my brother: but taking my leave of them, I went from thence into Macedonia.","ref":"2 Corinthians 2:12-13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Once Paul reached Macedonia, however, Titus brought good news to Paul!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For, when we were come into Macedonia, our flesh had no rest, but we were troubled on every side; without were fightings, within were fears. Nevertheless God, that comforteth those that are cast down, comforted us by the coming of Titus; And not by his coming only, but by the consolation wherewith he was comforted in you, when he told us your earnest desire, your mourning, your fervent mind toward me; so that I rejoiced the more. For though I made you sorry with a letter, I do not repent, though I did repent: for I perceive that the same epistle hath made you sorry, though it were but for a season. Now I rejoice, not that ye were made sorry, but that ye sorrowed to repentance: for ye were made sorry after a godly manner, that ye might receive damage by us in nothing. For godly sorrow worketh repentance to salvation not to be repented of: but the sorrow of the world worketh death. For behold this selfsame thing, that ye sorrowed after a godly sort, what carefulness it wrought in you, yea, what clearing of yourselves, yea, what indignation, yea, what fear, yea, what vehement desire, yea, what zeal, yea, what revenge! In all things ye have approved yourselves to be clear in this matter. Wherefore, though I wrote unto you, I did it not for his cause that had done the wrong, nor for his cause that suffered wrong, but that our care for you in the sight of God might appear unto you. Therefore we were comforted in your comfort: yea, and exceedingly the more joyed we for the joy of Titus, because his spirit was refreshed by you all. For if I have boasted any thing to him of you, I am not ashamed; but as we spake all things to you in truth, even so our boasting, which I made before Titus, is found a truth. And his inward affection is more abundant toward you, whilst he remembereth the obedience of you all, how with fear and trembling ye received him. I rejoice therefore that I have confidence in you in all things.","ref":"2 Corinthians 7:5-16 2 And when he had gone over those parts, and had given them much exhortation, he came into Greece,"},{"kind":"para","text":"Greece is another name for the province of Achaea, where Corinth was located. 3 And there abode three months. And when the Jews laid wait for him, as he was about to sail into Syria, he purposed to return through Macedonia."},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, Paul’s plan was to leave off to Syria and head back to Jerusalem."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"After these things were ended, Paul purposed in the spirit, when he had passed through Macedonia and Achaia, to go to Jerusalem, saying, After I have been there, I must also see Rome.","ref":"Acts 19:21"},{"kind":"para","text":"This would have been the time when Paul would write the letter to the Romans, because it was his final visit to Corinth. Paul clarifies in Romans that he went to Jerusalem to deliver a collection from Gentile churches for needy Christians there."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For which cause also I have been much hindered from coming to you. But now having no more place in these parts, and having a great desire these many years to come unto you; Whensoever I take my journey into Spain, I will come to you: for I trust to see you in my journey, and to be brought on my way thitherward by you, if first I be somewhat filled with your company. But now I go unto Jerusalem to minister unto the saints. For it hath pleased them of Macedonia and Achaia to make a certain contribution for the poor saints which are at Jerusalem. It hath pleased them verily; and their debtors they are. For if the Gentiles have been made partakers of their spiritual things, their duty is also to minister unto them in carnal things. When therefore I have performed this, and have sealed to them this fruit, I will come by you into Spain. And I am sure that, when I come unto you, I shall come in the fulness of the blessing of the gospel of Christ.","ref":"Romans 15:22-29"},{"kind":"para","text":"Because he went back through Macedonia, this means Paul had to go back to the churches in Berea, Thessalonica, and Philippi. 4 And there accompanied him into Asia Sopater of Berea; and of the Thessalonians, Aristarchus and Secundus; and Gaius of Derbe, and Timotheus; and of Asia, Tychicus and Trophimus."},{"kind":"para","text":"These men who were chosen were very specific and carefully sought out. These men would not only keep Paul accountable for the money as it was being transported, but they would also help protect the offering from any robbers they might face on their travels."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now concerning the collection for the saints, as I have given order to the churches of Galatia, even so do ye. Upon the first day of the week let every one of you lay by him in store, as God hath prospered him, that there be no gatherings when I come. And when I come, whomsoever ye shall approve by your letters, them will I send to bring your liberality unto Jerusalem. And if it be meet that I go also, they shall go with me.","ref":"1 Corinthians 16:1-4 5 These going before tarried for us at Troas. 6 And we sailed away from Philippi after the days of unleavened bread, and came unto them to Troas in five days; where we abode seven days."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were cautious and careful with the ministry in which the Lord had provided for them. They handled the money with care, knowing it would fulfill a much greater cause if they could just get it there safely."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":16,"title":"Eutychus Raised From the Dead","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is the first indication of worshipping on Sunday. This won’t be the last time either."}]},{"start":8,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"And this is why you don’t fall asleep during the sermon. Honestly, there’s plenty of spiritual application to this as well. The people who are spiritually asleep during the sermon are only causing pain to their own spiritual needs. We must make sure we are ready and rested before we are to be filled with God’s Word again. 10 And Paul went down, and fell on him, and embracing him said, Trouble not yourselves; for his life is in him. 11 When he therefore was come up again, and had broken bread, and eaten, and talked a long while, even till break of day, so he departed. 12 And they brought the young man alive, and were not a little comforted."},{"kind":"para","text":"A miracle by God? I don’t know. Honestly, I can’t find much information on this situation, probably because there isn’t much that’s being portrayed here. This seems like such an odd story to just glance over and continue the journey with. It kind of doesn’t even seem necessary, but God thought it important to provide it. Whether that be because He wants to take the preaching of His Word seriously, or because He just thought it was an interesting story for everyone to hear about this silly young man who was so tired he fell asleep during a sermon, I couldn’t tell you. 13 And we went before to ship, and sailed unto Assos, there intending to take in Paul: for so had he appointed, minding himself to go afoot. 14 And when he met with us at Assos, we took him in, and came to Mitylene."},{"kind":"para","text":"So this band of misfits slowly started to separate as they tended to the wounds of their friends, not through fighting off robbers for their money, or through some sort of enemy trying to stop them on their journey, but simply through self-stupidity. Why was this kid sleeping in the way he was? What was his goal? 15 And we sailed thence, and came the next day over against Chios; and the next day we arrived at Samos, and tarried at Trogyllium; and the next day we came to Miletus."},{"kind":"para","text":"Miletus was a major harbor in Asia during Paul’s time. 16 For Paul had determined to sail by Ephesus, because he would not spend the time in Asia: for he hasted, if it were possible for him, to be at Jerusalem the day of Pentecost."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul desired to spend time with the brothers for the necessary events at all costs. Preaching the gospel to so many territories is great, and has its need and purpose, but there’s nothing like sitting with other believers and growing together, using your time in building others up. This is important because many Christians today have the tenacious desire to do as much ministry as possible without the actual fellowship. They want to worship without talking to others, preach without hearing others out, study without being corrected, and increase their knowledge without relationship."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And let us consider one another to provoke unto love and to good works: Not forsaking the assembling of ourselves together, as the manner of some is; but exhorting one another: and so much the more, as ye see the day approaching.","ref":"Hebrews 10:24-25"},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems there are many in the church today who are more prone to preach and teach than they are to be around others in the faith. It’s a sad testimony to leave behind that you were a great preacher but not a good friend."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is another time that Paul was in a hurry to get to a specific location for the simple sake of fellowship, and this is the most admirable trait about Paul or any Christian for that matter. One who has priorities in perspective will be one who has a faith worthy to follow. If you see a man put his ministry before his compassion for the brothers, that man is no minister. If you see him put the church before his family, he is no leader. If you see him put his might and heart into his message but not into conversations with others, he is a false preacher."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"This is a true saying, If a man desire the office of a bishop, he desireth a good work. A bishop then must be blameless, the husband of one wife, vigilant, sober, of good behaviour, given to hospitality, apt to teach; Not given to wine, no striker, not greedy of filthy lucre; but patient, not a brawler, not covetous; One that ruleth well his own house, having his children in subjection with all gravity; (For if a man know not how to rule his own house, how shall he take care of the church of God?) Not a novice, lest being lifted up with pride he fall into the condemnation of the devil. Moreover he must have a good report of them which are without; lest he fall into reproach and the snare of the devil. Likewise must the deacons be grave, not doubletongued, not given to much wine, not greedy of filthy lucre; Holding the mystery of the faith in a pure conscience. And let these also first be proved; then let them use the office of a deacon, being found blameless. Even so must their wives be grave, not slanderers, sober, faithful in all things. Let the deacons be the husbands of one wife, ruling their children and their own houses well.","ref":"1 Timothy 3:1-12"}]}]},{"start":17,"end":38,"title":"Paul Speaks to the Ephesian Elders","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The leaders at the Ephesians church were called elders. In verse 28, they addressed them as “overseers” or “bishops.” Their job was not to just simply give a sermon every week, or even every day. Their job was primarily to “watch over” the flock and “care for” (shepherd) the flock of God. 18 And when they were come to him, he said unto them, Ye know, from the first day that I came into Asia, after what manner I have been with you at all seasons, 19 Serving the Lord with all humility of mind, and with many tears, and temptations, which befell me by the lying in wait of the Jews:"},{"kind":"para","text":"May this human side not be missed by us as we read this. I feel like a lot of emotions and characteristics can be overlooked because we are simply reading about the great leading and impossible feats that God has expounded upon through His Word using the writer Luke. It’s difficult for writers to put down every single emotion, feeling, and position that everyone’s in, especially when it involves traveling so much. This particular time gives an opening for Luke to portray the emotions of Paul and the leadership that is taking place here in Ephesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"May we work in the same manner as Paul. Not with the mindset of being “strong” “self-willed” Christians who are so affirmative on the Word of God that the feelings of mere mortals are beneath us, but rather as weak, needy people who desperately don’t want to see anyone spend an eternity in hell on their own free volition without being warned. 20 And how I kept back nothing that was profitable unto you, but have shewed you, and have taught you publicly, and from house to house, 21 Testifying both to the Jews, and also to the Greeks, repentance toward God, and faith toward our Lord Jesus Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"Not as though he was being forceful, but rather fervent in the matter. You never know how much time you will have to tell others the gospel, or how much you will be able to pour into others. There is certainly a sort of transient feeling we should all have as if it might be our last time ever being able to tell others the gospel. None of us know how long we have left. Christians don’t know it either, but they do know they can prepare themselves, and they can prepare others before our time is up. 22 And now, behold, I go bound in the spirit unto Jerusalem, not knowing the things that shall befall me there: 23 Save that the Holy Ghost witnesseth in every city, saying that bonds and afflictions abide me."},{"kind":"para","text":"I love this. Paul says, “I don’t know what will happen there, but I do know that I have pretty much come to expect from God’s Spirit that we will face imprisonment, or threatenings, or possible stonings, and the list could go on…” This is how every Christian must approach life situations. Expect there to be fire that will try you, but don’t let the fire overcome you. Use the fire to your advantage so that others can see your faith and mimic it. D.L. Moody said, “Careful for nothing, prayerful for everything, thankful for anything.” 24 But none of these things move me, neither count I my life dear unto myself, so that I might finish my course with joy, and the ministry, which I have received of the Lord Jesus, to testify the gospel of the grace of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we have Christ, He is all that is valuable to us. Everything we have is His, and so we cannot hold any value for ourselves. Someone preached about how worthless we are. He said we are literally trash. But Christ found value in this trash that is me, and so I might be trash, but I’m worth the Lord’s life. Because He gave His life for me, I have become valuable trash."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For ye are bought with a price: therefore glorify God in your body, and in your spirit, which are God's.","ref":"1 Corinthians 6:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"25 And now, behold, I know that ye all, among whom I have gone preaching the kingdom of God, shall see my face no more. 26 Wherefore I take you to record this day, that I am pure from the blood of all men. 27 For I have not shunned to declare unto you all the counsel of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is what it means to walk away with clean hands. There may have been some who did not accept the Lord as their Savior, but that wasn’t because Paul didn’t tell them. As terrible as it is for those that rejected Christ, Paul cannot be put to blame for any of their eternities. Paul’s hands were clean."},{"kind":"para","text":"28 Take heed therefore unto yourselves, and to all the flock, over the which the Holy Ghost hath made you overseers, to feed the church of God, which he hath purchased with his own blood."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve seen this verse quoted numerous times, as well as…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Obey them that have the rule over you, and submit yourselves: for they watch for your souls, as they that must give account, that they may do it with joy, and not with grief: for that is unprofitable for you.","ref":"Hebrews 13:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"The context (that this is usually misquoted by) is that we should be treating our leaders with dignity and respect no matter what their doctrinal ideas are or what kind of spirit they have. There’s some truth to that, and I won’t get into it in this particular lesson or chapter, but I want to point out one thing that seems to always be missed by those that misquote it… In verse 28, the first part Paul tells them to do is, “take heed therefore unto YOURSELVES…” Look at yourself before you look to others. What are you lacking in? Even in Hebrews 13 (I love this) in verse 16;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But to do good and to communicate forget not: for with such sacrifices God is well pleased.","ref":"Hebrews 13:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is an important trait and command for the leader to follow if he is to expect obedience and submission from those he is leading. Moody said, “God cannot fill those who are already full of themselves.” We must empty ourselves before we can come to the Lord for a filling of His Spirit. Many people don’t have the right Spirit from God because they can’t figure out how to empty themselves first. “This should make every congregation shake, and every pastor shutter.” I’d argue to say, the only way to lead people correctly is if you are first being led by God correctly. 29 For I know this, that after my departing shall grievous wolves enter in among you, not sparing the flock. 30 Also of your own selves shall men arise, speaking perverse things, to draw away disciples after them."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is incredibly insightful. Paul understood that the wolves were not going to simply be people that were easy to spot, and they were not going to just come out and attack right off the bat. The wolves would be people from right among themselves. This is why it’s important to take the commands to “examine ourselves.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Examine yourselves, whether ye be in the faith; prove your own selves. Know ye not your own selves, how that Jesus Christ is in you, except ye be reprobates?","ref":"2 Corinthians 13:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is also a repeated warning from Jesus Himself."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.","ref":"Matthew 7:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"They are sitting among us all the time, and they are very difficult to point out. This does not mean we cannot love others, but it does mean we should be prepared for an attack like Paul was. This is important to note as well: Jesus knew who the wolf was among them, but that didn’t change His love for Judas. Paul didn’t know who the false sheep were, but that didn’t stop him from literally crying over others and going through the trials. We will be blindsided by the false sheep, but that does not give us the right to stop being transparent and sensitive to the needs of others and to the leading of the Spirit. 31 Therefore watch, and remember, that by the space of three years I ceased not to warn every one night and day with tears."},{"kind":"para","text":"This verse, once again, showcases the human side of Paul. Rejection was painful for Paul. Seeing people turn away from the truth of the gospel is not an easy thing to witness. If we are not careful, it can really harden us from trust and love for the work that God has given to us. We must chip away at the callousness of this world, so that it doesn’t callous us."},{"kind":"para","text":"I liken it to when I play guitar. My finger tips build up what is called calluses. After a while of playing, the calluses get stronger, and it makes it easier to play. However, I have a bad habit of peeling the callousness off my fingers, and making them extremely tender to playing the guitar again. I have to literally break in the calluses on my fingers all over again. This is the use of the gospel."},{"kind":"para","text":"32 And now, brethren, I commend you to God, and to the word of his grace, which is able to build you up, and to give you an inheritance among all them which are sanctified. 33 I have coveted no man's silver, or gold, or apparel."},{"kind":"para","text":"The only thing that will suffice for the Christian is Gods Word. That’s all I want. You can keep everything else in this world, I just want Christ. 34 Yea, ye yourselves know, that these hands have ministered unto my necessities, and to them that were with me. 35 I have shewed you all things, how that so labouring ye ought to support the weak, and to remember the words of the Lord Jesus, how he said, It is more blessed to give than to receive."},{"kind":"para","text":"Interestingly enough, this is not recorded in the gospels. When Jesus said this, it isn’t clarified. But there are many things that Jesus did that were not recorded in the gospels."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And there are also many other things which Jesus did, the which, if they should be written every one, I suppose that even the world itself could not contain the books that should be written. Amen.","ref":"John 21:25"},{"kind":"para","text":"With that said, this is obviously (and evidently) something that certainly lines up with the rest of Scripture. That’s why I don’t understand modern translations taking out specific Scriptures. That’s a conversation for a different time. This saying was more than likely passed along by one of the other Apostles that heard Jesus teach. The important point to notice here is that giving is to be far more important than receiving for the Christian."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For ye know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that, though he was rich, yet for your sakes he became poor, that ye through his poverty might be rich. And herein I give my advice: for this is expedient for you, who have begun before, not only to do, but also to be forward a year ago. Now therefore perform the doing of it; that as there was a readiness to will, so there may be a performance also out of that which ye have. For if there be first a willing mind, it is accepted according to that a man hath, and not according to that he hath not. For I mean not that other men be eased, and ye burdened: But by an equality, that now at this time your abundance may be a supply for their want, that their abundance also may be a supply for your want: that there may be equality: As it is written, He that had gathered much had nothing over; and he that had gathered little had no lack.","ref":"2 Corinthians 8:9-15"},{"kind":"para","text":"36 And when he had thus spoken, he kneeled down, and prayed with them all. 37 And they all wept sore, and fell on Paul's neck, and kissed him, 38 Sorrowing most of all for the words which he spake, that they should see his face no more. And they accompanied him unto the ship."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul made a strong impact on those that he witnessed to. He spent a good majority of time in Ephesus, and this was the last time he was to see these people. This was not an easy goodbye. Honestly, if you are a Christian that is strong in the Lord and in His work, it shouldn’t be easy to say goodbye. Men like Paul are very few and far between. Ephesus would have to continue to build up without him (which doesn’t really seem to be the case as John points out in Revelation)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Unto the angel of the church of Ephesus write; These things saith he that holdeth the seven stars in his right hand, who walketh in the midst of the seven golden candlesticks; I know thy works, and thy labour, and thy patience, and how thou canst not bear them which are evil: and thou hast tried them which say they are apostles, and are not, and hast found them liars: And hast borne, and hast patience, and for my name's sake hast laboured, and hast not fainted. Nevertheless I have somewhat against thee, because thou hast left thy first love.","ref":"Revelation 2:1-4"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":9},{"start":17,"end":17}],"verses":{"1":"And after the uproar was ceased, Paul called unto him the disciples, and embraced them, and departed for to go into Macedonia.","7":"And upon the first day of the week, when the disciples came together to break bread, Paul preached unto them, ready to depart on the morrow; and continued his speech until midnight.","8":"And there were many lights in the upper chamber, where they were gathered together.","9":"And there sat in a window a certain young man named Eutychus, being fallen into a deep sleep: and as Paul was long preaching, he sunk down with sleep, and fell down from the third loft, and was taken up dead.","17":"And from Miletus he sent to Ephesus, and called the elders of the church."}},
    "21": {"chapter":21,"intro":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they all wept sore, and fell on Paul's neck, and kissed him, Sorrowing most of all for the words which he spake, that they should see his face no more. And they accompanied him unto the ship.","ref":"Acts 20:37-38"},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we pick up with him making his journey to Jerusalem."}],"previously":["Paul is making his way back to Jerusalem to keep his commitment in a feast of some sort. He finishes providing an offering for a few churches on the way there, and speaks to the Ephesian Elders about his love for them, and his hope for their continuation, letting them also know that he doesn’t know what will happen next or if he will ever even see them again. It was a difficult parting on both sides…"],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"From Miletus to Tyre","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul seems to have a ship that stays fairly close to shore most of the time. I’m assuming that each place that is mentioned took about a day’s journey, and more than likely where they would stop for the night on their way over to Jerusalem."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, the travel to Phoenicia was going to be a little more difficult with their current ship, which stayed close to shore, so they had to get something a little sturdier to travel on open waters. 3 Now when we had discovered Cyprus, we left it on the left hand, and sailed into Syria, and landed at Tyre: for there the ship was to unlade her burden."},{"kind":"para","text":"Tyre was a place in Phoenicia, so they obviously made this stop here because that’s where they wanted to go, but also because this ship had cargo to take to this city anyway."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":9,"title":"Paul is Forbidden to go to Jerusalem","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We’ve seen through the previous chapters and verses so far that the Holy Spirit was blatantly and obviously leading Paul to Jerusalem."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"After these things were ended, Paul purposed in the spirit, when he had passed through Macedonia and Achaia, to go to Jerusalem, saying, After I have been there, I must also see Rome.","ref":"Acts 19:21"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And now, behold, I go bound in the spirit unto Jerusalem, not knowing the things that shall befall me there: Save that the Holy Ghost witnesseth in every city, saying that bonds and afflictions abide me. But none of these things move me, neither count I my life dear unto myself, so that I might finish my course with joy, and the ministry, which I have received of the Lord Jesus, to testify the gospel of the grace of God.","ref":"Acts 20:22-24"},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet, here, we see the prophets telling Paul to do the opposite. There’s really no clarification as to why they were stopping Paul from going to Jerusalem. Paul does tell us to test prophecies, though."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let the prophets speak two or three, and let the other judge.","ref":"1 Corinthians 14:29"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Despise not prophesyings. Prove all things; hold fast that which is good.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:20-21"}]},{"start":5,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, there were no ill feelings against Paul, otherwise they wouldn’t have taken their time to follow him out of the city and taken their whole families with them. They also wouldn’t have taken the time to pray with Paul if they were just against his presence in general. They must have heard something regarding Paul’s visit and were just worried about what might happen to him if he made the visit without warning. The Spirit causes us to speak about and bring up a great deal of things we don’t understand at the moment, but usually, it benefits someone else. There have been many sermons I have preached where I did not quite understand why God had me there, but later found out it was effective in much more than I anticipated."},{"kind":"para","text":"One particular setting was a time I preached on the necessity of transition. It was at a church that had experienced quite a bit of transition outside of their own wills, and they were ready to quit the transition process. There was a particular event that was about to take place that I knew would be an issue with the church, so I preached on this topic of transition in hopes that they would be more acclimated to the necessary change (for it was to preach the gospel to more people than just our church family). When the change took place, people started to complain and harass the man in charge of the event. Adding to this change (it was not just a change of circumstance, it was a change of date as well), the day he chose just happened to be the rainiest day of the year. He had no idea it would rain. It seemed like all hope was lost. We came to find a lot of new people coming into our church, however, even through the rain, and the idea for change helped bring in unsaved people from all around the area to hear the gospel for the first time."},{"kind":"para","text":"God wants to reach people. That is all He is concerned about. If we aren’t willing to be used by Him to transition people through change and to move people forward for the sake of the gospel, then He will certainly find a church that will."}]},{"start":7,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Good ol’ Philip reappears at last! He’s obviously doing well; otherwise, he wouldn’t have made another appearance. Not only so, but it seems his daughters were apparently gifted with prophecy. Obviously, God’s spiritual gifting wasn’t just meant specifically for men in this matter."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh; and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions: And also upon the servants and upon the handmaids in those days will I pour out my spirit.","ref":"Joel 2:28-29"}]}]},{"start":10,"end":13,"title":"The Holy Spirit Warns Paul (Again)","entries":[{"start":10,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A little dramatic, but well understood. The Holy Spirit couldn’t be blamed for being misunderstood when it is presented as literally as that. Nobody standing by Paul at this moment would be like, “Gee, I wonder if he means Paul, and I wonder if Paul is going to be bound and imprisoned?” The same man that was warning Paul here was already a proven man. We’ve actually heard of him before!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And there stood up one of them named Agabus, and signified by the Spirit that there should be great dearth throughout all the world: which came to pass in the days of Claudius Caesar.","ref":"Acts 11:28"},{"kind":"para","text":"The “acting out” of a prophecy was really common through the Old Testament."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Moreover the LORD said unto me, Take thee a great roll, and write in it with a man's pen concerning Mahershalalhashbaz. And I took unto me faithful witnesses to record, Uriah the priest, and Zechariah the son of Jeberechiah. And I went unto the prophetess; and she conceived, and bare a son. Then said the LORD to me, Call his name Mahershalalhashbaz. For before the child shall have knowledge to cry, My father, and my mother, the riches of Damascus and the spoil of Samaria shall be taken away before the king of Assyria.","ref":"Isaiah 8:1-4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Thus saith the LORD unto me, Go and get thee a linen girdle, and put it upon thy loins, and put it not in water. So I got a girdle according to the word of the LORD, and put it on my loins. And the word of the LORD came unto me the second time, saying, Take the girdle that thou hast got, which is upon thy loins, and arise, go to Euphrates, and hide it there in a hole of the rock. So I went, and hid it by Euphrates, as the LORD commanded me. And it came to pass after many days, that the LORD said unto me, Arise, go to Euphrates, and take the girdle from thence, which I commanded thee to hide there. Then I went to Euphrates, and digged, and took the girdle from the place where I had hid it: and, behold, the girdle was marred, it was profitable for nothing. Then the word of the LORD came unto me, saying, Thus saith the LORD, After this manner will I mar the pride of Judah, and the great pride of Jerusalem. This evil people, which refuse to hear my words, which walk in the imagination of their heart, and walk after other gods, to serve them, and to worship them, shall even be as this girdle, which is good for nothing. For as the girdle cleaveth to the loins of a man, so have I caused to cleave unto me the whole house of Israel and the whole house of Judah, saith the LORD; that they might be unto me for a people, and for a name, and for a praise, and for a glory: but they would not hear.","ref":"Jeremiah 13:1-11"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In the year that Tartan came unto Ashdod, (when Sargon the king of Assyria sent him,) and fought against Ashdod, and took it; At the same time spake the LORD by Isaiah the son of Amoz, saying, Go and loose the sackcloth from off thy loins, and put off thy shoe from thy foot. And he did so, walking naked and barefoot. And the LORD said, Like as my servant Isaiah hath walked naked and barefoot three years for a sign and wonder upon Egypt and upon Ethiopia; So shall the king of Assyria lead away the Egyptians prisoners, and the Ethiopians captives, young and old, naked and barefoot, even with their buttocks uncovered, to the shame of Egypt.","ref":"Isaiah 20:1-4"},{"kind":"para","text":"Most of these prophets put many preachers to shame these days. Then again, I wouldn’t want to be walking around naked and preaching the gospel to people either… Though people tend not to forget when the preacher does something embarrassing. Either way, Agabus was not just being strange; he was being led, and he was making an obvious and memorable warning. 12 And when we heard these things, both we, and they of that place, besought him not to go up to Jerusalem."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even Luke is on this same train of keeping Paul from going into something perilous. It seems like a rollercoaster from the Spirit at this point. Paul was so certain he was going in the right direction, in the right way through the Spirit of God, yet (for some reason) God was telling everyone else that this seems like a bad idea. What is Paul to do in this matter? Should he listen to the Holy Spirit’s prompting on him personally, or should he listen to the Holy Spirit’s prompting on everyone else who is providing him counsel? This is an important circumstance to note, and the conclusion of what Paul does is needed for us to make wise decisions as well, because we will be faced with this same dilemma ourselves more times than not. 13 Then Paul answered, What mean ye to weep and to break mine heart? for I am ready not to be bound only, but also to die at Jerusalem for the name of the Lord Jesus."},{"kind":"para","text":"What an amazing response! Honestly, when God puts a calling on someone’s heart to go to a place that they aren’t sure about, it doesn’t matter what everyone else thinks about what will happen. They could know (down to the letter) all of the falls and trials others will face, but God’s calling on you personally should be motivation enough to just do it anyway. It’s not up to you to figure out what will work and what won’t. We are simply supposed to follow, and whatever the Lord allows to work is up to the Lord. If we are led to a ministry that will ultimately fail, we should understand that God has a purpose and plan for it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, a good minister will have in his heart and mind that God will supply whatever God wants to supply. Ministry does not grow because we are great soulwinners or because we are the most eloquent speakers and have all the answers. Ministry grows because God is faithful to supply. Period."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":17,"title":"Paul at Jerusalem","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That’s what must happen. These men of God should be there to back up Paul, however, knowing that it will be a difficult journey for him. We cannot stop God’s will from being done, but we can certainly prepare those that are following His will. 15 And after those days we took up our carriages, and went up to Jerusalem. 16 There went with us also certain of the disciples of Caesarea, and brought with them one Mnason of Cyprus, an old disciple, with whom we should lodge."},{"kind":"para","text":"“WE” being the keyword here. They didn’t just leave Paul to it. They backed him up for the time being, while they could. The most important lesson here is not so much that Paul listened to God’s calling, as it was that people helped support Paul in his decision to listen to God’s calling. There’s nothing worse than “Christians” who neglect the opportunity to encourage, edify, and build up their brothers in the calling that God has given them. 17 And when we were come to Jerusalem, the brethren received us gladly."},{"kind":"para","text":"The greatest falls always seem to occur after the calmest moments of our lives."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":26,"title":"Paul Takes a Jewish Vow","entries":[{"start":18,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Jewish believers here in this church (now apparently run by elders) were rejoicing over Paul’s Gentile ministry, but concerned over the idea that Paul was turning away Jews from appropriate traditions that would glorify God. Of course, Paul wasn’t doing this, and the people that were accusing Paul of this felt convicted of his message in regards to giving up vain traditions because of what they provide."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men, after the rudiments of the world, and not after Christ.","ref":"Colossians 2:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul understood that there were some traditions that were biblical and were given for a specific purpose, knowing that they were important to the faith and testimonies of those that practiced them."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Therefore, brethren, stand fast, and hold the traditions which ye have been taught, whether by word, or our epistle.","ref":"2 Thessalonians 2:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"These complainers were just looking to try and destroy the testimony and ministry of the disciples, as they have been trying to do since the beginning of the church’s start. They didn’t like that there were people being converted to a faith in Christ, because they hated Jesus."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If the world hate you, ye know that it hated me before it hated you.","ref":"John 15:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"The only reason that Paul made this vow was to try and disprove the rumors about him. This is what is considered a Nazarite vow (forgive me because we have already read this out in a previous chapter of this book, but just in case we have forgotten…)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the LORD spake unto Moses, saying, Speak unto the children of Israel, and say unto them, When either man or woman shall separate themselves to vow a vow of a Nazarite, to separate themselves unto the LORD: He shall separate himself from wine and strong drink, and shall drink no vinegar of wine, or vinegar of strong drink, neither shall he drink any liquor of grapes, nor eat moist grapes, or dried. All the days of his separation shall he eat nothing that is made of the vine tree, from the kernels even to the husk. All the days of the vow of his separation there shall no razor come upon his head: until the days be fulfilled, in the which he separateth himself unto the LORD, he shall be holy, and shall let the locks of the hair of his head grow. All the days that he separateth himself unto the LORD he shall come at no dead body. He shall not make himself unclean for his father, or for his mother, for his brother, or for his sister, when they die: because the consecration of his God is upon his head. All the days of his separation he is holy unto the LORD. And if any man die very suddenly by him, and he hath defiled the head of his consecration; then he shall shave his head in the day of his cleansing, on the seventh day shall he shave it. And on the eighth day he shall bring two turtles, or two young pigeons, to the priest, to the door of the tabernacle of the congregation: And the priest shall offer the one for a sin offering, and the other for a burnt offering, and make an atonement for him, for that he sinned by the dead, and shall hallow his head that same day. And he shall consecrate unto the LORD the days of his separation, and shall bring a lamb of the first year for a trespass offering: but the days that were before shall be lost, because his separation was defiled. And this is the law of the Nazarite, when the days of his separation are fulfilled: he shall be brought unto the door of the tabernacle of the congregation: And he shall offer his offering unto the LORD, one he lamb of the first year without blemish for a burnt offering, and one ewe lamb of the first year without blemish for a sin offering, and one ram without blemish for peace offerings, And a basket of unleavened bread, cakes of fine flour mingled with oil, and wafers of unleavened bread anointed with oil, and their meat offering, and their drink offerings. And the priest shall bring them before the LORD, and shall offer his sin offering, and his burnt offering: And he shall offer the ram for a sacrifice of peace offerings unto the LORD, with the basket of unleavened bread: the priest shall offer also his meat offering, and his drink offering. And the Nazarite shall shave the head of his separation at the door of the tabernacle of the congregation, and shall take the hair of the head of his separation, and put it in the fire which is under the sacrifice of the peace offerings. And the priest shall take the sodden shoulder of the ram, and one unleavened cake out of the basket, and one unleavened wafer, and shall put them upon the hands of the Nazarite, after the hair of his separation is shaven: And the priest shall wave them for a wave offering before the LORD: this is holy for the priest, with the wave breast and heave shoulder: and after that the Nazarite may drink wine. This is the law of the Nazarite who hath vowed, and of his offering unto the LORD for his separation, beside that that his hand shall get: according to the vow which he vowed, so he must do after the law of his separation.","ref":"Numbers 6:1-21"},{"kind":"para","text":"The number one issue with the Jews was the custom of circumcision. I think this is funny because Paul was literally seen making sure that Timothy was circumcised for this exact reason, and yet now he’s being accused of rejecting circumcision."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":32,"title":"Paul Seized in the Temple by the Jews","entries":[{"start":27,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These people were overdramatic about their faith practices. You know a faith is wrong when it can be disproven, and you know it’s wrong when people can’t stand up for their beliefs but rather cry and complain because of what others said about it. Many scholars believe that the Jews from Asia were more than likely those from Ephesus, and knew Paul from his three years in their city. 30 And all the city was moved, and the people ran together: and they took Paul, and drew him out of the temple: and forthwith the doors were shut."},{"kind":"para","text":"They didn’t just walk Paul out or ask him to follow them. No, that would have been too simple. They dragged him out of the temple. 31 And as they went about to kill him, tidings came unto the chief captain of the band, that all Jerusalem was in an uproar. 32 Who immediately took soldiers and centurions, and ran down unto them: and when they saw the chief captain and the soldiers, they left beating of Paul."},{"kind":"para","text":"I mean… It’s not like Paul wasn’t warned. Paul has pretty much come to expect this treatment everywhere he goes now. A Roman tribune was the commander of a cohort. They consisted of up to one thousand soldiers, under the command of central centurions. They obviously had to be housed close by in order to respond to this incident so quickly. I assume they were housed close so that they could put a stop to unruly crowds before they got out of hand. They may have had a problem with this in the past already."},{"kind":"para","text":"Ver 33-40: Paul Bound with Chains 33 Then the chief captain came near, and took him, and commanded him to be bound with two chains; and demanded who he was, and what he had done."},{"kind":"para","text":"Most scholars believe that being bound with “two chains” just meant he was bound to a soldier on each side of him. 34 And some cried one thing, some another, among the multitude: and when he could not know the certainty for the tumult, he commanded him to be carried into the castle."},{"kind":"para","text":"This same craziness happened in Ephesus before. Everyone was screaming about something different. Most of them didn’t even know why they wanted to see Paul suffer; they just liked to complain and they liked the thought of someone being hurt because of their own hurts. 35 And when he came upon the stairs, so it was, that he was borne of the soldiers for the violence of the people. 36 For the multitude of the people followed after, crying, Away with him."},{"kind":"para","text":"This almost mimics the crowd for Christ’s crucifixion."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But they cried out, Away with him, away with him, crucify him. Pilate saith unto them, Shall I crucify your King? The chief priests answered, We have no king but Caesar.","ref":"John 19:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"The streets have been shouting the same thing since Christ’s crucifixion. Whenever we share the gospel, the crowd chants “Away with him, away with him!”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Yea, and all that will live godly in Christ Jesus shall suffer persecution.","ref":"2 Timothy 3:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"As I’m sure Paul has come to expect this treatment at this point, we should be prepared to be mistreated for our faith in the same. 37 And as Paul was to be led into the castle, he said unto the chief captain, May I speak unto thee? Who said, Canst thou speak Greek? 38 Art not thou that Egyptian, which before these days madest an uproar, and leddest out into the wilderness four thousand men that were murderers?"},{"kind":"para","text":"The what? Uncertain about the events that this soldier is speaking of, I had to look up what occurred, and according to Jewish historian Josephus, there was an Egyptian Jew who led a revolt in which the Roman governor (Felix) had stopped. The Egyptian apparently ran away, and his movement was put to an end. The soldiers must have assumed that the Egyptian returned and stirred up a crowd to cause a revolt against the Roman government. 39 But Paul said, I am a man which am a Jew of Tarsus, a city in Cilicia, a citizen of no mean city: and, I beseech thee, suffer me to speak unto the people. 40 And when he had given him licence, Paul stood on the stairs, and beckoned with the hand unto the people. And when there was made a great silence, he spake unto them in the Hebrew tongue, saying,"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul’s chance to witness to a very large crowd was about to commence, but we are left on a cliffhanger… We will literally have to find out in the next chapter what happens next. A very common theme in the book of Acts is cliffhangers, so we must get used to it now."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":11},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":18,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":29}],"verses":{"1":"And it came to pass, that after we were gotten from them, and had launched, we came with a straight course unto Coos, and the day following unto Rhodes, and from thence unto Patara:","2":"And finding a ship sailing over unto Phenicia, we went aboard, and set forth.","4":"And finding disciples, we tarried there seven days: who said to Paul through the Spirit, that he should not go up to Jerusalem.","5":"And when we had accomplished those days, we departed and went our way; and they all brought us on our way, with wives and children, till we were out of the city: and we kneeled down on the shore, and prayed.","6":"And when we had taken our leave one of another, we took ship; and they returned home again.","7":"And when we had finished our course from Tyre, we came to Ptolemais, and saluted the brethren, and abode with them one day.","8":"And the next day we that were of Paul's company departed, and came unto Caesarea: and we entered into the house of Philip the evangelist, which was one of the seven; and abode with him.","9":"And the same man had four daughters, virgins, which did prophesy.","10":"And as we tarried there many days, there came down from Judaea a certain prophet, named Agabus.","11":"And when he was come unto us, he took Paul's girdle, and bound his own hands and feet, and said, Thus saith the Holy Ghost, So shall the Jews at Jerusalem bind the man that owneth this girdle, and shall deliver him into the hands of the Gentiles.","14":"And when he would not be persuaded, we ceased, saying, The will of the Lord be done.","18":"And the day following Paul went in with us unto James; and all the elders were present.","19":"And when he had saluted them, he declared particularly what things God had wrought among the Gentiles by his ministry.","20":"And when they heard it, they glorified the Lord, and said unto him, Thou seest, brother, how many thousands of Jews there are which believe; and they are all zealous of the law:","21":"And they are informed of thee, that thou teachest all the Jews which are among the Gentiles to forsake Moses, saying that they ought not to circumcise their children, neither to walk after the customs.","22":"What is it therefore? the multitude must needs come together: for they will hear that thou art come.","23":"Do therefore this that we say to thee: We have four men which have a vow on them;","24":"Them take, and purify thyself with them, and be at charges with them, that they may shave their heads: and all may know that those things, whereof they were informed concerning thee, are nothing; but that thou thyself also walkest orderly, and keepest the law.","25":"As touching the Gentiles which believe, we have written and concluded that they observe no such thing, save only that they keep themselves from things offered to idols, and from blood, and from strangled, and from fornication.","26":"Then Paul took the men, and the next day purifying himself with them entered into the temple, to signify the accomplishment of the days of purification, until that an offering should be offered for every one of them.","27":"And when the seven days were almost ended, the Jews which were of Asia, when they saw him in the temple, stirred up all the people, and laid hands on him,","28":"Crying out, Men of Israel, help: This is the man, that teacheth all men every where against the people, and the law, and this place: and further brought Greeks also into the temple, and hath polluted this holy place.","29":"(For they had seen before with him in the city Trophimus an Ephesian, whom they supposed that Paul had brought into the temple.)"}},
    "22": {"chapter":22,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 22"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In the last chapter, we see Paul heading back to Jerusalem, knowing that the Spirit was leading him there, and then to Rome. Multiple people warned Paul (also being led of the Spirit) to NOT go to Jerusalem. Paul desired to listen to the Lord on the matter, and ended up being beaten and imprisoned (something that was warned to happen if he went to Jerusalem anyway), and now Paul is given the opportunity to speak his peace in front of a large crowd of those that were there to see his imprisonment."},{"kind":"para","text":"We were last left off on a cliffhanger wondering what Paul was going to say, and now we will see how he decides to use this opportunity to preach the gospel to others."}],"previously":["In the last chapter, we see Paul heading back to Jerusalem, knowing that the Spirit was leading him there, and then to Rome. Multiple people warned Paul (also being led by the Spirit) to NOT go to Jerusalem. Paul desired to listen to the Lord on the matter, and ended up being beaten and imprisoned (something that was warned to happen if he went to Jerusalem anyway), and now Paul is given the opportunity to speak his peace in front of a large crowd of those that were there to see his imprisonment. We were last left off on a cliffhanger wondering what Paul was going to say, and now we will see how he decides to use this opportunity to preach the gospel to others."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":16,"title":"Paul’s Defense and Testimony","entries":[{"start":1,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul knew that if he spoke to them in their native language, they would be more inclined to listen and heed what he had to say. He was willing to do whatever he could to get the attention of those that were around him, even to those he knew hated him and were just seen beating him. He desired that ALL (including even his worst enemies) would hear the gospel and be changed. 3 I am verily a man which am a Jew, born in Tarsus, a city in Cilicia, yet brought up in this city at the feet of Gamaliel, and taught according to the perfect manner of the law of the fathers, and was zealous toward God, as ye all are this day."},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, either Paul’s parents moved to Jerusalem when he was young, or Paul moved here himself when he was training to be a rabbi. Either way, he points out his fleshly zeal that he had to be the holiest person in the city. He wanted to be more righteous than any other, and he was willing to cross any lines necessary to do so. He certainly was a very religious man. Paul was probably more religious than any of the Jews listening to him speak right now. 4 And I persecuted this way unto the death, binding and delivering into prisons both men and women. 5 As also the high priest doth bear me witness, and all the estate of the elders: from whom also I received letters unto the brethren, and went to Damascus, to bring them which were there bound unto Jerusalem, for to be punished."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul was honestly able to say how much he despised all of the things he is now literally doing in his own life. He was so far against the Christian way, and he didn’t even want Christians to live, gaining permission from some of the “holiest” religious people of the day to literally lock up, imprison, and kill people that preached Jesus Christ being God in the flesh. Now he’s part of the group. I think we can all identify with Paul here. Unless you grew up in a Christian household, we have all faced, at some point, disdain for organized religion, or anyone that would talk about the Bible, thinking they were just trying to judge us. But when we became Christians, we suddenly understood their motives. They weren’t judging us, they were warning us! 6 And it came to pass, that, as I made my journey, and was come nigh unto Damascus about noon, suddenly there shone from heaven a great light round about me. 7 And I fell unto the ground, and heard a voice saying unto me, Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me? 8 And I answered, Who art thou, Lord? And he said unto me, I am Jesus of Nazareth, whom thou persecutest."},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, this is a great opportunity to share your testimony. Sometimes, sharing your salvation story is more impactful than just reading a bunch of Bible verses to people, hoping they would just understand God’s Word right off the bat."},{"kind":"para","text":"Before I was saved, several people read verses to me in an attempt to get me saved. Every time they would open their Bibles, it was as if my brain would just shut off and my ears would just close up. I could tell you every person that tried to witness to me and every word they said, but I could not tell you what Bible verses they used or if they spoke God’s Word. I know they did. They had to. But Satan was able to close my ears, and I could not hear."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul isn’t just preaching God’s Word or Old Testament knowledge (at least not yet). Paul understood that they all knew this already. What they didn’t know was that God did a miracle in his own life, and that might be what got their attention the most! 9 And they that were with me saw indeed the light, and were afraid; but they heard not the voice of him that spake to me."},{"kind":"para","text":"This gives obvious indication that the people who were with Paul heard a voice, but could not understand who it was. Whether or not those that were following Paul were saved that day is not mentioned, but we do know that they witnessed a miracle right in front of them, even if they didn’t understand what was happening at that moment. 10 And I said, What shall I do, Lord? And the Lord said unto me, Arise, and go into Damascus; and there it shall be told thee of all things which are appointed for thee to do. 11 And when I could not see for the glory of that light, being led by the hand of them that were with me, I came into Damascus. 12 And one Ananias, a devout man according to the law, having a good report of all the Jews which dwelt there,"},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s not easy for Paul to name-drop when he is in this position. Paul tries to make the case that Ananias can and has been trusted by these same Jews who were more than likely trying to see Paul suffer at this point in time. Many Jews knew Ananias, and many Jews liked Ananias. If they wouldn’t take Paul’s testimony, they could trust Ananias’. 13 Came unto me, and stood, and said unto me, Brother Saul, receive thy sight. And the same hour I looked up upon him. 14 And he said, The God of our fathers hath chosen thee, that thou shouldest know his will, and see that Just One, and shouldest hear the voice of his mouth. 15 For thou shalt be his witness unto all men of what thou hast seen and heard. 16 And now why tarriest thou? arise, and be baptized, and wash away thy sins, calling on the name of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, the actual purpose of baptism is not to wash away sins. I think that is commonly understood by the readers of this commentary. Baptism is an outward symbol of the cleansing from sin that occurs when someone trusts in Jesus."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The like figure whereunto even baptism doth also now save us (not the putting away of the filth of the flesh, but the answer of a good conscience toward God,) by the resurrection of Jesus Christ:","ref":"1 Peter 3:21"},{"kind":"para","text":"Baptism should only point to the cleansing that only Christ can provide through His death, burial, and resurrection, after living His perfect life that we could not live."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Having therefore, brethren, boldness to enter into the holiest by the blood of Jesus, By a new and living way, which he hath consecrated for us, through the veil, that is to say, his flesh; And having an high priest over the house of God; Let us draw near with a true heart in full assurance of faith, having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience, and our bodies washed with pure water.","ref":"Hebrews 10:19-22"}]}]},{"start":17,"end":24,"title":"Paul Was Warned to Stay Away","entries":[{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We don’t have a report of this happening earlier in the book of Acts, so I assume it probably happened right after his conversion at some point. This helps his testimony though, because it happens on what Jews know as Holy ground being placed in the Jerusalem temple. 19 And I said, Lord, they know that I imprisoned and beat in every synagogue them that believed on thee: 20 And when the blood of thy martyr Stephen was shed, I also was standing by, and consenting unto his death, and kept the raiment of them that slew him. 21 And he said unto me, Depart: for I will send thee far hence unto the Gentiles."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul thought, “Everyone knows how much I hated you, and how much I disdained those that would even speak about you. If you would just let me share my testimony, they would believe!” Jesus tells him, “That’s just not going to be the case, and you will be sent to a different place until the time is right.” Jesus does this often. Listen to what He says to the rich man in hell."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Abraham saith unto him, They have Moses and the prophets; let them hear them. And he said, Nay, father Abraham: but if one went unto them from the dead, they will repent. And he said unto him, If they hear not Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded, though one rose from the dead.","ref":"Luke 16:29-31"},{"kind":"para","text":"A rule of thumb that I’ve learned, and I do believe it’s true, is that if there are names mentioned in the story, it’s not a parable; it’s a real event. Parables don’t have names in them. This is the only “parable” where Jesus mentions specific names. What’s even more interesting about this story is that one did rise from the dead, and he was seen by thousands of people before His ascension, yet many still didn’t believe."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul’s testimony wouldn’t have been as valuable unless he went through the rest of his training, which was through his missionary journeys. I think that’s an important detail to note as well. Jesus could have sent Paul right back to Jerusalem to give his testimony after the three years of training he received from the disciples. Instead, God knew it was important for Paul to learn through experience rather than just through being told or taught for this particular cause. Paul was ready to go through whatever it took to get to this moment, and it was because of all of his past experiences leading up to this point."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Paul answered, What mean ye to weep and to break mine heart? for I am ready not to be bound only, but also to die at Jerusalem for the name of the Lord Jesus.","ref":"Acts 21:13 22 And they gave him audience unto this word, and then lifted up their voices, and said, Away with such a fellow from the earth: for it is not fit that he should live. 23 And as they cried out, and cast off their clothes, and threw dust into the air, 24 The chief captain commanded him to be brought into the castle, and bade that he should be examined by scourging; that he might know wherefore they cried so against him."},{"kind":"para","text":"So was Paul’s witness a waste of time? Of course not! If there was only one person in the crowd that day who listened and opened their heart to the Lord, it was well worth the flogging that Paul was about to receive. Even if no one listened, Paul could rejoice in the fact that he was able to witness to so many people, and now they are all without excuse. Paul didn’t say anything that was particularly controversial. He didn’t bash on their religious practices (like he could have and has done in the past). He didn’t insult them and call them “stiff-necked” like Stephen did. He could have said some really hurtful things and generally prodded and pried into their conscience’s matter. He didn’t do any of that."},{"kind":"para","text":"He simply shared his testimony, and they hated that. They hated it because they saw him as a hypocrite. They hated it because they knew there was a true, genuine change, and they didn’t want to believe it could be from Jesus Christ. Because if Paul was telling the truth, and this change really did take place through Christ, they would no longer have an excuse to continue to reject Christ as being God. That’s why people hate testimonies and people witnessing today."},{"kind":"para","text":"They don’t want to be wrong. If they are wrong, they will have to change. If they have to change, they have to give up their sin and ungodly living to please God. What they don’t understand is that they are in bondage to their sin, and they think that trusting in Christ will be miserable. But once they get to know Christ, they realize how much He means, and instead of doing things because you feel like you have to out of the sake of righteousness for Him, you will start to do things because you want to out of the sake of love for Him."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":30,"title":"Paul Is a Roman Citizen","entries":[{"start":25,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think we spoke about this before when Paul was imprisoned unlawfully in a different chapter. Roman law forbade beating a Roman citizen without a hearing or formal sentence. They were about to commit a crime, and they were about to do it without even considering the crimes they were about to commit. This further proves Paul’s testimony."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what sin does. It points out those who are living in righteousness and uses itself to get even at them through more unrighteousness, which proves the righteousness of those that you are trying to deny the proof of!"}]},{"start":27,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In those days, you could become a Roman citizen through sponsorship or payment from another Roman citizen. That would allow you citizenship to Rome, but it was kind of capped out when you started committing crimes or there was reason to beat you. However, those that were born as a Roman citizen had no cap, and they had extremely high status. 29 Then straightway they departed from him which should have examined him: and the chief captain also was afraid, after he knew that he was a Roman, and because he had bound him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Thankfully, the government didn’t just kill Paul and then try to cover it up. They did what was right by their own law and understanding and thought through the best course of action for this bad situation."},{"kind":"para","text":"30 On the morrow, because he would have known the certainty wherefore he was accused of the Jews, he loosed him from his bands, and commanded the chief priests and all their council to appear, and brought Paul down, and set him before them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul has a chance to witness to some very heavy hitters in government and world authority during that time. Roman leadership was some of the highest revered leadership that the world knew when Paul was there, and now Paul will literally be face to face with some very intimidating people. But, alas, we are left on yet another cliffhanger in which we will have to wait for the next chapter to see what happens."},{"kind":"para","text":"Again, get used to cliffhangers. Even the last chapter of this book ends on a cliffhanger."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":17,"end":18},{"start":25,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":28}],"verses":{"1":"Men, brethren, and fathers, hear ye my defence which I make now unto you.","2":"(And when they heard that he spake in the Hebrew tongue to them, they kept the more silence: and he saith,)","17":"And it came to pass, that, when I was come again to Jerusalem, even while I prayed in the temple, I was in a trance;","18":"And saw him saying unto me, Make haste, and get thee quickly out of Jerusalem: for they will not receive thy testimony concerning me.","25":"And as they bound him with thongs, Paul said unto the centurion that stood by, Is it lawful for you to scourge a man that is a Roman, and uncondemned?","26":"When the centurion heard that, he went and told the chief captain, saying, Take heed what thou doest: for this man is a Roman.","27":"Then the chief captain came, and said unto him, Tell me, art thou a Roman? He said, Yea.","28":"And the chief captain answered, With a great sum obtained I this freedom. And Paul said, But I was free born."}},
    "23": {"chapter":23,"intro":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"On the morrow, because he would have known the certainty wherefore he was accused of the Jews, he loosed him from his bands, and commanded the chief priests and all their council to appear, and brought Paul down, and set him before them.","ref":"Acts 22:30"}],"previously":["In chapter 21, we see Paul go to Jerusalem after being warned that he will be imprisoned if he does. Once he ignores the warnings, understanding the dangers that were coming, and being prepared to do whatever the Lord would allow him to, he ends up in a riot of men who are trying to kill him for his preaching (specifically preaching against supposedly rejecting circumcision) and is then arrested, being assumed from the arresting officers that he was an Egyptian man who was trying to cause another riot (that apparently happened not that long ago). Once it was found out that he was not the Egyptian, Paul had the opportunity to witness to the crowd (given permission by the guard). After sharing his testimony, and providing ample evidence to his conversion and pre-salvation lifestyle, the crowd hates him even more. He is then taken to be whipped and flogged, trying to get answers about who he is. Before they have the chance to do that, he explains to the guards that he is a Roman citizen, which would make the guards guilty of wounding and possibly killing one of their own, which would go against their own law. So they take Paul to some of the most prolific governmental and religious leaders in Rome to be questioned, which is where our last chapter leaves off and this one picks up!"],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":11,"title":"Paul Before the Council (Cont.)","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The basic truth was all that could be said during a time like this. Paul didn’t need some new speech or eloquence to the message that he already knew so well. He didn’t need to try and convince these men of who he was (particularly based on his citizenship, for they had already known and believed this testimony and were about to get an even clearer picture). Paul wanted to simply give the good news of salvation that he had already been giving to everyone else. He was innocent. There was nothing this council could hold against him. He would only, at this point, be punished for telling the whole truth."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, this is how it must be viewed by all of us if people are upset with us for telling them the gospel message. I have had friends and family who have threatened to kill me and blow up my church because of the message of Christ. Paul asks the best question in Galatians."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Am I therefore become your enemy, because I tell you the truth?","ref":"Galatians 4:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"People will love you if you tell them lies. They will love you if you flatter them with vain and meaningless things. But you can be assured that they will hate you for telling the truth. People always say, “The truth hurts!” That it does… Because it inflicts pain on our conscience. The truth reveals what our conscience has warned us about all along, but we’ve been too prideful and stubborn to pay attention to it. 2 And the high priest Ananias commanded them that stood by him to smite him on the mouth. 3 Then said Paul unto him, God shall smite thee, thou whited wall: for sittest thou to judge me after the law, and commandest me to be smitten contrary to the law?"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a controversial thing to say, but I will say it anyway because I believe it to be true. Religious tradition always leads to dwelling in hypocrisy. The biggest hypocrites of our day (and obviously even back then) are those who have a false zeal for a tradition that they follow simply because they were told to follow it. You won’t have to wait long to see a religionist break their own commands to others."},{"kind":"para","text":"A Catholic priest will tell everyone to live Godly, yet will be found in multiple cases of molestation, or every other kind of sin (they are far from perfect. I believe, in fact, that the more we try and become perfect on our own, the more we sin against the Lord). A Baptist preacher will tell everyone to stay away from adultery but will be found cheating on their spouse or addicted to pornography. A Presbyterian Pastor will say that if you are not living right for the Lord than you are not chosen by the Lord, yet will be living in multiple styles of idolatry, addiction, and unrepentant sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is not to say that they are all the same, but it is to say that if we are holding onto our traditions more than we are holding onto the grace of God, we will all be doomed to sinning and failure against the Lord. We must hold onto HIM as our utmost director. God must direct our path, not our church or religion!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Trust in the LORD with all thine heart; and lean not unto thine own understanding. In all thy ways acknowledge him, and he shall direct thy paths.","ref":"Proverbs 3:5-6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul was still speaking the truth by calling these religionists and traditionalists “whited walls” or “whitewashed,” which is just another term for “hypocrite.” Jesus uses this same metaphor in Matthew."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Woe unto you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! for ye are like unto whited sepulchres, which indeed appear beautiful outward, but are within full of dead men's bones, and of all uncleanness.","ref":"Matthew 23:27"},{"kind":"para","text":"Beautiful on the outside, but on the inside is nothing but rottenness… Ananias went against his priestly duty by allowing Paul to be stricken."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ye shall do no unrighteousness in judgment: thou shalt not respect the person of the poor, nor honour the person of the mighty: but in righteousness shalt thou judge thy neighbour.","ref":"Leviticus 19:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Before Ananias (as high priest) even judged Paul, he was stricken in the mouth."},{"kind":"para","text":"4 And they that stood by said, Revilest thou God's high priest?"},{"kind":"para","text":"These men proved that they were more faithful to the priest than they were to the God that the priest was supposedly serving."},{"kind":"para","text":"5 Then said Paul, I wist not, brethren, that he was the high priest: for it is written, Thou shalt not speak evil of the ruler of thy people."},{"kind":"para","text":"Some scholars believe that Paul just had really bad eyesight, which is why he didn’t know he was the high priest."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Brethren, I beseech you, be as I am; for I am as ye are: ye have not injured me at all. Ye know how through infirmity of the flesh I preached the gospel unto you at the first. And my temptation which was in my flesh ye despised not, nor rejected; but received me as an angel of God, even as Christ Jesus. Where is then the blessedness ye spake of? for I bear you record, that, if it had been possible, ye would have plucked out your own eyes, and have given them to me. Am I therefore become your enemy, because I tell you the truth?","ref":"Galatians 4:12-16"},{"kind":"para","text":"I find that a bit of a stretch, but I also don’t know Paul personally, so I couldn’t say. I assume that the reason that Paul didn’t know was more likely either because Paul didn’t think a high priest would be so dishonoring in his position, or because Paul simply didn’t recognize the high priest because he hadn’t been to Jerusalem for a very long time at this point. 6 But when Paul perceived that the one part were Sadducees, and the other Pharisees, he cried out in the council, Men and brethren, I am a Pharisee, the son of a Pharisee: of the hope and resurrection of the dead I am called in question."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul understands that this opportunity isn’t just an accident or a hazard. No, this was an opportunity to speak to some of the highest leaders possible and witness to them for the cause of Christ and the hope of salvation. 7 And when he had so said, there arose a dissension between the Pharisees and the Sadducees: and the multitude was divided."},{"kind":"para","text":"As if to say, “Oh yeah, I remember why we can’t hang out with each other.” 8 For the Sadducees say that there is no resurrection, neither angel, nor spirit: but the Pharisees confess both."},{"kind":"para","text":"We spoke about this before, and declared that the Sadducees’ ultimate representation of the afterlife was limited to the Torah (or the first five books of the Bible). They didn’t believe in an immortal soul or a presence in the afterlife. That’s what makes them sad, you see? (I’m sorry…) 9 And there arose a great cry: and the scribes that were of the Pharisees' part arose, and strove, saying, We find no evil in this man: but if a spirit or an angel hath spoken to him, let us not fight against God."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Pharisee’s language was sparking unrest with the Sadducees, which is funny because that is literally why Paul is in the predicament that he is currently in. He prided himself against the beliefs of the current time religionists, and they were mad at him. These people seem to just like to get mad. At this point, they aren’t even divided primarily about Christ being God, but rather the idea that Paul might have been given information because of an angel or some sort of spirit. Little did they understand (from his testimony which had already been given) that he literally spoke to Jesus himself. It wasn’t just a spirit, or a vision, or an angel of the Lord; it was the actual flesh and body of Jesus Christ that Paul spoke to. Paul seems to be awkwardly silent at this moment, though… Probably for the better, honestly. 10 And when there arose a great dissension, the chief captain, fearing lest Paul should have been pulled in pieces of them, commanded the soldiers to go down, and to take him by force from among them, and to bring him into the castle."},{"kind":"para","text":"False religion and bad traditions also breed disunity. Augustine said, “In essentials, unity. In non-essentials, liberty. In all things, charity.” D.L. Moody said, “I have never yet known the Spirit of God to work where the Lord’s people were divided.” C.H. Spurgeon said, “Unity without truth is hazardous.” 11 And the night following the Lord stood by him, and said, Be of good cheer, Paul: for as thou hast testified of me in Jerusalem, so must thou bear witness also at Rome."},{"kind":"para","text":"Feeling the presence of the Lord in an otherwise discontented situation is all that a Christian can hope for. It is with the presence of God that we can all gain full confidence that what we are doing is worth it. If we don’t feel His presence and the multitude has been out to slaughter us, we will know that what we are doing is certainly not for the Lord. This reassurance gives Paul the privilege to get excited about speaking to the Romans, who are even higher up than these high priests in Jerusalem."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul could rest assured that no matter what happens at this point, God will keep His promise and get him to Rome. Even if it’s only a piece of Paul that gets there, he was assured he would be there alive."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":22,"title":"A Plot to Kill Paul","entries":[{"start":12,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These people were serious about their hate for Paul, and it was truly for no reason. Their hate had no foundation or basis; they were just hateful people. There are a lot of people like that in the world today. They don’t know why they hate people; they just do, especially if they are talking about something that they simply aren’t interested in. They know that people will not meet up to their standards of thinking or practice, so they just jump to hating that person without understanding that person."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul had it worse because he was not just being hated for preaching; he was being hated for believing in what he was preaching. The high priests and elders here were mostly Sadducees, which is why they were so upset with Paul and why these people came to them to get permission to take Paul and kill him whenever they were allowed."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is one of the only times that Paul’s family is mentioned. We know that Paul never married and never had children. He doesn’t seem to have an interest in starting a family for himself, although he certainly speaks about family a lot, including in Ephesians. Paul’s nephew was the only person liable at this point, and he did the right thing to let Paul know. It was certainly dangerous for him to be around Paul during this time, so this act should not be just glanced over. This is why I believe Luke thought it was valuable to include it in this book of the Bible. 17 Then Paul called one of the centurions unto him, and said, Bring this young man unto the chief captain: for he hath a certain thing to tell him. 18 So he took him, and brought him to the chief captain, and said, Paul the prisoner called me unto him, and prayed me to bring this young man unto thee, who hath something to say unto thee. 19 Then the chief captain took him by the hand, and went with him aside privately, and asked him, What is that thou hast to tell me? 20 And he said, The Jews have agreed to desire thee that thou wouldest bring down Paul to morrow into the council, as though they would enquire somewhat of him more perfectly. 21 But do not thou yield unto them: for there lie in wait for him of them more than forty men, which have bound themselves with an oath, that they will neither eat nor drink till they have killed him: and now are they ready, looking for a promise from thee. 22 So the chief captain then let the young man depart, and charged him, See thou tell no man that thou hast shewed these things to me."},{"kind":"para","text":"In America, we have patriots who look out for their own. They would be willing to go to great lengths for someone of their own country to secure them because they are of the same country. However, there are many here who would not be willing to look out for their own. Even in our own government, we would find plenty who would easily try and get rid of you simply because of your political view."},{"kind":"para","text":"The tribunes were serious about taking care of their own, no matter what their religious or political views were. They were trained for one job, and they were fulfilling that one job right now through the advice and confidence of Paul’s nephew."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":35,"title":"Paul Sent to Felix the Governor","entries":[{"start":23,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was safety found in the higher leadership, and the tribune knew this. The issue here was that Felix was a weak leader (which we will find out in the next chapter…). 25 And he wrote a letter after this manner: 26 Claudius Lysias unto the most excellent governor Felix sendeth greeting. 27 This man was taken of the Jews, and should have been killed of them: then came I with an army, and rescued him, having understood that he was a Roman. 28 And when I would have known the cause wherefore they accused him, I brought him forth into their council: 29 Whom I perceived to be accused of questions of their law, but to have nothing laid to his charge worthy of death or of bonds. 30 And when it was told me how that the Jews laid wait for the man, I sent straightway to thee, and gave commandment to his accusers also to say before thee what they had against him. Farewell."},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, Lysias had no charges against Paul. Everyone that complained simply complained against Paul’s questions regarding “Jewish law,” not “Roman law.” Through the rest of Acts, all the Roman officials testify to Paul’s innocence of any charges against him. 31 Then the soldiers, as it was commanded them, took Paul, and brought him by night to Antipatris."},{"kind":"para","text":"This trip would have been about sixty-two miles from where Paul currently was in Jerusalem to Caesarea, where he was supposed to finish going. They made it to Antipatris, which is about thirty-five miles during the night, and even that trip would have been difficult. Obviously not impossible. 32 On the morrow they left the horsemen to go with him, and returned to the castle:"},{"kind":"para","text":"The horseman would have much more strength and stamina to afford going further than the soldier on foot."},{"kind":"para","text":"33 Who, when they came to Caesarea and delivered the epistle to the governor, presented Paul also before him. 34 And when the governor had read the letter, he asked of what province he was. And when he understood that he was of Cilicia; 35 I will hear thee, said he, when thine accusers are also come. And he commanded him to be kept in Herod's judgment hall."},{"kind":"para","text":"If no one came to accuse him, Paul would be let go, and there would be nothing to charge Paul against (as if there would be anything anyway when the accusers actually do arrive). Paul is kept in a very secure place called Herod’s judgment hall, which is also the Roman governor’s quarters. We are left on somewhat of a cliffhanger once more because we have not read what will happen to Paul at this point. The end is drawing near, and it seems that all hope is lost for Paul, especially as he gets higher and higher up in the governmental leadership chain here in the next two chapters."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":12,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":23,"end":24}],"verses":{"1":"And Paul, earnestly beholding the council, said, Men and brethren, I have lived in all good conscience before God until this day.","12":"And when it was day, certain of the Jews banded together, and bound themselves under a curse, saying that they would neither eat nor drink till they had killed Paul.","13":"And they were more than forty which had made this conspiracy.","14":"And they came to the chief priests and elders, and said, We have bound ourselves under a great curse, that we will eat nothing until we have slain Paul.","15":"Now therefore ye with the council signify to the chief captain that he bring him down unto you to morrow, as though ye would enquire something more perfectly concerning him: and we, or ever he come near, are ready to kill him.","16":"And when Paul's sister's son heard of their lying in wait, he went and entered into the castle, and told Paul.","23":"And he called unto him two centurions, saying, Make ready two hundred soldiers to go to Caesarea, and horsemen threescore and ten, and spearmen two hundred, at the third hour of the night;","24":"And provide them beasts, that they may set Paul on, and bring him safe unto Felix the governor."}},
    "24": {"chapter":24,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 24"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: After Paul had been imprisoned, there was word from his nephew that the unbelievers were waiting for Paul to be released to kill him. Paul’s nephew then warns Paul, who sends him to the captain of the guards to warn them, who is then determined to protect Paul over this matter (seeing as Paul was a Roman citizen). Paul is then sent to the governor’s house (Felix at this time) in Caesarea to be protected from the people who are attempting to assassinate Paul. Felix allows him to stay in his quarters to keep him safe, awaiting his accusers to come and charge him."}],"previously":["After Paul had been imprisoned, there was word from his nephew that the unbelievers were waiting for Paul to be released to kill him. Paul’s nephew then warns Paul, who sends him to the captain of the guards to warn them, who is then determined to protect Paul over this matter (seeing as Paul was a Roman citizen). Paul is then sent to the governor’s house (Felix at this time) in Caesarea to be protected from the people who are attempting to assassinate him. Felix allows him to stay in his quarters to keep him safe, awaiting his accusers to come and charge him."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":9,"title":"The Accusation","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here comes Ananias the “high priest” who isn’t much of a priest as we saw in the last chapter."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the high priest Ananias commanded them that stood by him to smite him on the mouth. Then said Paul unto him, God shall smite thee, thou whited wall: for sittest thou to judge me after the law, and commandest me to be smitten contrary to the law?","ref":"Acts 23:2-3"},{"kind":"para","text":"The orator in this verse was probably somewhat like a lawyer by today’s standards. It took Ananias about five days to finally figure out what had happened to Paul, and he wasn’t about to just let this go. The religionists of that day were extremely zealous against the Christian faith, so much to the point that they were willing to lose their lives if it meant that the Christians would also lose theirs. 2 And when he was called forth, Tertullus began to accuse him, saying, Seeing that by thee we enjoy great quietness, and that very worthy deeds are done unto this nation by thy providence, 3 We accept it always, and in all places, most noble Felix, with all thankfulness."},{"kind":"para","text":"Like any good lawyer, Tertullus tries to flatter the judge so that he can be more easily swayed. Felix was a crooked governor who could be easily bribed, so what Tertullus says about it being “peaceful” for them was actually not true. Felix had one of the least peaceful terms of any Roman administrator at that time. The Jews hated him. He was better known for taking bribes than helping the Jews. It’s believed that Tertullus was more than likely a Gentile, and that would be more favorable to the governor than a Jewish orator. 4 Notwithstanding, that I be not further tedious unto thee, I pray thee that thou wouldest hear us of thy clemency a few words."},{"kind":"para","text":"In other words, “We really enjoy scratching your back, and we are asking you for this one opportunity to scratch our backs, seeing as we have done so much back scratching for you.” (The polite way of putting it.) 5 For we have found this man a pestilent fellow, and a mover of sedition among all the Jews throughout the world, and a ringleader of the sect of the Nazarenes: 6 Who also hath gone about to profane the temple: whom we took, and would have judged according to our law."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are three accusations that Tertullius tries to throw against Paul. One was causing riots. One was for being a “ringleader” of the Christians, speaking against their religious philosophies. The last charge is for profaning the temple. As we see in the coming verses, Paul agrees to the second charge, but denies the first and third charges (the second charge was the only true one). 7 But the chief captain Lysias came upon us, and with great violence took him away out of our hands,"},{"kind":"para","text":"Many Bible versions take this verse out. There’s a lot of argument as to what should be included in Scripture and what shouldn’t. This verse certainly gives assertion to the fact that the testimony from Tertullius is a bit over-dramatized, and fits with the whole narrative of the false accusations that he was trying to present to Paul. It’s a seemingly small verse to question, but it’s one that is questioned nevertheless."},{"kind":"para","text":"Usually when a verse is taken out, it’s because of one of two reasons."},{"kind":"para","text":"It doesn’t exist in the oldest manuscripts we have (which is a really lame argument… Especially because we don’t have the original copies of the manuscripts to give account for that)."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was already mentioned somewhere else close to this chapter of scripture which is an even WORSE argument than the previous. Repetitiveness helps breed understanding."},{"kind":"para","text":"I won’t get too deep into the differences between the Textus Receptus, Majority Text, and Critical Text, or formal equivalency versus dynamic equivalency… It’s all just apples and oranges in the end… It would really draw away from the whole premise of this chapter, which is kind of funny seeing as that is what Tertullius is literally trying to do here with the governor Felix. He sprinkled a part of the truth in with all of his lies."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even more importantly about this verse is that Lysias is thrown under the bus, which will affect Paul’s testimony later (without this verse here, we don’t really get a clear understanding as to why Lysias’ testimony really matters all that much). 8 Commanding his accusers to come unto thee: by examining of whom thyself mayest take knowledge of all these things, whereof we accuse him."},{"kind":"para","text":"He will find out the truth in all honesty, but even if he does, his judgement won’t be just when he is easily bribed and flattered. The most important issue that would be taken into account is the accusation of starting riots (which, of course, Paul is innocent of). They were serious about keeping their authority over the people at all costs. 9 And the Jews also assented, saying that these things were so."},{"kind":"para","text":"They didn’t care about the truth. They just cared about their religion."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":23,"title":"Paul’s Defense","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul isn’t going to try and flatter Felix. All Paul can do (and all Paul WILL do) is hope that Felix will judge based on the facts and evidence provided through the truth of Paul’s testimony, rather than the flattery of Tertullius. 11 Because that thou mayest understand, that there are yet but twelve days since I went up to Jerusalem for to worship. 12 And they neither found me in the temple disputing with any man, neither raising up the people, neither in the synagogues, nor in the city:"},{"kind":"para","text":"The first thing that Paul addresses is the main issue that would certainly be cause for imprisonment, which was the accusation of stirring riots. He refutes this issue by stating the truth and allowing the fact that his time of worship is actually witnessed by others and accounted for. 13 Neither can they prove the things whereof they now accuse me."},{"kind":"para","text":"Ultimately, this is a great truth that Paul brings up. Even if Paul was specifically guilty of doing any of these things, the Jews had no proof of it. In fact, because Paul wasn’t doing the things his accusers are trying to pin on him, there’s more proof against his accusers than there is for his accusers."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For we can do nothing against the truth, but for the truth.","ref":"2 Corinthians 13:8 14 But this I confess unto thee, that after the way which they call heresy, so worship I the God of my fathers, believing all things which are written in the law and in the prophets: 15 And have hope toward God, which they themselves also allow, that there shall be a resurrection of the dead, both of the just and unjust."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was the one thing that Paul couldn’t deny. He was a follower of Christ, and he wasn’t ashamed of it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For I am not ashamed of the gospel of Christ: for it is the power of God unto salvation to every one that believeth; to the Jew first, and also to the Greek.","ref":"Romans 1:16 16 And herein do I exercise myself, to have always a conscience void to offence toward God, and toward men."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a very sobering thought from Paul that is lacking in the church today. It is something we would most likely call “conviction.” Conviction involves having standards on what we do, think, see, or hear. Each person has a differing viewpoint of conviction, and God convicts others in certain areas. For example, where God may convict one person in terms of TV influences, He may not convict another. The same applies to alcohol, church preference, medications, music, influence, and more."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, we should all have a base level of conviction on specific matters, such as doctrine, specific locations, and keeping the law out of love for God rather than out of fear. However, that doesn’t mean we will all have the same necessities laid upon us through those convictions. For instance, some of us may choose to stay away from bars or alcohol if we have an addictive personality or a past issue with alcoholism. Others may not have that issue and find the privilege and necessity to give the gospel to people in those areas, if they feel it is risky."},{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s where I’m going with this: Most Christians get the term conviction confused with desertion. Our conviction should guide our behavior, not prevent us from being a witness. How will your lost family hear the gospel if you are deserting them due to using your conviction to determine your necessity to be around them? You have the ability to be around those who drink alcohol (unless you are an addict, in which case do not hang around others who participate in this), or to be around those who curse and use the Lord’s name, while you yourself abstain from those things, because you have conviction from the Holy Spirit keeping you from even desiring to be like them. Your friends can curse, but your conviction should keep you from it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let no corrupt communication proceed out of your mouth, but that which is good to the use of edifying, that it may minister grace unto the hearers.","ref":"Ephesians 4:29"},{"kind":"para","text":"Conviction should help you make right decisions in a culture that is constantly pursuing and pushing wrong things. 17 Now after many years I came to bring alms to my nation, and offerings."},{"kind":"para","text":"This gives credence to God and to those that are listening. He honored his nation, and he honored the leadership that God put into place. The best way a man can honor leadership is not by restricting worship to God, but by giving them a reason to worship God through your leadership."},{"kind":"para","text":"18 Whereupon certain Jews from Asia found me purified in the temple, neither with multitude, nor with tumult. 19 Who ought to have been here before thee, and object, if they had ought against me."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is funny. The people that really wanted to accuse Paul weren’t even present for this trial. All of this was started by those accusers from Asia, and they weren’t even there to plead their case. Instead, we have bystanders and word-of-mouth testimony. 20 Or else let these same here say, if they have found any evil doing in me, while I stood before the council, 21 Except it be for this one voice, that I cried standing among them, Touching the resurrection of the dead I am called in question by you this day."},{"kind":"para","text":"The real issue here was based on the resurrection, not just the general possibility (though that was obviously an issue in our last chapter), but rather the truth and reality of Jesus’ resurrection. This is the same issue even still…"},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve had many conversations with atheists on the issue of the accuracy of the Bible. Many atheists would attest that the Bible is real and accurate in every area (including Jonah being swallowed by a whale and thrown up on the shore of the city he was supposed to go to originally), but the one area they must draw a line in their affirmation of the Bible is in the resurrection of Christ. They simply can’t believe that to be true. They will try to circumstance or “scientifically” explain away the resurrection, or just reject the whole account altogether, stating that Jesus never resurrected. There are too many flaws in that belief, more flaws than a believer without physical or tangible proof that Jesus resurrected."},{"kind":"para","text":"An empty tomb isn’t enough for a nonbeliever to accept the legitimacy of Jesus’ resurrection, but it’s enough for us. The eye-witness accounts of more than five hundred people are not enough to accept the legitimacy of Jesus’ resurrection, but they’re enough for us. The accuracy and proof of God continuously keeping His covenant promises are not enough to accept the legitimacy of Jesus’ resurrection, but they’re enough for us. The proof of the church consistently existing today (though many have tried to snuff it out multiple times over) is not enough to accept the legitimacy of Jesus’ resurrection, but it’s enough for us. The Holy Spirit changing a person’s life through the acceptance of the resurrection of Christ is not enough to accept the legitimacy of Jesus’ resurrection, but it’s enough for us. Let’s not forget…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"…he shewed himself alive after his passion by many infallible proofs…","ref":"Acts 1:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"The whole New Testament is a proof of Jesus’ resurrection, which won’t ever be enough for a nonbeliever, because they can’t accept the truth of Scripture and the proof that God continuously shoves in their faces. 22 And when Felix heard these things, having more perfect knowledge of that way, he deferred them, and said, When Lysias the chief captain shall come down, I will know the uttermost of your matter."},{"kind":"para","text":"Lysias was the only one that was mentioned by Tertullius that would have a trustworthy account of what took place. Lysias does not have a testimony of being part of the way, but only being part of the leadership under which Paul was detained. Interestingly enough, though, Felix had some idea of what Paul was speaking about pertaining to “the Way” of Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"23 And he commanded a centurion to keep Paul, and to let him have liberty, and that he should forbid none of his acquaintance to minister or come unto him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul was basically on house arrest. He had some freedoms still, I believe because he was a Roman citizen. That fact was working well in Paul’s favor, which God knew would be the case far before Paul was even born."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":26,"title":"Paul Before Felix Again","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is huge. It’s assumed that Felix knew about “the Way” simply because of his wife, who was a Jew and had knowledge about this particular stance on faith. Paul had the opportunity, and didn’t hesitate, to speak to them about Jesus Christ and what He did for them. He used the time he had to witness to some very powerful people."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, there are some hard buttons being pushed (anyone that rejects Christ will get upset when you speak to them about Christ). But it was still a great opportunity, and it’s a fascinating account. It’s not just said that they spoke about Jesus and His resurrection (which might have been spoken on specifically because that was where the biggest questions seemed to stem). It says they talked about righteousness, self-control, and the judgement of God. They had some really heavy and serious conversations on matters that would (prayerfully to Paul) eventually get them to think about their necessity for a Savior."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, Felix is easily swayed with money. But, as should be all of our mindsets, we don’t want to sway someone into the faith by bribery, but rather help them come to a heart of knowledge of Christ through necessity. If they are bribed into Christ (which many churches do today through prosperity preaching), then it will only be surface-level Christianity and it won’t last."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"title":"The Silent Two Years","entries":[{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Scholars say that Felix was removed from office in AD 60 for failing to deal properly with a dispute between the Jews and Gentiles in Caesarea. Paul wouldn’t have another chance to sway or speak about Christ with a leader for quite some time. Of course, Felix wanted to be liked by the masses rather than stand up for truth, so he never took Paul out of prison, though there was no point in keeping Paul there anymore. This is, again, somewhat of a cliffhanger (not as bad as the others). We will see the account, and toll in which being imprisoned takes on Paul in the coming chapter, and what an amazing opportunity Paul gets because of it."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":24,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":27}],"verses":{"1":"And after five days Ananias the high priest descended with the elders, and with a certain orator named Tertullus, who informed the governor against Paul.","10":"Then Paul, after that the governor had beckoned unto him to speak, answered, Forasmuch as I know that thou hast been of many years a judge unto this nation, I do the more cheerfully answer for myself:","24":"And after certain days, when Felix came with his wife Drusilla, which was a Jewess, he sent for Paul, and heard him concerning the faith in Christ.","25":"And as he reasoned of righteousness, temperance, and judgment to come, Felix trembled, and answered, Go thy way for this time; when I have a convenient season, I will call for thee.","26":"He hoped also that money should have been given him of Paul, that he might loose him: wherefore he sent for him the oftener, and communed with him.","27":"But after two years Porcius Festus came into Felix' room: and Felix, willing to shew the Jews a pleasure, left Paul bound."}},
    "25": {"chapter":25,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 25"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Last chapter we see Paul still in custody, trying to witness to Felix (the governor) at this time. Of course Felix doesn’t accept Christ or take kindly to what Paul says, and keeps him in custody, even after he is fired from his position, and Festus takes the reign from him. Now, in this chapter, we see Paul plead before Festus, after having spent two years in prison without a particular reason as to why."}],"previously":["Last chapter, we see Paul still in custody, trying to witness to Felix (the governor) at this time. Of course, Felix doesn’t accept Christ or take kindly to what Paul says, and keeps him in custody, even after he is fired from his position, and Festus takes the reins from him. Now, in this chapter, we see Paul plead before Festus, after having spent two years in prison without a particular reason as to why."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":9,"title":"Paul Before Festus","entries":[{"start":1,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, after some time, the Jews obviously haven’t forgotten their hatred for Paul. I don’t know about you, but if there was an issue with me and someone else (no matter what they might have done), if they hadn’t been in my life for more than a month, I probably would have forgotten about it or gotten over it. Not these Jews. They detested Paul and his message of Christ. They hated him with a literal passion and were still trying to sway the government over them to allow Paul to be taken to Jerusalem where they would be able to kill him a little easier. Of course, they didn’t outright say that was what they were going to do. Enticing a riot was illegal during this time (something they tried to blame Paul for in our previous chapter)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For we have found this man a pestilent fellow, and a mover of sedition among all the Jews throughout the world, and a ringleader of the sect of the Nazarenes:","ref":"Acts 24:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"These Jews were about to do what they tried to accuse Paul of doing (unsuccessfully, mind you) because they let their hate dictate how they moved rather than their faith they acclaimed to believe so strongly in. 4 But Festus answered, that Paul should be kept at Caesarea, and that he himself would depart shortly thither."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t believe Festus was purposely protecting Paul here. I think he unintentionally protected him by insisting that if a hearing is going to take place, it will specifically take place in his province in Caesarea, that way he could take care of the matters himself. Once again, he didn’t know that the Jews were anticipating Paul’s release to try and kill him. 5 Let them therefore, said he, which among you are able, go down with me, and accuse this man, if there be any wickedness in him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Festus may not have been informed of the multiple previous trials that Paul had already gone through. It seems Festus was going into this blind and was about to receive the same exact treatment from Paul as was previously given to so many others. Just because Paul had spent years in prison didn’t change his testimony or the truth of his imprisonment."},{"kind":"para","text":"In fact, I believe that Paul’s time in jail gave him more solidity to his faith and purpose, knowing that there would be an ultimate cause to come. Trials speak much louder for a testimony than words do. Your faith through difficulty will prove the evidence of the Holy Spirit more than simply telling people about the Holy Spirit of your faith. 6 And when he had tarried among them more than ten days, he went down unto Caesarea; and the next day sitting on the judgment seat commanded Paul to be brought."},{"kind":"para","text":"Thankfully, Festus didn’t just leave Paul or forget about this issue. With as long as Paul had been bound, it wouldn’t have been much to just ignore the issue even further. Eventually, Paul would just die of old age or some other thing. Festus wasn’t doing that though, and took the matter seriously."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe the Jews probably wouldn’t let Festus forget it, especially because the last governor was fired for not taking care of the issues among the Jews and Greeks, as we spoke about in our last chapter. 7 And when he was come, the Jews which came down from Jerusalem stood round about, and laid many and grievous complaints against Paul, which they could not prove."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Jews had more time to figure out what other things they could blame on Paul. But because of the longevity of Paul’s imprisonment, there was no way for the unbelieving Jews to prove anything that they were saying. 8 While he answered for himself, Neither against the law of the Jews, neither against the temple, nor yet against Caesar, have I offended any thing at all."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul was easily able to defend himself because their accusations were weak. The thing I love the most about a changed life of a believer is that no matter what this world will try and throw at you in hopes that it will stick and hurt you, it will not be able to land because Jesus has proved His testimony, through the Holy Ghost, in your life. Satan can remind you of who you used to be forever, but Jesus had to say three words to prove His love to us, “…It is finished…” 9 But Festus, willing to do the Jews a pleasure, answered Paul, and said, Wilt thou go up to Jerusalem, and there be judged of these things before me?"},{"kind":"para","text":"The easy thing for Festus to do would be to just give up on one of his own. The Jews would be content with that, without proof of it, but it would cause upheaval for the Roman citizens who would begin to feel unprotected by their government, knowing that they would be willing to just give up to the Jews instantly for personal profit or gain."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":12,"title":"Paul Appeals to Caesar","entries":[{"start":10,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a bold strategy from Paul. He’s taking this to Festus’ manager, which would be Caesar. Who would be of higher ranking authority than Caesar at this time? Because Paul was a Roman citizen, he had every right to bring this issue before Caesar. Paul knew that if he had done anything wrong, he had no case or response to receiving the death sentence. However, Paul was innocent, and the charges laid against him couldn’t be proven because they didn’t actually happen."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If Paul was going to die, what better way to die than at the opportunity to witness one of the highest-ranking officials of that time and day? During this time, as many of you might know (however), the Caesar (which is a title for a Roman emperor) was Nero. The first five years of his reign were relatively peaceful. The events that occurred through this conversation with Nero may be what ultimately led to Nero becoming the monster we all know and have been assured of."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":27,"title":"Paul Before Agrippa and Bernice","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Agrippa the King was the son of Herod Agrippa I, also a great-grandson of Herod the Great. Bernice would be Agrippa’s sister, and she would go with him to most of the meetings and orders for which he had rule. 14 And when they had been there many days, Festus declared Paul's cause unto the king, saying, There is a certain man left in bonds by Felix: 15 About whom, when I was at Jerusalem, the chief priests and the elders of the Jews informed me, desiring to have judgment against him. 16 To whom I answered, It is not the manner of the Romans to deliver any man to die, before that he which is accused have the accusers face to face, and have licence to answer for himself concerning the crime laid against him. 17 Therefore, when they were come hither, without any delay on the morrow I sat on the judgment seat, and commanded the man to be brought forth."},{"kind":"para","text":"This recap is accurate enough. If Festus wanted to, he could have lied just like all the Jews were trying to do just to get Paul off his plate and off his problem. However, because of the appeal that Paul desired from Caesar, there were too many witnesses for Festus to lie about the situation. Not to mention, I think they kind of enjoyed the drama. 18 Against whom when the accusers stood up, they brought none accusation of such things as I supposed: 19 But had certain questions against him of their own superstition, and of one Jesus, which was dead, whom Paul affirmed to be alive."},{"kind":"para","text":"Festus (obviously) understood that there was no reason for Paul to be imprisoned for anything against Roman law. He knew this was all just a religious dispute. He might have wanted something more worthy of his time, but ultimately it had nothing to do with him or his household. 20 And because I doubted of such manner of questions, I asked him whether he would go to Jerusalem, and there be judged of these matters. 21 But when Paul had appealed to be reserved unto the hearing of Augustus, I commanded him to be kept till I might send him to Caesar."},{"kind":"para","text":"Festus didn’t really want to deal with the issue at hand, but only to keep peace with the Jews. Of course, their accusations were nothing he could even appeal to the Jews about, because while they disagreed with Paul about the resurrection, they couldn’t prove that Paul was wrong, and Paul couldn’t force them to believe in anything. Really, it just boiled down to pride in the end. The Jews just wanted to be self-righteous, and they weren’t able to be made righteous according to the God that Paul was preaching (which was one that had openly agreed that there is none righteous)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Behold, I was shapen in iniquity; and in sin did my mother conceive me.","ref":"Psalm 51:5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If thou doest well, shalt thou not be accepted? and if thou doest not well, sin lieth at the door. And unto thee shall be his desire, and thou shalt rule over him.","ref":"Genesis 4:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"The truth is, they had not kept the law like they claimed they did, and Paul pointed out their sin through the light of the gospel, and the gospel proved that their sin left them without excuse. 22 Then Agrippa said unto Festus, I would also hear the man myself. To morrow, said he, thou shalt hear him."},{"kind":"para","text":"The king is going to hear Paul’s defense, and Paul will give him a thought on eternity and truth. Paul is really making strides here, even though it looks bleak and hopeless because he is still imprisoned. Paul knows that the purpose of his imprisonment is for the glory of God (ultimately), and he understood the consequences going into this would be worth it if the highest up of people in their government could hear even the smallest hint of the gospel. 23 And on the morrow, when Agrippa was come, and Bernice, with great pomp, and was entered into the place of hearing, with the chief captains, and principal men of the city, at Festus' commandment Paul was brought forth."},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems either Agrippa was excited to finally hear from this Paul man that he had heard so much about, or Agrippa just likes drama. This has turned into a telenovela, but there will be truth proclaimed in the end that will honor Christ. 24 And Festus said, King Agrippa, and all men which are here present with us, ye see this man, about whom all the multitude of the Jews have dealt with me, both at Jerusalem, and also here, crying that he ought not to live any longer. 25 But when I found that he had committed nothing worthy of death, and that he himself hath appealed to Augustus, I have determined to send him. 26 Of whom I have no certain thing to write unto my lord. Wherefore I have brought him forth before you, and specially before thee, O king Agrippa, that, after examination had, I might have somewhat to write. 27 For it seemeth to me unreasonable to send a prisoner, and not withal to signify the crimes laid against him."},{"kind":"para","text":"They needed some reason to bring this matter before the emperor; otherwise, it would just be a waste of time to Nero. Of course, it really was a waste of time to the world to see Paul take it to this extreme, but because of the issue that Paul has found himself in, it was the only way to save his life and hopefully be set free and protected. However, God has used this so far to continuously move up the line to people Paul would have never had the opportunity to speak with had it been in any other way."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, we are left on another cliffhanger (this one a little more upsetting, seeing as we know that Paul has the ability to witness to one of the most powerful people of that day (Nero)). We will see the unraveling of this book as Paul continues to be faithful to the Lord in the face of some of the scariest leaders of that age, and because of Paul’s faith, he will have the boldness to proclaim the truth with such fervency and compassion that would make anyone listening to this witness think about their own eternities."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":10,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13}],"verses":{"1":"Now when Festus was come into the province, after three days he ascended from Caesarea to Jerusalem.","2":"Then the high priest and the chief of the Jews informed him against Paul, and besought him,","3":"And desired favour against him, that he would send for him to Jerusalem, laying wait in the way to kill him.","10":"Then said Paul, I stand at Caesar's judgment seat, where I ought to be judged: to the Jews have I done no wrong, as thou very well knowest.","11":"For if I be an offender, or have committed any thing worthy of death, I refuse not to die: but if there be none of these things whereof these accuse me, no man may deliver me unto them. I appeal unto Caesar.","12":"Then Festus, when he had conferred with the council, answered, Hast thou appealed unto Caesar? unto Caesar shalt thou go.","13":"And after certain days king Agrippa and Bernice came unto Caesarea to salute Festus."}},
    "26": {"chapter":26,"intro":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"And now I say unto you, Refrain from these men, and let them alone: for if this counsel or this work be of men, it will come to nought: But if it be of God, ye cannot overthrow it; lest haply ye be found even to fight against God.","ref":"Acts 5:38-39"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, they can’t overthrow it, and God has more of a plan for Paul than just debating with non-believing Jews. Paul has slowly been working up the governmental chain to reach the top government official of Rome, leaving us off (last time) with speaking to King Agrippa and requesting an audience with the Caesar (Nero) himself!"},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Paul interacting with the king and pleading his case after having spent quite some time in prison for literally no reason (other than Felix was a joke and wanted to get glory…). Before Agrippa allows Paul to see Nero, he first wants to hear Paul’s defense to see if it’s even worth the Caesar’s time."}],"previously":["In the last few chapters, things haven’t been looking great for Paul and his circumstances. He’s been imprisoned, neglected, and continuously mistreated. The Jewish unbelievers are not willing to let their pride go and just let Paul babble on. In all honesty, if the things that Paul is saying aren’t true, then the Jewish unbelievers have nothing to worry about."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":11,"title":"Paul’s Defense Before Agrippa","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This, of course, isn’t Paul’s first rodeo in defending himself. He’s had to do this three times now, and he gets the gist of what needs to be said. You will notice that his account doesn’t change. What does change, however (in this particular defense), is Paul’s depth of the gospel. He really seems to expound the gospel before the king here. 2 I think myself happy, king Agrippa, because I shall answer for myself this day before thee touching all the things whereof I am accused of the Jews:"},{"kind":"para","text":"Fortunate, not because he has to make a defense, or because he believes the king will side with him, but fortunate because he gets to share the gospel with a very highly revered person."},{"kind":"para","text":"3 Especially because I know thee to be expert in all customs and questions which are among the Jews: wherefore I beseech thee to hear me patiently. 4 My manner of life from my youth, which was at the first among mine own nation at Jerusalem, know all the Jews; 5 Which knew me from the beginning, if they would testify, that after the most straitest sect of our religion I lived a Pharisee. 6 And now I stand and am judged for the hope of the promise made of God, unto our fathers: 7 Unto which promise our twelve tribes, instantly serving God day and night, hope to come. For which hope's sake, king Agrippa, I am accused of the Jews."},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, most that are hearing this testimony again have heard it time and time again. They will not receive any new stances by Paul because they have tried to bully him, but the most important question Paul asks is found in the next verse."},{"kind":"para","text":"8 Why should it be thought a thing incredible with you, that God should raise the dead?"},{"kind":"para","text":"If God is God, then raising anyone from the dead should be nothing of a surprise. In fact, it shouldn’t surprise us that God can do anything at all."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For with God nothing shall be impossible.","ref":"Luke 1:37"},{"kind":"para","text":"The only way that we can come to a conclusion that God is not capable of doing anything He very well pleases would be to say that there is no God. That’s why the atheists say what they say. Not because they believe there’s no God, but because they don’t want to believe God can do whatever He pleases."},{"kind":"para","text":"If God does do whatever pleases Him, then why would He allow rape, murder, pain, or suffering? This question holds no weight if they don’t believe in a morally just God. Their morals would say that these things are okay because they have no God to justify their actions on. Their god says that truth is relative, while the Bible says God is truth."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus saith unto him, I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me.","ref":"John 14:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Howbeit when he, the Spirit of truth, is come, he will guide you into all truth: for he shall not speak of himself; but whatsoever he shall hear, that shall he speak: and he will shew you things to come.","ref":"John 16:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Truth doesn’t bend according to our ideas or philosophies. If it did, then rapists would say that rape is okay according to their truth. We have to have a moral standard to live by, and God can be that ONLY moral standard because He is truth! Even furthermore, God doesn’t allow murderers and rapists to get away with what they have done without consequence, either through repentance and salvation, or through eternal hellfire. That will be up to the Lord in that day. We should believe that God is able, and willing, to do ONLY what is right, and true. If we can’t believe that, then we are seriously just cheapening the power of God. 9 I verily thought with myself, that I ought to do many things contrary to the name of Jesus of Nazareth. 10 Which thing I also did in Jerusalem: and many of the saints did I shut up in prison, having received authority from the chief priests; and when they were put to death, I gave my voice against them. 11 And I punished them oft in every synagogue, and compelled them to blaspheme; and being exceedingly mad against them, I persecuted them even unto strange cities."},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, Paul was under the same mindset as these unbelieving Jews. He would have agreed with them if Christ hadn’t changed his life drastically. Paul was a new man, and because of the newness that Christ gives, he couldn’t lie about what was true. This, ultimately, is what gave Paul wisdom and boldness."},{"kind":"para","text":"Oftentimes, when we go out soul-winning, or we try to preach the gospel to others, we shell up and cower because we don’t want to be attacked, or asked a question we don’t know the answer to. If we truly know God, then it doesn’t matter what people throw our way, we cannot lie and say “You’re right, maybe I don’t know God.” Unless you truly don’t know God, then (in which case) you can’t speak any kind of truth about Him."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":32,"title":"Paul Tells of His Conversion","entries":[{"start":12,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We’ve heard Paul’s testimony so many times at this point that it should be painfully obvious. One of the things that Paul doesn’t know, however, is that God had already revealed why Paul was in the position he was in. It’s something we haven’t really talked about because I wanted to wait until this point to bring it back up (after seeing all that Paul has gone through)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the Lord said unto him, Go thy way: for he is a chosen vessel unto me, to bear my name before the Gentiles, and kings, and the children of Israel: For I will shew him how great things he must suffer for my name's sake.","ref":"Acts 9:15-16"},{"kind":"para","text":"This revelation was given to Ananias (a disciple) so that he could help give Paul his sight back. Of course, the Lord would give Paul his sight any time He wanted to, but He chose Ananias for a specific reason: to help initiate the testimony for the remaining disciples (or apostles) to accept Paul (even though they would be seen scared of Paul and not believing he was truly one of them). In order to get Ananias to obey, God tells him that Paul will suffer for His name’s sake, but only through the preaching of the gospel to Gentiles, kings, and the children of Israel. That is what Paul is doing now."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul didn’t know it though, because this wasn’t told to Paul; this was only told to Ananias, and if you read back through the account, Ananias doesn’t let Paul know that he will be suffering for the name of the Lord (not as if it would even matter anyway, because I know Paul truly believed in Christ by what he saw and was secure in the truth of his salvation). His salvation testimony would be stronger than he even realized, and it’s still just as strong today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Many don’t have a “Paul Conversion” or a “Damascus Road” testimony, but we all can relate to Paul. He desired to do one thing, and then Christ met with him and brought him into a different direction. Paul was certainly suffering for the sake of Christ, but He was doing it with joy, willing and ready. Why? Because He knew he deserved a lot worse than what He was getting."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For I reckon that the sufferings of this present time are not worthy to be compared with the glory which shall be revealed in us.","ref":"Romans 8:18 19 Whereupon, O king Agrippa, I was not disobedient unto the heavenly vision: 20 But shewed first unto them of Damascus, and at Jerusalem, and throughout all the coasts of Judaea, and then to the Gentiles, that they should repent and turn to God, and do works meet for repentance. 21 For these causes the Jews caught me in the temple, and went about to kill me. 22 Having therefore obtained help of God, I continue unto this day, witnessing both to small and great, saying none other things than those which the prophets and Moses did say should come: 23 That Christ should suffer, and that he should be the first that should rise from the dead, and should shew light unto the people, and to the Gentiles."},{"kind":"para","text":"In other words, if these people knew their scriptures better, they would know that the things in which Paul is preaching are the same things the Old Testament prophets and those of the faith were preaching (which the unbelieving Jews held in high reverence). Their message was the same! The only difference was that they promised of a Messiah and Savior who would eventually come, and Paul promises of a Messiah and Savior who has already come. Both point to the same person and both clarify the exact same things."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Who hath believed our report? and to whom is the arm of the LORD revealed? For he shall grow up before him as a tender plant, and as a root out of a dry ground: he hath no form nor comeliness; and when we shall see him, there is no beauty that we should desire him. He is despised and rejected of men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief: and we hid as it were our faces from him; he was despised, and we esteemed him not. Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows: yet we did esteem him stricken, smitten of God, and afflicted. But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed. All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and the LORD hath laid on him the iniquity of us all. He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth: he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his mouth. He was taken from prison and from judgment: and who shall declare his generation? for he was cut off out of the land of the living: for the transgression of my people was he stricken. And he made his grave with the wicked, and with the rich in his death; because he had done no violence, neither was any deceit in his mouth. Yet it pleased the LORD to bruise him; he hath put him to grief: when thou shalt make his soul an offering for sin, he shall see his seed, he shall prolong his days, and the pleasure of the LORD shall prosper in his hand. He shall see of the travail of his soul, and shall be satisfied: by his knowledge shall my righteous servant justify many; for he shall bear their iniquities. Therefore will I divide him a portion with the great, and he shall divide the spoil with the strong; because he hath poured out his soul unto death: and he was numbered with the transgressors; and he bare the sin of many, and made intercession for the transgressors.” Isaiah 53"},{"kind":"para","text":"Both are treated with disrespect and both thrown into prison on numerous occasions for the same messages preached. Our world loves to reject Christ, yet they don’t want to reject religion. They agree with everything the Bible says, but they will not agree on Jesus Christ. Christ is the focal point of the whole book, and so if we reject Christ, we reject God, and we reject religion, and we reject truth, and righteousness, and good, and holiness, and perfection. We reject anything that is pure and proven. That is why Paul clarifies this here. His message is not new. Only the people hearing the message are new. 24 And as he thus spake for himself, Festus said with a loud voice, Paul, thou art beside thyself; much learning doth make thee mad."},{"kind":"para","text":"An interruption from Festus to try and take away the notion that Paul really knows what he’s talking about. As if Paul hasn’t spent enough time thinking and preparing to speak about this exact situation. If Paul were out of his mind, he would have changed his mind at some point during his stint in prison, or even the many years before when he was witnessing and preaching to all the cities around him. Obviously, Paul was more sane now than he ever was before in his life. The only obvious answer to a convicted conscience is that the person giving truth must be crazy rather than the one believing all the lies about themselves. 25 But he said, I am not mad, most noble Festus; but speak forth the words of truth and soberness. 26 For the king knoweth of these things, before whom also I speak freely: for I am persuaded that none of these things are hidden from him; for this thing was not done in a corner. 27 King Agrippa, believest thou the prophets? I know that thou believest."},{"kind":"para","text":"Agrippa was a faithful Jew. If he denied anything that Paul was saying, it would prove that he rejects the Old Testament prophecies and the truth that he claims to believe in. Then again, if he agrees with Paul, he will be hated by the surrounding Jews, and it could mean an uproar and a riot. He had to be precise here. 28 Then Agrippa said unto Paul, Almost thou persuadest me to be a Christian."},{"kind":"para","text":"Yes! If only we could persuade anyone to look truth in the face and accept it as truth. 29 And Paul said, I would to God, that not only thou, but also all that hear me this day, were both almost, and altogether such as I am, except these bonds."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is so funny to me. I believe Paul meant it as a joke. This is something I would have done because I don’t do well with conflict. I would answer truthfully, and then add a little lighthearted joke at the end in hopes that it will relieve some of the tension surrounding the situation. Paul says, “I hope you are just like me… Except for being in prison, I mean…” 30 And when he had thus spoken, the king rose up, and the governor, and Bernice, and they that sat with them: 31 And when they were gone aside, they talked between themselves, saying, This man doeth nothing worthy of death or of bonds. 32 Then said Agrippa unto Festus, This man might have been set at liberty, if he had not appealed unto Caesar."},{"kind":"para","text":"Yeah, well, if he hadn’t appealed to Caesar, then he would most likely be dead. Paul knew that if he wasn’t released in two years, the chances of him being released were slim. The appeal was his only hope at a chance for freedom, or at the very least to go down having spoken to the highest authority in the land. We won’t know though because we are left on yet another cliffhanger!"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":12,"end":18}],"verses":{"1":"Then Agrippa said unto Paul, Thou art permitted to speak for thyself. Then Paul stretched forth the hand, and answered for himself:","12":"Whereupon as I went to Damascus with authority and commission from the chief priests,","13":"At midday, O king, I saw in the way a light from heaven, above the brightness of the sun, shining round about me and them which journeyed with me.","14":"And when we were all fallen to the earth, I heard a voice speaking unto me, and saying in the Hebrew tongue, Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me? it is hard for thee to kick against the pricks.","15":"And I said, Who art thou, Lord? And he said, I am Jesus whom thou persecutest.","16":"But rise, and stand upon thy feet: for I have appeared unto thee for this purpose, to make thee a minister and a witness both of these things which thou hast seen, and of those things in the which I will appear unto thee;","17":"Delivering thee from the people, and from the Gentiles, unto whom now I send thee,","18":"To open their eyes, and to turn them from darkness to light, and from the power of Satan unto God, that they may receive forgiveness of sins, and inheritance among them which are sanctified by faith that is in me."}},
    "27": {"chapter":27,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 27"},{"kind":"para","text":"Groups: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In chapter 22, Paul speaks before the crowd at Jerusalem about his conversion and testimony in regards to the Lord and His resurrection, to cover the sins of the world. Obviously, people hate that, and they desired to kill Paul, so the tribune took Paul into custody to give him a beating, to find out what he was really up too, which is when they find out he is a Roman citizen. Being a Roman citizen allows him special rights, so that he is able to have a proper trial, and while he is in prison can have a little more access to people that noncitizens wouldn’t have the chance to obtain (like visits from his nephew, who would find out about the plot from the Jews to kill Paul which would lead to Paul’s transfer over to the governor at the time (Felix)’s house."},{"kind":"para","text":"In chapter 24 Paul talks to Felix about the Lord and has great reasoning with Felix, until Felix is thrown out of his office by some shady dealings, and is replaced with Festus. Felix, however, leaves Paul in prison for two years to try and please the Jews. Once Festus tries to get a hold of Paul and get rid of this issue (by simply giving him over to the Jews) he realizes there’s no reason for the Jews, or for the guards to keep Paul in prison. Trying to figure out the matter on his own, he thinks the trial would be best suited in Jerusalem (which would ultimately give Paul over to the Jews). Paul decides his best bet is to appeal to Caesar, and Festus must comply to his appeal, because Paul is a Roman citizen."},{"kind":"para","text":"Before complying, however, King Agrippa wanted to see the matter himself, and in chapter 26 Paul defends himself before the king and gives his testimony once more. The king see’s that there’s no reason for his imprisonment and says, “He could have been set free if he didn’t appeal to Caesar” which isn’t true seeing as Paul was continuously in jail even without appealing to Caesar."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul made a wise move to appeal to Caesar (or Nero at the time) because it meant that he would have the opportunity to bring it up to the most authoritative person in the land. Every chapter so far has ended on a cliffhanger. This chapter, however, has somewhat of a conclusion, but it still leaves you wanting more."}],"previously":["In chapter 22, Paul speaks before the crowd at Jerusalem about his conversion and testimony in regards to the Lord and His resurrection, to cover the sins of the world. Obviously, people hate that, and they desired to kill Paul, so the tribune took Paul into custody to give him a beating, to find out what he was really up to, which is when they find out he is a Roman citizen. Being a Roman citizen allows him special rights, so that he is able to have a proper trial, and while he is in prison can have a little more access to people that noncitizens wouldn’t have the chance to obtain (like visits from his nephew, who would find out about the plot from the Jews to kill Paul which would lead to Paul’s transfer over to the governor at the time (Felix’s) house). In chapter 24, Paul talks to Felix about the Lord and has great reasoning with Felix, until Felix is thrown out of his office by some shady dealings, and is replaced with Festus. Felix, however, leaves Paul in prison for two years to try and please the Jews. Once Festus tries to get a hold of Paul and get rid of this issue (by simply giving him over to the Jews), he realizes there’s no reason for the Jews, or for the guards to keep Paul in prison. Trying to figure out the matter on his own, he thinks the trial would be best suited in Jerusalem (which would ultimately give Paul over to the Jews). Paul decides his best bet is to appeal to Caesar, and Festus must comply to his appeal, because Paul is a Roman citizen. Before complying, however, King Agrippa wanted to see the matter himself, and in chapter 26, Paul defends himself before the king and gives his testimony once more. The king sees that there’s no reason for his imprisonment and says, “He could have been set free if he didn’t appeal to Caesar,” which isn’t true, seeing as Paul was continuously in jail even without appealing to Caesar. Paul made a wise move to appeal to Caesar (or Nero at the time) because it meant that he would have the opportunity to bring it up to the most authoritative person in the land. Every chapter so far has ended on a cliffhanger. This chapter, however, has somewhat of a conclusion, but it still leaves you wanting more."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":12,"title":"Paul Sails to Rome","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Luke has re-entered the journey with Paul to Rome, by the indication of “we” in this verse. Of course, Luke couldn’t be with Paul during those years of prison, but obviously Luke had a heart and prayer for Paul, otherwise he wouldn’t have been available and capable of coming with him at this point in the dialogue. 2 And entering into a ship of Adramyttium, we launched, meaning to sail by the coasts of Asia; one Aristarchus, a Macedonian of Thessalonica, being with us."},{"kind":"para","text":"If it remained close to the coast, this means the ship may have been somewhat small comparatively so that it could easily maneuver around the coast. 3 And the next day we touched at Sidon. And Julius courteously entreated Paul, and gave him liberty to go unto his friends to refresh himself."},{"kind":"para","text":"Julius may not have been a Christian, but something about Paul allowed Julius to be kind to him. Perhaps it was the friends that Paul hung around, or maybe it was the testimony that Paul had. It could have been the fact that after two years of imprisonment, there were still people trying to kill him, yet Paul still had friends who cared very much about him. Maybe Julius desired to believe in the message that Paul preached but just wasn’t quite there yet. Or perhaps it was an accumulation of all these things. I don’t know, but I do know that he did something very wonderful for Paul, maybe not even realizing how wonderful it was, and it had to be mentioned in the holiest book to ever be written. Julius may not have been remembered for many things, but he will be remembered for his kindness to a saint who was being mistreated everywhere else he seemed to go. 4 And when we had launched from thence, we sailed under Cyprus, because the winds were contrary."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sailing “under” Cyprus would mean that they found shelter while at sea so that they could be protected from the crazy winds while sailing close to the island. 5 And when we had sailed over the sea of Cilicia and Pamphylia, we came to Myra, a city of Lycia. 6 And there the centurion found a ship of Alexandria sailing into Italy; and he put us therein."},{"kind":"para","text":"These are places that are along the southern coast of what is today modern Turkey. Myra was directly north of Alexandria. It was a good stopping place for a ship that was sailing for Italy, probably carrying Egyptian grain. 7 And when we had sailed slowly many days, and scarce were come over against Cnidus, the wind not suffering us, we sailed under Crete, over against Salmone;"},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that the weather was getting pretty bad. Could it be a sign of something to come? That would be too obvious though… wouldn’t it? The normal route at this time from Myra to Rome would have been south of Rhodes and north of Crete to Sicily. They, obviously, were already way off course when they reached Cnidus (which was the southwestern tip of Asia/Turkey). When they looked for shelter in Crete, this brought them even further off course. 8 And, hardly passing it, came unto a place which is called The fair havens; nigh whereunto was the city of Lasea."},{"kind":"para","text":"I tried to find this place on a map, and couldn’t seem to get a straight answer. There is a place with that name today on the southern coast of Crete, but I don’t think that’s the same place… 9 Now when much time was spent, and when sailing was now dangerous, because the fast was now already past, Paul admonished them, 10 And said unto them, Sirs, I perceive that this voyage will be with hurt and much damage, not only of the lading and ship, but also of our lives."},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, during this time of year (the time of year of “the Fast?”) the weather would have been impossible to sail in. Paul probably knew this from personal experience, so he was suggesting a much safer option if they wanted to get to their destination alive. Ultimately, it was not up to Paul, however, and so Paul had to just go with the flow of the commander at this point…"},{"kind":"para","text":"11 Nevertheless the centurion believed the master and the owner of the ship, more than those things which were spoken by Paul."},{"kind":"para","text":"Bold strategy, let’s see how that plays out for him. 12 And because the haven was not commodious to winter in, the more part advised to depart thence also, if by any means they might attain to Phenice, and there to winter; which is an haven of Crete, and lieth toward the south west and north west."},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems the shipmates and crew were ready and willing to ignore the advice of Paul, and probably believed they had everything under control. I suppose this is what they do for a living, so they would know better, right?"}]}]},{"start":13,"end":38,"title":"The Storm at Sea","entries":[{"start":13,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, in those days, ships were controlled and pushed by the wind. If there was no wind, there was no going. The wind had to work in their favor, and they had to have specifically scheduled routes so that they could depend on the wind to work in their favor. Of course, they knew that it would be dangerous to sail with this weather, and Paul also understood the danger of it (warning them that it might not be the best idea to go just a few verses back), but they decided to ignore Paul and move on anyway. If Paul says anything now, it would just be adding salt to the wound… 16 And running under a certain island which is called Clauda, we had much work to come by the boat: 17 Which when they had taken up, they used helps, undergirding the ship; and, fearing lest they should fall into the quicksands, strake sail, and so were driven."},{"kind":"para","text":"This was apparently a very common place where ships went to die. It was a sandy shoal off the North African coast, and a graveyard of shipwrecks. Their ship could have been one of the many there had they not thought fast and lowered sail. 18 And we being exceedingly tossed with a tempest, the next day they lightened the ship; 19 And the third day we cast out with our own hands the tackling of the ship."},{"kind":"para","text":"Making the ship lighter would make it easier to control, but it would have been difficult for them to survive if they ever got stuck somewhere, not to mention these were more than likely trade items. Their lives were much more important than their trade at this moment, though."},{"kind":"para","text":"20 And when neither sun nor stars in many days appeared, and no small tempest lay on us, all hope that we should be saved was then taken away."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t understand why they didn’t just use their Tom Tom… (Is TomTom still a thing? Am I aging myself?) They could have just pulled navigation up on their smartphones and found land (obviously I’m joking here). They relied heavily on the sun and stars to find direction and course; otherwise, they could not navigate properly and they would end up in a completely different place or lost at sea with no way to know where they are supposed to be going. The storm was so bad that it covered the skies, and they were just floating along. 21 But after long abstinence Paul stood forth in the midst of them, and said, Sirs, ye should have hearkened unto me, and not have loosed from Crete, and to have gained this harm and loss. 22 And now I exhort you to be of good cheer: for there shall be no loss of any man's life among you, but of the ship. 23 For there stood by me this night the angel of God, whose I am, and whom I serve, 24 Saying, Fear not, Paul; thou must be brought before Caesar: and, lo, God hath given thee all them that sail with thee. 25 Wherefore, sirs, be of good cheer: for I believe God, that it shall be even as it was told me. 26 Howbeit we must be cast upon a certain island."},{"kind":"para","text":"There’s that salt in the wound I was speaking about before. Paul didn’t just leave them feeling bad for their negligence but gave them courage and hope. After he had said his peace about not sailing, he stayed suspiciously silent. My guess is that Paul was busy praying about what was going to happen next, knowing that God had led him in this direction on purpose. Paul has already been promised to be at Rome from the Lord previously."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the night following the Lord stood by him, and said, Be of good cheer, Paul: for as thou hast testified of me in Jerusalem, so must thou bear witness also at Rome.","ref":"Acts 23:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"He knew his life was already secure. Paul knew he would get to Rome somehow, but he didn’t see any way that everyone else was going to get there too. Luke, of course, is also among them and was probably just as worried about it as the rest of the crew there. But Paul gets an answer from an angel of the Lord, promising that every person on that ship would be safe. That was about to be tested in time, and I have no doubt that the crew of that ship doubted everything Paul was saying. But what choice did they have but to accept their fate? 27 But when the fourteenth night was come, as we were driven up and down in Adria, about midnight the shipmen deemed that they drew near to some country; 28 And sounded, and found it twenty fathoms: and when they had gone a little further, they sounded again, and found it fifteen fathoms. 29 Then fearing lest we should have fallen upon rocks, they cast four anchors out of the stern, and wished for the day."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were seen here “wishing” that day would come. They had no hope in anything else. The more accurate term would be “praying” for the day to come, but they weren’t praying in a biblical manner. They just wanted a god (whichever one was real) to give them protection. When turmoil strikes anybody, I don’t care how anti-Bible you are, you will be found on your knees praying that the pain would be gone. I’ve seen bigger men on their knees because of a tragedy in their lives. It is, sometimes, the only way that God will get a person’s attention. 30 And as the shipmen were about to flee out of the ship, when they had let down the boat into the sea, under colour as though they would have cast anchors out of the foreship, 31 Paul said to the centurion and to the soldiers, Except these abide in the ship, ye cannot be saved. 32 Then the soldiers cut off the ropes of the boat, and let her fall off."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were not going to trust Paul… They were going to have to trust the God that Paul serves. Paul says he spoke to the Lord on this matter, and an angel answered his request with victory, but only if they are willing to be controlled by God’s providence. God, seemingly, did not tell Paul that it will be smooth sailing (that’s a lie of the devil if they believed that). Rather, the angel said they would be cast on an island somewhere. It wouldn’t be easy, but it would be worth it. That is how it always is with the Lord. It is hardly ever easy listening to the Lord’s Will and following through with it, but I can’t say that I have ever regretted doing what the Lord bid me, even if it seemed that I got hurt in the process or made shipwreck… 33 And while the day was coming on, Paul besought them all to take meat, saying, This day is the fourteenth day that ye have tarried and continued fasting, having taken nothing. 34 Wherefore I pray you to take some meat: for this is for your health: for there shall not an hair fall from the head of any of you."},{"kind":"para","text":"They obviously didn’t have much food left. This may have been the last of what they had, and if they didn’t make it to shore, they wouldn’t have any food to continue on and would probably die of starvation. They would have to trust Paul now in this matter. 35 And when he had thus spoken, he took bread, and gave thanks to God in presence of them all: and when he had broken it, he began to eat. 36 Then were they all of good cheer, and they also took some meat. 37 And we were in all in the ship two hundred threescore and sixteen souls."},{"kind":"para","text":"It does not say there was a multiplication factor in this scene like when Jesus fed the five thousand. I don’t know how much food they actually had on board. Even if it was a little, Paul’s giving of thanks was a testimony of his trust in the Lord for this last provision, and it seemed to help encourage those that were witnessing Paul’s testimony in this. 38 And when they had eaten enough, they lightened the ship, and cast out the wheat into the sea."},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, they were getting rid of some of the last of their cargo to lighten the ship just a little bit more to control it better."}]}]},{"start":39,"end":44,"title":"The Shipwreck","entries":[{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They would have no choice but to stop the ship by running it into the shore. At this point, it would be far better worth it to just get to shore as quickly as possible than to try and stop the boat as light as it was. 40 And when they had taken up the anchors, they committed themselves unto the sea, and loosed the rudder bands, and hoised up the mainsail to the wind, and made toward shore. 41 And falling into a place where two seas met, they ran the ship aground; and the forepart stuck fast, and remained unmoveable, but the hinder part was broken with the violence of the waves. 42 And the soldiers' counsel was to kill the prisoners, lest any of them should swim out, and escape."},{"kind":"para","text":"Wait a second… This wasn’t in the plan at all! God said they would all make it safely to the shore, and now we are seeing the prisoners’ lives being threatened and they haven’t even hit shore! Rather, they are stuck in a reef. This incident goes to further prove that just because God wills someone to do something, doesn’t mean it will always be easy to do it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t like the term “easy beliefism.” It’s the ideology that if you have God in your life, everything will become easier. No, things actually get harder, but the DECISIONS become easier. Before, I would struggle with my flesh. If I did the right thing, those that don’t do the right thing will abuse that. If I did the wrong thing, I would have an easier time, but I would feel bad for not doing what was right. I could easily just cover up the bad and make myself feel good for doing it. Whereas, now that I know Christ, I must do the right thing, no matter the consequences."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul has been a testimony of that again and again, and will continuously be a testimony of that until the end of his life (actually, even after his life ends, because we are seeing his testimony still today). 43 But the centurion, willing to save Paul, kept them from their purpose; and commanded that they which could swim should cast themselves first into the sea, and get to land: 44 And the rest, some on boards, and some on broken pieces of the ship. And so it came to pass, that they escaped all safe to land."},{"kind":"para","text":"Okay, so we are on another cliffhanger… But at least we know they got to shore appropriately. None of them died, just like Paul said! They were all still well and fine… for now…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":13,"end":15},{"start":39,"end":39}],"verses":{"1":"And when it was determined that we should sail into Italy, they delivered Paul and certain other prisoners unto one named Julius, a centurion of Augustus' band.","13":"And when the south wind blew softly, supposing that they had obtained their purpose, loosing thence, they sailed close by Crete.","14":"But not long after there arose against it a tempestuous wind, called Euroclydon.","15":"And when the ship was caught, and could not bear up into the wind, we let her drive.","39":"And when it was day, they knew not the land: but they discovered a certain creek with a shore, into the which they were minded, if it were possible, to thrust in the ship."}},
    "28": {"chapter":28,"intro":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"…This day is the fourteenth day that ye have tarried and continued fasting, having taken nothing. Wherefore I pray you to take some meat: for this is for your health: for there shall not an hair fall from the head of any of you.","ref":"Acts 27:33-34"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul had promised by the Lord that he would make it to Rome on numerous occasions, and now this chapter will take up where the last chapter left off, on the island where they had shipwrecked."}],"previously":["Paul and his crew of men were being shipped (literally) over to Rome so that Paul could plead his case before Caesar Nero. During his travels, however, there was a great storm that passed through and caused them to become shipwrecked. They had faith in Paul’s testimony when he said…"],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":10,"title":"Coming to Malta","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Somehow, through the trials and trouble of the storm, and not even knowing which direction they were going…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when neither sun nor stars in many days appeared, and no small tempest lay on us, all hope that we should be saved was then taken away.","ref":"Acts 27:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"They somehow ended up on an island that was still on the same path to Rome. Melita was on that same route they were supposed to take anyway from Myra in the last chapter. Obviously, this is due to God’s providence, and only His providence alone, once again proving His promises kept to Paul and even those that were on the ship…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Saying, Fear not, Paul; thou must be brought before Caesar: and, lo, God hath given thee all them that sail with thee.","ref":"Acts 27:24 2 And the barbarous people shewed us no little kindness: for they kindled a fire, and received us every one, because of the present rain, and because of the cold. 3 And when Paul had gathered a bundle of sticks, and laid them on the fire, there came a viper out of the heat, and fastened on his hand."},{"kind":"para","text":"The kindness of these people was notable to Paul and Luke. It seems they felt undeserving of the kindness, and suddenly a strange disruption takes place among them when Paul is seemingly bitten by (not just an ordinary snake) a viper! Normal snakes usually don’t have short, sharp teeth, whereas vipers have fangs that will not only rip someone apart with them, but will inject poison and cause great health harm to whomever receives the bite. Yet, somehow, God is about to use this circumstance in a pretty miraculous way. 4 And when the barbarians saw the venomous beast hang on his hand, they said among themselves, No doubt this man is a murderer, whom, though he hath escaped the sea, yet vengeance suffereth not to live."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were quite quick to judge Paul based on the act of this snake. They probably had some sort of religion or tradition that teaches that snake bites are only meant for people that are deserving of it. They thought Paul was a murderer (and he once was), but that was far from the truth of how the snake ended up biting him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Today, there are churches who have snake tests that would determine whether or not you truly are chosen of God. If you survive the bite, then you must be chosen. If you don’t survive… well, you get the picture. They use this particular instance to try and prove their false concepts of God’s chosen, when it’s not through the bite of the snake that we are saved, but through the shed blood of Christ! 5 And he shook off the beast into the fire, and felt no harm. 6 Howbeit they looked when he should have swollen, or fallen down dead suddenly: but after they had looked a great while, and saw no harm come to him, they changed their minds, and said that he was a god."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a really funny picture to me. I imagine after the bite, they were all sitting around the fire, just staring at Paul, probably not even speaking to each other, expecting him to die or at least show signs of poisoning and death. After a while of waiting, staring, and anticipating in silence and thought… nothing happened… Paul was okay… So they changed their minds and said, “obviously this Paul guy must be a god if he doesn’t even die from a snake bite.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, Paul could have used this as a springboard into the gospel, but he doesn’t seem to do that here. At least it isn’t mentioned. He might be saving his energy for when he arrives at Rome, or maybe he was also confused as to why he didn’t feel any effects from the snake bite. He knew full well that Rome was already given to him, but that didn’t to say he wouldn’t get there poisoned and sick. God’s providence (once again) provides for Paul, and allows him to experience something that would be fatal to most people. It certainly got these people thinking and wondering what had just happened. 7 In the same quarters were possessions of the chief man of the island, whose name was Publius; who received us, and lodged us three days courteously. 8 And it came to pass, that the father of Publius lay sick of a fever and of a bloody flux: to whom Paul entered in, and prayed, and laid his hands on him, and healed him. 9 So when this was done, others also, which had diseases in the island, came, and were healed: 10 Who also honoured us with many honours; and when we departed, they laded us with such things as were necessary."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, multiple miracles are taking place, and no mention of the gospel being shared. Paul has not been the type to ignore the gospel, so my assumption is he either shared it and it wasn’t mentioned, or he used it as an opportunity to be an example so that someone else could come along and share it. There’s really no telling, but it certainly was a great means to helping them on the rest of their journey, so much so that there was basically no loss at all, even to their food and sustenance."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":16,"title":"Paul Finally Reaches Rome","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They had to wait three months so that it was safe for them to sail the rest of the way without weather being a possible issue again. 12 And landing at Syracuse, we tarried there three days. 13 And from thence we fetched a compass, and came to Rhegium: and after one day the south wind blew, and we came the next day to Puteoli: 14 Where we found brethren, and were desired to tarry with them seven days: and so we went toward Rome."},{"kind":"para","text":"The trip still seemed long, but it was much more doable now that they waited for the weather to clear up. Finally, we see Paul in Rome, which has been a huge build-up for the last several chapters now. It seems that everything has worked out for the better in Paul’s life, and he will finally get to see Rome, speak to Caesar, and share the gospel with as many as possible. 15 And from thence, when the brethren heard of us, they came to meet us as far as Appii forum, and The three taverns: whom when Paul saw, he thanked God, and took courage."},{"kind":"para","text":"Interesting enough, Paul didn’t know anybody from Rome. The letter to the Romans that Paul wrote was written three years prior to this moment, and it obviously had some sort of impact and allowed for the gospel to prosper so that they were greeted by Christian brothers. That alone is a huge encouragement to anybody, and especially to Paul who already had high hopes and great plans for the gospel in this great city. Not only so, but the people that met with Paul here travelled by foot over forty miles just to see this man whom they had never met before, knowing his testimony and anticipating his words and ministry (even though he was still a prisoner). 16 And when we came to Rome, the centurion delivered the prisoners to the captain of the guard: but Paul was suffered to dwell by himself with a soldier that kept him."},{"kind":"para","text":"A luxury only a high-status prisoner would receive (simply because Paul was a Roman citizen). The local believers may have had a say in helping with this housing situation as well."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":22,"title":"Paul’s Interview with Roman Jews","entries":[{"start":17,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul needed to plead his case before the leaders so they understood the reason he was in the predicament he was in. It would help encourage them as well when Nero certainly became ballistic and caused a great scene against the Christians of that day. Of course, Paul didn’t know that would take place, but he knew that there was bound to be trial and tribulation for those that followed after Christ, just as there was trial and tribulation for him, and for many of the other believers (including James (who was beheaded for his faith) and Peter (who was almost beheaded for his faith)). 21 And they said unto him, We neither received letters out of Judaea concerning thee, neither any of the brethren that came shewed or spake any harm of thee. 22 But we desire to hear of thee what thou thinkest: for as concerning this sect, we know that every where it is spoken against."},{"kind":"para","text":"Interesting. The news didn’t reach Rome. It probably didn’t reach Rome because the Jews knew they had no pull in that area for their personal religious beliefs. Even still, they wanted to know how to navigate these issues because it seems they were dealing with some of the same problems themselves with unbelieving Jews who would stand against the belief of Christ rising from the dead."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":28,"title":"The Response to Paul","entries":[{"start":23,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was important to Paul, and it should be important to every believer. There’s a need to speak about the things pertaining to Jesus and the kingdom of God. When we see people who are hurting, we might not be able to give them much, but we can give them the healer. Casting Crows writes, “More than comfort, we need the King. More than gifts, we need the Giver. More than blessings, we need His presence. More than the healing, we need the Healer.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul understood their need was not for more wisdom or for more of their religious ideologies. Their need was for more Jesus alone. Paul used the law to show their need for the Savior and then showed them the Savior of Jesus Christ. This is the way in which the Bible continuously points out representing the gospel. Every single witnessing opportunity, from Jesus to this last chapter of Acts, has had one thing in common. It starts off by presenting the law (which they all knew at that time, and no one can deny today) and then showing them the Savior who paid for their breaking of the law."},{"kind":"para","text":"The gospel cannot be the “Good news” if they don’t even know what that bad news is first. We must share the law if we are going to give the gospel accurately. Spurgeon said, “I do not believe that any man can preach the gospel who does not preach the Law. Lower the Law and you dim the light by which man perceives his guilt; this is a very serious loss to the sinner rather than a gain; for it lessons the likelihood of his conviction and conversion.” D.L. Moody said, “The law can only chase a man to Calvary, no further.” The law is what leads people to Jesus! Paul understood this all too well."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore the law was our schoolmaster to bring us unto Christ, that we might be justified by faith. But after that faith is come, we are no longer under a schoolmaster.","ref":"Galatians 3:24-25"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul used the law to help them understand the necessity of Jesus more, and hope that they would open their eyes to the truth of the gospel. 25 And when they agreed not among themselves, they departed, after that Paul had spoken one word, Well spake the Holy Ghost by Esaias the prophet unto our fathers, 26 Saying, Go unto this people, and say, Hearing ye shall hear, and shall not understand; and seeing ye shall see, and not perceive: 27 For the heart of this people is waxed gross, and their ears are dull of hearing, and their eyes have they closed; lest they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and should be converted, and I should heal them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Conviction hits hard when the truth is revealed to actually be the truth. May I just point out that Paul was not unloving or ungraceful in his tactic of witnessing here. He was using what they would understand to help them accept the only Savior that was promised to them from the Old Testament, which they claimed to believe. He desperately desired that they would believe, even though there was a whole troop after his head for preaching this exact same message. He wasn’t out to make enemies, but rather to make converts."},{"kind":"para","text":"28 Be it known therefore unto you, that the salvation of God is sent unto the Gentiles, and that they will hear it."},{"kind":"para","text":"They will listen because they weren’t nearly as entrenched with their religion and beliefs as the Jews were. It’s much more difficult to talk to a religionist than it is to talk to an atheist."}]}]},{"start":29,"end":31,"title":"Paul’s Ministry Accepted","entries":[{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Interestingly enough, this verse is also taken out of most modern translations, and it’s a verse that speaks volumes about what Paul said and the importance of leaving an impact even if people aren’t converted. The talk others have after you share the gospel may be negative, but it’s better that they think and talk about it behind your back than for them to completely miss what you said and never think about it at all. If people are upset about the gospel being presented, this is a good thing because it means they listened."}]},{"start":30,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a sweet ending… Except it’s not the end! He’s still on arrest. He hasn’t been let go, and it seems he was there for two more years this way, still not having the opportunity to talk to the Caesar, and not even knowing if Nero even knew who he was! The issue was probably never brought up to Nero, but Nero would eventually find out about this Jesus fellow, and the rest goes into secular history."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are left on a cliffhanger again, and this time there is no next chapter to find out what happens (some believe in an Acts Chapter 29, but I won’t get into that here). We know that Paul is eventually beheaded by a sword (The Foxes Book of Martyrs gives this account). Foxes Book might be the most accurate information as to what we have as far as Paul’s continued journey after this."},{"kind":"para","text":"It reads, “After suffering various persecutions at Jerusalem, Iconium, Lystra, Philippi, and Thessalonica, he was carried prisoner to Rome, where he continued for two years, and was then released. He afterwards visited the churches of Greece and Rome, and preached the gospel in Spain and France, but returning to Rome, he was apprehended by order of Nero, and beheaded.” Paul does not receive a fairytale ending (nor does any of the other disciples, including John the Revelator), but he certainly has a valiant ending, because he is seen remaining faithful even until the day his head was cut off."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":17,"end":20},{"start":23,"end":24},{"start":29,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":31}],"verses":{"1":"And when they were escaped, then they knew that the island was called Melita.","11":"And after three months we departed in a ship of Alexandria, which had wintered in the isle, whose sign was Castor and Pollux.","17":"And it came to pass, that after three days Paul called the chief of the Jews together: and when they were come together, he said unto them, Men and brethren, though I have committed nothing against the people, or customs of our fathers, yet was I delivered prisoner from Jerusalem into the hands of the Romans.","18":"Who, when they had examined me, would have let me go, because there was no cause of death in me.","19":"But when the Jews spake against it, I was constrained to appeal unto Caesar; not that I had ought to accuse my nation of.","20":"For this cause therefore have I called for you, to see you, and to speak with you: because that for the hope of Israel I am bound with this chain.","23":"And when they had appointed him a day, there came many to him into his lodging; to whom he expounded and testified the kingdom of God, persuading them concerning Jesus, both out of the law of Moses, and out of the prophets, from morning till evening.","24":"And some believed the things which were spoken, and some believed not.","29":"And when he had said these words, the Jews departed, and had great reasoning among themselves.","30":"And Paul dwelt two whole years in his own hired house, and received all that came in unto him,","31":"Preaching the kingdom of God, and teaching those things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ, with all confidence, no man forbidding him."}},
  },
};
